/* In-course Quiz Centre bank: the same quizzes as the QTI packages (generated by tools/build_qti.js) */
window.COURSE_QUIZBANK = [{"pkg":"End-of-Module Tests","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_33","title":"Module 1: Module 1 Test","desc":"<p>Module 1: Gathering, Organising and Processing Information. 22 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Which of the following is the MAIN reason a researcher should write a focused research question before choosing a data collection method?</p>","options":["It makes the final report look more academic.","It allows the researcher to skip the analysis stage.","It guarantees that respondents will answer honestly.","It determines what information is needed and therefore which methods are suitable."],"correct":3,"why":"The research question decides what data must be collected, so it guides the choice of method and sample. Option A is tempting because good questions do sound academic, but appearance is not the purpose; the question shapes every later stage of the research process.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>A Form 6 student wants to find out how teenagers in Dominica feel about a proposed curfew during Carnival. She asks only the members of her own youth group. Which of the following BEST describes the weakness of this sample?</p>","options":["It uses random sampling.","It breaks the rule of informed consent.","It is not representative of the population.","It is too large to analyse."],"correct":2,"why":"A convenience sample drawn from one youth group is unlikely to reflect the views of all teenagers on the island, so it is not representative and the findings would be biased. Option B is tempting because ethics matter, but nothing in the scenario suggests consent was ignored; the problem is who was chosen.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>Before handing out questionnaires on vaping, a researcher sends home a letter explaining the study and asks parents of students under 18 to sign it. This action reflects the ethical principle of</p>","options":["anonymity","corroboration","validity","informed consent"],"correct":3,"why":"Explaining the research and obtaining written permission, including from parents of minors, is informed consent. Anonymity is tempting because it is also an ethical principle, but anonymity is about not identifying participants in the findings, not about getting permission.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Items 4–6:</b> A research team from a community college wants to investigate why fewer young people in the Dutch Caribbean are joining traditional string bands.</p></blockquote><p>Which of the following are primary sources from which the team could gather information?</p>","options":["Textbooks on Caribbean music history","Encyclopedia entries on folk music","Newspaper reviews of past festivals","Interviews with string band members"],"correct":3,"why":"Interviews conducted by the team produce first-hand data, so they are primary. Newspaper reviews (C) are tempting because they come from the period, but they are reports by someone else interpreting the events, so they are secondary sources.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>Which of the following would be the BEST data collection instrument for probing young people's attitudes towards string band music in depth?</p>","options":["Structured observation schedule","Questionnaire with closed items","Focus group discussion","Document analysis"],"correct":2,"why":"A focus group lets young people discuss and build on each other's views, which is ideal for probing attitudes in depth. A closed questionnaire (B) is tempting because it reaches many people easily, but fixed options limit the depth of explanation the team needs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>If observation of band rehearsals is used, which of the following could be an ADVANTAGE for the research team?</p>","options":["Witnessing natural behaviour directly as it occurs","Controlling the behaviour of the musicians","Obtaining large amounts of numerical data quickly","Predicting the results before the study begins"],"correct":0,"why":"Observation allows researchers to see what people actually do, as it happens, rather than relying on what they say. Option C is tempting, but large numerical data sets come more readily from questionnaires and surveys.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>A questionnaire item reads: \"Do you agree that the new bus fares are unfair and that the service is poor?\" Which TWO weaknesses does this item show?</p>","options":["It is anonymous and closed.","It is vague and uses jargon.","It is open and too long.","It is leading and double-barrelled."],"correct":3,"why":"\"Do you agree that... unfair\" pushes the respondent to agree (leading), and it asks about fares AND service at once (double-barrelled). Option B is tempting because \"poor\" is a little vague, but the clearest faults are the leading wording and the two questions in one.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>A test intended to measure students' listening skills contains mainly questions on spelling. Which of the following BEST describes this test?</p>","options":["It is highly reliable.","It lacks validity.","It lacks currency.","It is a secondary source."],"correct":1,"why":"Validity is the extent to which a test measures what it aims to measure; a spelling-based test does not measure listening. Option A is tempting because the test might give the same results each time (reliability), but consistent results do not make it measure the right thing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<blockquote><blockquote>\"Every serious athlete on the island is drinking PowerCane. Shouldn't you be drinking it too?\" (advertisement for a sports drink)</blockquote></blockquote><p>The reasoning in this advertisement is an example of</p>","options":["a bandwagon fallacy","corroboration","an authoritative source","documentary evidence"],"correct":0,"why":"The advertisement suggests you should drink the product simply because others do, which is the bandwagon fallacy: popularity does not prove quality. Option C is tempting because athletes are mentioned, but no named expert or authority gives evidence.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<blockquote><blockquote>A website run by a cruise company states that \"cruise tourism brings only benefits to small islands\". No author, date or supporting data are given.</blockquote></blockquote><p>Which of the following BEST evaluates the credibility of this information?</p>","options":["It is less credible because the producer has a commercial interest and the claim is undated and unsupported.","It is less credible because it is a primary source.","It is credible because it comes from a company with experience in tourism.","It is credible because it is published online."],"correct":0,"why":"The company benefits if readers accept the claim (motive), and with no author, date or data, the claim cannot be checked for currency or corroborated. Option C is tempting because experience can suggest authority, but experience does not cancel out a clear commercial bias.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<blockquote><blockquote>Our reefs are the island's quiet guardians. They break the force of storm waves, shelter young fish and draw divers from around the world. Yet each year, more of them turn ghostly white.</blockquote></blockquote><p>The phrase \"the island's quiet guardians\" is an example of</p>","options":["statistics and expert opinion","rhetorical question and repetition","metaphor and personification","simile and hyperbole"],"correct":2,"why":"The reefs are directly called guardians (metaphor) and given the human role of protecting (personification). Option D is tempting, but there is no \"like\" or \"as\", so it is not a simile, and the claim is not an exaggeration.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>The writer's purpose in this extract is MOST likely to</p>","options":["entertain readers with a story about divers","compare Caribbean reefs with reefs elsewhere","warn readers that the island's protective reefs are being lost","explain how fish reproduce in reef habitats"],"correct":2,"why":"The writer first lists how reefs protect and benefit the island, then uses \"Yet\" to reveal that they are dying, which creates a warning. Option D is tempting because young fish are mentioned, but that detail supports the main point rather than being the focus.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>Which of the following is the MOST complete statement of a writer's main point?</p>","options":["Social media","Social media and Caribbean families","Constant social media use is reducing the time Caribbean teenagers spend talking with their families, weakening family relationships.","The effects of social media on teenagers"],"correct":2,"why":"A main point must be a complete idea that states what the writer claims, not just a topic. Option D is tempting because it is more specific than A, but it is still a phrase that does not say what those effects are.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<blockquote><p><b>Item 14</b> is based on a short talk read aloud twice by your teacher about preparing for hurricane season.</p></blockquote><p>While the talk is being read, which of the following is the BEST listening practice?</p>","options":["Note key words and signal words, and wait until the reading is complete before answering.","Listen only to the first reading and use the second reading to answer.","Begin answering the questions as soon as the main idea becomes clear.","Write down every word the speaker says."],"correct":0,"why":"Selective notes on key words and signal words capture the structure without falling behind, and waiting until the end ensures you do not miss information that completes or changes the meaning. Option C is tempting to save time, but the final part of a talk often contains the speaker's real purpose.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>In a Module 1 essay, which of the following should the conclusion do?</p>","options":["Introduce one final language technique not discussed earlier.","Be longer than the introduction to show depth.","Evaluate a source not mentioned in the body paragraphs.","Restate the main point and purpose briefly without new analysis."],"correct":3,"why":"A conclusion sums up the main point, purpose and how the techniques achieve it; it must be shorter than the introduction and contain no new analysis. Options A and C are tempting because they add content, but new points belong in the body, where they can be developed with PEEL.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>\"The steelpan rang out across the savannah as the morning sun turned the dust to gold.\" This sentence is MAINLY an example of which form of discourse?</p>","options":["Descriptive","Argumentative","Persuasive","Expository"],"correct":0,"why":"The sentence uses sound and sight imagery to help the reader experience a scene, which is descriptive discourse. Expository is tempting because the sentence states what happened, but it does not explain or inform; its aim is to evoke.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>A vlog shows viewers, step by step, how to secure hurricane shutters safely. This vlog is BEST classified as</p>","options":["descriptive","expository","argumentative","narrative"],"correct":1,"why":"Instructions and explanations that inform the audience are expository, even when delivered as a vlog. Argumentative is tempting because the topic is safety, but the vlog does not take a position or argue a claim.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>An article opens with a story about one family's flooded home, then explains how storm surge forms, and ends by demanding that government build sea defences. Its dominant form and purpose are</p>","options":["argumentative, to persuade government to act","narrative, to entertain","descriptive, to evoke the flood","expository, to explain storm surge"],"correct":0,"why":"The whole extract builds towards a claim and a call to action, so the dominant form is argumentative and the purpose is to persuade. The narrative opening is tempting, but it is a hook that serves the argument.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>\"The more we tried to save time with the new road, the more time we spent in traffic.\" This sentence is an example of</p>","options":["euphemism","synecdoche","paradox","oxymoron"],"correct":2,"why":"A whole statement that seems contradictory but reveals a truth is a paradox. Oxymoron is tempting, but an oxymoron places two contradictory words side by side, as in \"deafening silence\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>Which of the following is an assumption commonly associated with traditional media such as newspapers and radio?</p>","options":["Hypertextual, networked content","Susceptibility to conspiracy theories","Responsible reporting and objectivity","A narrowly targeted audience"],"correct":2,"why":"Traditional media are commonly assumed to be objective, reliable and responsible, though they may face censorship. Susceptibility to conspiracy theories is tempting as a media issue, but it is an assumption usually made about new media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>A research team needs to find out both how many fishermen have changed fishing grounds since sargassum increased and why they did so. Which research design is MOST appropriate?</p>","options":["A single case study","Mixed methods","An experiment","Document analysis only"],"correct":1,"why":"\"How many\" requires quantitative data and \"why\" requires qualitative data, so mixed methods (for example, questionnaires plus interviews) fit best. A case study is tempting for depth, but one case cannot show how many fishermen changed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>A student writes in a formal report: \"At the end of the day, the council needs to think outside the box.\" The expressions \"at the end of the day\" and \"think outside the box\" are BEST described as</p>","options":["jargon","clichés","argot","idiomatic Creole expressions"],"correct":1,"why":"Overused phrases that have lost their force are clichés and weaken formal writing. Jargon is tempting, but jargon is the specialised vocabulary of a profession, not worn-out everyday phrases.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_6v","title":"Module 2: Module 2 Test","desc":"<p>Module 2: Language and Community. 27 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Fourteen-year-old Javon creates the word \"phonezombie\" for a classmate who never looks up from his phone, and soon the whole form uses it. This demonstrates that language is</p>","options":["dynamic","arbitrary","displaced","symbolic"],"correct":0,"why":"A new word entering a group's everyday use shows that language changes and grows: it is dynamic. \"Arbitrary\" is tempting because any new word is arbitrary, but the point of the item is the creation and spread of a new form, which is change.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>Shania refers to her friends as \"fam\" and \"bestie\" when they lime after school. This usage is an illustration of</p>","options":["slang","jargon","register","dialect"],"correct":0,"why":"Very informal, in-group words used among friends are slang. Jargon is tempting, but jargon is the specialised vocabulary of a profession or activity, not friendly in-group talk.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>In which of the following countries does a Creole have official language status alongside a European language?</p>","options":["Barbados","Haiti","Jamaica","Saint Lucia"],"correct":1,"why":"Haiti recognises both Haitian Creole and French as official languages. Saint Lucia is tempting because Kwéyòl is widely spoken there, but its official language is English.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>Which group of Caribbean territories shares a French-lexified Creole heritage?</p>","options":["Barbados, Jamaica, Guyana","Aruba, Curaçao, Bonaire","Belize, Sint Maarten, Antigua","Dominica, Haiti, Saint Lucia"],"correct":3,"why":"Kwéyòl in Dominica and Saint Lucia and Haitian Creole all take most of their vocabulary from French. Aruba, Curaçao and Bonaire are tempting because they are not English-speaking, but their Papiamentu draws mainly on Portuguese and Spanish.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>A speaker of an English-based Creole says \"spector\" for \"inspector\" and \"gainst\" for \"against\". Which phonological process is at work?</p>","options":["Colloquial aphesis","Metathesis","Epenthesis","Consonant cluster reduction"],"correct":0,"why":"Colloquial aphesis is the loss of an unstressed sound or syllable at the beginning of a word. Cluster reduction is tempting because sounds are lost, but cluster reduction drops a consonant at the END of a word, as in \"han'\" for \"hand\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>The pronunciations \"aks\" for \"ask\" and \"cripsy\" for \"crispy\" are examples of</p>","options":["aphesis","th-stopping","metathesis","epenthesis"],"correct":2,"why":"In metathesis, sounds swap places in a word. Epenthesis is tempting, but epenthesis ADDS a sound, as in \"sinake\" for \"snake\"; nothing is added here.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<blockquote><p>\"Di teacher does give we plenty homework.\"</p></blockquote><p>In the sentence above, \"does\" functions as a</p>","options":["marker of past tense","main verb meaning \"performs\"","pre-verbal particle marker showing habitual action","question marker"],"correct":2,"why":"\"Does\" comes systematically before the verb \"give\" to show that the action happens regularly: a habitual pre-verbal particle marker. \"Past tense\" is tempting, but past time in many Caribbean Creoles is marked by \"did\", \"ben\" or \"en\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>The sentences \"Mi siki\" (Jamaican) and \"Mwen malad\" (Kwéyòl), both meaning \"I am sick\", illustrate which feature of Caribbean Creole grammar?</p>","options":["Plural marking with \"dem\"","Predicative use of adjectives without a form of \"be\"","Subject-verb agreement","Multiple negation"],"correct":1,"why":"The adjective itself acts as the predicate with no copula, a structure patterned on West African sources. Subject-verb agreement is tempting, but there is no verb to agree with; that is the point.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<blockquote><p>At the bank, Ms. Richardson tells a client, \"Your loan application has been approved.\" Turning to her colleague, she adds, \"Girl, dat man face light up like Christmas!\"</p></blockquote><p>Ms. Richardson's change of variety is BEST described as</p>","options":["code-switching","diglossia","a pidgin","colloquial aphesis"],"correct":0,"why":"She moves between Standard English and Creole within a conversation to suit two different audiences: code-switching. Diglossia is tempting, but it describes the separate functions of two varieties in a whole community, not one speaker's moves.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>A child whose first language is an English-based Creole is taught entirely in Standard English. Which of the following is NOT a challenge this child is likely to face?</p>","options":["Similarities between English and other European languages","Fear of making mistakes because of negative attitudes to Creole","Confusion caused by overlaps between Creole and English vocabulary","Believing she already speaks English because English is official"],"correct":0,"why":"Similarities between English and other European languages, such as French, do not affect a Creole speaker learning English. The overlap option is tempting to choose, but overlaps between Creole and English are a real and well-known challenge.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<blockquote><p>During a job interview at a Montego Bay hotel, Devon leans back, puts his feet on the chair rung and greets the panel with \"Wha' happenin', boss?\"</p></blockquote><p>Which factor did Devon FAIL to consider in choosing his register?</p>","options":["The language of the hotel's guests","The context of the communication","The channel of the message","The length of his message"],"correct":1,"why":"A job interview is a formal context calling for a formal register and an upright posture. \"Channel\" is tempting, but face-to-face speech was appropriate; it was his register and body language that ignored the context.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<blockquote><p>\"Ever since my teacher told me Creole would keep me from getting a job, I feel ashamed when I use it,\" Nadia admits.</p></blockquote><p>Nadia's attitude and the factor that produced it are BEST described as</p>","options":["indifference, produced by personal experience","pride, produced by socialisation","embarrassment, produced by education","admiration, produced by history and society"],"correct":2,"why":"\"Ashamed\" signals embarrassment, and the source is a teacher's comment: education. Socialisation is tempting, but socialisation refers to family and community, not schooling.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>At a funeral in Trinidad and Tobago, most mourners wear white. This use of colour to convey a cultural message is called</p>","options":["haptics","chromatics","chronemics","proxemics"],"correct":1,"why":"Chromatics is the use of colour to convey meanings, emotions or cultural messages. Proxemics is tempting as another \"-emics\" term, but it refers to the use of space.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>A teenager uses the username \"@CastriesFinest\" and a Saint Lucian flag as her profile picture. These are BEST described as</p>","options":["jargon","idiomatic expressions","digital identifiers","paralanguage"],"correct":2,"why":"A digital identifier is any digital representation, such as a name, icon or picture, used to uniquely identify a person. Idiomatic expressions are tempting because the name carries meaning, but idioms are figurative sayings like \"cut your eye\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>\"Mi foot a hurt mi\" uses \"foot\" to mean the whole leg. This is an example of</p>","options":["cluster reduction","metathesis","epenthesis","semantic shift"],"correct":3,"why":"An English word has taken on a different meaning in Creole: semantic shift, a lexical feature. The other options are phonological processes about sounds, but the pronunciation of \"foot\" has not changed, only its meaning.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>Which theory of creole origin argues that children who heard only an unstable pidgin used an inborn human capacity for language to create a full creole grammar?</p>","options":["The language bioprogram (universalist) hypothesis","Relexification","Superstrate theory","Monogenesis"],"correct":0,"why":"The bioprogram hypothesis explains creole grammar through children's innate language ability. Monogenesis is tempting because it is also a theory of origin, but it traces creoles to a single early Portuguese-based pidgin, not to an inborn capacity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>Similarities between English-lexified and French-lexified creoles are explained by some linguists as the result of one ancestral pidgin whose vocabulary was later replaced word by word. This replacement process is called</p>","options":["semantic shift","code-switching","relexification","language shift"],"correct":2,"why":"Relexification is the replacement of a language's vocabulary while its grammar remains, a key idea in monogenesis. Language shift is tempting, but it describes a community abandoning one language for another, not swapping vocabulary within one.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>The MOST appropriate label for languages such as Garifuna, Lokono, Wapishana and Warao, which are spoken in the Caribbean region, is</p>","options":["Creole languages","registers","indigenous languages","dialects"],"correct":2,"why":"These languages were born in the region and its mainland rim before European colonisation, so they are indigenous languages. Creole languages is tempting because they are Caribbean, but creoles arose later from contact situations such as the plantation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>Caribbean Hindustani and Bhojpuri, still heard in parts of Trinidad, Guyana and Suriname, are BEST described as</p>","options":["dialects of Hindi brought by indentured labourers","English-lexified creoles","indigenous languages","varieties of Papiamentu"],"correct":0,"why":"The syllabus describes Hindustani and Bhojpuri as dialects of Hindi; they arrived with Indian indentured labourers after emancipation. Indigenous languages is tempting because they have long been in the region, but they were brought from Asia, not born here.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>The majority of speakers of a French Creole in one territory are elderly, and few children are learning it. Which role of language in Caribbean identity is MOST threatened?</p>","options":["Demarcation of social groups","Building of community","Assertion of authority","Transmission of culture"],"correct":3,"why":"When a language is not passed to children, the culture it carries cannot be transmitted across generations. Building of community is tempting, but the key problem is the break between generations.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>A hotel refuses to employ a qualified applicant only because she speaks with a strong Creole accent, although the post involves no contact with guests. Which role of language is MOST likely being breached?</p>","options":["Social","Ethical","Political","Psychological"],"correct":1,"why":"Unfair treatment and discrimination because of the way someone speaks is an ethical issue. Psychological is tempting because the applicant may lose confidence, but the hotel's action itself breaches fairness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>During a conversation with a visiting American, a straw-market vendor in Nassau gradually adjusts her speech to sound more like the visitor's. This is an example of</p>","options":["relexification","divergence","convergence","diglossia"],"correct":2,"why":"Adjusting one's speech to become more like the listener's is convergence. Divergence is tempting because it is the paired term, but divergence emphasises differences rather than reducing them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>A government plans to send an urgent hurricane warning to the whole population by text message. Which of the following is likely to present the GREATEST problem?</p>","options":["Cost of each message to the government","Production volume of messages","Access to devices by the population","Speed of dissemination"],"correct":2,"why":"Not everyone has a mobile phone, signal or power, so access is the greatest obstacle to reaching the whole population. Cost is tempting, but text messages are relatively cheap compared with the risk of people not receiving the warning.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>During a tense meeting, a student gives his teacher a long, hard stare instead of replying. This non-verbal behaviour is BEST classified as</p>","options":["chronemics","haptics","artefacts","oculesics"],"correct":3,"why":"Oculesics is the study of eye behaviour, such as staring, gaze and eye contact. Artefacts is tempting as another non-verbal code, but it refers to objects, clothing and appearance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>A candidate applying for a bank job uses the email address \"partyqueen_sxm@...\" This is BEST described as</p>","options":["code-mixing","a phatic expression","an inappropriate digital identifier","jargon"],"correct":2,"why":"A digital identifier, such as an email address or username, should suit the context; a party nickname is inappropriate for a formal job application. Code-mixing is tempting because it involves language choice, but it refers to blending two languages in one utterance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>\"Over time, the meaning of language changes and language becomes more complex.\" This statement BEST describes which characteristic of language?</p>","options":["Non-instinctive","Arbitrary","Maturational","Symbolic"],"correct":2,"why":"Growth in complexity over time is the maturational characteristic. Non-instinctive is tempting because it is about acquisition, but it refers to language being learned from a community rather than inherited.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>The Creole phrase \"eye-water\" (tears) is an example of which vocabulary feature?</p>","options":["Serial verb construction","Hypercorrection","Semantic transparency","Metathesis"],"correct":2,"why":"\"Eye-water\" shows its meaning on the surface: water from the eye. Hypercorrection is tempting as another named feature, but it is a phonological process, such as adding /h/ in \"hegg\".","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_as","title":"Module 3: Module 3 Test","desc":"<p>Module 3: Speaking and Writing. 28 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Ms. Hodge, Head of the Business Department at Palmetto Point Secondary in Montserrat, must explain a new coursework late-submission policy to her department. Which sequence describes what she should do to communicate effectively?</p>","options":["Select the channel, transmit the message, encode the message","Transmit the message, encode the message, select the channel","Decode the message, select the channel, transmit the message","Encode the message, select the appropriate channel, transmit the message"],"correct":3,"why":"The sender first encodes the idea into words, then chooses a suitable channel and transmits the message. Option A is tempting because channel choice is important, but a message cannot be sent before it has been encoded; decoding is the receiver's task, not the sender's.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>What is the BEST way for Ms. Hodge to judge whether her colleagues have understood the new policy?</p>","options":["Restating the policy several times","Assessing the feedback she receives","Giving them additional written instructions","Assuming they understood because nobody objected"],"correct":1,"why":"Feedback is the only way a sender can verify that a message was understood; without it, communication cannot be confirmed as complete. Restating the policy (A) may help, but it still gives her no evidence of understanding, and silence (B) is not proof.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>During a face-to-face meeting, a manager notices a worker frowning while she explains a new roster, so she rephrases her point mid-sentence. Which model of communication BEST describes this exchange?</p>","options":["Transactional","Shannon–Weaver","Interactive","Linear"],"correct":0,"why":"Both people are sending and receiving messages at the same time and adjusting to each other, which is the transactional model. Interactive (C) is tempting, but it describes turn-taking, where feedback comes only after the sender has finished.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>A notice says, \"Frederick Street closes to traffic at 6 p.m.\" It is posted on the morning of the Carnival parade, for drivers, to prevent traffic jams. The details about when, for whom and why the notice was posted describe its</p>","options":["channel","feedback","context","content"],"correct":2,"why":"Context is where, when, to whom and why a message is sent. The content (D) is the actual words, \"Frederick Street closes to traffic at 6 p.m.\", not the circumstances around them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>The disaster management office plans to send an urgent hurricane warning to the entire population of St. Vincent and the Grenadines by text message only. Which of the following presents the GREATEST problem for this plan?</p>","options":["Cost of sending the message","Length of the message","Access to mobile devices across the population","Speed of sending the message"],"correct":2,"why":"Not everyone owns a phone or has a working network after a storm, so access is the greatest barrier to reaching the entire population. Speed (A) is actually an advantage of text messaging, not a problem.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>Jerome greets an interview panel at a hotel in Negril with \"Wassup, guys?\" Which factor did Jerome FAIL to consider?</p>","options":["Context","Message","Gender","Medium"],"correct":0,"why":"A job interview is a formal context that requires a formal register, so Jerome misjudged the context. Gender (C) is tempting because \"guys\" is not gender sensitive, but the main error is using a casual greeting in a formal situation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<p>A rumour that the principal will be transferred spreads quickly through the staff room and WhatsApp chats before any official announcement. This is an example of</p>","options":["the grapevine","horizontal communication","downward communication","upward communication"],"correct":0,"why":"Unofficial, unconfirmed information passed informally is the grapevine. It may travel between peers, which makes horizontal communication (B) tempting, but horizontal communication refers to formal coordination between people at the same level.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>A clinic wants to remind residents over 70, many with limited schooling and no smartphones, about free eye tests. Which medium is MOST suitable?</p>","options":["A QR code on posters","A spoken announcement at church and community centres, with large-print flyers","An Instagram post","An email newsletter"],"correct":1,"why":"The audience profile (age, educational background, limited access) points to spoken and large-print media where these residents already gather. The QR code (D) and Instagram (A) depend on smartphones the audience does not have.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>At a parents' meeting, a guest speaker addresses the audience only as \"gentlemen\" and assumes all the nurses in the room are women. Which barrier is MOST evident?</p>","options":["Gender","Technological","Semantic","Physical"],"correct":0,"why":"Gender-exclusive language and assumptions about roles create a gender barrier; the repair is gender sensitive language such as \"everyone\" and \"nurses\". Semantic (C) is tempting because it involves word choice, but the problem is exclusion by gender, not confusing vocabulary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>A Spanish-speaking family in Belmopan cannot understand an English-only flyer about a dengue outbreak. Which repair BEST addresses this barrier?</p>","options":["Repeating the English flyer on social media","Posting the flyer in more locations","Printing the flyer in larger type","Translating the flyer into Spanish and adding clear pictures"],"correct":3,"why":"The barrier is semantic (language), so the repair must change the language: translation, supported by pictures. Posting more copies (D) or using social media (C) spreads the same English message without making it understandable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>A video circulates in which a public figure's face has been digitally placed on another person's body so that he appears to say something he never said. This is BEST described as</p>","options":["a deepfake","a big lie","gaslighting","a false flag"],"correct":0,"why":"A video in which a person's face or body is digitally altered to make them appear to be someone else is a deepfake. A big lie (A) is a gross, repeated distortion of facts used as propaganda, which does not depend on altered video.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>A group vandalises its own campaign posters and then publicly blames a rival group to win public sympathy. This tactic is BEST described as</p>","options":["misinformation","bias","opinion","a false flag"],"correct":3,"why":"A harmful action designed to look as though someone else carried it out is a false flag. Misinformation (A) is false information shared without intent to deceive, but this act is deliberate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13","q":"<p>\"Hurricane season starts in two weeks: the time to prepare is now.\" Which rhetorical appeal does this line MAINLY use?</p>","options":["Logos","Kairos","Ethos","Pathos"],"correct":1,"why":"The line uses timeliness, the urgency of the approaching season, which is kairos. Logos (A) is tempting because a date is a fact, but the persuasive force comes from the timing, not from reasoning or evidence.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14","q":"<p>A Carnival fete advert says: \"Feel the soca in your soul! Half-price tickets until Wednesday, and free shuttles so nobody drinks and drives.\" Which appeals are combined?</p>","options":["Logos only","Ethos and kairos","Pathos, kairos and logos","Ethos and pathos only"],"correct":2,"why":"\"Feel the soca in your soul\" stirs excitement (pathos), \"until Wednesday\" uses timing (kairos), and cheap tickets and free shuttles give practical reasons (logos). There is no credible speaker or authority, so options with ethos (B, B) are incorrect.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15","q":"<p>Which of the following qualifies as digital media technology that could enhance a presentation to school administrators?</p>","options":["A chalkboard","A Canva slideshow displayed on a smart TV","A printed handout","A transistor radio"],"correct":1,"why":"Presentation software on a smart TV is digital media technology that is attractive, easy to use and plays back instantly. A transistor radio (A) is an analogue device and is not accepted as digital media technology unless it is specified as digital.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16","q":"<p>\"Who says what, in which channel, to whom, with what effect?\" This question summarises</p>","options":["the transactional model","Berlo's SMCR model","Schramm's interactive model","Lasswell's model"],"correct":3,"why":"Lasswell's model frames communication as five questions ending with the effect on the audience. Berlo's SMCR model is tempting because it is also linear, but it is built around source, message, channel and receiver factors, not the five questions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17","q":"<p>In Berlo's SMCR model, the \"channel\" refers to</p>","options":["the feedback the receiver gives","the five senses through which a message is received","the noise that interferes with a message","the context in which communication occurs"],"correct":1,"why":"Berlo describes channels as the senses: hearing, seeing, touching, smelling and tasting. Feedback is tempting, but the SMCR model, like other linear models, does not include feedback.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18","q":"<p>At a hotel, the head of the accounts department emails a junior chef in the kitchen directly about a missing invoice. This is an example of</p>","options":["upward communication","the grapevine","horizontal communication","diagonal communication"],"correct":3,"why":"The message crosses both departments and levels of the hierarchy, which is diagonal (cross-channel) communication. Horizontal is tempting because two departments are involved, but horizontal communication is between people at the same level.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19","q":"<p>\"Those who want a curfew for minors want to lock all young people indoors forever.\" This statement is an example of which fallacy?</p>","options":["Begging the question","Red herring","Strawman","Hasty generalisation"],"correct":2,"why":"The speaker exaggerates and misrepresents the opposing view to make it easier to attack: a strawman. Red herring is tempting, but a red herring changes the subject rather than distorting the opponent's position.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20","q":"<p>\"Either we build the new cruise pier or the island's economy will collapse.\" This statement is BEST described as</p>","options":["an either/or fallacy","faulty causation","a testimonial","a false analogy"],"correct":0,"why":"Only two options are offered when others exist, which is the either/or fallacy (false dilemma). Faulty causation is tempting because the economy is mentioned, but the error is the false choice, not a mistaken cause.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21","q":"<p>A student says \"I'm fine, Miss\" in a flat voice while avoiding eye contact and slumping in her chair. Her non-verbal cues are MAINLY</p>","options":["reinforcing her words","contradicting her words","regulating the conversation","substituting for her words"],"correct":1,"why":"Her voice, eyes and posture send a message opposite to her words, so they contradict them. Reinforcing is tempting, but reinforcing cues match and strengthen the words.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22","q":"<p>A friend tells you about her parents' divorce. You listen patiently, nod and say, \"That sounds really hard.\" You are MAINLY using</p>","options":["critical listening","comprehensive listening","empathic listening","appreciative listening"],"correct":2,"why":"Listening to offer emotional and moral support is empathic listening. Comprehensive listening is tempting because you do understand her, but your main purpose is support.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23","q":"<p>A Minister reads a carefully worded statement on a new immigration policy at a press conference. This mode of delivery is</p>","options":["manuscript","extemporaneous","memorised","impromptu"],"correct":0,"why":"Reading aloud from a full script is manuscript delivery, chosen when exact wording matters. Extemporaneous is tempting for a prepared talk, but extemporaneous speakers use brief notes, not a full script.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24","q":"<p>Which of the following is the MOST serious weakness in a presenter's visual aids?</p>","options":["Reading aloud a slide filled with a full page of text","Using one main idea per slide","Using large dark font on a pale background","Labelling each chart clearly"],"correct":0,"why":"A text-heavy slide read aloud overloads the audience and makes the speaker redundant. The other options are principles of effective design, not weaknesses.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25","q":"<p>Which of the following sentences is written in the correct style for minutes of a meeting?</p>","options":["\"The Treasurer reported that the car wash had made a profit.\"","\"I think the car wash was a great success.\"","\"We will discuss the car wash at the next meeting.\"","\"Malik says the car wash made loads of money!\""],"correct":0,"why":"Minutes use reported speech, the past tense and the third person, and they stay objective. The first option is tempting because it records the report, but it uses the present tense and slang.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26","q":"<p>A document sent to members before a meeting, listing apologies, confirmation of minutes, matters arising and items of business in order, is</p>","options":["an agenda","a notice","a memorandum","the minutes"],"correct":0,"why":"An agenda sets out, in order, the items to be discussed. A notice is tempting because it is also sent before the meeting, but a notice mainly announces the date, time and venue.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27","q":"<p>Which of the following BEST describes a person's digital footprint?</p>","options":["A professional email address","The speed of a person's internet connection","The number of devices a person owns","The permanent trail of content a person posts, shares and interacts with online"],"correct":3,"why":"A digital footprint is the lasting record of a person's online activity, which others, including employers, may see. A professional email address is tempting because it is part of an online identity, but it is a digital identifier, not the whole footprint.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28","q":"<p>A student moves her strongest argument to the final paragraph and deletes a point that does not support her thesis. At which stage of the writing process is she working?</p>","options":["Revising","Proofreading","Publication","Prewriting"],"correct":0,"why":"Revising improves content and organisation, such as reordering and cutting points. Proofreading is tempting because it is also a checking stage, but it deals only with surface errors such as spelling and punctuation.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_ch","title":"Paper 3: Paper 3 (Paper 032) Test","desc":"<p>Paper 3: Paper 3: Research, Audience, Purpose and Production. 12 multiple-choice items. Pass mark 70%.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1","q":"<p>Which statement correctly describes Paper 032?</p>","options":["One long essay on the student's own topic","Three optional questions on three different stimuli","Three compulsory questions covering the three Modules, based on a central stimulus","Two essays chosen from five topics"],"correct":2,"why":"The regulations state three compulsory questions, covering the three Modules and based on a central stimulus. Option B is tempting because it mentions three questions, but they are compulsory and share one stimulus.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2","q":"<p>What may a candidate bring into Paper 032?</p>","options":["Two sheets with 5 points each","One sheet of notes plus a printed guide","Any handwritten notes","A single sheet of double-sided A4 paper with no more than 10 points related to the topic of the response"],"correct":3,"why":"For the 2027 examination, only a single double-sided A4 sheet with no more than 10 points, and nothing else, is permitted. Option A looks equivalent because the total is still 10 points, but only one card is allowed.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3","q":"<p>What are the topic and intended audience for 2027?</p>","options":["Non-communicable diseases in children, parents","Climate change, teenagers","Natural disasters, homeowners","Cyberbullying, parents"],"correct":0,"why":"2027 is non-communicable diseases in children, for parents. Option D is tempting because its audience is also parents, but cyberbullying is the 2030 topic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4","q":"<p>A candidate answers Q1(a) with: \"Literacy and cost.\" Based on the specimen mark scheme, what is this likely to earn out of 4?</p>","options":["1","4","0","3"],"correct":0,"why":"Two challenges identified but not explained earn 1 mark; 2 marks each require clear explanation. Option C is too harsh, because correct identification is still credited.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5","q":"<p>Which answer best fits: \"Justify ONE appropriate traditional medium, other than the printed medium, for informing elderly homeowners in a rural village about free roof inspections\"?</p>","options":["\"Radio, because it is cheap to own, reaches rural districts and can repeat the message several times a day while older listeners go about their chores.\"","\"A flyer, because people can keep it.\"","\"Instagram, because it is popular.\"","\"A newspaper advert, because it reaches many readers.\""],"correct":0,"why":"Radio is traditional, not print, and the answer gives reasons linked to the target group. Options D and B are print, which the question excludes, and Instagram is digital rather than traditional.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6","q":"<p>Which is the strongest comment on a local source of information about a children's health screening programme?</p>","options":["\"Parents could consult the nurse at their community clinic, who is trained and works directly with the screening programme, so her information is accurate and current.\"","\"A friend's group chat, because it is fast.\"","\"The internet.\"","\"Television.\""],"correct":0,"why":"It names a credible local source and explains its authority and currency, earning the full 2 marks. Option B is fast but unreliable, and options C and D are identification without comment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7","q":"<blockquote><p>\"Homeowners are advised that applications for the shutter subsidy close on 30 April. Proof of ownership must be submitted.\"</p></blockquote><p>Which answer to \"Explain the dialectal variety and register used\" earns full marks?</p>","options":["\"Creole, to connect with homeowners.\"","\"Standard English and formal.\"","\"Good, clear language.\"","\"Standard English, because it is the official variety used by government; formal register, because the notice is official and concerns a serious financial matter.\""],"correct":3,"why":"Each feature is identified and explained, earning 1 + 1 twice. Option B identifies both correctly but explains neither, so it earns only 1 mark.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8","q":"<p>\"A strap in time saves the roof.\" Which literary technique does this slogan use?</p>","options":["Allusion","Personification","Onomatopoeia","Rhetorical question"],"correct":0,"why":"It alludes to the proverb \"A stitch in time saves nine\", suggesting early action prevents greater damage. It is not a rhetorical question, since it is a statement, not a question.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9","q":"<p>How is Question 3(a), the 200–250 word piece, marked in the specimen?</p>","options":["Out of 40, for length","Out of 10, for grammar only","Out of 25, in bands, for creativity, organisation and expression","Out of 15, for purpose, audience and context only"],"correct":2,"why":"The piece is marked out of 25 in bands considering creativity, organisation and expression. Option D describes the justification, which is the separate 15-mark part.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10","q":"<p>Which sentence in a justification names the CONTEXT of communication?</p>","options":["\"The intended audience is homeowners in hillside districts.\"","\"The purpose is to persuade homeowners to secure their roofs.\"","\"It is about hurricanes.\"","\"It would be published in the home section of a weekend newspaper in May.\""],"correct":3,"why":"Context means where and how the piece appears in the real world. Option C is tempting, but \"about hurricanes\" is content, not context.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11","q":"<p>\"With hurricane season six weeks away, there has never been a better moment to check your roof.\" Which rhetorical appeal is this?</p>","options":["Ethos","Logos","Pathos","Kairos"],"correct":3,"why":"Kairos appeals to timeliness: why act now. Logos may seem possible because a reason is given, but the reason rests on timing rather than evidence.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12","q":"<p>A student has 15 minutes left. Section B's piece is finished, but there is no justification. What should the student do FIRST?</p>","options":["Polish the piece's opening","Leave early","Write the 50–100 word justification of purpose, intended audience and context","Recheck Section A"],"correct":2,"why":"The justification is worth 15 marks, so securing it is the priority. Polishing the opening (A) feels productive but cannot recover 15 missing marks.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Module 1","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_ea","title":"Module 1 · Unit 1 quiz: Information and the Research Process","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 1: Information and the Research Process. 15 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Put the research in order","q":"<p>Kaylee is investigating why fewer students at her school attend the Friday sports clubs. Put her steps in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["She chooses the topic: student participation in after-school sports.","She writes the question: Why has attendance at Friday sports clubs fallen among Form 3 students this year?","She decides to use a questionnaire for 60 Form 3 students and an interview with the PE teacher.","She gets consent forms signed and distributes the questionnaires.","She counts the responses and notices that most students mention the new bus schedule.","She presents her findings in a short report with a bar chart to the principal."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which is the best research question?","q":"<p>A student wants to research the effect of the tourism season on young people in The Bahamas. Which is the BEST research question?</p>","options":["What is tourism?","Is tourism good or bad for the island?","How does part-time work during the cruise season affect the school attendance of Form 5 students in Nassau?","Why do tourists love The Bahamas so much?"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["This is a definition question. It can be answered from a dictionary and does not require you to collect or analyse any data.","This is too broad and invites a yes/no opinion. Good for whom? In what way? A research question must be narrow enough to investigate.","Excellent. This question is focused (part-time work, school attendance), names a specific group and place, and can be investigated by collecting data from students and school records.","This question assumes an answer (that tourists love the island), which makes it biased. It also moves away from the stated focus on young people."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sampling quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Jayden is researching how students at his school travel to school. Answer each quick question.</p><p>All 520 students at the school are the...</p>","options":["population","sample","respondents"],"correct":0,"why":"The population is the whole group you want to know about. The sample is the smaller group you actually ask.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sampling quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>Jayden puts every student's name in a box and draws 50. This is...</p>","options":["convenience sampling","random sampling","purposive sampling"],"correct":1,"why":"Every student had an equal chance of being drawn, which is the definition of random sampling.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sampling quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>He only asks students waiting at the bus stop. Why is this a problem?</p>","options":["Students who walk or are driven are left out, so the sample is biased.","The sample is too large.","Bus stops are noisy."],"correct":0,"why":"The sample would over-represent bus users and give a misleading picture of how the whole school travels.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sampling quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>He chooses 10 students from each form level, Form 1 to Form 5. This is...</p>","options":["convenience sampling","stratified sampling","systematic sampling"],"correct":1,"why":"He divided the population into groups (form levels) and chose from each, which is stratified sampling.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sampling quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>To understand the school's transport policy, he interviews the deputy principal. This is...</p>","options":["purposive sampling","random sampling","systematic sampling"],"correct":0,"why":"He chose the deputy principal on purpose because of her special knowledge. That is purposive sampling.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which research design?","q":"<blockquote><p>A youth council in Barbados wants to find out how many secondary students have stopped using public buses since fares rose, and the reasons behind their decisions. It has six weeks and a small team.</p></blockquote><p>Which research design is MOST suitable, and why?</p>","options":["A case study of one student who no longer takes the bus.","Qualitative interviews only, with the bus drivers.","Mixed methods: a questionnaire to a sample of students to count how many have stopped, followed by focus groups to explore their reasons.","An experiment in which fares are lowered for one school to see what happens."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["One student gives depth but cannot tell the Council how many students have stopped. The design must match both parts of the question.","Drivers are not the population being studied, and interviews alone will not produce reliable numbers about students.","Correct. The Council needs both numbers (\"how many\") and reasons (\"why\"). A questionnaire supplies the first and focus groups the second, and both fit a six-week timeline.","An experiment can show cause and effect, but the Council cannot control bus fares, and changing them for one school would be impractical and unfair."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Ethical or unethical? (1)","q":"<p>A group of Form 6 students is researching vaping among teenagers in the Cayman Islands. Sort each action.</p><p>Is this research action ethical?</p><p><b>They send home consent forms for parents of participants under 18.</b></p>","options":["Ethical","Unethical"],"correct":0,"why":"Minors need parental consent. This is exactly right.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Ethical or unethical? (2)","q":"<p>Is this research action ethical?</p><p><b>They post a funny screenshot of one student's answers in the class group chat.</b></p>","options":["Ethical","Unethical"],"correct":1,"why":"This breaks confidentiality and could cause real embarrassment or harm.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Ethical or unethical? (3)","q":"<p>Is this research action ethical?</p><p><b>Questionnaires do not ask for names.</b></p>","options":["Ethical","Unethical"],"correct":0,"why":"This protects anonymity, which also encourages honest answers on a sensitive topic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Ethical or unethical? (4)","q":"<p>Is this research action ethical?</p><p><b>They tell participants they can skip any question or stop at any time.</b></p>","options":["Unethical","Ethical"],"correct":1,"why":"This respects the right to withdraw.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Ethical or unethical? (5)","q":"<p>Is this research action ethical?</p><p><b>They secretly record conversations in the bathroom.</b></p>","options":["Unethical","Ethical"],"correct":0,"why":"Covert recording without consent invades privacy and breaks the principle of informed consent.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Ethical or unethical? (6)","q":"<p>Is this research action ethical?</p><p><b>They change a few answers so the results look more dramatic.</b></p>","options":["Unethical","Ethical"],"correct":0,"why":"Falsifying data is dishonest and destroys the reliability of the whole study.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Ethical or unethical? (7)","q":"<p>Is this research action ethical?</p><p><b>They ask the principal for permission before handing out questionnaires at school.</b></p>","options":["Unethical","Ethical"],"correct":1,"why":"Getting permission from those responsible for the premises is good practice.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_ha","title":"Module 1 · Unit 2 quiz: Sources and Methods of Data Collection","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 2: Sources and Methods of Data Collection. 26 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the sources (1)","q":"<p>Shania is researching how the 2017 hurricanes affected fishing families in Dominica. Sort her sources.</p><p>What type of source is each item?</p><p><b>Her recorded interview with a fisherman from Scotts Head.</b></p>","options":["Secondary","Primary","Tertiary"],"correct":1,"why":"She collected this first-hand, so it is primary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the sources (2)","q":"<p>What type of source is each item?</p><p><b>Photographs a fisherman took of his damaged boat.</b></p>","options":["Secondary","Primary","Tertiary"],"correct":1,"why":"Original images created at the time of the event are primary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the sources (3)","q":"<p>What type of source is each item?</p><p><b>A magazine feature analysing the recovery of the fishing industry.</b></p>","options":["Secondary","Tertiary","Primary"],"correct":0,"why":"The writer interprets and reports on other people's information, so it is secondary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the sources (4)","q":"<p>What type of source is each item?</p><p><b>An online encyclopedia entry on Atlantic hurricanes.</b></p>","options":["Primary","Tertiary","Secondary"],"correct":1,"why":"Encyclopedias summarise many sources, so they are tertiary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the sources (5)","q":"<p>What type of source is each item?</p><p><b>A documentary that combines interviews with a narrator's commentary.</b></p>","options":["Secondary","Tertiary","Primary"],"correct":0,"why":"The documentary interprets primary material for an audience, so it is secondary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the sources (6)","q":"<p>What type of source is each item?</p><p><b>Her own questionnaire completed by 30 fishing households.</b></p>","options":["Tertiary","Primary","Secondary"],"correct":1,"why":"Data she collected herself is primary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the sources (7)","q":"<p>What type of source is each item?</p><p><b>A dictionary of Caribbean English used to define \"seine\".</b></p>","options":["Tertiary","Secondary","Primary"],"correct":0,"why":"Dictionaries are reference collections, so they are tertiary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it or reject it? (1)","q":"<p>Two Form 6 students are planning research. Study A: <b>garbage disposal practices in your community</b> (needs reliable sources). Study B: <b>experiences of COVID-19 patients in Trinidad and Tobago</b> (needs valid sources). Decide whether each source should be used or rejected.</p><p>Should the researcher use this source?</p><p><b>Study A: residents who live in the community</b></p>","options":["Use it","Reject it"],"correct":0,"why":"They experience the disposal practices directly, so they are a reliable source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it or reject it? (2)","q":"<p>Should the researcher use this source?</p><p><b>Study A: the community's sanitation workers</b></p>","options":["Reject it","Use it"],"correct":1,"why":"They collect the garbage and know the volumes and types of waste: reliable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it or reject it? (3)","q":"<p>Should the researcher use this source?</p><p><b>Study A: an internet article on garbage disposal</b></p>","options":["Reject it","Use it"],"correct":0,"why":"A general secondary source that does not speak to your community.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it or reject it? (4)","q":"<p>Should the researcher use this source?</p><p><b>Study A: people from the next community</b></p>","options":["Use it","Reject it"],"correct":1,"why":"Their collection days and practices may differ, so they are not representative.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it or reject it? (5)","q":"<p>Should the researcher use this source?</p><p><b>Study B: Couva Hospital records, March to May 2020</b></p>","options":["Reject it","Use it"],"correct":1,"why":"Right territory, right disease, right time: valid.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it or reject it? (6)","q":"<p>Should the researcher use this source?</p><p><b>Study B: interviews with COVID-19 survivors in Trinidad and Tobago</b></p>","options":["Reject it","Use it"],"correct":1,"why":"First-hand experience in the right place and period: valid.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it or reject it? (7)","q":"<p>Should the researcher use this source?</p><p><b>Study B: flu data from 2018</b></p>","options":["Reject it","Use it"],"correct":0,"why":"A different virus, before the pandemic began: not valid.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Use it or reject it? (8)","q":"<p>Should the researcher use this source?</p><p><b>Study B: Jamaican hospital records, March to May 2020</b></p>","options":["Use it","Reject it"],"correct":1,"why":"The right period but the wrong territory: not valid for Trinidad and Tobago.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the flawed questions","q":"<p>Darnell drafted a questionnaire on students' use of the school library. Some items have wording problems.</p><p>Select EVERY question that is badly worded.</p>","options":["3. Do you find the library quiet and well stocked?","2. Don't you think the library should open on Saturdays like every sensible school?","1. Which form are you in? (Form 4 / Form 5 / Form 6)","6. What one change would make the library more useful to you?","5. Do you go to the library often?","4. How many times did you visit the library last week? (0 / 1–2 / 3–4 / 5 or more)"],"correct":[0, 1, 4],"why":"Well spotted. Question 2 is leading and loaded (\"every sensible school\"), question 3 is double-barrelled (quiet AND well stocked), and question 5 is vague (\"often\" means different things to different people). Questions 1, 4 and 6 are clear."},{"t":"match","title":"Match the method","q":"<p>Match each method to the situation it suits best.</p>","left":["Questionnaire","Semi-structured interview","Non-participant observation","Focus group","Documentary research","Participant observation"],"right":["Finding out how 200 students rate the canteen menu","Exploring how a group of teenagers feels about a proposed curfew","Learning in depth why a vendor at the cruise pier changed her prices","Recording how drivers behave at a busy Bush Road junction","Tracing how the island's tourism figures have changed over twenty years","Joining a Carnival troupe to understand how costumes are made"],"correct":[0,2,3,1,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which instrument or tool? (1)","q":"<p>Choose the best instrument or tool for each research task.</p><p>Counting how many drivers use a phone at the Bush Road roundabout in one hour.</p>","options":["questionnaire","interview schedule","observation checklist"],"correct":2,"why":"You are recording behaviour as it happens, so you tick an observation checklist.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which instrument or tool? (2)","q":"<p>Guiding a discussion with eight teenagers about a new curfew.</p>","options":["card catalogue","observation checklist","interview schedule for a focus group"],"correct":2,"why":"A focus group moderator follows an interview schedule of questions and prompts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which instrument or tool? (3)","q":"<p>Finding peer-reviewed journal articles on coral bleaching.</p>","options":["electronic database","note-taking app","telephone"],"correct":0,"why":"Electronic databases store journal articles and let you search them quickly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which instrument or tool? (4)","q":"<p>Collecting responses from 300 students across three schools in one week.</p>","options":["document analysis","online survey app","face-to-face interviews"],"correct":1,"why":"An online survey reaches many people quickly and tallies the answers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which instrument or tool? (5)","q":"<p>Studying how the island's newspapers reported a hurricane in 1995.</p>","options":["observation checklist","questionnaire","document analysis"],"correct":2,"why":"The newspapers are existing documents, so you analyse them.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a method: the market study (step 1)","q":"<p>You have been asked by the Kingstown Market Vendors' Association to find out why fewer local residents shop at the Saturday market. Make the best research decisions.</p><p>The Association wants to know <b>how many</b> residents have stopped shopping at the market and <b>why</b>. Which approach do you choose?</p>","options":["A questionnaire for a large sample of residents, followed by a few interviews.","Just read online reviews of the market.","Ask three of your friends at school what they think."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good thinking. The questionnaire gives you numbers; the interviews give you the reasons behind them. Combining methods suits a \"how many and why\" purpose.","Online reviews are written mostly by visitors and by people with strong feelings. They will not tell you how many residents stopped coming.","This is a tiny convenience sample. Your friends are not representative of island residents, and many do not do the family shopping."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a method: the market study (step 2)","q":"<p>You draft your first question: <i>\"Don't you agree that the market has become too expensive?\"</i> Your partner frowns. What do you do?</p>","options":["Rewrite it as: \"Which of the following best explains why you shop at the market less often? (Prices / Parking / Opening hours / Choice of goods / Other: ____)\"","Keep it. Price is probably the reason anyway."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. The question is neutral, offers clear options and includes \"Other\" so respondents are not forced into your categories.","This is a leading question. You are assuming the answer before collecting data, which will bias your results."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing a method: the market study (step 3)","q":"<p>You are ready to collect data. Where and how will you find respondents?</p>","options":["Distribute questionnaires in several districts on different days, with a short note explaining the purpose and that answers are anonymous.","Stand outside one supermarket in Calliaqua on Saturday morning."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong choice. Spreading collection across districts and days makes the sample more representative, and the note supports informed consent and anonymity.","Better than asking friends, but you would reach only one area and one type of shopper. The sample would be biased."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Interview types","q":"<p>A reporter for a school newspaper interviews the chef of a popular fish-fry stall in Gros Islet. She prepares eight questions but also asks follow-up questions when the chef mentions something interesting. This is a...</p>","options":["unstructured interview","focus group","structured interview","semi-structured interview"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["An unstructured interview has no prepared list, just a topic and a free conversation. The reporter prepared eight questions.","A focus group involves several participants discussing a topic together. Here there is only one interviewee.","A structured interview sticks to fixed questions in a fixed order with no follow-ups. The reporter went beyond her list.","Correct. She had prepared questions (structure) but was flexible enough to probe interesting answers. This combination is what makes it semi-structured."]}]},{"id":"assess_iz","title":"Module 1 · Unit 3 quiz: Evaluating Information and Sources","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 3: Evaluating Information and Sources. 16 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Which test is it? (1)","q":"<p>A student is evaluating sources for an essay on coral reef damage around Jamaica. Sort each comment under the test it applies.</p><p>Which evaluation test does each comment address?</p><p><b>The article was written by a marine scientist with twenty years of reef research.</b></p>","options":["Reliability","Currency and Relevance","Credibility and Authority"],"correct":2,"why":"This is about who produced it and their expertise: credibility and authority.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which test is it? (2)","q":"<p>Which evaluation test does each comment address?</p><p><b>The report was published in 2009, before the major bleaching events.</b></p>","options":["Reliability","Credibility and Authority","Currency and Relevance"],"correct":2,"why":"This is about how up to date it is: currency.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which test is it? (3)","q":"<p>Which evaluation test does each comment address?</p><p><b>The same figures appear in two other independent reports.</b></p>","options":["Reliability","Credibility and Authority","Currency and Relevance"],"correct":0,"why":"Information that can be cross-checked and is consistent is reliable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which test is it? (4)","q":"<p>Which evaluation test does each comment address?</p><p><b>The study is about reefs in Australia, not the Caribbean.</b></p>","options":["Credibility and Authority","Reliability","Currency and Relevance"],"correct":2,"why":"This questions whether it fits the topic and setting: relevance.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which test is it? (5)","q":"<p>Which evaluation test does each comment address?</p><p><b>The website belongs to a dive company that wants more tourists.</b></p>","options":["Currency and Relevance","Credibility and Authority","Reliability"],"correct":1,"why":"This questions the producer's motive, which affects credibility.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which test is it? (6)","q":"<p>Which evaluation test does each comment address?</p><p><b>The post says \"experts agree\" but names no experts and gives no data.</b></p>","options":["Credibility and Authority","Reliability","Currency and Relevance"],"correct":1,"why":"Without named sources or data, the claim cannot be verified, so its reliability is low.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Find the opinions","q":"<p>Read this letter to a local newspaper about a proposed new hotel at a beach.</p><p>Select EVERY sentence that is an opinion, not a fact.</p>","options":["The proposed hotel would have 300 rooms.","This monstrous project is the worst idea the government has ever approved.","The developer held one public meeting in March.","We must stop these greedy investors before it is too late.","It would be built on the northern end of the beach.","Any sensible resident can see that the beach will be ruined forever."],"correct":[1, 3, 5],"why":"Well done. Sentences 3, 5 and 6 contain judgements and loaded language (\"monstrous\", \"worst\", \"sensible\", \"ruined forever\", \"greedy\", \"must\"). Sentences 1, 2 and 4 can be checked against records, so they are facts."},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or new media? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each characteristic or assumption into the type of media it is usually associated with.</p><p>Traditional media or new media?</p><p><b>A centralised structure in which editors decide what is published.</b></p>","options":["Traditional media","New media"],"correct":0,"why":"Editors and owners control the content: a centralised, traditional structure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or new media? (2)","q":"<p>Traditional media or new media?</p><p><b>Hyperlinks connect one article to dozens of others.</b></p>","options":["New media","Traditional media"],"correct":0,"why":"Hypertextuality is a feature of digital, new media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or new media? (3)","q":"<p>Traditional media or new media?</p><p><b>Aimed at a general audience: everyone who tunes in to the evening news.</b></p>","options":["Traditional media","New media"],"correct":0,"why":"Traditional broadcasts address a broad, general audience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or new media? (4)","q":"<p>Traditional media or new media?</p><p><b>Algorithms show posts to a targeted audience based on their interests.</b></p>","options":["Traditional media","New media"],"correct":1,"why":"Targeting individual users is a hallmark of new media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or new media? (5)","q":"<p>Traditional media or new media?</p><p><b>Assumed to practise responsible reporting, though it may face censorship.</b></p>","options":["New media","Traditional media"],"correct":1,"why":"These are the assumptions usually made about traditional media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or new media? (6)","q":"<p>Traditional media or new media?</p><p><b>Assumed to be more susceptible to fake news and conspiracy theories.</b></p>","options":["New media","Traditional media"],"correct":0,"why":"Because anyone can publish, new media are assumed to be less reliable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or new media? (7)","q":"<p>Traditional media or new media?</p><p><b>Readers comment, share and reply within seconds.</b></p>","options":["New media","Traditional media"],"correct":0,"why":"Instant interaction is a key feature of new media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Traditional or new media? (8)","q":"<p>Traditional media or new media?</p><p><b>Little or no chance for the audience to respond directly.</b></p>","options":["New media","Traditional media"],"correct":1,"why":"Limited interaction is typical of traditional media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluating a source in context","q":"<blockquote><blockquote>\"Electric cars are the future for small islands,\" says the website <i>IslandDrive.com</i>, which sells imported electric cars. \"Our customers save thousands every year, and nine out of ten would never go back to petrol.\"</blockquote></blockquote><p>Which statement is the BEST evaluation of this source?</p>","options":["It is reliable because it gives a statistic.","It is unreliable because it is about cars.","It is a primary source, so it is trustworthy.","Its credibility is weakened because the producer sells electric cars and benefits if readers believe the claim, and the statistic cannot be verified."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["A statistic is not automatically reliable. We do not know how many customers were asked, when or how, so \"nine out of ten\" cannot be verified.","The topic is not the problem. The issue is who produced it, why, and whether the evidence can be checked.","Source type does not decide trustworthiness. Primary sources can still be biased, and here the producer has a clear commercial motive.","Excellent. You have identified the producer's motive (credibility) and the unverifiable statistic (reliability). This is the kind of evaluation examiners reward."]}]},{"id":"assess_kc","title":"Module 1 · Unit 4 quiz: Main Point, Author's Purpose and Reading Strategies","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 4: Main Point, Author's Purpose and Reading Strategies. 8 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"From topic to complete main point","q":"<p>An extract discusses tourism on Nevis. Arrange these attempts from the vaguest (top) to the complete main point (bottom).</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Tourism","Tourism and the environment","The negative effects of tourism","Rapid, poorly planned tourism development is damaging the island's beaches and reefs, which threatens the very environment that attracts visitors."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4"],"correct":[0,1,2,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Find the connecting idea","q":"<blockquote><blockquote><p>Every evening across the island, families gather around the dinner table, but many are no longer really together. Parents scroll through work emails while children watch videos with earphones in.</p><p>Grandparents, who once told stories of the old salt-pond days, now find that no one is listening. Their knowledge, passed down orally for generations, risks being lost.</p><p>Teachers report that some students struggle to hold a conversation, maintain eye contact or read the emotions on a classmate's face.</p></blockquote></blockquote><p>Which statement BEST expresses the main point of this extract?</p>","options":["Technology","Heavy use of personal devices is weakening face-to-face communication across generations, in families and in schools.","Families eat dinner together every evening.","Grandparents tell stories about the salt ponds."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This is only a topic. It does not say what the writer claims about technology.","Excellent. This connects all three paragraphs: the dinner table, the grandparents and the classroom. It is a complete statement, not a topic.","This is a detail from paragraph 1, and the extract actually suggests they are not truly together. A main point must cover the whole extract.","This is a detail from paragraph 2 only. It does not connect to the classroom paragraph."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the purpose verb","q":"<p>Match each extract description to the most precise purpose verb.</p>","left":["A Met Office bulletin describes how a tropical wave forms and moves.","An editorial attacks the council for leaving potholes unrepaired for months.","A health article cautions that sharing vapes spreads infections.","A coach's column urges young athletes to keep training after a loss.","An article sets local fishing methods beside industrial methods to show the differences.","A humorous column describes a chaotic family trip to the beach on Easter Monday."],"right":["criticize","entertain","explain","compare","warn","encourage"],"correct":[2,0,4,5,3,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the tone","q":"<blockquote><blockquote><p>Every morning the treadmill and I renew our quarrel. Its belt seems to be moving me backwards as I begrudgingly barrel forward, staring at an outdated screen that glows like burning coals. The brochure promised that running indoors would leave me somehow connected to nature. Perhaps it meant one of nature's cruel parts.</p></blockquote></blockquote><p>Which word BEST describes the writer's tone in this passage?</p>","options":["Exuberant","Neutral","Sarcastic","Nostalgic"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Exuberant writing is full of excitement and joy. Nothing here is joyful: the writer is mocking the machine.","A neutral tone reports without feeling. Words like \"quarrel\" and \"cruel\" carry strong feeling.","Correct. \"Begrudgingly\", \"burning coals\" and the mocking \"somehow connected to nature\" show a sarcastic, critical attitude.","Nostalgia is a fond longing for the past. The writer is not looking back fondly at anything."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which reading strategy? (1)","q":"<p>Choose the best strategy for each situation.</p><p>You need to find the date of the next ferry to Saba in a long timetable.</p>","options":["scanning","SQ3R","skimming"],"correct":0,"why":"You are looking for one specific detail, so you scan.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which reading strategy? (2)","q":"<p>You have two minutes to get the general idea of a Paper 2 extract before reading it closely.</p>","options":["intensive reading","skimming","scanning"],"correct":1,"why":"Getting the gist quickly is skimming.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which reading strategy? (3)","q":"<p>You are studying a textbook chapter for a test next week and want to remember it.</p>","options":["SQ3R","skimming","scanning"],"correct":0,"why":"SQ3R is a full study method designed for understanding and remembering.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which reading strategy? (4)","q":"<p>You must analyse the writer's word choice in paragraph 3.</p>","options":["skimming","intensive reading","scanning"],"correct":1,"why":"Analysing language requires slow, careful, intensive reading.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_nd","title":"Module 1 · Unit 5 quiz: Forms of Discourse, Modes of Reading and Usage","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 5: Forms of Discourse, Modes of Reading and Usage. 24 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Which form of discourse? (1)","q":"<p>Each excerpt comes from a text. Sort it by its main form of discourse.</p><p>Narrative, descriptive, expository or argumentative/persuasive?</p><p><b>\"When the ferry finally reached Saba, Keisha grabbed her bag and ran up the pier, terrified she had missed her grandmother's birthday.\"</b></p>","options":["Descriptive","Argumentative / persuasive","Narrative","Expository"],"correct":2,"why":"Events in sequence, a character and rising tension: narrative.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form of discourse? (2)","q":"<p>Narrative, descriptive, expository or argumentative/persuasive?</p><p><b>\"The morning I sat my first CAPE exam, I woke at four, prayed, ate nothing, and walked the long way to school.\"</b></p>","options":["Expository","Narrative","Descriptive","Argumentative / persuasive"],"correct":1,"why":"A personal sequence of past events: a narrative journal-style account.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form of discourse? (3)","q":"<p>Narrative, descriptive, expository or argumentative/persuasive?</p><p><b>\"The old salt pond on Grand Turk lay flat and silver under the noon sun, its edges crusted white and smelling faintly of sea and mud.\"</b></p>","options":["Argumentative / persuasive","Narrative","Expository","Descriptive"],"correct":3,"why":"Sight, touch and smell images with no argument and no story: descriptive.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form of discourse? (4)","q":"<p>Narrative, descriptive, expository or argumentative/persuasive?</p><p><b>\"Her market stall was a riot of colour: scarlet peppers, golden mangoes and bunches of green thyme piled high.\"</b></p>","options":["Argumentative / persuasive","Narrative","Descriptive","Expository"],"correct":2,"why":"Vivid sensory detail to help the reader see the stall: descriptive.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form of discourse? (5)","q":"<p>Narrative, descriptive, expository or argumentative/persuasive?</p><p><b>\"To register for the scholarship, first download the form, then attach your transcript, and finally submit it before 30 June.\"</b></p>","options":["Expository","Argumentative / persuasive","Descriptive","Narrative"],"correct":0,"why":"Step-by-step instructions with sequence words: expository.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form of discourse? (6)","q":"<p>Narrative, descriptive, expository or argumentative/persuasive?</p><p><b>\"Sargassum is a brown seaweed that drifts in large mats across the Atlantic and can release hydrogen sulphide as it rots.\"</b></p>","options":["Expository","Descriptive","Narrative","Argumentative / persuasive"],"correct":0,"why":"A definition and explanation in a neutral tone: expository.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form of discourse? (7)","q":"<p>Narrative, descriptive, expository or argumentative/persuasive?</p><p><b>\"If we truly love this island, we must ban single-use plastics now, before our reefs are lost for good.\"</b></p>","options":["Argumentative / persuasive","Expository","Descriptive","Narrative"],"correct":0,"why":"A clear position, \"must\", an emotional appeal and urgency: persuasive.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which form of discourse? (8)","q":"<p>Narrative, descriptive, expository or argumentative/persuasive?</p><p><b>\"Although some argue that a curfew protects teenagers, the evidence suggests that youth programmes reduce crime more effectively.\"</b></p>","options":["Descriptive","Expository","Argumentative / persuasive","Narrative"],"correct":2,"why":"A claim, an opposing view and evidence-based reasoning: argumentative.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"P, I or E? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each text type by its most likely main purpose.</p><p>What is the most likely purpose of this text?</p><p><b>A radio advertisement for a new phone plan</b></p>","options":["Entertain","Persuade","Inform"],"correct":1,"why":"Advertisements aim to persuade you to buy.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"P, I or E? (2)","q":"<p>What is the most likely purpose of this text?</p><p><b>A school debate on banning phones in class</b></p>","options":["Inform","Persuade","Entertain"],"correct":1,"why":"Debates exist to persuade the audience and judges.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"P, I or E? (3)","q":"<p>What is the most likely purpose of this text?</p><p><b>A statistical report on tourist arrivals at V. C. Bird International Airport</b></p>","options":["Inform","Entertain","Persuade"],"correct":0,"why":"Statistical reports present data objectively: inform.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"P, I or E? (4)","q":"<p>What is the most likely purpose of this text?</p><p><b>A police incident report on a minibus collision</b></p>","options":["Persuade","Entertain","Inform"],"correct":2,"why":"Incident reports record facts: inform.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"P, I or E? (5)","q":"<p>What is the most likely purpose of this text?</p><p><b>A biology textbook chapter on photosynthesis</b></p>","options":["Inform","Entertain","Persuade"],"correct":0,"why":"Academic textbooks explain and educate: inform.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"P, I or E? (6)","q":"<p>What is the most likely purpose of this text?</p><p><b>An Anansi story told at a storytelling evening</b></p>","options":["Persuade","Inform","Entertain"],"correct":2,"why":"Folklore engages imagination and emotion: entertain.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"P, I or E? (7)","q":"<p>What is the most likely purpose of this text?</p><p><b>A calypso performed at the Carnival Village</b></p>","options":["Persuade","Inform","Entertain"],"correct":2,"why":"Songs are mainly written to entertain.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"P, I or E? (8)","q":"<p>What is the most likely purpose of this text?</p><p><b>A one-act play staged by the drama club</b></p>","options":["Persuade","Inform","Entertain"],"correct":2,"why":"Plays tell stories through characters and dialogue: entertain.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"P, I or E? (9)","q":"<p>What is the most likely purpose of this text?</p><p><b>A candidate's campaign speech in Basseterre</b></p>","options":["Inform","Persuade","Entertain"],"correct":1,"why":"Campaign speeches aim to win votes: persuade.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Form and purpose in an extract","q":"<blockquote><blockquote><p>Last August, fifteen-year-old Dario cycled to Willemstad every morning, a hot, dusty ride along a road with no shoulder and trucks roaring past within an arm's length.</p><p>Road safety data from many countries show that separated cycle lanes reduce injuries. Painted lines cost little; a protective kerb costs more but saves lives.</p><p>Our island cannot keep asking children to gamble with their lives to get to summer jobs. The Ministry must build a protected cycle lane on this route before next summer.</p></blockquote></blockquote><p>Which statement BEST identifies the dominant form of this extract and the writer's purpose?</p>","options":["It is mainly argumentative/persuasive; the writer's purpose is to persuade the Ministry to build a protected cycle lane, using a narrative hook and expository evidence to support the claim.","It is mainly expository; the writer's purpose is to explain how cycle lanes are built.","It is mainly narrative; the writer's purpose is to entertain readers with Dario's story.","It is mainly descriptive; the writer's purpose is to describe the road to Willemstad."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. The dominant form is argumentative: a clear claim (\"must build\") supported by an anecdote, description and evidence. You also named how the other forms serve the purpose.","Paragraph 2 is expository, but the extract does not explain construction; it uses facts to support an argument.","The extract opens with a narrative hook, but Dario's story is a strategy, not the goal. The final paragraph shows what the whole extract is building towards.","The description of the road makes the danger vivid, but it serves the argument rather than being the point of the extract."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Find the argument","q":"<p>This blog post mixes several forms of discourse.</p><p>Select a sentence that is argumentative or persuasive (it states a position or urges action). Three sentences qualify; the feedback names them all.</p>","options":["We cannot allow this treasure to be bulldozed for yet another car park.","Sign the petition today and share this post with everyone who loves our history.","The council should restore the schoolhouse and open it as a museum of island education.","The old wooden schoolhouse in Grand Bay still stands at the top of the hill, its green paint peeling in the sun.","My grandmother walked there every morning in the 1950s, carrying her slate and a piece of bread.","Heritage buildings are structures recognised as having historical or cultural importance."],"correct":[0, 1, 2],"why":"Well done. Sentence 1 is descriptive, sentence 2 narrative and sentence 3 expository. Sentences 4–6 state a position and urge action, so the post's dominant form is persuasive."},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the usage (1)","q":"<p>Identify the usage category of the underlined wording in each situation.</p><p>A student writes in a formal report: \"<b>In this day and age</b>, technology is everywhere.\"</p>","options":["argot","cliché","jargon"],"correct":1,"why":"An overused phrase that has lost its force: a cliché. Write \"Today\" instead.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the usage (2)","q":"<p>A Met Office officer tells a press conference that the \"<b>storm surge</b>\" could reach two metres.</p>","options":["colloquialism","slang","jargon"],"correct":2,"why":"Specialised vocabulary of meteorology: jargon. For the public, it should be explained.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the usage (3)","q":"<p>A group of online gamers invents coded words so that parents reading the chat cannot understand them.</p>","options":["idiomatic expression","argot","cliché"],"correct":1,"why":"A private vocabulary designed to exclude outsiders is argot.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the usage (4)","q":"<p>In an essay a student writes: \"The council's plan really <b>gets on residents' nerves</b>.\"</p>","options":["colloquialism","argot","jargon"],"correct":0,"why":"Everyday conversational wording: a colloquialism. In formal writing: \"irritates residents\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the usage (5)","q":"<p>Granny says the boy has \"<b>hard ears</b>\" because he never listens.</p>","options":["jargon","idiomatic expression","cliché"],"correct":1,"why":"A figurative phrase whose meaning (stubborn, disobedient) cannot be worked out from the words: an idiomatic expression.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_pj","title":"Module 1 · Unit 6 quiz: Literary Devices and Organisational Strategies with PEEL","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 6: Literary Devices and Organisational Strategies with PEEL. 11 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Name that device","q":"<p>Each line comes from an article about plastic pollution in the Caribbean. Match it to the device.</p>","left":["\"Our beaches have become graveyards for plastic bottles.\"","\"Will we wait until the last turtle chokes before we act?\"","\"Last July, nine-year-old Malik found a hawksbill turtle tangled in a fishing net at Half Moon Bay.\"","\"The reef is gasping for breath.\"","\"There is more plastic on that beach than sand in the Sahara.\"","\"Helpless, innocent creatures are dying in agony.\""],"right":["Personification","Metaphor","Anecdote","Hyperbole","Rhetorical question","Emotive language"],"correct":[1,4,2,0,3,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Name the technique: round two","q":"<p>Each line comes from a feature about life after a hurricane. Match it to the technique.</p>","left":["\"The roofs were 'temporarily relocated' to the neighbour's yard.\"","\"After the storm came a deafening silence.\"","\"We needed every pair of hands we could find.\"","\"Bam! Crack! The breadfruit tree split in two.\"","\"Dark clouds brooded over the ruined town.\"","\"Like Noah, Mr. Lake had built his boat before the rain.\"","\"The less we had, the more we shared.\""],"right":["Euphemism","Oxymoron","Synecdoche","Onomatopoeia","Pathetic fallacy","Allusion","Paradox"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"match","title":"Build a PEEL paragraph","q":"<p>These sentences analyse a rhetorical question in an article urging residents to prepare for hurricane season. Put them in PEEL order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The writer uses a rhetorical question to make readers confront their own lack of preparation.","She asks, \"If a storm turned toward the island tonight, would your family know what to do?\"","The question does not need an answer; instead it forces each reader to answer silently, and many will realise they have no plan. The word \"tonight\" creates immediacy, making the danger feel close rather than distant.","This uncomfortable self-realisation supports the writer's purpose of warning residents and persuading them to prepare an emergency plan before the season peaks."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4"],"correct":[0,1,2,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which paragraph earns the marks?","q":"<blockquote><blockquote>\"Every cruise ship that docks in Falmouth brings thousands of visitors, yet many vendors still go home with empty pockets.\"</blockquote></blockquote><p>Which response analyses this sentence BEST?</p>","options":["The writer uses contrast in this sentence.","The writer uses contrast between \"thousands of visitors\" and \"empty pockets\" to expose an unfair gap: the abundance of tourists sharpens the reader's sense that the benefits are not reaching ordinary vendors. This supports the writer's purpose of criticizing how cruise tourism profits are distributed.","The writer is clearly against tourism and wants it banned.","The writer uses contrast: \"thousands of visitors\" and \"empty pockets\". This means vendors are poor."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This only identifies a device. Finding a device earns zero on its own: there is no evidence, no explanation and no link to purpose.","Excellent. Point, evidence, an explanation of how the contrast works on the reader, and a link to a precise purpose (criticize). This is full PEEL.","This misreads the purpose and gives no evidence or analysis. The writer criticizes how benefits are shared, not tourism itself.","Better, because there is evidence, but the explanation only translates the words and there is no link to the writer's purpose."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which organisational strategy? (1)","q":"<p>Each excerpt is from an article. Sort it by its main organisational strategy.</p><p>Identify the organisational strategy.</p><p><b>Because sargassum piles up on the beaches, hotels lose bookings and fishermen cannot launch their boats.</b></p>","options":["Problem and solution","Cause and effect","Compare and contrast","Chronological order"],"correct":1,"why":"\"Because\" introduces a cause and the results follow: cause and effect.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which organisational strategy? (2)","q":"<p>Identify the organisational strategy.</p><p><b>Whereas older residents read the newspaper each morning, teenagers get their news from short videos.</b></p>","options":["Cause and effect","Chronological order","Compare and contrast","Problem and solution"],"correct":2,"why":"\"Whereas\" sets two groups side by side: compare and contrast.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which organisational strategy? (3)","q":"<p>Identify the organisational strategy.</p><p><b>In 1980 the airport handled a few flights a day; by 2000 traffic had grown sharply; today it is one of the busiest in the region.</b></p>","options":["Problem and solution","Chronological order","Compare and contrast","Cause and effect"],"correct":1,"why":"Dates move forward in time: chronological order.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which organisational strategy? (4)","q":"<p>Identify the organisational strategy.</p><p><b>Students are arriving late because buses are unreliable. The school proposes a staggered start time.</b></p>","options":["Chronological order","Cause and effect","Compare and contrast","Problem and solution"],"correct":3,"why":"An issue is stated and a remedy is proposed: problem and solution.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which organisational strategy? (5)","q":"<p>Identify the organisational strategy.</p><p><b>Heavy rains flood Frederick Street, which in turn forces shops to close for days.</b></p>","options":["Problem and solution","Chronological order","Cause and effect","Compare and contrast"],"correct":2,"why":"\"Which in turn forces\" links a cause to its consequence.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which organisational strategy? (6)","q":"<p>Identify the organisational strategy.</p><p><b>Unlike traditional lolos, the new food courts are air-conditioned but far more expensive.</b></p>","options":["Problem and solution","Chronological order","Compare and contrast","Cause and effect"],"correct":2,"why":"\"Unlike\" signals contrast.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which organisational strategy? (7)","q":"<p>Identify the organisational strategy.</p><p><b>Litter at Mayaro beach is a growing concern. One answer is a deposit scheme for bottles.</b></p>","options":["Problem and solution","Compare and contrast","Cause and effect","Chronological order"],"correct":0,"why":"A concern followed by \"one answer\" is problem and solution.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_qv","title":"Module 1 · Unit 7 quiz: Summary Skills, Listening and the Module 1 Essay","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 7: Summary Skills, Listening and the Module 1 Essay. 12 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Main idea or subsidiary detail? (1)","q":"<p>An article argues that the island should invest in solar energy. Sort each sentence for a summary.</p><p>Keep it (main idea) or drop it (subsidiary detail)?</p><p><b>Electricity costs on the island are among the highest household expenses.</b></p>","options":["Main idea: keep","Subsidiary detail: drop"],"correct":0,"why":"This is a key reason for the argument, so it belongs in the summary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Main idea or subsidiary detail? (2)","q":"<p>Keep it (main idea) or drop it (subsidiary detail)?</p><p><b>My neighbour Mrs. Hodge pays more for power than for groceries.</b></p>","options":["Subsidiary detail: drop","Main idea: keep"],"correct":0,"why":"This anecdote illustrates the main idea but is a detail. Drop it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Main idea or subsidiary detail? (3)","q":"<p>Keep it (main idea) or drop it (subsidiary detail)?</p><p><b>Solar panels can reduce dependence on imported fuel.</b></p>","options":["Main idea: keep","Subsidiary detail: drop"],"correct":0,"why":"This is a main benefit and central to the argument.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Main idea or subsidiary detail? (4)","q":"<p>Keep it (main idea) or drop it (subsidiary detail)?</p><p><b>The sun shone brightly on the morning I wrote this.</b></p>","options":["Main idea: keep","Subsidiary detail: drop"],"correct":1,"why":"This is decoration with no argumentative value.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Main idea or subsidiary detail? (5)","q":"<p>Keep it (main idea) or drop it (subsidiary detail)?</p><p><b>Government incentives would make solar affordable for more households.</b></p>","options":["Subsidiary detail: drop","Main idea: keep"],"correct":1,"why":"This is the writer's proposed solution, a main idea.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Main idea or subsidiary detail? (6)","q":"<p>Keep it (main idea) or drop it (subsidiary detail)?</p><p><b>Panels come in blue and black finishes.</b></p>","options":["Subsidiary detail: drop","Main idea: keep"],"correct":0,"why":"This is a minor detail, irrelevant to the argument.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"The listening test (step 1)","q":"<p>During the first reading, you miss two sentences because of the fan. What now?</p>","options":["Leave gaps in your notes and listen especially for those parts on the second reading.","Guess what was said and start answering now."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Sensible recovery. Gaps show you exactly what to listen for next time.","Guessing before the reading is complete leads to errors. Wait and use the second reading."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The listening test (step 2)","q":"<p>The reading begins. How do you take notes?</p>","options":["Try to write down every word the teacher says.","Jot key words, figures and signal words (\"firstly\", \"however\", \"the main reason\") with abbreviations."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This is a barrier in itself: while you write one sentence, you miss the next. Notes should be key words, not dictation.","Excellent. Signal words show the structure, and key words capture the main ideas."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The listening test (step 3)","q":"<p>Halfway through, you are sure you know the speaker's main point. The question sheet is in front of you. What do you do?</p>","options":["Keep listening and taking notes until the reading is complete, then answer.","Start answering the questions now to save time."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Exactly right. Take notes during the reading, but wait until it is complete before answering.","The ending often changes or completes the meaning. Answering early means you stop listening."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choosing the summary format","q":"<p>A question says: \"In <b>not more than 60 words</b>, and in <b>continuous prose</b>, summarise the writer's reasons for supporting solar energy.\" Which response best follows the instructions?</p>","options":["1. Lower costs 2. Less imported fuel 3. Govt incentives","A paragraph that keeps the neighbour's anecdote and the description of panel colours to make it interesting.","A 150-word paragraph copying the writer's best sentences.","A short paragraph in your own words explaining that solar energy would lower household costs and reduce reliance on imported fuel, and that government incentives would make it affordable."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["This is note form. The question says \"continuous prose\", and examiners deliberately change one or two words in questions. Read every instruction.","A summary must drop subsidiary details such as anecdotes and minor facts. These waste words and show you cannot distinguish main from supporting ideas.","This ignores the word limit and copies rather than condenses. Summaries must be in your own words and within the limit.","Correct. It is continuous prose, in your own words, keeps only the main ideas and is within the word limit."]},{"t":"match","title":"Assemble the introduction","q":"<p>These sentences form the introduction to an essay on an article, \"Screens at the Supper Table\". Put them in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["In \"Screens at the Supper Table\", columnist Ana Richardson discusses the place of mobile devices in Caribbean family life.","Her main point is that excessive dependence on technology has weakened face-to-face communication and family relationships.","Her purpose is to warn parents of this damage and to encourage them to create device-free family time.","She achieves this through emotive anecdote, a then-and-now contrast and a teacher's testimony, which is relevant but cannot be fully verified."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4"],"correct":[0,1,2,3]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the conclusion errors","q":"<p>Read this student's conclusion to an essay on the mangroves extract.</p><p>Select EVERY sentence that should NOT be in a conclusion.</p>","options":["The writer's warning is therefore both clear and convincing.","In conclusion, the writer warns residents that destroying mangroves leaves the island exposed to storms.","Through a powerful metaphor and a then-and-now contrast, the writer makes the loss feel foolish and urgent.","Also, the writer's source, Lagoon Watch, might be biased because it is an environmental group, which I will now explain in detail.","Another device I did not mention earlier is the alliteration in \"parking lots and marinas\", which creates rhythm."],"correct":[3, 4],"why":"Well spotted. Sentences 2 and 4 introduce new analysis. A conclusion restates the main point and purpose and sums up; it never opens new points. Keep it shorter than your introduction."}]},{"id":"assess_rx","title":"Module 1 · Unit 8 quiz: Traditional vs New Media","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 8: Traditional vs New Media. 9 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Medium meets its weakness","q":"<p>Match each medium with the limitation a careful researcher should keep in mind.</p>","left":["A viral social media post","A printed newspaper article","A government press release","A personal blog","A television news broadcast"],"right":["Often an opinion; the writer's expertise may be unknown.","Edited and checked, but it may reflect the owner's or advertisers' interests.","Official and authoritative, but it presents the government's side.","No editor or fact-checker; it can spread false claims very quickly.","Short time slots mean complex issues may be simplified."],"correct":[3,1,2,0,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which source would you cite?","q":"<p>A student is writing about rising sea levels around the Turks and Caicos Islands. Which source is MOST credible for the essay?</p>","options":["A 1998 encyclopaedia entry on Caribbean geography","A hotel website promoting its beachfront rooms","A viral video in which a fisherman says the sea \"has never changed\"","A 2024 report by a regional climate research centre, with named scientists and data tables"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Authoritative once, but far too old to describe current sea levels.","The hotel has a commercial motive to play down any risk.","One person's opinion, with no data and no editorial checking.","Correct. It is current, written by named experts, and its data can be checked."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the medium: which source would you trust? (step 1)","q":"<p>You are writing a Paper 2 essay on the credibility of information about climate change in the Caribbean. You find four sources. For each, note the medium and how it affects credibility.</p><p><b>Source 1:</b> A Facebook post by a Form 6 student: \"Rising sea levels are a hoax made up by scientists to get government money. My uncle says the sea hasn't changed in 40 years.\"</p><p>What medium is this?</p>","options":["Social media post from a private citizen","A credible news report"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. The creator is unknown, no editor has checked it, and the claim lacks evidence.","No. There is no journalist, editor or fact-checking. This is a personal opinion."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the medium: which source would you trust? (step 2)","q":"<p>Would you cite this in your essay as credible information about climate change?</p>","options":["No. It is not credible. You might mention it as an example of a conspiracy theory, but it is not evidence.","Yes, because it is on social media, so many people have seen it."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. You can use it as an example of misinformation, but not as support for your own argument.","Popularity does not equal credibility. Misinformation spreads just as fast as truth on social media."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the medium: which source would you trust? (step 3)","q":"<p><b>Source 2:</b> A peer-reviewed article in the <i>Caribbean Journal of Marine Science</i> titled \"Sea Level Rise in the Dutch Caribbean: Data from 1980–2025\" by Dr. Amara Osei, a university researcher, and three other marine scientists. The abstract cites 47 studies and includes a data table and statistical analysis.</p><p>What medium is this?</p>","options":["Academic journal article, a traditional media format with gatekeeping","Social media"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Yes. Other scientists reviewed it before publication; the authors' credentials are documented; sources are cited.","No. A journal article passes through peer review, unlike social media posts."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the medium: which source would you trust? (step 4)","q":"<p>How does the medium affect credibility?</p>","options":["The journal had editors and peer reviewers who checked the science before it was published. The authors are named experts. The article cites 47 other studies, so it is corroborated.","All journal articles are true because they are in journals."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. The medium—academic publishing—built in multiple checks.","Journals have better standards, but they can still publish flawed work. The medium makes it more likely to be credible, but check the evidence yourself."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the medium: which source would you trust? (step 5)","q":"<p><b>Source 3:</b> A YouTube video titled \"The TRUTH About Climate Change!!!\" posted by a channel called \"Real Facts Now\" with 3 million subscribers. The creator is not named. The video claims the same thing as your Source 1 (the hoax), but with dramatic music and edited clips from news reports.</p><p>What medium is this?</p>","options":["Credible because 3 million people subscribe","New media (social video) with no transparent creator or gatekeeping"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["View count shows popularity, not accuracy. Viral videos are often misinformation.","Correct. High view count does not mean credible; the algorithm promotes engaging content, not accurate content."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the medium: which source would you trust? (step 6)","q":"<p>Why would this be unreliable for your essay?</p>","options":["It is on YouTube, so it must be wrong.","The creator is anonymous, there is no fact-checking, and the claims contradict published science. You cannot cite it as evidence."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Some YouTube content is credible (educational channels, interviews with experts), and some is not. Judge each source on its own merits, not the platform alone.","Right. You might mention it as an example of how misinformation looks, but not as a source."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Evaluate the medium: which source would you trust? (step 7)","q":"<p><b>Source 4:</b> An article from <i>The Guardian</i> (a reputable international newspaper) by a named journalist, reporting on a recent study from the University of Cambridge on Caribbean coastal erosion. The article quotes the lead researcher, links to the original study, and includes interviews with two residents whose homes have been affected.</p><p>For your essay, you would cite this as:</p>","options":["Not credible because it is not from an academic journal.","Largely credible. It is a named journalist in an established traditional medium (newspaper). The source is named, quotes are from real people, and the original research is linked."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Journals are one credible source, but newspapers with good reputations, named journalists and verification are also credible. Different media serve different purposes.","Excellent. This medium—professional journalism—included editorial review, attribution and corroboration. It is fit to cite."]}]},{"id":"assess_t9","title":"Module 1 · Unit 9 quiz: Speaking Skills and Writing Organisation","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 1, Unit 9: Speaking Skills and Writing Organisation. 9 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Research methods: the student council scenario (1)","q":"<p>The student council is researching the reasons for the decline in student participation in co-curricular activities. Answer each question as you would in Paper 1.</p><p>The most appropriate data collection method for this study is...</p>","options":["a focus group","document research","a questionnaire","direct observation"],"correct":2,"why":"The research needs a wide cross-section of the student population, and questionnaires allow the largest number of participants. Observation and focus groups reach smaller groups; document research is a secondary source.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Research methods: the student council scenario (2)","q":"<p>One weakness of conducting interviews as a data collection method is...</p>","options":["they are costly to conduct","they capture verbal and non-verbal language","the flexibility of the questions","the limited size of the interviewee population"],"correct":3,"why":"Interviews limit the sample size: you cannot reach as wide a cross-section of students as with a questionnaire. Flexibility and capturing non-verbal language are strengths.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Research methods: the student council scenario (3)","q":"<p>The most efficient method of administering the questionnaire is...</p>","options":["online","by conventional mail","face-to-face","by telephone"],"correct":2,"why":"Getting responses back is a key difficulty with questionnaires. Face-to-face administration lets you distribute and collect on the spot, which maximises the response rate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Research methods: the student council scenario (4)","q":"<p>The research design in this case will be...</p>","options":["quantitative","mixed method","qualitative","ethnographic"],"correct":1,"why":"The study needs closed questions (quantitative tallies) and open questions (qualitative reasons). It is not ethnographic, because there is no prolonged immersive observation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build a strong essay paragraph using PEEL (step 1)","q":"<p>You are writing an essay on the credibility of an article on family communication. The article opens with this anecdote:</p>\n<blockquote>\"Sunday evening in the Richardson home: parents scroll, teenagers text, the TV mutters to itself. They share a sofa but live on separate islands.\"</blockquote>\n<p>Guide yourself through PEEL to analyse this anecdote.</p><p><b>STEP 1: POINT</b></p><p>You must name the technique. What is it?</p>","options":["A metaphor","An anecdote (a short, true-sounding story)"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["An anecdote is a short story. A metaphor is a comparison (e.g., \"living on separate islands\" is the metaphor within the anecdote).","Correct. The writer opens with a scene from a family's evening."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build a strong essay paragraph using PEEL (step 2)","q":"<p><b>STEP 3: EXPLANATION</b></p><p>Explain how this technique works on the reader. What does the anecdote make you think or feel?</p>","options":["It is a story.","It places readers inside a familiar scene, so they recognize their own homes before the writer makes any argument. The embedded metaphor \"separate island\" (literal closeness but emotional distance) is especially powerful for a Caribbean audience where islands symbolize isolation."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A description alone is not an explanation. Explain what the story does to the reader — does it make them recognize something? Feel guilty? See a problem?","Excellent explanation. You have described the effect on the reader and why it works in a Caribbean context."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Build a strong essay paragraph using PEEL (step 3)","q":"<p><b>STEP 4: LINK</b></p><p>Connect this technique to the writer's purpose. Why does the writer use an anecdote and metaphor here?</p>","options":["It is a good anecdote.","The writer's purpose is to warn parents about device dependence. The anecdote and metaphor make the warning personal and relatable rather than abstract, so parents feel the problem is real and happening in their own homes. This makes them more likely to take the warning seriously."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Do not stop at \"it is good.\" Always ask: Why is the writer using this? What is the writer trying to make the reader think or do? Link the technique to the purpose.","Perfect PEEL paragraph. You have shown how a technique serves the writer's purpose."]},{"t":"match","title":"Plan the Module 1 essay","q":"<p>Put the six components of a full Module 1 essay plan in the order in which they usually appear in the question and in a strong answer.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["State the writer's main point in no more than 30 words.","Identify the writer's purpose (P, I or E) with evidence.","Discuss the organisational strategies used.","Discuss the language techniques used.","Comment on the writer's tone, supported by word choice.","Comment on the reliability and/or validity of the information."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"How many readings?","q":"<p>In the listening section of Paper 1 (Items 1–7), how is the extract presented?</p>","options":["It is read aloud twice, and you may take notes during and between the readings.","It is read once only, and no notes are allowed.","It is played as a video recording.","It is printed in the question paper for you to read."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Use the first reading for the main idea and the second for details, devices and tone.","No. It is read twice and note-taking is allowed.","No. The extract is read aloud by the invigilator.","No. In the examination the listening extract is not printed; it is read aloud."]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Module 2","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_w0","title":"Module 2 · Unit 1 quiz: Language, Identity and Ideology","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 1: Language, Identity and Ideology. 20 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the characteristic (1)","q":"<p>Read each situation and drag it to the characteristic of language it best shows.</p><p>Which characteristic of language is shown?</p><p><b>The English say \"dog\", the Dutch say \"hond\" and Papiamentu speakers say \"kachó\".</b></p>","options":["Systematic","Arbitrary","Displacement","Dynamic"],"correct":1,"why":"Three different words for the same animal: nothing about the animal decides the word. That is arbitrariness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the characteristic (2)","q":"<p>Which characteristic of language is shown?</p><p><b>There is no reason why the sounds in \"tree\" should mean a tall plant.</b></p>","options":["Dynamic","Arbitrary","Displacement","Systematic"],"correct":1,"why":"The link between sound and meaning is agreed by the community, not natural, so this is arbitrary.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the characteristic (3)","q":"<p>Which characteristic of language is shown?</p><p><b>\"Cloud\" now also means online storage.</b></p>","options":["Arbitrary","Systematic","Displacement","Dynamic"],"correct":3,"why":"The word has gained a new meaning over time: language is dynamic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the characteristic (4)","q":"<p>Which characteristic of language is shown?</p><p><b>Words like \"selfie\" and \"livestream\" entered dictionaries recently.</b></p>","options":["Dynamic","Displacement","Systematic","Arbitrary"],"correct":0,"why":"New words appear as society changes. That is dynamism.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the characteristic (5)","q":"<p>Which characteristic of language is shown?</p><p><b>Uncle tells the story of the 1995 hurricane at the family lime.</b></p>","options":["Systematic","Arbitrary","Dynamic","Displacement"],"correct":3,"why":"He is talking about the past, something not here and now. That is displacement.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the characteristic (6)","q":"<p>Which characteristic of language is shown?</p><p><b>Two students plan what they will do after CAPE exams next year.</b></p>","options":["Systematic","Dynamic","Arbitrary","Displacement"],"correct":3,"why":"Talking about the future is displacement too.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the characteristic (7)","q":"<p>Which characteristic of language is shown?</p><p><b>A Bajan speaker says \"I does go to school\" but never \"Does I go school to\".</b></p>","options":["Arbitrary","Dynamic","Systematic","Displacement"],"correct":2,"why":"The habitual marker \"does\" has a fixed position in the sentence. Creoles are rule-governed, so this is systematic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the characteristic (8)","q":"<p>Which characteristic of language is shown?</p><p><b>In Jamaican Creole the plural marker \"dem\" always follows the noun: \"di gyal dem\".</b></p>","options":["Systematic","Dynamic","Displacement","Arbitrary"],"correct":0,"why":"A fixed rule about word order shows that the language is systematic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristics: quick-fire (1)","q":"<p>Choose the characteristic or concept that each situation BEST illustrates.</p><p>Over a few generations, a contact language develops a full tense system and a much larger vocabulary.</p>","options":["arbitrary","maturational","displacement"],"correct":1,"why":"The language has grown more complex over time: maturational.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristics: quick-fire (2)","q":"<p>Twins adopted at birth by a family in Aruba grow up speaking Papiamentu, not the language of their birth parents.</p>","options":["symbolic","dynamic","non-instinctive"],"correct":2,"why":"Language is not inherited; it is acquired from the community around the child: non-instinctive.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristics: quick-fire (3)","q":"<p>Students in a deaf school in Kingston debate in Jamaican Sign Language.</p>","options":["sign is not language","sign languages are full languages","sign is a dialect of English"],"correct":1,"why":"The syllabus defines language as spoken and sign. Sign languages have their own grammar.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristics: quick-fire (4)","q":"<p>\"Every human community has language.\" Here \"language\" is used in the...</p>","options":["generic sense","regional sense","individual sense"],"correct":0,"why":"It refers to the human ability in general: the generic sense.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Characteristics: quick-fire (5)","q":"<p>The word \"ghost\" now also means to stop replying to someone's messages.</p>","options":["maturational","dynamic","non-instinctive"],"correct":1,"why":"A new meaning for an existing word is change: dynamic. Maturational is about growing complexity.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the function","q":"<p>Match each function of language with the Caribbean utterance that best shows it.</p>","left":["Expressive","Informative","Directive","Phatic","Aesthetic","Metalingual"],"right":["\"Salt breeze sweet like sugar cake on Grand Anse.\"","\"Boy, I so proud of my little sister graduation!\"","\"Stock up on water and batteries before Friday.\"","\"The airport will reopen at 6 a.m. on Thursday.\"","\"In Trinidad 'liming' means hanging out with friends.\"","\"Evening, Miss Rose! How the family?\""],"correct":[1,3,2,5,0,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which function?","q":"<blockquote><p>At the bus stop on Bay Street, Mr. Lake nods to a young man he sees every morning. \"Morning, youth. Hot today, eh?\" The young man smiles. \"Hot bad, Mr. Lake. Have a good one.\"</p></blockquote><p>What is the MAIN function of the language in this exchange?</p>","options":["Informative","Metalingual","Directive","Phatic"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["It looks informative because they mention the weather, but both men already know it is hot. The words are not there to inform; they are there to be sociable.","Metalingual language talks about language itself, e.g. explaining a word. Nobody here comments on words or meanings.","\"Have a good one\" looks like an imperative, but it is a friendly closing formula, not a real attempt to make the man do anything.","Correct. Neither speaker is really sharing new information; both already know it is hot. The exchange keeps a friendly social bond going, which is the phatic function."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Identity, ideology and society: quick questions (1)","q":"<p>Answer each question. Think about what the language reveals.</p><p>A teenager uses Standard English in a scholarship interview and Guyanese Creolese on the basketball court. This mainly shows that language...</p>","options":["is arbitrary","reflects and performs different parts of one identity","has no rules"],"correct":1,"why":"She is the same person showing different sides of herself to different audiences: language is linked to identity.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Identity, ideology and society: quick questions (2)","q":"<p>A teacher says, \"Creole is just broken English.\" This statement reflects...</p>","options":["a language ideology","a linguistic fact","the phatic function"],"correct":0,"why":"It is a belief about which variety is \"proper\", not a fact. Linguists recognise creoles as full languages with their own rules.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Identity, ideology and society: quick questions (3)","q":"<p>\"Awful\" once meant \"full of awe\" and now means \"very bad\". This shows...</p>","options":["a shift in accepted word meaning over time","code-mixing","displacement"],"correct":0,"why":"The accepted meaning has changed as society used the word differently: language is dynamic and reflects social change.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Identity, ideology and society: quick questions (4)","q":"<p>A fishing village has many detailed words for types of nets, winds and currents. This suggests...</p>","options":["the community values and depends on fishing","the language is frozen","the community speaks badly"],"correct":0,"why":"Rich vocabulary in one area shows what matters to a society: language gives insight into the society that produces it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Identity, ideology and society: quick questions (5)","q":"<p>Saying \"speaky-spokey\" about someone who uses very formal English reveals...</p>","options":["a pidgin","an attitude linked to class and belonging","the aesthetic function"],"correct":1,"why":"The label hints that very formal speech can be seen as showing off or rejecting the group. It reveals social attitudes.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_x3","title":"Module 2 · Unit 2 quiz: Register and Language Varieties","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 2: Register and Language Varieties. 7 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"From frozen to intimate","q":"<p>Arrange these examples from the <b>most formal (frozen)</b> at the top to the <b>most intimate</b> at the bottom.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["\"In plenty and in time of need...\" sung at the start of a school ceremony","\"Ladies and gentlemen, it is my honour to welcome you to this year's prize-giving.\"","\"Doctor, could you explain what these test results mean for me?\"","\"Wha' going on, bro? You reach the beach yet?\"","\"Night, Mommy. Love you, sweetpea.\""],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"The job interview (step 1)","q":"<p>You are interviewing for a summer job as a guest services assistant at a hotel in Grace Bay, Providenciales. Choose your words carefully: your register is being assessed as much as your answers.</p><p>The manager, Ms. Hodge, stands and offers her hand. \"Good morning. Please, have a seat.\"</p><p>How do you respond?</p>","options":["\"Good morning, Ms. Hodge. Thank you for seeing me.\"","\"Morning, morning! Wha' happenin'?\"","Nod and sit down without speaking."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent: a formal greeting, her title and surname, and a polite thank-you. You have matched her register.","This is a warm casual greeting, perfect at the bus stop but too familiar for a first meeting with a possible employer.","Silence at a greeting can read as nervousness or rudeness. A short formal greeting is expected."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The job interview (step 2)","q":"<p>Ms. Hodge asks, \"Why do you want to work in guest services?\"</p>","options":["\"Pursuant to my aspirations, I endeavour to facilitate tourism-related synergies.\"","\"I enjoy meeting people and I am proud of our island. I would like to help visitors have a great experience while I build my customer-service skills.\"","\"Ah need di money fi summer, dat's all.\""],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Too much formal-sounding vocabulary makes the answer hard to follow and sounds rehearsed. Formal does not mean complicated.","Strong consultative-to-formal answer: complete sentences, a clear reason and a link to the job.","Honest, but the casual register and the very short reason suggest you have not thought about the job. Save this style for friends."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The job interview (step 3)","q":"<p>At the end Ms. Hodge smiles: \"Do you have any questions for me?\"</p>","options":["\"Nah, I good. Laters!\"","\"Yes, thank you. What would a typical day in this role involve?\""],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Slipping into casual register at the very end undoes your good impression. Close formally.","A polite, relevant question in a consultative register shows real interest."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the jargon","q":"<p>Read this extract from a radio cricket commentary on a local station.</p><p>Select the TWO lines that contain cricket jargon.</p>","options":["Good afternoon, listeners, and welcome back to the ground.","The crowd here is loving every minute, music blasting.","What a day for the young people of this island.","Appeal! And the umpire gives him out LBW.","Phillips runs in and that is a beautiful yorker, right in the blockhole!"],"correct":[3, 4],"why":"Spot on. \"Yorker\", \"blockhole\" and \"LBW\" are specialised terms that cricket fans understand but outsiders may not: that is jargon."},{"t":"mc","title":"Accent or dialect?","q":"<p>Andre from Guyana and Shanice from Barbados both read the same Standard English news script on air. Listeners can tell where each is from. What differs between them?</p>","options":["Their idiolects","Their registers","Their dialects","Their accents"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["Idiolect is each person's unique overall way of speaking. The question points to where they are from, which is about regional pronunciation: accent.","Both are using the same formal news register. Register is about formality, not regional sound.","A dialect difference includes grammar and vocabulary. Here the words and grammar are identical because they are reading the same script.","Correct. They use the same words and grammar (the script), so only pronunciation differs. That is accent."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Standard and non-standard","q":"<p>Which statement about standard and non-standard varieties is MOST accurate?</p>","options":["Standard English is spoken only in England.","A standard variety is the one codified and used for official purposes such as education, law and the media.","Non-standard varieties have no grammar rules.","Non-standard means incorrect."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["There are several Standard Englishes, including Caribbean Standard English, which follows the standard grammar but has its own vocabulary and pronunciation.","Correct. \"Standard\" describes the variety's official role and codification, not its quality.","This is a common misconception. Every variety, including every creole, is rule-governed. Its rules are simply not the ones taught in grammar books.","Non-standard means \"not the codified official variety\". Calling it incorrect repeats an ideology rather than a linguistic fact."]}]},{"id":"assess_zn","title":"Module 2 · Unit 3 quiz: Creole and Standard English: History and the Continuum","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 3: Creole and Standard English: History and the Continuum. 17 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"From contact to creole","q":"<p>Put the stages in the development of a Caribbean creole in the correct order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Africans speaking many different languages are brought to Caribbean plantations.","Groups with no shared language come into contact with each other and with the European language.","A simplified contact language (a pidgin) develops for basic communication; nobody speaks it as a first language.","Children grow up speaking the contact language as their mother tongue, and it becomes a creole.","The creole expands its vocabulary and grammar into a full language used for every purpose of daily life."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the creole to its lexifier","q":"<p>Match each Caribbean language to the description that fits it best.</p>","left":["Haitian Creole","Kwéyòl","Papiamentu","Sranan Tongo","Jamaican Creole","Sint Maarten vernacular"],"right":["English-lexified variety of the island, alongside official Dutch","French-lexified; spoken in Saint Lucia and Dominica even though English is official","English-lexified with Dutch and Portuguese influence; a lingua franca in Suriname","English-lexified; uses forms like \"unu\" and \"di book dem\"","Mainly Portuguese/Spanish vocabulary; spoken in Aruba, Curaçao and Bonaire","French-lexified; the language of the majority in Haiti"],"correct":[5,1,4,2,3,0]},{"t":"mc","title":"Place it on the continuum (1)","q":"<p>Sort each sentence into the level of the continuum it best represents.</p><p>Basilect, mesolect or acrolect?</p><p><b>\"Mi did a nyam mi food when im come.\"</b></p>","options":["Acrolect","Basilect","Mesolect"],"correct":1,"why":"\"Mi\", the pre-verbal markers \"did a\" and \"nyam\" make this broad creole: basilect.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Place it on the continuum (2)","q":"<p>Basilect, mesolect or acrolect?</p><p><b>\"Unu ena go a di beach?\"</b></p>","options":["Acrolect","Basilect","Mesolect"],"correct":1,"why":"\"Unu\" (you, plural) and \"a di\" are basilectal forms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Place it on the continuum (3)","q":"<p>Basilect, mesolect or acrolect?</p><p><b>\"Dem pickney a play ina di yard.\"</b></p>","options":["Mesolect","Basilect","Acrolect"],"correct":1,"why":"\"Pickney\", progressive \"a\" and \"ina\" mark this as basilect.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Place it on the continuum (4)","q":"<p>Basilect, mesolect or acrolect?</p><p><b>\"She was going to the shop when it start rain.\"</b></p>","options":["Mesolect","Acrolect","Basilect"],"correct":0,"why":"Mostly standard, but \"start rain\" drops the past tense -ed and \"to\". A mix: mesolect.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Place it on the continuum (5)","q":"<p>Basilect, mesolect or acrolect?</p><p><b>\"I not going to the fete tonight.\"</b></p>","options":["Basilect","Mesolect","Acrolect"],"correct":1,"why":"Standard words, but the copula \"am\" is missing. Mixed features: mesolect.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Place it on the continuum (6)","q":"<p>Basilect, mesolect or acrolect?</p><p><b>\"Them children always playing in the yard.\"</b></p>","options":["Basilect","Acrolect","Mesolect"],"correct":2,"why":"\"Them\" as a determiner and the missing \"are\" show creole influence on mostly English vocabulary: mesolect.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Place it on the continuum (7)","q":"<p>Basilect, mesolect or acrolect?</p><p><b>\"I was eating my food when he arrived.\"</b></p>","options":["Mesolect","Acrolect","Basilect"],"correct":1,"why":"Fully standard grammar: acrolect.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Place it on the continuum (8)","q":"<p>Basilect, mesolect or acrolect?</p><p><b>\"Are you all going to the beach?\"</b></p>","options":["Basilect","Mesolect","Acrolect"],"correct":2,"why":"Standard question form and copula: acrolect.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Pidgin or creole?","q":"<p>Which of the following BEST explains why Haitian Creole is classified as a creole rather than a pidgin?</p>","options":["Most of its words come from French.","It is spoken in the Caribbean.","It is simpler than French.","It is the first language of millions of speakers and has a complete grammar used for all purposes."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["That tells us its lexifier, not whether it is a pidgin or a creole. Pidgins also take words from a lexifier.","Location does not decide the category. Pidgins and creoles exist all over the world.","This is a misconception. Creoles are not simplified languages; they have complex grammar of their own.","Correct. Having native speakers and a full, expanded grammar is what makes a language a creole rather than a pidgin."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language and creole: exam practice (1)","q":"<p>Six Paper 1-style questions from your class notes. Read every option before you choose.</p><p>Which of the following is a characteristic of language? It is...</p>","options":["directing","instinctive","static","symbolic"],"correct":3,"why":"Language is symbolic: words and signs stand for things and ideas. It is not instinctive (it is acquired from the community) or static (it is dynamic), and \"directing\" is a purpose, not a characteristic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language and creole: exam practice (2)","q":"<p>All of the following are characteristics of language EXCEPT that it is...</p>","options":["maturational","symbolic","instinctive","dynamic"],"correct":2,"why":"Language is non-instinctive: it is acquired from the people around us, not inherited. Maturational, symbolic and dynamic are all characteristics named in the syllabus.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language and creole: exam practice (3)","q":"<p>Which indicate the functions of language in society? I. A medium for expression. II. A means to impart moral development. III. A mechanism for the growth of civilisation.</p>","options":["I only","I and II only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":3,"why":"All three are roles language plays in society: it is a medium for expression, it passes on values and morals from one generation to the next (an ethical role), and it lets knowledge be recorded and built on, which supports the growth of civilisation (a social role). The syllabus asks you to analyse these social, political, ethical and psychological roles as well as the six functions.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language and creole: exam practice (4)","q":"<p>The national anthem is sung at school every Monday. Which function of language is shown?</p>","options":["cognitive","poetic","expressive","informative"],"correct":1,"why":"The anthem is a song with a structured, literary form: the poetic (aesthetic) function. It gives no new information each week and is not private self-expression.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language and creole: exam practice (5)","q":"<p>Which of the following can be considered lingua francas?</p>","options":["Standard English, English Creole, Hindi","Papiamento, Arawakan, Patois","English Creole, French Creole, Spanish Creole","English, Spanish, French"],"correct":3,"why":"The colonial source languages served as lingua francas. The creoles in the second option developed from them as native languages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Language and creole: exam practice (6)","q":"<p>All of the following account for the development of a creole EXCEPT...</p>","options":["recognition of creole as an official UN language","a concentration of pidgin speakers in one area","the process of nativisation","colonisation and the slave trade"],"correct":0,"why":"The UN's six official languages do not include a creole, and official recognition describes status, not how a creole develops.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_11l","title":"Module 2 · Unit 4 quiz: The Wider Caribbean Language Situation: Origins, Diversity and Change","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 4: The Wider Caribbean Language Situation: Origins, Diversity and Change. 12 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the theory","q":"<p>Match each theory of creole origin to the statement that best summarises it.</p>","left":["Monogenesis","Relexification","Polygenesis","Substrate theory","Superstrate theory","Language bioprogram hypothesis"],"right":["The vocabulary of a language is replaced with words from another language while the grammar stays.","Many Atlantic creoles descend from a single early Portuguese-based trade pidgin.","Creoles grew mainly from regional and non-standard varieties of European languages.","West African languages shaped the grammar, sounds and meanings of the creoles.","Children used an inborn capacity for language to turn an unstable pidgin into a full creole.","Each creole arose independently from its own local contact situation."],"correct":[1,0,5,3,2,4]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the language","q":"<p>Match each language to the description that fits it.</p>","left":["Garifuna","Wapishana","Caribbean Hindustani / Bhojpuri","Hakka","Papiamentu","Saramaccan","Sranan Tongo"],"right":["A Suriname Maroon creole with English and many Portuguese-derived words","An indigenous Arawakan language spoken in Belize and Central America","An indigenous language of southern Guyana","An English-lexified creole used as Suriname's lingua franca","A Chinese variety brought by labourers to Trinidad, Jamaica and Guyana","Dialects of Hindi brought by indentured labourers to Trinidad, Guyana and Suriname","An Iberian-lexified creole with official status in the ABC islands"],"correct":[1,2,5,4,6,0,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the category","q":"<p>Languages such as Garifuna, Lokono, Patamona, Wapishana and Warao, all spoken in the Caribbean region, are MOST appropriately labelled</p>","options":["registers","indigenous languages","Creole languages","dialects of English"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A register is a level of formality within a language, not a separate language.","Correct. These languages were born in the region and its mainland rim, not created through colonial contact or brought from elsewhere.","Creoles arose from contact situations such as the plantation. These languages existed long before European colonisation.","They are completely separate languages with no English base."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Creole, indigenous or brought from elsewhere? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each language into the correct type, using the syllabus distinction between Creole and non-Creole languages.</p><p>What type of language is it?</p><p><b>Papiamentu</b></p>","options":["Non-Creole: indigenous","Non-Creole: brought from outside the region","Creole"],"correct":2,"why":"A native language that began in a contact situation: Creole.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Creole, indigenous or brought from elsewhere? (2)","q":"<p>What type of language is it?</p><p><b>Sranan Tongo</b></p>","options":["Non-Creole: brought from outside the region","Creole","Non-Creole: indigenous"],"correct":1,"why":"An English-lexified creole of Suriname.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Creole, indigenous or brought from elsewhere? (3)","q":"<p>What type of language is it?</p><p><b>Haitian Creole</b></p>","options":["Non-Creole: indigenous","Non-Creole: brought from outside the region","Creole"],"correct":2,"why":"A French-lexified creole.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Creole, indigenous or brought from elsewhere? (4)","q":"<p>What type of language is it?</p><p><b>Warao</b></p>","options":["Creole","Non-Creole: indigenous","Non-Creole: brought from outside the region"],"correct":1,"why":"An indigenous language of Guyana and Venezuela, not a creole.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Creole, indigenous or brought from elsewhere? (5)","q":"<p>What type of language is it?</p><p><b>Garifuna</b></p>","options":["Non-Creole: indigenous","Creole","Non-Creole: brought from outside the region"],"correct":0,"why":"An indigenous Arawakan language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Creole, indigenous or brought from elsewhere? (6)","q":"<p>What type of language is it?</p><p><b>Q'eqchi' (Kekchi)</b></p>","options":["Creole","Non-Creole: brought from outside the region","Non-Creole: indigenous"],"correct":2,"why":"A Mayan language spoken in Belize: indigenous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Creole, indigenous or brought from elsewhere? (7)","q":"<p>What type of language is it?</p><p><b>Hindi / Bhojpuri</b></p>","options":["Non-Creole: brought from outside the region","Non-Creole: indigenous","Creole"],"correct":0,"why":"Brought from India by indentured labourers: non-Creole.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Creole, indigenous or brought from elsewhere? (8)","q":"<p>What type of language is it?</p><p><b>Mandarin and Hakka</b></p>","options":["Creole","Non-Creole: indigenous","Non-Creole: brought from outside the region"],"correct":2,"why":"Chinese varieties brought from Asia: non-Creole.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Creole, indigenous or brought from elsewhere? (9)","q":"<p>What type of language is it?</p><p><b>Dutch</b></p>","options":["Non-Creole: indigenous","Creole","Non-Creole: brought from outside the region"],"correct":2,"why":"A European language brought by colonisers: non-Creole.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_13l","title":"Module 2 · Unit 5 quiz: Features of Creole and Standard English","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 5: Features of Creole and Standard English. 12 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"ma","title":"Pick the creole feature","q":"<p>Read this WhatsApp voice note transcript from Shanice, in Bridgetown, to her cousin.</p><p>Select the TWO lines that use a pre-verbal tense or aspect marker.</p>","options":["Girl, the cruise ship crowd big today.","I did tell Mommy we go reach late.","Broad Street full a people taking pictures.","Tomorrow I goin pick up the cake by Auntie.","Two ship come in this morning."],"correct":[1,3],"why":"Correct! \"did tell\" uses the past marker \"did\" (and \"go reach\" the future marker), and \"goin\" is a future marker before the verb \"pick up\". Line 1 shows zero copula and line 5 shows no plural -s after a numeral: different features."},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, structure or word? (1)","q":"<p>Classify each underlined feature as phonological, grammatical or lexical.</p><p>Which level of language does the feature belong to?</p><p><b>\"Mi <b>tink</b> so.\" (think)</b></p>","options":["Grammatical","Phonological","Lexical"],"correct":1,"why":"\"th\" becomes \"t\": th-stopping, a sound feature.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, structure or word? (2)","q":"<p>Which level of language does the feature belong to?</p><p><b>\"Dat is di <b>bes'</b> one.\" (best)</b></p>","options":["Grammatical","Phonological","Lexical"],"correct":1,"why":"The final \"t\" of the cluster \"st\" is dropped: consonant cluster reduction.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, structure or word? (3)","q":"<p>Which level of language does the feature belong to?</p><p><b>\"Di pickney <b>dem</b> outside.\" (the children)</b></p>","options":["Lexical","Phonological","Grammatical"],"correct":2,"why":"\"dem\" after the noun marks plural: a grammatical feature.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, structure or word? (4)","q":"<p>Which level of language does the feature belong to?</p><p><b>\"He <b>en</b> come yet.\" (hasn't come)</b></p>","options":["Grammatical","Phonological","Lexical"],"correct":0,"why":"\"en\" is a pre-verbal negative/past marker: grammatical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, structure or word? (5)","q":"<p>Which level of language does the feature belong to?</p><p><b>\"Mi have <b>three book</b>.\" (three books)</b></p>","options":["Grammatical","Phonological","Lexical"],"correct":0,"why":"No plural -s after a numeral is a grammatical feature.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, structure or word? (6)","q":"<p>Which level of language does the feature belong to?</p><p><b>\"Come <b>nyam</b> yuh food.\" (eat)</b></p>","options":["Grammatical","Lexical","Phonological"],"correct":1,"why":"\"nyam\" is an African retention: a lexical feature.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, structure or word? (7)","q":"<p>Which level of language does the feature belong to?</p><p><b>\"Mi <b>foot</b> hurting from the knee.\" (leg)</b></p>","options":["Phonological","Lexical","Grammatical"],"correct":1,"why":"\"foot\" meaning the whole leg is semantic shift: lexical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sound, structure or word? (8)","q":"<p>Which level of language does the feature belong to?</p><p><b>\"Pass the <b>roti</b>.\" (Indian flatbread)</b></p>","options":["Phonological","Grammatical","Lexical"],"correct":2,"why":"\"roti\" is a loanword from Indian languages: lexical.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Name the sound process","q":"<p>Match each phonological process to the pair of examples that shows it. Say the words aloud to hear the change.</p>","left":["Th-stopping","Consonant cluster reduction","Colloquial aphesis","Epenthesis","Metathesis","Palatalisation"],"right":["\"dem\" for them; \"somting\" for something","\"gyorl\" for girl; \"kyan\" for can","\"understan\" for understand; \"wan\" for want","\"aks\" for ask; \"flim\" for film","\"Simith\" for Smith; \"sinake\" for snake","\"spector\" for inspector; \"gainst\" for against"],"correct":[0,2,5,4,3,1]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the salient feature","q":"<p>Match each Creole example to the salient feature it shows.</p>","left":["\"Run go call yu fada.\"","\"Mi hear se di shop close.\"","\"Pass me di hegg, please.\"","\"Mi eye-water start run.\"","\"Dat chile have hard ears.\"","\"Di dress priti priti.\"","\"Don't be so ignorant!\" (meaning bad-tempered)"],"right":["Serial verb construction","\"Say\" used as a complementiser","Shared word, different meaning","Idiom not derived from English","Semantically transparent body-part word","Hypercorrection of /h/","Reduplication for emphasis"],"correct":[0,1,5,4,3,6,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Switch or mix?","q":"<blockquote><p>During the hurricane meeting at the community centre, the district officer explains the shelter plan in clear Standard English. Then he looks at the elderly residents in the front row and says, \"Unu nuh fraid, we go come fi unu early.\"</p></blockquote><p>What is the officer doing, and why?</p>","options":["Using diglossia, because Creole is the High variety.","Code-switching, to reassure the elderly residents and build trust.","Code-mixing, because he does not know enough Standard English.","Using a frozen register to show respect."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Diglossia describes a whole community's pattern, not one speaker's choice. Also, in this situation Creole would be the Low variety, not the High one.","Correct. He moves from one complete sentence in Standard English to another in Creole, and the purpose is emotional: to comfort and show solidarity.","He has just given a whole explanation in Standard English, so it is not a lack of ability. And the change happens between sentences, so it is switching, not mixing.","Frozen register uses fixed wording like an anthem or oath. His comforting words are spontaneous."]}]},{"id":"assess_15s","title":"Module 2 · Unit 6 quiz: Attitudes to Language","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 6: Attitudes to Language. 15 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the attitude to the evidence","q":"<p>Match each attitude adjective to the piece of evidence that best shows it.</p>","left":["Scorn","Pride","Embarrassment","Admiration","Acceptance","Loyalty"],"right":["Mrs. Brooks rolls her eyes: \"Listen to that gutter talk. No wonder they can't find jobs.\"","Leah lowers her voice and apologises to the visitor: \"Sorry, I talk too local sometimes.\"","Devon grins on stage: \"I perform in we language because it is the voice of my ancestors.\"","The coach shrugs: \"Talk how you comfortable, once the team understand the play.\"","Grandpa refuses to change: \"I go talk mi own way till I dead. Nobody making me give it up.\"","\"Listen how nicely the news anchor speaks. I wish I could sound like that,\" Kai sighs."],"correct":[0,2,1,5,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (1)","q":"<p>Each statement shows an attitude. Sort it by the factor that best explains where the attitude came from.</p><p>Which factor produced this attitude?</p><p><b>\"After a tourist couldn't understand me at work, I felt ashamed of my vernacular.\"</b></p>","options":["Education","History and society","Personal experience","Socialisation"],"correct":2,"why":"A specific event in the speaker's life produced the embarrassment: personal experience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (2)","q":"<p>Which factor produced this attitude?</p><p><b>\"When I went to study in Holland, people loved my island English, and now I'm proud of it.\"</b></p>","options":["Socialisation","Personal experience","History and society","Education"],"correct":1,"why":"A particular experience abroad changed the speaker's feelings: personal experience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (3)","q":"<p>Which factor produced this attitude?</p><p><b>\"In primary school we lost points for every Creole word in our compositions.\"</b></p>","options":["History and society","Personal experience","Education","Socialisation"],"correct":2,"why":"Schooling taught that Creole is \"wrong\": education.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (4)","q":"<p>Which factor produced this attitude?</p><p><b>\"Our English teacher said nobody would hire us if we spoke dialect.\"</b></p>","options":["Personal experience","Education","Socialisation","History and society"],"correct":1,"why":"The attitude was taught through school: education.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (5)","q":"<p>Which factor produced this attitude?</p><p><b>\"Grandma always slapped my hand if I answered her in dialect.\"</b></p>","options":["Socialisation","Education","History and society","Personal experience"],"correct":0,"why":"Family rules pass on the community's language ideology: socialisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (6)","q":"<p>Which factor produced this attitude?</p><p><b>\"In our church, everybody pray in proper English; dialect is for the street.\"</b></p>","options":["Socialisation","History and society","Personal experience","Education"],"correct":0,"why":"The community's beliefs shape the attitude: socialisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (7)","q":"<p>Which factor produced this attitude?</p><p><b>\"The colonial masters' language became the 'proper' one, and that idea never left.\"</b></p>","options":["Personal experience","History and society","Education","Socialisation"],"correct":1,"why":"The attitude is explained by the colonial past: history and society.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the factor (8)","q":"<p>Which factor produced this attitude?</p><p><b>\"Because enslaved Africans' speech was mocked for centuries, Creole still carries stigma.\"</b></p>","options":["Socialisation","Education","Personal experience","History and society"],"correct":3,"why":"The sociohistorical development of Creole explains the stigma: history and society.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name it precisely","q":"<blockquote><p>When her son answered the phone with \"Who dis?\", Mrs. Carty snatched it from him. \"How many times I must tell you? We don't talk like that in this house. People will think we have no class.\" Later she told her sister, \"My own mother beat that dialect out of me, and I am doing the same for him.\"</p></blockquote><p>Which answer BEST identifies Mrs. Carty's attitude and the factor that produced it?</p>","options":["A negative attitude, produced by education.","Indifference, produced by history and society.","Contempt for the vernacular, produced mainly by socialisation in her family.","Pride in Standard English, produced by personal experience."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["\"Negative\" is too vague and earns few marks. And there is no mention of school here; the evidence points to her mother and her home.","Snatching the phone is not indifference. It is a strong reaction. Always match the adjective to the strength of the evidence.","Correct. \"People will think we have no class\" shows contempt, linking the vernacular to low status, and \"My own mother beat that dialect out of me\" shows the attitude was passed down through her family: socialisation.","She may value Standard English, but the question is about her reaction to her son's vernacular, which is contempt. Her attitude came from how she was raised, not from a single personal event."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Attitudes: exam drill (1)","q":"<p>Choose the BEST answer for each short exam-style question.</p><p>\"I don't care how people talk, once they get the job done.\" The best adjective for this attitude is...</p>","options":["scorn","admiration","indifference"],"correct":2,"why":"The speaker places no value either way on the variety: indifference.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Attitudes: exam drill (2)","q":"<p>Which phrase would earn the MOST credit in an essay?</p>","options":["\"She has a negative attitude.\"","\"She does not like Creole.\"","\"She shows embarrassment, evident when she whispers, 'Sorry for my bad English.'\""],"correct":2,"why":"It names a precise feeling AND supports it with an exact quotation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Attitudes: exam drill (3)","q":"<p>A radio station plays Creole jingles but reads all news in Standard English. This may suggest that Creole is viewed as...</p>","options":["a frozen register","a pidgin","suitable for entertainment but not for serious information"],"correct":2,"why":"The split in use reflects an evaluation of Creole's status in \"serious\" domains.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Attitudes: exam drill (4)","q":"<p>\"Miss always told us Creole would hold us back in life.\" The factor is...</p>","options":["socialisation","education","history and society"],"correct":1,"why":"\"Miss\" is a teacher, so the attitude came through schooling: education.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Attitudes: exam drill (5)","q":"<p>In Sint Maarten, the language most residents use in daily life is...</p>","options":["English","Papiamentu","Dutch"],"correct":0,"why":"Dutch is used for law and much government business, but English is the everyday language of most residents.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_17k","title":"Module 2 · Unit 7 quiz: Language, Identity, Power and Technology","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 7: Language, Identity, Power and Technology. 13 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the identity role","q":"<p>Match each situation to the role of language in Caribbean identity that it MAINLY shows.</p>","left":["A grandmother in Saint Lucia teaches her grandchildren Kwéyòl folk songs.","At a regional youth summit, delegates from six islands laugh at shared Creole proverbs.","A calypsonian uses Creole to satirise a political scandal.","Villagers can tell from his speech that a visitor comes from the other end of the island."],"right":["Transmission of culture","Building community and regional integration","Demarcation of social and regional communities","Expression of culture"],"correct":[0,1,3,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which role of language? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each situation by the role of language it MAINLY shows.</p><p>Social, political, ethical or psychological?</p><p><b>Friends in a group chat use island slang that outsiders cannot follow.</b></p>","options":["Political","Ethical","Psychological","Social"],"correct":3,"why":"Language marks group membership and solidarity: social.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which role of language? (2)","q":"<p>Social, political, ethical or psychological?</p><p><b>A church choir greets each other with the same Papiamentu blessing every Sunday.</b></p>","options":["Political","Psychological","Social","Ethical"],"correct":2,"why":"Shared language builds belonging: social.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which role of language? (3)","q":"<p>Social, political, ethical or psychological?</p><p><b>Parliament debates whether to make a creole an official language.</b></p>","options":["Social","Ethical","Psychological","Political"],"correct":3,"why":"Language policy and status are political.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which role of language? (4)","q":"<p>Social, political, ethical or psychological?</p><p><b>Marchers chant slogans in Creole outside the Government Administration Building.</b></p>","options":["Political","Social","Psychological","Ethical"],"correct":0,"why":"Language used to signal protest is political.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which role of language? (5)","q":"<p>Social, political, ethical or psychological?</p><p><b>A bank refuses to serve a customer because she cannot speak Standard English.</b></p>","options":["Social","Psychological","Political","Ethical"],"correct":3,"why":"Unfair treatment because of language is an ethical issue.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which role of language? (6)","q":"<p>Social, political, ethical or psychological?</p><p><b>Hospital forms are printed only in a language many patients cannot read.</b></p>","options":["Ethical","Social","Political","Psychological"],"correct":0,"why":"It denies people fair access to information: ethical.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which role of language? (7)","q":"<p>Social, political, ethical or psychological?</p><p><b>After being mocked for his accent, a boy refuses to read aloud in class.</b></p>","options":["Social","Political","Ethical","Psychological"],"correct":3,"why":"Language affecting confidence and face: psychological.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which role of language? (8)","q":"<p>Social, political, ethical or psychological?</p><p><b>A student feels proud and confident performing a Creole poem.</b></p>","options":["Psychological","Political","Ethical","Social"],"correct":0,"why":"Language shaping self-esteem: psychological.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which role is breached?","q":"<blockquote><p>Marisol, who grew up in the Cayman Islands speaking Spanish at home and the island's English vernacular with friends, applies for a job as a hotel cleaner. The supervisor rejects her, saying, \"We don't hire people who talk like that,\" although the job does not involve speaking to guests.</p></blockquote><p>Which role of language is MOST clearly being breached by the supervisor?</p>","options":["Aesthetic","Ethical","Political","Social"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Aesthetic refers to the beauty of language, not to fairness.","Correct. Marisol is treated unfairly and denied an opportunity because of the way she speaks, even though it does not affect the job. That is discrimination: an ethical issue.","There is no government policy, protest or power struggle here. The issue is unfair treatment of an individual.","Language does mark Marisol as part of a group, but the key problem is the unfair decision, which makes it ethical."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The job application email (step 1)","q":"<p>Jaden, 18, is applying for a summer internship at a Bridgetown law firm. Help him make the right choices.</p><p>Jaden opens his email. Which address should he send the application from?</p>","options":["jaden.richardson@...","kingjay_247@..."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good choice. A name-based address is professional and easy to recognise.","A playful nickname is an inappropriate digital identifier for a law firm."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The job application email (step 2)","q":"<p>Which opening line suits the email?</p>","options":["\"Hey!! 😊 Wanna apply 4 the internship ting\"","\"Dear Ms. Arrindell, I am writing to apply for the summer internship advertised on your website.\""],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Emojis, textspeak and slang are inappropriate in a formal application.","Formal register, a correct salutation and a clear purpose."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The job application email (step 3)","q":"<p>Jaden's friend says, \"Just write it how you text, they'll understand.\" What should Jaden do?</p>","options":["Leave in abbreviations like \"u\" and \"bc\" to save time.","Proofread for full spelling and punctuation, keep a formal register, and sign off with his full name and phone number."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Text-message spelling signals carelessness to an employer.","Exactly. Matching register to context shows he understands his audience."]}]},{"id":"assess_1be","title":"Module 2 · Unit 8 quiz: Communicative Behaviours and the Module 2 Essay","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 2, Unit 8: Communicative Behaviours and the Module 2 Essay. 21 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Name the behaviour (1)","q":"<p>Sort each description into the type of non-verbal behaviour it shows.</p><p>Which non-verbal code is this?</p><p><b>Auntie lets out a long steups when the bus driver passes her.</b></p>","options":["Proxemics","Haptics","Chromatics","Chronemics","Paralanguage"],"correct":4,"why":"A vocal sound without words: paralanguage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the behaviour (2)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is this?</p><p><b>The principal lowers his voice to almost a whisper before announcing the results.</b></p>","options":["Chronemics","Chromatics","Paralanguage","Haptics","Proxemics"],"correct":2,"why":"Changing volume and tone is paralanguage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the behaviour (3)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is this?</p><p><b>The student moves her chair away from the boy who teased her.</b></p>","options":["Proxemics","Chronemics","Paralanguage","Haptics","Chromatics"],"correct":0,"why":"Using distance to send a message: proxemics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the behaviour (4)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is this?</p><p><b>The minister arrives forty minutes late to the community meeting.</b></p>","options":["Chronemics","Proxemics","Haptics","Chromatics","Paralanguage"],"correct":0,"why":"Lateness sends a message about priorities: chronemics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the behaviour (5)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is this?</p><p><b>The coach squeezes the nervous player's shoulder.</b></p>","options":["Paralanguage","Chronemics","Haptics","Proxemics","Chromatics"],"correct":2,"why":"Touch: haptics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the behaviour (6)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is this?</p><p><b>Everyone wears red, white and blue on Constitution Day.</b></p>","options":["Haptics","Chronemics","Paralanguage","Chromatics","Proxemics"],"correct":3,"why":"National colours carry a cultural message: chromatics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the behaviour (7)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is this?</p><p><b>The family dresses in white for the thanksgiving service.</b></p>","options":["Chromatics","Proxemics","Chronemics","Haptics","Paralanguage"],"correct":0,"why":"The colour white conveys meaning (purity, celebration or mourning, depending on context): chromatics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read it in context (step 1)","q":"<p>You are a CAPE student analysing an extract for Paper 2. Choose the best interpretation at each step. Remember: ask WHO and WHY.</p><p>Before the final exam, Ms. Daniel stands at the door. As Rhea passes, Ms. Daniel squeezes her hand and holds her gaze for a moment.</p><p>What does the hand squeeze most likely communicate here?</p>","options":["A warning to stop misbehaving.","Encouragement and reassurance before a stressful exam."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A squeeze can warn in some contexts, but nothing here suggests misbehaviour. You ignored the context.","Yes. Who: a teacher. Why: a student is about to sit an exam. In that context, touch and steady eye contact signal support."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read it in context (step 2)","q":"<p>Later, in the staff room, Mr. Gumbs arrives twenty minutes late to the department meeting, sits at the back, and scrolls on his phone. The head of department glances at the clock and folds her arms.</p><p>Which interpretation is best supported?</p>","options":["He is clearly very busy and committed to his work.","His chronemics, proxemics and phone use suggest disengagement; her glance at the clock and folded arms suggest disapproval."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["That reading is not supported. Arriving late, sitting at a distance and scrolling point the other way.","Excellent: you named several behaviours and interpreted each in context."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read it in context (step 3)","q":"<p>How would you write this in your essay?</p>","options":["\"Mr. Gumbs's late arrival (chronemics) and choice to sit at the back (proxemics) suggest indifference, which the head of department meets with visible disapproval, shown by her glance at the clock and folded arms.\"","\"There is a lot of body language in the staff room.\""],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["This names the behaviours, gives evidence and interprets in context. Full credit territory.","Too vague: no named behaviour, no evidence, no interpretation."]},{"t":"mc","title":"All the non-verbal codes (1)","q":"<p>Now sort across the full set of non-verbal codes, including <b>oculesics</b> (eye behaviour) and <b>artefacts</b> (objects and appearance).</p><p>Which non-verbal code is MAINLY at work?</p><p><b>The prefect folds her arms and shakes her head as the boys push into the lunch line.</b></p>","options":["Haptics","Kinesics","Paralanguage","Artefacts","Proxemics","Oculesics","Chronemics"],"correct":1,"why":"Posture and head movement: kinesics (body language).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"All the non-verbal codes (2)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is MAINLY at work?</p><p><b>Granny gives the child a long cut-eye across the church pew.</b></p>","options":["Kinesics","Chronemics","Haptics","Artefacts","Oculesics","Paralanguage","Proxemics"],"correct":4,"why":"A look that carries scorn: oculesics, the study of eye behaviour.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"All the non-verbal codes (3)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is MAINLY at work?</p><p><b>The interviewee holds steady eye contact with each panel member as she answers.</b></p>","options":["Chronemics","Proxemics","Oculesics","Haptics","Kinesics","Paralanguage","Artefacts"],"correct":2,"why":"Eye contact signals confidence and honesty here: oculesics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"All the non-verbal codes (4)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is MAINLY at work?</p><p><b>The new student sits at the far end of the bench, away from everyone.</b></p>","options":["Kinesics","Artefacts","Paralanguage","Oculesics","Chronemics","Haptics","Proxemics"],"correct":6,"why":"Use of distance and space: proxemics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"All the non-verbal codes (5)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is MAINLY at work?</p><p><b>The tour guide slows down and lowers his pitch to tell the ghost story of the old fort.</b></p>","options":["Artefacts","Chronemics","Haptics","Kinesics","Paralanguage","Proxemics","Oculesics"],"correct":4,"why":"Pace and pitch of the voice: paralanguage (vocalics).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"All the non-verbal codes (6)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is MAINLY at work?</p><p><b>Teammates exchange fist bumps after the winning goal.</b></p>","options":["Chronemics","Artefacts","Haptics","Paralanguage","Proxemics","Kinesics","Oculesics"],"correct":2,"why":"Touch: haptics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"All the non-verbal codes (7)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is MAINLY at work?</p><p><b>The applicant arrives fifteen minutes early for the interview.</b></p>","options":["Artefacts","Kinesics","Oculesics","Chronemics","Haptics","Proxemics","Paralanguage"],"correct":3,"why":"Use of time sends a message of respect: chronemics.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"All the non-verbal codes (8)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is MAINLY at work?</p><p><b>The lawyer wears a dark suit, a gold watch and carries a leather briefcase into court.</b></p>","options":["Chronemics","Oculesics","Proxemics","Artefacts","Paralanguage","Haptics","Kinesics"],"correct":3,"why":"Clothing and objects communicate status and professionalism: artefacts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"All the non-verbal codes (9)","q":"<p>Which non-verbal code is MAINLY at work?</p><p><b>The receptionist's desk displays her diplomas and a small Belizean flag.</b></p>","options":["Oculesics","Kinesics","Chronemics","Paralanguage","Proxemics","Artefacts","Haptics"],"correct":5,"why":"Objects arranged in a space communicate identity and credibility: artefacts.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Build the introduction","q":"<p>Put these sentences in the correct order to form a strong Module 2 introduction.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["The extract describes a family gathering in Mandeville where three generations discuss how a teenage girl should speak at her scholarship interview.","It shows that attitudes to Creole within a single family can range from shame to fierce pride, shaped by education and the region's history.","The writer's purpose is to highlight and examine how these attitudes affect young people's sense of identity.","This essay will discuss the language varieties and registers used, the attitudes displayed and the communicative behaviours that reveal them."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4"],"correct":[0,1,2,3]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which thesis?","q":"<p>The question asks you to discuss the language varieties, attitudes and communicative behaviours in an extract. Which thesis statement is BEST?</p>","options":["\"This essay will discuss the attitudes in the extract.\"","\"This essay will talk about language in the extract.\"","\"Creole is a beautiful language that everyone should be proud of.\"","\"This essay will examine the shift between Creole and Standard English, the contrasting attitudes of embarrassment and pride, and the gestures and paralanguage that reveal them.\""],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["It covers only one of the three parts required. Always check that your thesis includes everything the question asks.","Far too vague. It does not mention varieties, attitudes or behaviours, so it gives no roadmap.","This is a personal opinion, not a thesis for an analytical essay about an extract.","Correct. It covers all three parts of the question, in a clear order, and is specific enough to guide the body paragraphs."]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Module 3","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_1dl","title":"Module 3 · Unit 1 quiz: The Process of Communication","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 1: The Process of Communication. 17 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Put the process in order","q":"<p>Ms. Richardson, a tour operator in St. George's, wants to tell a cruise passenger that the island tour will leave 30 minutes late. Arrange the stages of the communication process in the correct sequence.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Ms. Richardson has the idea that the passenger must be told about the delay (source).","She chooses her words: \"Good morning! The tour will now leave at 10:30.\" (encoding)","She sends the message by text message to the passenger's phone (medium and channel).","The passenger receives the text on the ship (receiver).","The passenger reads and interprets the text: \"I have extra time for breakfast.\" (decoding)","The passenger replies, \"Thanks, see you at 10:30!\" (feedback)"],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model fits?","q":"<blockquote><p>During a parent-teacher meeting, Mr. Hodge explains Kayla's grades. As he speaks, he notices her mother frowning and folding her arms, so he slows down and adds, \"But she has improved a lot since October.\" The mother relaxes and nods while asking a question.</p></blockquote><p>Which model of communication BEST describes this exchange?</p>","options":["Transactional model","Linear model","Interactive model","Shannon–Weaver model"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Both Mr. Hodge and the mother are sending and receiving messages simultaneously (he speaks while she frowns; she nods while asking) and they adjust to each other. That simultaneous, co-created meaning is the hallmark of the transactional model.","Not quite. A linear model shows a one-way flow with no feedback. Here Mr. Hodge is constantly reading and responding to the mother's reactions, so the exchange is clearly not one-way.","Close, but the interactive model suggests people take turns: send, then reply. Here Mr. Hodge is receiving non-verbal messages <i>while</i> he is still speaking, which goes beyond turn-taking.","The Shannon–Weaver model is a linear model built for technical signals. It does not account for the immediate feedback and adjustment we see in this meeting."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the model","q":"<p>Match each model of communication to the description that fits it best.</p>","left":["Shannon–Weaver model","Lasswell's model","Berlo's SMCR model","Interactive model","Transactional model"],"right":["Linear model designed for telephone signals; introduced the idea of noise","Both participants send and receive at the same time and co-create meaning","Who says what, in which channel, to whom, with what effect?","Source, message, channel and receiver, each shaped by skills, attitudes, knowledge, social system and culture","Adds feedback and fields of experience; participants take turns"],"correct":[0,2,3,4,1]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which model helps most?","q":"<blockquote><p>A tourism office in Saint Lucia ran a month-long radio and Instagram campaign urging cruise visitors to shop at local craft markets. The director now wants to know whether the campaign changed visitors' behaviour.</p></blockquote><p>Which model would be MOST useful for analysing this campaign, and why?</p>","options":["The interactive model, because Instagram users take turns posting.","Lasswell's model, because its final question, \"with what effect?\", focuses on the campaign's impact on its audience.","The transactional model, because the director and visitors talk face to face.","Berlo's model, because it shows that channels are the five senses."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Some interaction occurs on Instagram, but the question asks about measuring the campaign's effect, which Lasswell's model highlights.","Correct. Lasswell's model was designed for analysing mass communication, and its focus on effect matches the director's question exactly.","There is no face-to-face, simultaneous exchange here; it is a mass media campaign.","Berlo's model is useful for analysing sender and receiver factors, but the director's question is about effect, which is Lasswell's focus."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Content or context? (1)","q":"<p>A station, Harbour FM, broadcasts a hurricane watch for Anguilla. Sort each detail: is it part of the <b>content</b> (what is said) or the <b>context</b> (where, when, to whom, why)?</p><p>Content or context?</p><p><b>\"A hurricane watch is now in effect for Anguilla.\"</b></p>","options":["Context (where, when, to whom, why)","Content (what is said)"],"correct":1,"why":"These are the actual words of the message, so this is content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Content or context? (2)","q":"<p>Content or context?</p><p><b>\"Secure loose objects in your yard and fill water containers.\"</b></p>","options":["Content (what is said)","Context (where, when, to whom, why)"],"correct":0,"why":"Instructions spoken in the broadcast are part of what is said: content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Content or context? (3)","q":"<p>Content or context?</p><p><b>\"Shelters will open at noon tomorrow.\"</b></p>","options":["Content (what is said)","Context (where, when, to whom, why)"],"correct":0,"why":"Another piece of information in the message itself: content.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Content or context? (4)","q":"<p>Content or context?</p><p><b>It is broadcast at 6 a.m., while many people are getting ready for work.</b></p>","options":["Context (where, when, to whom, why)","Content (what is said)"],"correct":0,"why":"This tells us WHEN the message is sent, so it is context.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Content or context? (5)","q":"<p>Content or context?</p><p><b>Listeners include fishermen, hotel workers and elderly residents.</b></p>","options":["Context (where, when, to whom, why)","Content (what is said)"],"correct":0,"why":"This tells us TO WHOM the message is sent, so it is context.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Content or context? (6)","q":"<p>Content or context?</p><p><b>The aim is to help people prepare before the winds arrive.</b></p>","options":["Context (where, when, to whom, why)","Content (what is said)"],"correct":0,"why":"This is WHY the message is sent, its purpose, which is part of the context.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Content or context? (7)","q":"<p>Content or context?</p><p><b>It is the peak of hurricane season, two weeks after another storm.</b></p>","options":["Context (where, when, to whom, why)","Content (what is said)"],"correct":0,"why":"The wider situation and timing shape how people hear the message: context.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Content or context? (8)","q":"<p>Content or context?</p><p><b>The message is read from the station's studio in The Valley.</b></p>","options":["Content (what is said)","Context (where, when, to whom, why)"],"correct":1,"why":"This is WHERE the message comes from: context.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the element (1)","q":"<p>Each sentence describes one element of the communication process. Identify it.</p><p>A loud generator outside the classroom drowns out the teacher's voice.</p>","options":["Feedback","Channel","Noise"],"correct":2,"why":"The generator interferes with the message, so it is physical noise.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the element (2)","q":"<p>A student raises her hand and says, \"Sir, can you repeat that?\"</p>","options":["Encoding","Feedback","Context"],"correct":1,"why":"The student is responding to the message, which is feedback. Asking for repetition is very useful feedback because it shows the message was not fully received.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the element (3)","q":"<p>The minister chooses simple words and a local example for his press release.</p>","options":["Decoding","Receiver","Encoding"],"correct":2,"why":"Choosing words and examples is encoding: turning ideas into symbols.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the element (4)","q":"<p>Listeners work out that \"batten down\" means they must secure their homes.</p>","options":["Noise","Decoding","Medium"],"correct":1,"why":"Interpreting the meaning of words is decoding.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the element (5)","q":"<p>The message is posted as a flyer on the supermarket notice board.</p>","options":["Feedback","Sender","Medium"],"correct":2,"why":"The flyer is the form or vehicle carrying the message, the medium.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_1fu","title":"Module 3 · Unit 2 quiz: Audience, Context and Forms of Communication","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 2: Audience, Context and Forms of Communication. 14 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Profile, then choose","q":"<blockquote><p>The Sandy Ground Seniors' Club wants to tell its members, most of them over 70 and many of whom left school early, about a change in the date of its monthly lunch. Members live in several districts; a few do not own mobile phones, but almost all attend Sunday service.</p></blockquote><p>Based on the audience profile, which medium and channel would be MOST effective?</p>","options":["A detailed email newsletter explaining the reasons for the change.","A spoken announcement after Sunday service, backed up by a large-print flyer and phone calls to those who were absent.","An Instagram story with a countdown sticker.","A press release to the national newspaper."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Email assumes every member has a device and is comfortable reading long text. The profile tells you some members have no phones and limited schooling, so a long email would exclude many of them.","Excellent. You used every part of the profile: age (spoken, large print), educational background (short, simple message), location and access (a place almost everyone attends, plus phone calls to reach the rest). Repetition through two channels also builds in a check that nobody is missed.","Instagram suits a young, online audience. This profile describes people over 70, some without phones, so most members would never see the story. The medium does not match the audience's age or access.","A press release goes to journalists, who decide whether to print it, and it targets the whole public rather than a small club. It is too indirect and uncertain for a simple date change."]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the level","q":"<p>Match each situation to the level of communication it shows.</p>","left":["Intrapersonal","Interpersonal","Small group","Public","Mass","Organisational"],"right":["A pastor delivers a sermon to a congregation of 300.","Jaden mentally rehearses what he will say to the coach before practice.","Five students on the Student Council plan a fundraiser.","A radio station broadcasts a road-safety jingle across the island.","Two friends chat about Carnival costumes while waiting for the bus.","The HR department sends a circular about new uniform rules to all hotel staff."],"correct":[1,4,2,0,3,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which direction? (1)","q":"<p>At Flamboyant Heights College in Barbados, decide the direction or network of each message.</p><p>The principal sends a circular to all teachers about new coursework deadlines.</p>","options":["Downward","Upward","Horizontal"],"correct":0,"why":"From the top of the hierarchy to staff below: downward.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which direction? (2)","q":"<p>A form teacher writes to the vice-principal requesting more desks for her class.</p>","options":["Grapevine","Upward","Downward"],"correct":1,"why":"A request from a subordinate to a superior: upward.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which direction? (3)","q":"<p>The heads of Science and Mathematics meet to plan a STEM week together.</p>","options":["Upward","Downward","Horizontal"],"correct":2,"why":"Same level, different departments: horizontal (lateral).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which direction? (4)","q":"<p>In the staff room, someone whispers that the principal may be transferred.</p>","options":["Formal downward","Upward","Grapevine"],"correct":2,"why":"Unofficial talk that is not confirmed is the grapevine.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which direction? (5)","q":"<p>The Student Council president submits a report on canteen complaints to the principal.</p>","options":["Horizontal","Upward","Downward"],"correct":1,"why":"Students reporting to the principal is upward communication.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Up, down, across or diagonal? (1)","q":"<p>At the Harbour View Hotel in Montego Bay, sort each message by the direction or network it travels through.</p><p>Which direction of organisational communication?</p><p><b>The general manager emails all supervisors the new hurricane-season procedures.</b></p>","options":["Horizontal","Diagonal","Grapevine","Upward","Downward"],"correct":4,"why":"From the top of the hierarchy to those below: downward.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Up, down, across or diagonal? (2)","q":"<p>Which direction of organisational communication?</p><p><b>A housekeeper submits a written suggestion to her supervisor about safer cleaning chemicals.</b></p>","options":["Diagonal","Horizontal","Downward","Upward","Grapevine"],"correct":3,"why":"From a subordinate to a superior: upward.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Up, down, across or diagonal? (3)","q":"<p>Which direction of organisational communication?</p><p><b>The front-desk supervisor and the restaurant supervisor agree on breakfast times for a tour group.</b></p>","options":["Horizontal","Grapevine","Upward","Diagonal","Downward"],"correct":0,"why":"Two people at the same level coordinating: horizontal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Up, down, across or diagonal? (4)","q":"<p>Which direction of organisational communication?</p><p><b>The head of accounts phones a junior chef directly to query a missing supplier invoice.</b></p>","options":["Upward","Horizontal","Grapevine","Downward","Diagonal"],"correct":4,"why":"Different department AND different level, bypassing the chef's own manager: diagonal (cross-channel).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Up, down, across or diagonal? (5)","q":"<p>Which direction of organisational communication?</p><p><b>The marketing manager messages a front-desk clerk to collect guests' feedback cards for a report.</b></p>","options":["Diagonal","Upward","Grapevine","Horizontal","Downward"],"correct":0,"why":"A manager in one department contacting a junior worker in another: diagonal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Up, down, across or diagonal? (6)","q":"<p>Which direction of organisational communication?</p><p><b>Staff whisper in the canteen that the hotel is about to be sold.</b></p>","options":["Upward","Grapevine","Diagonal","Downward","Horizontal"],"correct":1,"why":"Unofficial, unconfirmed talk: the grapevine.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Pick the best medium","q":"<p>The manager of the Harbour Point Marina must tell an employee, privately, that his contract will not be renewed. Tap the ONE most appropriate medium.</p><p>Which medium is most appropriate for this message?</p>","options":["A private face-to-face meeting, followed by a formal letter.","A notice on the staff notice board.","A memo copied to all department heads.","A mention in the monthly newsletter.","A message in the staff WhatsApp group."],"correct":[0],"why":"Exactly right. Bad news that affects someone personally should be delivered face to face, in private, where the manager can show empathy and respond to questions. The formal letter then provides the official written record."}]},{"id":"assess_1hy","title":"Module 3 · Unit 3 quiz: Barriers (Noise) and How to Repair Them","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 3: Barriers (Noise) and How to Repair Them. 13 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the barriers (1)","q":"<p>A youth council in Antigua is holding a public meeting about a new skate park. Things keep going wrong. Sort each problem into the correct type of barrier.</p><p>What type of barrier is each problem?</p><p><b>The meeting is outdoors and a jet landing at the airport drowns out the speaker.</b></p>","options":["Semantic","Physical","Physiological","Technological","Psychological"],"correct":1,"why":"Noise from the environment is a physical barrier.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the barriers (2)","q":"<p>What type of barrier is each problem?</p><p><b>An elderly resident with a hearing impairment cannot follow the discussion.</b></p>","options":["Physical","Semantic","Psychological","Physiological","Technological"],"correct":3,"why":"A condition of the body is a physiological barrier.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the barriers (3)","q":"<p>What type of barrier is each problem?</p><p><b>One parent is so angry about noise from skaters that he refuses to listen to the plan.</b></p>","options":["Physiological","Semantic","Physical","Technological","Psychological"],"correct":4,"why":"Strong emotion and a closed mind are psychological barriers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the barriers (4)","q":"<p>What type of barrier is each problem?</p><p><b>The engineer keeps saying \"load-bearing substrate\" and \"ingress points\".</b></p>","options":["Physical","Physiological","Semantic","Technological","Psychological"],"correct":2,"why":"Technical jargon is a semantic barrier.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the barriers (5)","q":"<p>What type of barrier is each problem?</p><p><b>Several Spanish-speaking residents do not understand the English presentation.</b></p>","options":["Physiological","Semantic","Psychological","Physical","Technological"],"correct":1,"why":"A language difference is a semantic (linguistic) barrier. You could also discuss it as cultural, but the core problem is language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the barriers (6)","q":"<p>What type of barrier is each problem?</p><p><b>The projector will not connect to the laptop, so nobody sees the design.</b></p>","options":["Psychological","Technological","Physical","Physiological","Semantic"],"correct":1,"why":"Equipment failure is a technological barrier.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the barriers (7)","q":"<p>What type of barrier is each problem?</p><p><b>Teenagers stop listening because they assume the adults are against them.</b></p>","options":["Physiological","Physical","Semantic","Technological","Psychological"],"correct":4,"why":"Assumptions and prejudice are psychological barriers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Sort the barriers (8)","q":"<p>What type of barrier is each problem?</p><p><b>The livestream for people at home keeps freezing.</b></p>","options":["Technological","Semantic","Psychological","Physical","Physiological"],"correct":0,"why":"Poor connectivity is a technological barrier.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Barrier meets solution","q":"<p>Match each barrier to the BEST solution.</p>","left":["A guest speaker uses legal jargon with Form 4 students.","A construction site next door makes a class discussion impossible.","A worker is too upset after an argument to listen to new instructions.","A hurricane advisory is broadcast only in English in a multilingual district.","The video call keeps dropping during an online lesson.","Staff complain that messages pass through five managers before reaching them."],"right":["Test the connection beforehand and share notes as a back-up.","Translate the advisory into the main community languages.","Allow time to calm down, acknowledge feelings, then talk.","Move to a quieter room or reschedule the discussion.","Shorten the chain of command and hold direct staff briefings.","Explain technical terms in everyday language with local examples."],"correct":[5,3,2,1,0,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Choose the best strategy","q":"<blockquote><p>The Ministry of Public Health wants to warn residents in a densely populated district about a dengue outbreak. Many residents are recent migrants who speak Spanish or Haitian Creole, and some have low literacy. A previous English-language newspaper notice had little effect.</p></blockquote><p>Which strategy would MOST effectively overcome the main barriers in this situation?</p>","options":["Print a longer, more detailed notice in the English newspaper.","Ask school principals to email parents in formal English.","Post a detailed video on the Ministry's website.","Send community health workers door-to-door with picture-based flyers and messages in Spanish and Haitian Creole."],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["This repeats the original mistake. A longer English notice makes the semantic and literacy barriers worse, not better.","Email in formal English still faces the language, literacy and possible technology barriers. It also reaches only families with school-age children.","A video helps with literacy, but residents may not visit the Ministry website, and it does not solve the language barrier unless translated. Access may also be a technological barrier.","Correct. This tackles the semantic barrier (language) by translating, the literacy barrier with pictures, and builds trust through face-to-face interpersonal contact, which also allows immediate feedback and questions."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The breakdown at Bayside Hotel (step 1)","q":"<p>Two days later, three night staff say they never saw the email: they do not have work email on their phones. Another mix-up happens. What now?</p>","options":["Hold a meeting at shift changeover and go through the procedure in person.","Blame the night staff for not checking email."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Better. You have recognised a technological barrier and switched to a medium that reaches everyone.","Blame increases conflict and ignores the real barrier: they lack access to the medium you chose."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The breakdown at Bayside Hotel (step 2)","q":"<p>At the meeting, the day supervisor folds his arms and says, \"I posted it. Not my fault if they can't read a notice.\" A night clerk rolls her eyes. The tension is obvious.</p><p>How do you respond?</p>","options":["Acknowledge both sides calmly, focus on the system rather than blame, and ask, \"How can we make sure every shift gets every update?\"","Tell the supervisor in front of everyone that he is to blame."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. You are reducing psychological barriers by avoiding blame, and inviting upward communication and ideas from staff.","Public criticism humiliates him and will make him defensive. It also teaches staff that speaking up is dangerous."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The breakdown at Bayside Hotel (step 3)","q":"<p>Staff suggest a few ideas. Which plan do you adopt?</p>","options":["Continue with the notice board but make the font bigger.","A short written handover log signed by each shift, a weekly 10-minute briefing, and urgent updates read aloud at changeover and confirmed by signature."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["This does not solve the real problem: night staff do not visit that location. The medium itself is the barrier.","Strong plan. You use several media (written log, oral briefing), build in feedback (signatures confirm receipt) and create regular formal channels."]}]},{"id":"assess_1jn","title":"Module 3 · Unit 4 quiz: Technology, Culture, Globalisation and Ethics","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 4: Technology, Culture, Globalisation and Ethics. 13 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (1)","q":"<p>A Grenadian tour company is moving all staff communication from printed notices and phone calls to an online messaging app. Sort each point.</p><p>Is each point an advantage or a disadvantage of the new medium?</p><p><b>Tour guides at different beaches get updates instantly.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Speed and reach across locations is a key advantage of new media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (2)","q":"<p>Is each point an advantage or a disadvantage of the new medium?</p><p><b>Older drivers struggle to use the app.</b></p>","options":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Lack of skills is part of the digital divide, and it is also an age barrier.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (3)","q":"<p>Is each point an advantage or a disadvantage of the new medium?</p><p><b>Messages are stored, so instructions can be checked later.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":1,"why":"A searchable record is an advantage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (4)","q":"<p>Is each point an advantage or a disadvantage of the new medium?</p><p><b>A joke in the chat is taken as an insult because there is no tone of voice.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Loss of non-verbal cues causes misunderstanding in text-based media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (5)","q":"<p>Is each point an advantage or a disadvantage of the new medium?</p><p><b>Photos of damaged vehicles can be sent to the mechanic immediately.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":1,"why":"Multimedia makes the message clearer than words alone.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (6)","q":"<p>Is each point an advantage or a disadvantage of the new medium?</p><p><b>After a storm, the app is useless because mobile networks are down.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Dependence on infrastructure is a serious disadvantage in the Caribbean. This is why old media such as battery radio remain important.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (7)","q":"<p>Is each point an advantage or a disadvantage of the new medium?</p><p><b>Staff receive messages at 2 a.m. and feel they must reply.</b></p>","options":["Disadvantage","Advantage"],"correct":0,"why":"Blurred work-life boundaries can cause stress.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"The global village at home","q":"<p>Match each Caribbean example to the effect of the global village it shows best.</p>","left":["A St. George’s teenager saves for weeks to buy the same sneakers as an overseas influencer.","A grandmother watches a church service streamed live from New York every Sunday.","Burger and pizza chains are busier than the local johnnycake stand at lunchtime.","A local designer sells hand-made Carnival costumes to buyers in Toronto and London online.","A cook in Grand Anse posts videos of her nutmeg recipes that are watched by Caribbean families abroad."],"right":["Fashion: global brands and trends shape status and taste.","Two-way flow: Caribbean culture travels outward to the diaspora.","Opportunity: global markets for Caribbean creativity.","Religion: worship crosses borders through media.","Food: imported fast food competes with traditional dishes."],"correct":[0,3,4,2,1]},{"t":"match","title":"Spot the fallacy","q":"<p>Each statement comes from a debate about a proposed new landfill site. Match it to the fallacy it commits.</p>","left":["\"After they announced the landfill, my mango tree stopped bearing. The landfill is poisoning us already!\"","\"Either we accept this site or garbage will pile up in every street.\"","\"My opponent wants the landfill moved, so she obviously doesn't care whether our children have a clean island.\"","\"Why discuss the landfill when our football team just won the regional cup?\"","\"The plan is safe because it will not cause any harm.\"","\"I met one engineer who opposed it, so all engineers think it is dangerous.\""],"right":["Red herring","Strawman","Begging the question","Either/or (false dilemma)","Hasty generalisation","Faulty causation (post hoc)"],"correct":[5,3,1,0,2,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the deception","q":"<blockquote><p>Two days before a local election, a video spreads on WhatsApp. It appears to show a well-known candidate saying she will close the Grand Anse health clinic. The candidate never said this; her face and voice were digitally inserted into someone else's recording.</p></blockquote><p>Which term BEST describes this video?</p>","options":["Misinformation","Opinion","Deepfake","False flag"],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["Misinformation is false information shared without intent to deceive. Some people who forward the video may be spreading misinformation, but the video itself was digitally created on purpose, so a more precise term is needed.","An opinion is a view or judgement. This video falsely presents a statement as real, which is deception, not opinion.","Correct. Her face and voice were digitally altered so that she appears to say something she never said. That is the definition of a deepfake. Its release two days before the election also shows how kairos can be misused.","A false flag is a harmful act designed to look as if someone else did it. Here the trick is a digitally altered video of a person, which is a deepfake."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The viral video dilemma (step 1)","q":"<p>You lead the Clean Coast Youth Group in Grenada. Your campaign wants the government to install more bins at Gouyave. A member brings you a shocking video. Make the ethical decisions.</p><p>The video shows a beach buried in rubbish. It is getting thousands of views. Your member admits, \"It's actually from another country, but nobody will know. If we post it as Gouyave, people will finally care.\"</p><p>What do you do?</p>","options":["Refuse to post it as Gouyave, and instead plan to collect real evidence from the beach.","Post it with the caption \"Gouyave this morning! Shameful!\"","Post it without a caption and let people assume."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Good judgement. Persuasion must rest on truthful evidence. Real local evidence is also more convincing to a local audience.","This is deliberately misleading content used to persuade. It may work for a day, but it puts the group's credibility at risk and deceives the public.","Leaving out the truth so that people draw a false conclusion is still misleading. Silence is not neutral when you know the audience will be deceived."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The viral video dilemma (step 2)","q":"<p>On Saturday your team photographs real litter at Gouyave and counts the bottles collected in one hour. The photos are less dramatic than the viral video. A member says, \"These won't go viral. Let's at least add a few more bottles to the pile before we take the picture.\"</p><p>What do you say?</p>","options":["\"No. We use what we found. Let's make it powerful with honest storytelling and good timing instead.\"","\"Fine, a few extra bottles won't hurt anyone.\""],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Exactly. Staging the evidence is also deception. You can still use strong appeals: pathos through a real story, logos through your bottle count, kairos through timing.","Staging evidence is fabrication, even if it is small. If it is discovered, opponents will dismiss every true thing you say."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The viral video dilemma (step 3)","q":"<p>How will you present your honest campaign?</p>","options":["Post the real photos with no explanation at midnight on a Tuesday.","Post the real photos with your one-hour bottle count, a short video of a young turtle tangled in plastic you actually freed, and release it the week before the cruise season opens, tagging the relevant ministry."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["It is honest, but the message has no clear content and poor context: no data, no story, no call to action and bad timing.","Strong, ethical persuasion: logos (your real data), pathos (a true and moving story), kairos (cruise season, when the beach matters most) and ethos (honest, local evidence)."]}]},{"id":"assess_1lh","title":"Module 3 · Unit 5 quiz: Speech Writing and Rhetorical Appeals","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 5: Speech Writing and Rhetorical Appeals. 11 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Build the planning chain","q":"<p>Kiara, head of a youth environment network in Montserrat, is preparing a speech for a Rotary-style community lunch about plastic on our beaches. Put her planning decisions in the order of the planning chain.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Her listeners are business owners and retired professionals, most of them over 40.","She wants to persuade them to sponsor recycling bins at three beaches.","It is a lunchtime meeting at a hotel, just before the cruise season begins.","Her key message: \"Clean beaches are good business, and a small sponsorship protects a big investment.\"","She chooses formal Standard English with precise, business-friendly vocabulary.","She decides on a confident, respectful tone and a five-minute talk with three photo slides."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"ma","title":"Find the thesis and the closure","q":"<p>Read these lines from Andre's speech to his school's parents' association about a homework club. Tap the TWO lines that are (1) his <b>thesis</b> and (2) his <b>memorable closure</b>.</p><p>Which line is the thesis, and which line is the memorable closure?</p>","options":["So let us make sure that no child in this school ever has to face that ten o'clock problem alone again.","First, students who get help immediately make fewer repeated mistakes.","How many of you have watched your child stare at a Mathematics problem at ten o'clock at night, alone?","Furthermore, parents who work late would no longer have to choose between their jobs and their children's homework.","Good evening, parents, teachers and fellow students.","Tonight I will show you that a homework club would raise grades, reduce stress at home, and give our students a safe place after school."],"correct":[0, 5],"why":"Exactly. The thesis previews all three points in order, and the closure echoes the opening question (\"ten o'clock... alone\"), which makes it memorable. The second line is a hook; lines four and five are body points with transitions."},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the appeal (1)","q":"<p>These lines come from a student campaign encouraging residents to donate blood at the Kingstown Blood Drive. Sort each line by its MAIN appeal.</p><p>Which appeal does each line mainly use?</p><p><b>\"One donation takes about ten minutes and can help more than one patient.\"</b></p>","options":["Ethos","Pathos","Kairos","Logos"],"correct":3,"why":"A practical, reasoned benefit: logos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the appeal (2)","q":"<p>Which appeal does each line mainly use?</p><p><b>\"Donated blood cannot be manufactured; hospitals depend entirely on volunteers.\"</b></p>","options":["Pathos","Ethos","Logos","Kairos"],"correct":2,"why":"A logical fact that explains why donors are needed: logos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the appeal (3)","q":"<p>Which appeal does each line mainly use?</p><p><b>\"As a nurse at the hospital for fifteen years, I see every day what your donation does.\"</b></p>","options":["Ethos","Logos","Pathos","Kairos"],"correct":0,"why":"The speaker's experience and role build credibility: ethos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the appeal (4)","q":"<p>Which appeal does each line mainly use?</p><p><b>\"Endorsed by the island's medical association.\"</b></p>","options":["Ethos","Logos","Pathos","Kairos"],"correct":0,"why":"An authoritative source lends credibility: ethos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the appeal (5)","q":"<p>Which appeal does each line mainly use?</p><p><b>\"Imagine a little boy waiting for blood that nobody gave.\"</b></p>","options":["Kairos","Pathos","Logos","Ethos"],"correct":1,"why":"An emotional image to stir compassion: pathos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the appeal (6)","q":"<p>Which appeal does each line mainly use?</p><p><b>\"Be the hero your neighbour needs.\"</b></p>","options":["Logos","Pathos","Ethos","Kairos"],"correct":1,"why":"Pride and belonging: pathos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the appeal (7)","q":"<p>Which appeal does each line mainly use?</p><p><b>\"Hurricane season is coming, and the blood bank must be full before the storms.\"</b></p>","options":["Logos","Ethos","Kairos","Pathos"],"correct":2,"why":"Linking the message to the right moment: kairos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the appeal (8)","q":"<p>Which appeal does each line mainly use?</p><p><b>\"The drive ends this Saturday. Don't miss your chance.\"</b></p>","options":["Logos","Pathos","Kairos","Ethos"],"correct":2,"why":"A deadline creates urgency: kairos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"The Carnival advert","q":"<blockquote><p>\"It's Carnival time, St. Kitts! Feel the soca in your soul at Village Fete this Saturday night! Early-bird tickets are half price until Wednesday, and free shuttle buses run all night, so nobody has to drink and drive.\"</p></blockquote><p>Which combination of appeals does this radio advert MAINLY use?</p>","options":["Pathos, kairos and logos","Ethos and logos only","Pathos only","Kairos and ethos"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. \"Feel the soca in your soul\" stirs excitement (pathos); \"It's Carnival time\" and \"until Wednesday\" exploit timing (kairos); cheap tickets and safe transport give practical reasons (logos). One short advert blends three appeals.","There is no expert or trusted speaker building credibility here, so ethos is not a main appeal. Logos is present (half-price tickets, free shuttles), but this answer leaves out the strong emotional and timing appeals.","Pathos is certainly there, but look again at \"until Wednesday\" and \"free shuttle buses\". Those lines use timing and practical reasoning too. Most real persuasion blends appeals.","Kairos is right, but ethos needs a credible speaker or authority. The advert's emotional language and practical reasons are much stronger than any appeal to credibility."]}]},{"id":"assess_1nj","title":"Module 3 · Unit 6 quiz: Listening, Delivery and Visual Aids","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 6: Listening, Delivery and Visual Aids. 10 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Name the function (1)","q":"<p>Sort each example by the MAIN function the non-verbal or paralinguistic cue performs.</p><p>What is the cue doing?</p><p><b>The bus driver waves the passenger on without a word.</b></p>","options":["Establishing relationship","Reinforcing","Regulating","Contradicting","Managing impression","Substituting"],"correct":5,"why":"The wave replaces the words \"get on\": substituting.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the function (2)","q":"<p>What is the cue doing?</p><p><b>The principal says \"Absolutely not\" and shakes her head firmly.</b></p>","options":["Reinforcing","Establishing relationship","Substituting","Regulating","Managing impression","Contradicting"],"correct":0,"why":"The head shake repeats and strengthens the words: reinforcing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the function (3)","q":"<p>What is the cue doing?</p><p><b>The chairperson raises her hand and looks at the next speaker to give him the floor.</b></p>","options":["Contradicting","Regulating","Substituting","Establishing relationship","Managing impression","Reinforcing"],"correct":1,"why":"The cues control turn-taking: regulating.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the function (4)","q":"<p>What is the cue doing?</p><p><b>A student says \"I'm really excited about this project\" in a bored monotone.</b></p>","options":["Regulating","Establishing relationship","Contradicting","Reinforcing","Managing impression","Substituting"],"correct":2,"why":"The voice contradicts the words.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the function (5)","q":"<p>What is the cue doing?</p><p><b>A job applicant chooses a neat suit and a firm handshake to appear professional.</b></p>","options":["Establishing relationship","Reinforcing","Substituting","Regulating","Contradicting","Managing impression"],"correct":5,"why":"Dress and touch are chosen to shape how others see him: managing impression.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the function (6)","q":"<p>What is the cue doing?</p><p><b>A grandmother holds her grandson's hand throughout the hospital visit.</b></p>","options":["Substituting","Establishing relationship","Managing impression","Regulating","Reinforcing","Contradicting"],"correct":1,"why":"Touch expresses closeness and trust: establishing relationship.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Name the function (7)","q":"<p>What is the cue doing?</p><p><b>A speaker pauses and lowers her voice just before announcing the winner.</b></p>","options":["Substituting","Managing impression","Reinforcing","Establishing relationship","Regulating","Contradicting"],"correct":4,"why":"The pause signals that something important is coming, guiding how the message is received: regulating.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Stages of listening","q":"<p>Shania listens to a radio announcement about a water outage in her district. Put the stages of her listening in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["She notices the announcement over the sound of the kitchen fan.","She works out that water will be off in Holetown from 9 a.m. to 3 p.m. on Thursday.","She judges that the source, the water company's official spokesperson, is reliable.","She writes \"Thursday, 9 to 3\" on the fridge calendar so she will not forget.","She fills buckets on Wednesday night and texts her grandmother the details."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which type of listening?","q":"<blockquote><p>At Linstead Community Centre, Mr. Gumbs's neighbour has just lost her home in a fire. She talks for a long time about what happened. Mr. Gumbs says little, nods, keeps gentle eye contact and occasionally says, \"That must have been terrifying.\"</p></blockquote><p>Which type of listening is Mr. Gumbs MAINLY using?</p>","options":["Appreciative listening","Empathic listening","Comprehensive listening","Critical listening"],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Appreciative listening is for pleasure, such as enjoying music. This is a distressing situation.","Correct. He is listening to offer emotional and moral support, shown by his patience, gentle eye contact and words that acknowledge her feelings.","He does understand her, but his main purpose is to support her emotionally, not simply to gather information.","Critical listening evaluates the content and form of a message. Mr. Gumbs is not judging her account; he is supporting her."]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the visual-aid mistakes","q":"<p>Kemar is preparing a five-minute presentation to Form 3 students on hurricane preparedness. Here is his plan.</p><p>Select a decision that breaks the principles of effective visual aids. Three decisions do; the feedback names them all.</p>","options":["Each slide will have a clear title and one main idea.","Slide 3 will contain the full text of the government's 400-word preparedness guide, which I will read aloud.","I will use 32-point dark text on a pale background so the back row can read it.","Every slide will spin in with a different animation and a whooshing sound.","I will pass a real hurricane strap around the class while I keep talking.","I will show a 30-second clip of a storm surge to make the danger real.","I will copy my slides onto a flash drive in case the laptop fails."],"correct":[1,3,4],"why":"Well spotted. Reading a 400-word slide aloud, using distracting animations and passing a prop around while speaking all pull attention away from the speaker. The other decisions follow good design principles."}]},{"id":"assess_1qw","title":"Module 3 · Unit 7 quiz: The Writing Process, Meetings and Digital Communication","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 7: The Writing Process, Meetings and Digital Communication. 24 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Put the writing process in order","q":"<p>Aaliyah is writing a letter to the editor about poor street lighting in her district. Put her steps in order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["She draws a cluster map with \"street lights\" in the centre and links \"safety\", \"tourism\" and \"cost\".","She checks the utility company's website and interviews two neighbours.","She writes a complete first version in one sitting.","She moves her strongest argument to the final body paragraph and cuts an irrelevant point.","She fixes a run-on sentence and replaces \"a lot of\" with \"many\".","She checks every comma and corrects \"there\" to \"their\".","She emails the letter to the newspaper."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Speaking or writing? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each form of communication.</p><p>Is this mainly a form of speaking or of writing?</p><p><b>A toast at a wedding reception</b></p>","options":["Writing","Speaking"],"correct":1,"why":"A toast is delivered aloud.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Speaking or writing? (2)","q":"<p>Is this mainly a form of speaking or of writing?</p><p><b>A memorandum to all staff</b></p>","options":["Writing","Speaking"],"correct":0,"why":"A memo is a written document.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Speaking or writing? (3)","q":"<p>Is this mainly a form of speaking or of writing?</p><p><b>A eulogy at a funeral</b></p>","options":["Writing","Speaking"],"correct":1,"why":"A eulogy is spoken.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Speaking or writing? (4)","q":"<p>Is this mainly a form of speaking or of writing?</p><p><b>A meeting agenda</b></p>","options":["Speaking","Writing"],"correct":1,"why":"An agenda is written and circulated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Speaking or writing? (5)","q":"<p>Is this mainly a form of speaking or of writing?</p><p><b>A pamphlet on dengue prevention</b></p>","options":["Writing","Speaking"],"correct":0,"why":"A pamphlet is a written form.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Speaking or writing? (6)","q":"<p>Is this mainly a form of speaking or of writing?</p><p><b>A valedictory address at graduation</b></p>","options":["Speaking","Writing"],"correct":0,"why":"A valedictory address is a speech.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Speaking or writing? (7)","q":"<p>Is this mainly a form of speaking or of writing?</p><p><b>A press conference after a storm</b></p>","options":["Writing","Speaking"],"correct":1,"why":"A press conference is spoken and interactive.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Speaking or writing? (8)","q":"<p>Is this mainly a form of speaking or of writing?</p><p><b>A text message to a classmate</b></p>","options":["Writing","Speaking"],"correct":0,"why":"A text message is written, even if informal.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Match the transition to its job","q":"<p>Match each transitional word or phrase to the job it does.</p>","left":["Consequently","Nevertheless","Moreover","Likewise","Admittedly","Provided that"],"right":["Condition","Comparison","Result","Concession","Contrast","Addition"],"correct":[2,4,5,1,3,0]},{"t":"ma","title":"Fix the minutes","q":"<p>Here is an extract from the draft minutes of the Sea-Almond Secondary School Student Council meeting.</p><p>Select a line that breaks the conventions of minutes. Two lines do; the feedback explains both.</p>","options":["Minutes of the Student Council meeting held on Tuesday 14 October, at 3:00 p.m., in Room 12.","Present: J. Arrindell (Chair), K. Lake (Secretary), and six form representatives. Apologies: T. Brooks.","Item 2: The Treasurer reported that the bake sale had raised enough to buy two new benches.","Item 3: Shanice says, \"Girl, the canteen line is ridiculous, we need a second window!\"","It was moved by M. Peters and seconded by A. Gumbs that a letter be sent to the principal. Motion carried.","I personally think the principal will ignore it, as usual.","Action: K. Lake to draft the letter by Friday.","There being no other business, the meeting was adjourned at 4:05 p.m."],"correct":[3,5],"why":"Well done. Line 4 uses direct speech, slang and the present tense; it should read: \"S. Richardson raised concerns about long canteen lines and proposed a second serving window.\" Line 6 inserts the secretary's personal opinion, which has no place in an objective record."},{"t":"mc","title":"Which meeting document?","q":"<p>The secretary of the Carrot Bay Residents' Association needs a document, sent to members a week ahead, that lists in order: apologies, confirmation of last meeting's minutes, matters arising, a report on street lighting, and any other business. Which document is this?</p>","options":["A press release","The minutes","A memo","An agenda"],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["A press release informs the media and public; it is not a list of items for members to discuss.","Minutes are the record of what was discussed and decided, written after the meeting.","A memo is an internal message within an organisation; it does not set out the order of business for a meeting.","Correct. An agenda lists, in order, the items to be discussed at a forthcoming meeting."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Module 3 practice: speaking and writing (1)","q":"<p>Paper 1-style questions from your class notes. Read every option before you choose.</p><p>Two means of WRITING that could capture students' interest in a campaign about vaping are...</p>","options":["a post and drama","pamphlets and a poetry recital","pamphlets and posters","a speech and drama"],"correct":2,"why":"Drama, a poetry recital and a speech are spoken or performed, not written. Pamphlets and posters are both written forms.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Module 3 practice: speaking and writing (2)","q":"<p>A speaker's teenage audience becomes disengaged. Which of the following is NOT a feature of the speaker's voice that could explain this?</p>","options":["low pitch","dress","monotone","volume"],"correct":1,"why":"Pitch, monotone and volume are vocal features of delivery. Dress may affect how the audience receives the speaker, but it is not a feature of the voice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Module 3 practice: speaking and writing (3)","q":"<p>Which of the following is NOT essential for a good written report?</p>","options":["proper sentence structure","transitional words","proper paragraphing","bulleted points"],"correct":3,"why":"Bulleted points can be useful but are not a necessity. Paragraphing, sentence structure and transitions are essential.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Module 3 practice: speaking and writing (4)","q":"<p>A COVID-19 advertisement aimed at young people is MOST likely to be remembered because of its...</p>","options":["words used","subject matter","use of music","speaker's attire"],"correct":2,"why":"Music is the feature most likely to capture and hold young people's attention and stay in their memory.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Module 3 practice: speaking and writing (5)","q":"<p>In a \"Stay at Home\" advertisement, the words \"stay at home\" are repeated throughout as a punchline. The dominant language technique is...</p>","options":["rhetorical question","repetition","anecdote","metaphor"],"correct":1,"why":"The phrase is repeated again and again: repetition.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Module 3 practice: speaking and writing (6)","q":"<p>The Prime Minister addresses the nation on television. The context of communication is...</p>","options":["intercultural","public","intrapersonal","organisational"],"correct":1,"why":"A leader speaking to the whole nation is a public context.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Module 3 practice: speaking and writing (7)","q":"<p>Two pre-writing tasks for an essay on airborne diseases are...</p>","options":["brainstorming and reviewing","brainstorming and rewriting","brainstorming and editing","brainstorming and clustering"],"correct":3,"why":"Clustering is a pre-writing activity; editing, reviewing and rewriting belong to later stages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Module 3 practice: speaking and writing (8)","q":"<p>Which of the following is NOT a benefit of essay writing?</p>","options":["a permanent record","interaction with the recipient","dissemination of information","the chance to review information"],"correct":1,"why":"An essay is one-way communication: the reader cannot respond while reading.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Module 3 practice: speaking and writing (9)","q":"<p>Two audiovisual aids suitable for a nursery class of three- and four-year-olds are...</p>","options":["books and crayons","radio and television","television and computer","posters and charts"],"correct":2,"why":"Audiovisual means seen AND heard. Radio is audio only; posters, charts, books and crayons are visual only.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"The virtual planning meeting (step 1)","q":"<p>You are secretary of your school's Environment Club. Members are spread across the island during the holidays, and you must organise a planning meeting for a beach clean-up.</p><p>Twelve members need to discuss and agree on the clean-up plan this week, but they live in different districts. Which channel do you choose for the meeting?</p>","options":["A long voice note in the group chat explaining the plan.","A scheduled video conference, with the notice and agenda emailed three days ahead."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A voice note is one-way; members cannot discuss and agree together.","Good. A video call allows real-time discussion across distances, and the notice and agenda prepare members."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The virtual planning meeting (step 2)","q":"<p>During the call, one member keeps interrupting with his microphone on while music plays loudly in the background. What do you do as secretary, with the chair's agreement?</p>","options":["Post a message in capital letters telling him to SHUT UP.","Politely remind everyone to mute when not speaking and to use \"raise hand\" to take turns."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Writing in capitals reads as shouting and damages relationships.","Courteous and effective: you apply netiquette to regulate the meeting."]},{"t":"mc","title":"The virtual planning meeting (step 3)","q":"<p>After the meeting, a member suggests posting a screenshot of everyone's faces on the club's public page. What do you advise?</p>","options":["Ask for everyone's permission first, and post a group photo only if all agree.","Post it immediately; it's good publicity."],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["You protect members' privacy and digital footprints.","Posting people's images without consent ignores privacy and their digital footprint."]}]},{"id":"assess_1sh","title":"Module 3 · Unit 8 quiz: Letters, Proposals, Campaigns and the Module 3 Response","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Module 3, Unit 8: Letters, Proposals, Campaigns and the Module 3 Response. 10 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Build a campaign plan","q":"<p>The Five Cays Neighbourhood Watch in Providenciales wants residents to prepare hurricane kits before the season begins. Put the sections of their campaign plan in a logical order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Introduction: many households were caught unprepared last season; this campaign aims to persuade every household to assemble a hurricane kit by 1 June.","Audience profile: families and elderly residents of Five Cays, mixed educational backgrounds; English, Spanish and Haitian Creole speakers.","Message and appeals: \"A kit today keeps your family safe tomorrow\" (pathos); a checklist costing less than one week's groceries (logos); launch in May, before the season (kairos).","Media and channels: trilingual flyers at churches and shops, a community radio interview, a WhatsApp checklist, and a kit-packing demonstration at the community centre.","Barriers and repairs: language (translate materials); cost (show low-cost items, partner with a supermarket); older residents offline (door-to-door volunteers).","Conclusion: summary of benefits and a request to the community council for approval and a small budget."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"match","title":"Letter parts and their jobs","q":"<p>Match each part of a formal letter to the example that fits it.</p>","left":["Salutation (unknown name)","Complimentary close (after a named person)","Subject line","Opening paragraph","Closing paragraph","Sign-off details"],"right":["Yours sincerely,","I would be grateful for the opportunity to discuss this proposal with you at your convenience.","Tamara Lake, President, Student Council","Dear Sir/Madam,","I am writing on behalf of the Student Council to request...","Re: Request for a pedestrian crossing outside our school"],"correct":[3,0,5,4,1,2]},{"t":"mc","title":"Spot the letter error","q":"<blockquote><p>Dear Sir/Madam,<br>Re: Littering at the Hastings boardwalk<br>I'm writing as secretary of the Clean Coast Youth Group cause the boardwalk is real nasty after weekends. We'd like you to put more bins.<br>Yours sincerely,<br>Jalen</p></blockquote><p>Which revision would MOST improve this letter's register and conventions?</p>","options":["Remove the subject line because it is unnecessary.","Add more detail about how dirty the beach is, using stronger words.","Remove contractions and slang, use formal Standard English, change the close to \"Yours faithfully\", and add a full name and role.","Change \"Dear Sir/Madam\" to \"Hi there\" to sound friendlier."],"correct":2,"why":"","fb":["The subject line is helpful: it tells the busy reader the topic at once. Removing it would weaken the letter.","More vivid detail might add pathos, but the main problems are informal register (contractions, \"cause\", \"real nasty\") and the wrong complimentary close. Stronger words will not fix those.","Correct. \"I'm\", \"We'd\", \"cause\" and \"real nasty\" are too informal; \"Dear Sir/Madam\" must be matched with \"Yours faithfully\"; and an official letter needs a full name and role, such as \"Jalen Brooks, Secretary, Clean Coast Youth Group\".","That moves the letter further from a formal register. Officials expect a formal salutation."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the question carefully (1)","q":"<p>Examiners often change one or two words. For each task, decide what the answer must contain.</p><p>\"Explain TWO message points related to logos OR pathos appeals.\" What must you give?</p>","options":["One logos point and one ethos point","Two message points, each explained in terms of logos or pathos","A list of all four appeals"],"correct":1,"why":"Exactly two message points, each linked to logos or pathos and explained. Ethos and kairos are not asked for here.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the question carefully (2)","q":"<p>\"Justify ONE language register and ONE dialectal variety.\" Which pair answers this?</p>","options":["Formal register; PowerPoint","Pathos; Standard English","Formal register; Standard English"],"correct":2,"why":"A register (formal, consultative, casual) and a dialectal variety (Standard English, a Creole/vernacular). PowerPoint is a technology; pathos is an appeal.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the question carefully (3)","q":"<p>\"Justify ONE digital media technology.\" Which answer fits best?</p>","options":["A Canva slideshow on a smart TV, because it is attractive and easy to update","A transistor radio","\"Technology\" in general"],"correct":0,"why":"Name one specific digital tool and justify it. A transistor radio is not digital, and \"technology\" is too vague.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Read the question carefully (4)","q":"<p>Your answer is 380 words. What should you do?</p>","options":["Leave it; more words earn more marks","Cut it to 300 or fewer by removing repetition and padding","Remove the conclusion only"],"correct":1,"why":"Module 3 responses are capped at 300 words. Cut repetition and padding, but keep a short conclusion.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Planning the road safety campaign (step 1)","q":"<p>You chair the Coralstone College Road Safety Committee. You have been asked to plan a campaign to reduce speeding among young drivers in Grenada. Make the planning decisions.</p><p>Your committee is eager to start making posters immediately. What do you do first?</p>","options":["Design a poster with lots of statistics and start printing.","Profile the audience: drivers aged 17 to 25, their schooling, where they drive, and which media they use."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Jumping to the medium before knowing the audience is the most common planning mistake.","Exactly. The audience profile drives every later choice: message, appeals, medium and channel."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Planning the road safety campaign (step 2)","q":"<p>Your profile shows most young drivers spend hours on Instagram and TikTok, listen to the radio in the car, and see themselves as skilled drivers. Which message and appeals will you use?</p>","options":["\"Arrive alive: the road is not a racetrack.\" A short real story from a young crash survivor (pathos), with the simple fact that higher speed means longer stopping distance (logos), launched before Carnival weekend (kairos).","\"Speeding is illegal and you will be punished.\""],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Strong. A complete message, deliberate appeals and smart timing.","A threat with no reasoning or connection may trigger a psychological barrier: young drivers who see themselves as skilled tend to dismiss lectures."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Planning the road safety campaign (step 3)","q":"<p>Which media and channels, and which barrier plan?</p>","options":["A press release to the newspaper only.","Short videos on Instagram and TikTok, radio spots during evening drive time, and talks in fifth-form classes; repair barriers by using the young people's own language style (within good taste), adding captions for viewers who watch without sound, and posting in English and Spanish."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["A single traditional channel that most of this audience rarely reads will not reach them.","Excellent: media that match the profile, several channels, and a concrete repair for each likely barrier."]}]}]},{"pkg":"Topic Quizzes · Paper 3","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_1u2","title":"Paper 3 · Unit 1 quiz: Inside Paper 032","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Paper 3, Unit 1: Inside Paper 032. 13 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"match","title":"Match the year to its topic and audience","q":"<p>Match each exam year to its topic and intended audience.</p>","left":["2025","2026","2027","2029","2030","2032"],"right":["Climate change, for teenagers","Natural disasters, for homeowners","Non-communicable diseases in children, for parents","Mental health challenges, for community groups","Cyberbullying, for parents","Autism, for teachers"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5]},{"t":"mc","title":"Allowed in the exam room? (1)","q":"<p>Shania is packing for Paper 032 (2026 topic: natural disasters). Sort each item.</p><p>Items in Shania's bag</p><p><b>One double-sided A4 sheet with 9 points on the exam topic</b></p>","options":["Allowed","Not allowed"],"correct":0,"why":"One card, correct size, under 10 points, related to the topic. That is exactly what is permitted.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Allowed in the exam room? (2)","q":"<p>Items in Shania's bag</p><p><b>A second notes sheet \"just in case\"</b></p>","options":["Allowed","Not allowed"],"correct":1,"why":"Only a single notes sheet is allowed, even if she never looks at it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Allowed in the exam room? (3)","q":"<p>Items in Shania's bag</p><p><b>A notes sheet with 15 points in tiny writing</b></p>","options":["Not allowed","Allowed"],"correct":0,"why":"No more than 10 points. Fifteen breaks the rule, and tiny writing is useless under pressure anyway.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Allowed in the exam room? (4)","q":"<p>Items in Shania's bag</p><p><b>A printed government hurricane guide</b></p>","options":["Allowed","Not allowed"],"correct":1,"why":"Nothing else is permitted. The key facts from the guide belong on the card as short points.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Allowed in the exam room? (5)","q":"<p>Items in Shania's bag</p><p><b>A notes sheet with 10 short points on natural disasters</b></p>","options":["Not allowed","Allowed"],"correct":1,"why":"Ten points is the maximum, and it is allowed if the points relate to her topic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Allowed in the exam room? (6)","q":"<p>Items in Shania's bag</p><p><b>Her phone, switched off, \"for the figures\"</b></p>","options":["Not allowed","Allowed"],"correct":0,"why":"No phones. If a figure matters, it goes on the card.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which Module is it testing?","q":"<blockquote><p>A 2026-style stimulus: a newspaper PSA from a Disaster Management Office telling homeowners to register for a free roof inspection before hurricane season. One question reads: <i>\"Explain TWO reasons why the public service announcement uses the dialectal variety and the language register which it does.\"</i></p></blockquote><p>Which Module and question does this belong to?</p>","options":["Module 2, Question 2","It is not linked to a Module","Section B, Question 3","Module 1, Question 1"],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Correct. Dialectal variety (Standard English or Creole) and register (formal or informal) are Module 2 language and community skills, tested in Question 2(a).","All three questions cover the three Modules. \"Dialectal variety\" is a clear Module 2 term.","Section B is where you write your own piece and justification. This is a short-answer Section A question about the stimulus.","Not quite. Question 1 (Module 1) is about receiving information, media and sources. Dialectal variety and register are language ideas."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paper 032 fact check (1)","q":"<p>Quick-fire questions on the paper's format.</p><p>How long is Paper 032 according to the regulations?</p>","options":["3 hours","1 hour 30 minutes","2 hours 15 minutes"],"correct":2,"why":"The regulations give 2 hours 15 minutes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paper 032 fact check (2)","q":"<p>How much does it contribute to the final assessment?</p>","options":["40 per cent","20 per cent","10 per cent"],"correct":1,"why":"60 marks, contributing 20 per cent.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paper 032 fact check (3)","q":"<p>How many marks is Section B worth?</p>","options":["25","20","40"],"correct":2,"why":"Section B: piece (25) + justification (15) = 40.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paper 032 fact check (4)","q":"<p>What length is the Section B piece in the specimen?</p>","options":["400–500 words","200–250 words","100–150 words"],"correct":1,"why":"Between 200 and 250 words.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Paper 032 fact check (5)","q":"<p>The intended audience for 2027 is...</p>","options":["Teenagers","Parents","Homeowners"],"correct":1,"why":"2027: non-communicable diseases in children, for parents.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_1v0","title":"Paper 3 · Unit 2 quiz: Section A, Question 1: Receiving Information, Media and Sources","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Paper 3, Unit 2: Section A, Question 1: Receiving Information, Media and Sources. 9 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Which answer earns 2 marks?","q":"<blockquote><p>2027-style stimulus: A Ministry of Health PSA in a weekend newspaper tells parents that free blood-sugar screening for children aged 8–12 is available, but appointments must be booked through a new online portal.</p></blockquote><p>Which response would earn the FULL 2 marks for ONE challenge parents in a low-income district are likely to face in receiving this information?</p>","options":["\"Parents are busy.\"","\"Internet.\"","\"The newspaper is a good way to communicate with parents.\"","\"Some parents may not buy the weekend newspaper because of its cost, so they would never see the notice about free screening for their children.\""],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["This hints at a challenge but does not explain how being busy stops them receiving the information. Add the link: \"...so many do not have time to read the paper on a working weekend.\"","This names a challenge area but does not explain it. At best it contributes to the 1 mark given for identifying two challenges without explanation.","This is not a challenge at all; it praises the medium. Read the command word: you must explain a difficulty in receiving the information.","Full marks. The challenge (cost), the reason and the effect on this specific group are all there: the information never reaches them."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it fit the question? (1)","q":"<p>The question asks: <i>\"Justify TWO appropriate <b>traditional</b> media, <b>other than the printed medium</b>, for informing elderly homeowners in a hillside village about free roof inspections.\"</i> Sort each medium.</p><p>Media options</p><p><b>Radio</b></p>","options":["Acceptable answer","Breaks the question's limits"],"correct":0,"why":"Traditional, not print, and very well suited to older listeners across the island.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it fit the question? (2)","q":"<p>Media options</p><p><b>A mobile loudspeaker van driving through the village</b></p>","options":["Acceptable answer","Breaks the question's limits"],"correct":0,"why":"Traditional, oral, and reaches people where they live.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it fit the question? (3)","q":"<p>Media options</p><p><b>Word of mouth through church and village leaders</b></p>","options":["Acceptable answer","Breaks the question's limits"],"correct":0,"why":"Traditional and suited to people who prefer orature, though best backed up by another medium.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it fit the question? (4)","q":"<p>Media options</p><p><b>A newspaper advertisement</b></p>","options":["Breaks the question's limits","Acceptable answer"],"correct":0,"why":"The question says other than the printed medium. A newspaper is print.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it fit the question? (5)","q":"<p>Media options</p><p><b>An Instagram story</b></p>","options":["Acceptable answer","Breaks the question's limits"],"correct":1,"why":"Social media is digital, not traditional, and many elderly homeowners may not use it.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it fit the question? (6)","q":"<p>Media options</p><p><b>A flyer in the post office</b></p>","options":["Breaks the question's limits","Acceptable answer"],"correct":0,"why":"A flyer is printed, so it is excluded.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it fit the question? (7)","q":"<p>Media options</p><p><b>Television</b></p>","options":["Acceptable answer","Breaks the question's limits"],"correct":0,"why":"Traditional, not print, with visual and aural appeal for demonstrating repairs.","fb":[]},{"t":"ma","title":"Spot the full-mark answers","q":"<p>Six students answered Q1 on a 2026 stimulus (roof inspections, elderly homeowners in a hillside village). Tap the <b>THREE</b> answers that are clearly explained and would earn full marks for their part.</p><p>Select the three fully explained answers.</p>","options":["(c) \"Homeowners could ask the district building inspector, who is officially trained and knows the current inspection programme, so the information is reliable.\"","(a) \"Literacy.\"","(a) \"Some elderly homeowners cannot read well, so a printed notice in the newspaper would not reach them at all.\"","(b) \"A mobile loudspeaker van is appropriate because it brings the message to people's doorsteps in the village and suits older residents who prefer spoken information.\"","(c) \"The internet.\"","(b) \"Radio and Facebook.\""],"correct":[0, 2, 3],"why":"Exactly. Each full-mark answer names the point AND explains it in relation to the target group. That is the difference between 1 mark and 2."}]},{"id":"assess_1w6","title":"Paper 3 · Unit 3 quiz: Section A, Question 2: Variety, Register, Message Points and Slogans","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Paper 3, Unit 3: Section A, Question 2: Variety, Register, Message Points and Slogans. 7 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Which answer earns 4 marks?","q":"<blockquote><p>2027-style stimulus: a Ministry of Health PSA reads, \"Parents and guardians are advised that free blood-glucose screening for children aged 8–12 will be available at all community clinics from 2 March. Appointments must be scheduled in advance.\"</p></blockquote><p>Which answer to Q2(a) would earn the FULL 4 marks?</p>","options":["\"It uses Creole so that parents feel at home.\"","\"The language is good and easy to understand.\"","\"It uses Standard English and a formal register.\"","\"It uses Standard English because that is the official variety used by government, giving the notice authority. It uses a formal register because a ministry is addressing parents about a serious health matter, and a casual tone would make it seem less important.\""],"correct":3,"why":"","fb":["This misidentifies the variety. \"Parents and guardians are advised\" is Standard English, the acrolect, not Creole.","Too vague. It names neither the dialectal variety nor the register, so it cannot earn the identification marks.","Both are correctly identified, but there is no explanation, so this earns only 1 mark.","Full marks. The variety and the register are each identified and each given a clear, relevant reason."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Variety and register check (1)","q":"<p>For each 2026 message aimed at homeowners, choose the BEST description.</p><p>\"Homeowners are reminded that hurricane shutters must be installed before an official warning is issued.\"</p>","options":["Creole, informal","Standard English, intimate","Standard English, formal"],"correct":2,"why":"Passive voice, official vocabulary and no contractions: Standard English in a formal register.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Variety and register check (2)","q":"<p>\"Ay, neighbour! Yuh roof tie down good? Storm season reach!\"</p>","options":["Creole, informal","Standard English, frozen","Standard English, formal"],"correct":0,"why":"\"Yuh\", \"tie down good\" and \"reach\" are Creole features, and the greeting is casual.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Variety and register check (3)","q":"<p>\"Hey homeowners! Got your hurricane kit ready? Here are five quick checks.\"</p>","options":["Creole, informal","Standard English, informal","Creole, formal"],"correct":1,"why":"The grammar is Standard English, but the tone is chatty and friendly: informal register.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Variety and register check (4)","q":"<p>Why would a ministry use the FIRST message's style?</p>","options":["Creole is not allowed on paper","It wants to sound like a friend","It is official communication on a serious matter"],"correct":2,"why":"Government communication is official and serious, so it uses Standard English in a formal register.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Name the slogan's technique","q":"<p>Match each 2026 or 2027 slogan to the literary technique it uses.</p>","left":["Allusion","Rhetorical question","Rhyme","Alliteration","Pun"],"right":["\"A strap in time saves the roof.\"","\"You buy a lock for your door; why not straps for your roof?\"","\"Raise children, not their blood sugar.\"","\"Pour the water, save your daughter.\"","\"Shutters, straps and safety start today.\""],"correct":[0,1,3,4,2]},{"t":"match","title":"Build a full-mark slogan answer","q":"<p>Put the parts of Kemar's Q2(c) answer (2027 topic, parents) in the most effective order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Slogan: \"Don't wait, hydrate.\"","Technique: rhyme (\"wait\" / \"hydrate\").","How it works: the rhyme makes the message short and memorable, so parents recall it when packing lunch boxes.","Link to the issue: it urges parents to act now by replacing sweet drinks with water, reducing their children's risk of NCDs."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4"],"correct":[0,1,2,3]}]},{"id":"assess_1x3","title":"Paper 3 · Unit 4 quiz: Section B: The Reflective or Expository Piece","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Paper 3, Unit 4: Section B: The Reflective or Expository Piece. 10 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Reflective or expository? (1)","q":"<p>Sort each opening line (2026 or 2027 topic) by form.</p><p>Opening lines</p><p><b>\"I still remember the silence after the wind stopped, and the morning light pouring through the hole where our kitchen roof had been.\"</b></p>","options":["Expository","Reflective"],"correct":1,"why":"First person, memory and feeling: reflective.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflective or expository? (2)","q":"<p>Opening lines</p><p><b>\"Every homeowner in Jamaica can take five simple, low-cost steps to protect their property before hurricane season.\"</b></p>","options":["Reflective","Expository"],"correct":1,"why":"An informing thesis aimed at readers: expository.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflective or expository? (3)","q":"<p>Opening lines</p><p><b>\"Non-communicable diseases in children are rising across the Caribbean, but parents hold the most powerful tools to reverse the trend.\"</b></p>","options":["Reflective","Expository"],"correct":1,"why":"States a main point and promises explanation: expository essay.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflective or expository? (4)","q":"<p>Opening lines</p><p><b>\"The day the nurse said 'pre-diabetic', I looked at the juice box in my son's hand and felt my stomach drop.\"</b></p>","options":["Reflective","Expository"],"correct":0,"why":"A personal moment and a feeling: reflective.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflective or expository? (5)","q":"<p>Opening lines</p><p><b>\"Firstly, check that your roof is fastened to the walls with hurricane straps.\"</b></p>","options":["Expository","Reflective"],"correct":0,"why":"Ordered advice with a transition: expository.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Reflective or expository? (6)","q":"<p>Opening lines</p><p><b>\"Looking back, I realise our cistern saved us that September.\"</b></p>","options":["Reflective","Expository"],"correct":0,"why":"\"Looking back, I realise\" is classic reflective language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Planning for homeowners (step 1)","q":"<p>It is a 2026 practice paper. The topic is natural disasters; the audience is homeowners. Make the planning decisions.</p><p>You must write a reflective OR expository piece of 200–250 words. Which plan fits?</p>","options":["A rap for teenagers about earthquakes.","A newspaper feature advising homeowners how to prepare their homes before hurricane season."],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Wrong audience and the wrong form: the question wants reflective or expository writing for homeowners.","A strong expository choice: it suits the topic and speaks directly to homeowners."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Planning for homeowners (step 2)","q":"<p>Good. What is your purpose statement?</p>","options":["\"To persuade homeowners in Barbados to secure their roofs and windows before June.\"","\"To talk about hurricanes.\""],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Precise verb, specific audience, clear action.","That is a topic, not a purpose."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Planning for homeowners (step 3)","q":"<p>You have 230 words planned. Which appeal will you build in deliberately?</p>","options":["No appeal; just list facts from the stimulus.","Kairos and logos: \"With the season six weeks away, a set of straps costs far less than a new roof.\""],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["Copying the stimulus shows little creativity, and the piece will not engage its audience.","Timely and logical, perfectly suited to practical homeowners."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Which opening engages the audience?","q":"<p>For a 2027 expository piece aimed at parents, which opening BEST shows creativity and audience engagement?</p>","options":["\"Tomorrow morning, when you pack your child's lunch, you will make a health decision that could last a lifetime.\"","\"In this essay I will write about NCDs in children because it is the topic.\"","\"Non-communicable diseases are diseases that are not communicable.\"","\"Children de drink too much sweet drink an' dem parents ent care.\""],"correct":0,"why":"","fb":["Excellent. It speaks directly to parents (\"you\"), uses a familiar daily moment and creates urgency (kairos). It engages before it informs.","Announcing the topic like this sounds mechanical. A thesis should state your main point, not just your subject.","This is circular and flat. It defines the term with itself and gives parents no reason to keep reading.","Accidental Creole in an expository piece for parents costs expression marks, and blaming the audience will lose them."]}]},{"id":"assess_1yy","title":"Paper 3 · Unit 5 quiz: Section B: The Justification, the Notes Sheet and Exam Strategy","desc":"<p>Topic quiz for Paper 3, Unit 5: Section B: The Justification, the Notes Sheet and Exam Strategy. 15 items.</p>","minutes":0,"items":[{"t":"ma","title":"Find the context sentence","q":"<p>Read Kimani's 2027 justification. Tap the sentence that names the <b>context of communication</b>.</p><p>Select the context sentence.</p>","options":["The first-person voice and the image of the juice box create pathos so that parents see themselves in the story.","The purpose of this reflective piece is to encourage parents to replace sweet drinks with water by sharing one mother's experience.","It would be published in the parents' newsletter sent home in book bags during Health Week.","The intended audience is parents of primary school children in Barbados, many of them busy and without medical training."],"correct":[2],"why":"Correct. A newsletter sent home during Health Week is where and how the piece reaches its audience: the context of communication."},{"t":"mc","title":"Which justification reaches the top band?","q":"<p>For a 2026 reflective journal entry by a homeowner, which justification is strongest?</p>","options":["\"I wrote a journal because journals are personal. It is about hurricanes and it is for people.\"","\"The purpose of this reflective entry is to persuade fellow homeowners to prepare early by sharing the cost of my own delay. The intended audience is homeowners in Barbados who, like me, tend to wait for an official warning. The context of communication is a community blog run by a district residents' association, read in the weeks before hurricane season, so I used a personal first-person voice and pathos to make the lesson feel real.\"","\"My journal entry has imagery, a turning point and a conclusion, and it is well organised and interesting.\"","\"The purpose is to inform. The audience is homeowners. The context is a blog.\""],"correct":1,"why":"","fb":["No purpose verb, a vague audience (\"people\") and content (\"about hurricanes\") instead of context. This would sit in a low band.","Top band. Precise purpose, a specific audience with a relevant detail, a real-world context, and a clear explanation of how the voice and appeal suit that audience. About 80 words.","This describes features of the piece, which can be one supporting sentence, but it never addresses purpose, audience or context.","All three labels appear, but nothing is explained and it is far below 50 words. It names without connecting, so it cannot reach the top band."]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it earn a place on the sheet? (1)","q":"<p>Jaheim is building his 2027 notes sheet (NCDs in children; parents). Sort each possible point.</p><p>Possible notes sheet points</p><p><b>NCD = not contagious; e.g. type 2 diabetes, hypertension</b></p>","options":["Cut","Keep"],"correct":1,"why":"A key definition he can use in any form.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it earn a place on the sheet? (2)","q":"<p>Possible notes sheet points</p><p><b>Causes: daily sweet drinks; screens over outdoor play</b></p>","options":["Cut","Keep"],"correct":1,"why":"Short, relevant and easy to expand.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it earn a place on the sheet? (3)","q":"<p>Possible notes sheet points</p><p><b>Source: regional health agency report on school children's diets (recent)</b></p>","options":["Keep","Cut"],"correct":0,"why":"A credible, current source he can cite for ethos and logos.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it earn a place on the sheet? (4)","q":"<p>Possible notes sheet points</p><p><b>Adult lung cancer statistics from North America</b></p>","options":["Cut","Keep"],"correct":0,"why":"Wrong age group, wrong focus, wrong region.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it earn a place on the sheet? (5)","q":"<p>Possible notes sheet points</p><p><b>A copied paragraph from a website</b></p>","options":["Cut","Keep"],"correct":0,"why":"Paragraphs waste space and tempt copying. Turn it into a short keyword point.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it earn a place on the sheet? (6)","q":"<p>Possible notes sheet points</p><p><b>Hook: \"Small swaps today protect your child tomorrow\"</b></p>","options":["Cut","Keep"],"correct":1,"why":"A ready-made message line for parents.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Does it earn a place on the sheet? (7)","q":"<p>Possible notes sheet points</p><p><b>\"Health is important for everyone.\"</b></p>","options":["Cut","Keep"],"correct":0,"why":"Too vague. It carries no information you could not invent on the day.","fb":[]},{"t":"match","title":"Your 2 hours 15 minutes","q":"<p>Put Shanice's exam-day routine in the most effective order.</p><p>Match each step to its correct position in the sequence.</p>","left":["Read the central stimulus twice and read every question carefully, underlining command words.","Identify the marks for each part and plan time in proportion (Section A = 20 marks, Section B = 40).","Annotate the stimulus: target group, variety and register, key message points.","Answer Section A, Questions 1 and 2: explain, do not just identify.","Plan Section B with the planning chain, then draft the justification.","Write the 200–250 word piece, using notes sheet points as anchors.","Self-check: all parts answered, word limits kept, justification complete, Standard English proofread."],"right":["Step 1","Step 2","Step 3","Step 4","Step 5","Step 6","Step 7"],"correct":[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]},{"t":"mc","title":"Common mistakes clinic (1)","q":"<p>Each student made a common Paper 032 mistake. Choose the best fix.</p><p>Andre's Q1(a): \"Literacy and cost.\"</p>","options":["Add a third challenge","Write it in capitals","Explain each challenge and its effect on the target group"],"correct":2,"why":"Identifying only earns 1 mark in total; explaining each earns 2.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Common mistakes clinic (2)","q":"<p>Bianca's Q1(b) named the newspaper and Instagram.</p>","options":["Explain the newspaper more","Add Facebook too","Choose traditional media other than print, such as radio or a loudspeaker van"],"correct":2,"why":"The question excluded print and asked for traditional media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Common mistakes clinic (3)","q":"<p>Carlos wrote a 340-word Section B piece.</p>","options":["Remove the conclusion only","Cut to 200–250 words","Keep it; more is better"],"correct":1,"why":"Stay within 200–250 words; staying within the limit is part of the skill.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Common mistakes clinic (4)","q":"<p>Dana's justification: \"The audience is everyone.\"</p>","options":["Write \"the whole world\"","Leave the audience out","Name a specific group, e.g. \"homeowners over sixty in hillside districts\""],"correct":2,"why":"\"Everyone\" is not an audience. Be specific.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Common mistakes clinic (5)","q":"<p>Errol's slogan had no explanation.</p>","options":["Make the slogan longer","Add a picture","Name the technique and explain how it works for the issue"],"correct":2,"why":"The explanation of the technique is worth half the slogan marks.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 01 Practice Papers","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_258","title":"Paper 01 · Practice Paper A","desc":"<p><b>Paper 01 · Practice Paper A</b> · 1 hour 30 minutes</p>\n<ol>\n<li>This test consists of <b>45 items</b>: 15 on Module 1, 15 on Module 2 and 15 on Module 3. Answer <b>ALL</b> items.</li>\n<li><b>Items 1–7</b> are based on a listening extract. In the examination, the extract is NOT printed; it is read to you <b>twice</b> after you have had five minutes to read the items. For practice, ask a partner or your teacher to read it aloud before you look at the printed text.</li>\n<li>The remaining items are based on scenarios, extracts or dialogues, or stand alone. Answer each item based on the information given.</li>\n<li>Each item has four suggested answers lettered (A), (B), (C), (D). Read each item and decide which choice is <b>best</b>.</li>\n<li>If you cannot answer an item, go on to the next one and return to it later.</li>\n</ol>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1 (Module 1)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Items 1–7. In the examination this monologue is read aloud twice. Listen carefully before answering.</i></p>\n<blockquote><b>Salt Pond Mornings</b><br>\nEvery morning, before the sun open he eye, we was already by the pond.<br>\nMammy with the basket, me with the rake.<br>\nSalt shining white like somebody spread sugar over the whole of Sandy Ground.<br>\nFirst we rake, then we pile, then we carry.<br>\nBack bending, hand burning, but nobody complaining.<br>\nNow look.<br>\nTruck and car and dust where the salt used to sleep.<br>\nChildren passing and don't even turn they head.<br>\nWho going tell them?<br>\nWho going remember Mammy hand?<br>\nI tell them. I tell them every Sunday.<br>\nThe pond don't forget. Is we who forget.</blockquote>\n<p style=\"font-size:.85em\">Original monologue written for this practice paper.</p></blockquote><p>The MAIN purpose of the monologue is to</p>","options":["describe the traffic problems in present-day Sandy Ground","persuade the government to reopen the salt pond","lament the loss of a community tradition and its memory","instruct children in how salt is raked and stored"],"correct":2,"why":"The persona contrasts the busy salt-raking mornings of the past with the present, where \"Truck and car and dust\" have replaced the salt and children \"don't even turn they head\". The closing line \"Is we who forget\" shows the speaker mourning a lost tradition, not instructing, campaigning or reporting on traffic.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following devices are evident in the line \"Salt shining white like somebody spread sugar over the whole of Sandy Ground\"?</p>","options":["Simile and visual imagery","Onomatopoeia and simile","Metaphor and hyperbole","Personification and metaphor"],"correct":0,"why":"The comparison uses \"like\", so it is a simile, and the picture of white, glittering salt spread over a town appeals to the sense of sight (visual imagery). There is no direct comparison without \"like\" (metaphor) and no sound-word (onomatopoeia).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The line \"First we rake, then we pile, then we carry\" is organised MAINLY by</p>","options":["sequential (process) order","comparison and contrast","classification","cause and effect"],"correct":0,"why":"The signal words \"First\" and \"then ... then\" set out the steps of the work in the order they happened. This is sequential or process order. Nothing is being compared, classified or explained as a cause.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The lines \"The pond don't forget. Is we who forget.\" suggest that</p>","options":["the persona has a poor memory because of old age","the place keeps its history even though the community has neglected it","the children refuse to visit the pond on Sundays","the salt pond is now polluted and cannot be used"],"correct":1,"why":"The pond is personified as something that \"remembers\", and this is set against the people who have forgotten. The persona is blaming the community, not the place, for the loss of heritage.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following BEST describes the tone of the monologue?</p>","options":["Nostalgic and regretful","Joyful and celebratory","Angry and vengeful","Indifferent and detached"],"correct":0,"why":"Fond memories of \"Mammy with the basket\" (nostalgia) are followed by sadness at what has been lost and forgotten (regret). The persona is concerned, not angry, celebrating or unconcerned.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The rhetorical questions \"Who going tell them? Who going remember Mammy hand?\" are used MAINLY to</p>","options":["identify the children who pass the pond","ask the audience to volunteer as tour guides","blame Mammy for not teaching the children","express concern that the heritage will not be passed on"],"correct":3,"why":"The questions expect no answer; they voice the persona's worry that no one will carry the story of the salt pond and the people who worked it to the next generation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following practices would BEST help a listener to identify the main idea of the monologue while it is being read?</p>","options":["Noting repeated ideas, key words and contrasts","Focusing on the reader's accent and speed","Writing down every word the reader says","Deciding the meaning after the first two lines"],"correct":0,"why":"Active listening involves noting key words, repetition and shifts (for example \"Now look\" marks the move from past to present). Trying to write every word overloads the listener, focusing on accent is a distraction, and judging too early is a listening barrier.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8 (Module 1)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following scenario carefully and then answer Items 8–11.</i></p>\n<p>Fishers along the Tobago coast report that large mats of sargassum seaweed are reducing their catches. A group of sixth-form students from Sandbox Tree College plans to investigate how sargassum has affected the income of fishers in Charlotteville and Speyside over the last three years.</p></blockquote><p>Which of the following is a PRIMARY source from which the students could gather information?</p>","options":["Interviews with fishers who work in Charlotteville","A website summarising global seaweed research","A textbook on Caribbean marine plants","A news article on sargassum on another island"],"correct":0,"why":"A primary source gives first-hand information from people directly involved. The fishers experienced the drop in catches themselves. The textbook, news article and website report or interpret other people's findings, so they are secondary sources.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The students want to collect information about income changes from as MANY fishers as possible in a short time. Which instrument would be MOST suitable?</p>","options":["Focus groups","Observation schedules","Document analysis","Questionnaires"],"correct":3,"why":"Questionnaires are easy to administer to a large number of respondents quickly and produce data that can be compared. Focus groups involve few people, observation cannot show income, and document analysis depends on records that may not exist for individual fishers.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The students' findings would be LEAST valid if they</p>","options":["asked fishers mainly about their opinions on tourism","used the same set of questions with every fisher","included fishers from both Charlotteville and Speyside","compared present catches with catches three years ago"],"correct":0,"why":"Validity means the research measures what it set out to measure. Questions about tourism do not address the effect of sargassum on fishers' income, so the data would not answer the research question. The other options support valid findings.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following would be the MOST reliable SECONDARY source of data on fishers' catches?</p>","options":["Records kept by the government fisheries department","A tourist's travel blog","A fisher's post on social media","A radio call-in programme"],"correct":0,"why":"Official records are collected systematically over time by an accountable body, so they are consistent and trustworthy. A single social media post, a tourist blog or callers' opinions are anecdotal and may be biased or inaccurate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12 (Module 1)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following extract carefully and then answer Items 12–15.</i></p>\n<blockquote>For years, we have treated the bus as the last choice of the traveller who has no other choice. Yet every morning on our main roads, private cars crawl bumper to bumper, most carrying only one person, while schoolchildren wait under the sun at stops with no shelter. If Grenada invested in a scheduled, reliable public bus service, traffic would ease, fuel costs would fall and workers would reach their jobs on time. Other small islands have shown that a well-run timetable can move thousands of people daily. The question is not whether we can afford such a system. The question is whether we can afford to keep going without one.</blockquote>\n<p style=\"font-size:.85em\"><b>SOURCE:</b> Adapted from Dana Richardson, \"Waiting for the Bus\", <i>The Harbour Voice</i>, 2025. Dana Richardson is a transport planner who has advised several island governments in the Eastern Caribbean on public transport.</p></blockquote><p>Which of the following BEST states the main point of the extract?</p>","options":["Grenada should invest in a reliable public bus service to improve daily life.","Private cars are the main cause of air pollution on the island.","Other small islands have better transport systems than Grenada.","Schoolchildren on the island need bus shelters at every stop."],"correct":0,"why":"The whole extract builds towards the claim that a scheduled, reliable bus service would ease traffic, lower costs and help workers. The bus shelters and other islands are supporting details, and pollution is not mentioned.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The writer's MAIN purpose in the extract is to</p>","options":["persuade readers that investment in public buses is necessary","entertain readers with a story about a bus ride","describe the condition of roads in St. George's","inform readers about the history of public transport"],"correct":0,"why":"The writer presents a problem, proposes a solution and ends with a challenge to the reader (\"can we afford to keep going without one\"). These are features of persuasive writing, not narrative, historical or purely descriptive writing.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The final two sentences of the extract are effective MAINLY because they use</p>","options":["euphemism to soften criticism of the government","onomatopoeia to recreate traffic sounds","parallel structure to set up a contrast","a rhetorical question to involve the reader"],"correct":2,"why":"\"The question is not whether ... The question is whether ...\" repeats the same structure (parallelism) to contrast two ideas, turning the cost argument around. Neither sentence is a question, and there is no sound-word or euphemism.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following MOST enhances the credibility of the extract?</p>","options":["It was published in a weekly newspaper.","The writer is a transport planner who has advised island governments.","It is short and easy to read.","It describes children waiting in the sun."],"correct":1,"why":"Credibility depends largely on the writer's expertise. A transport planner with regional advisory experience is qualified to comment on public transport. The emotive image of children appeals to feelings (pathos) but does not prove expertise, and length or publication frequency says little about trustworthiness.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16 (Module 2)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following dialogue carefully and then answer Items 16–20.</i></p>\n<p>Shanice, who was born in Antigua, and her cousin Luis, who recently moved from the Dominican Republic, are waiting in a long line at a government office in St. John's.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<b>Shanice:</b> Boy, the line long, eh? We go be here till evening.<br>\n<b>Luis:</b> Sí, muy largo. But my teacher say I must speak English good if I want the hotel job.<br>\n<b>Shanice:</b> She would tell you \"speak English <i>well</i>\". But don't stress. When I with my grandmother, I talk one way; when I in the office, I talk another way.<br>\n<b>Luis:</b> My boss at the restaurant tell me my English \"too broken\" for the front desk.<br>\n<b>Shanice:</b> Hmm. Some people does look down on how we talk, but is still language.\n</blockquote></blockquote><p>Luis's movement from \"Sí, muy largo\" to English in the same turn is an example of</p>","options":["jargon","code-switching","register","slang"],"correct":1,"why":"Code-switching is alternating between two languages or varieties within a conversation. Luis moves from Spanish to English. Slang and jargon are types of vocabulary, and register refers to the level of formality.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Shanice says, \"When I with my grandmother, I talk one way; when I in the office, I talk another way.\" This shows that her language choice is influenced MAINLY by</p>","options":["age","gender","ethnicity","social context and relationship with the listener"],"correct":3,"why":"Shanice changes her speech according to who she is speaking to and where (home versus workplace). This is the influence of context and the relationship between speaker and listener on language choice.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The boss's comment that Luis's English is \"too broken\" for the front desk reflects the attitude that</p>","options":["Creole languages are complete, rule-governed systems","Spanish should be the main language of hotels","multilingual workers are an asset to the tourism industry","non-standard varieties are inferior to Standard English"],"correct":3,"why":"Calling a variety \"broken\" treats it as a faulty version of the standard rather than a legitimate system. This negative attitude to non-standard speech is common in Caribbean workplaces, especially in customer-facing jobs.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19 (Module 2)","q":"<p>In \"Some people does look down on how we talk\", the word \"does\" functions as a</p>","options":["habitual aspect marker","plural marker","future marker","past tense marker"],"correct":0,"why":"In many Eastern Caribbean Creoles, \"does\" placed before the verb shows that an action happens regularly or habitually. Here it means people habitually look down on Creole speech; it does not mark past time, future time or plurality.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The expressions \"the line long\" and \"When I in the office\" illustrate which grammatical feature of Caribbean Creole?</p>","options":["Aphesis","Metathesis","Double negation","Absence of the copula (the verb \"to be\")"],"correct":3,"why":"Standard English would say \"the line IS long\" and \"When I AM in the office\". Creole commonly omits the copula before adjectives and locations. Aphesis and metathesis are phonological features, and there is no negation here.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21 (Module 2)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Select the most appropriate answer for Items 21–30.</i></p></blockquote><p>English-lexicon Creoles in the Caribbean developed MAINLY from contact between</p>","options":["Amerindian languages and Asian languages","European languages and West African languages during slavery and colonisation","Spanish and Portuguese during the gold trade","Hindi and English after Emancipation"],"correct":1,"why":"On plantations, enslaved Africans speaking many West African languages needed to communicate with one another and with English-speaking Europeans. The resulting contact languages became Creoles that took most of their vocabulary from English and much of their structure from African languages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22 (Module 2)","q":"<p>In the formation of a Creole, the European language that supplies MOST of the vocabulary is referred to as the</p>","options":["substrate","superstrate (lexifier)","adstrate","interlanguage"],"correct":1,"why":"The superstrate or lexifier is the socially dominant language that provides most of the words. The substrate is the group of less dominant languages (for example, West African languages) that influenced the grammar and sound system.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The pronunciation of \"afraid\" as \"'fraid\" and \"remember\" as \"'member\" is an example of</p>","options":["aphesis","consonant cluster reduction","metathesis","epenthesis"],"correct":0,"why":"Aphesis is the loss of an unstressed sound or syllable at the beginning of a word. Epenthesis adds a sound, metathesis swaps sounds, and consonant cluster reduction drops a consonant from a group, usually at the end of a word.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A Creole speaker who pronounces \"film\" as \"fillum\" is demonstrating</p>","options":["aphesis","metathesis","epenthesis","th-stopping"],"correct":2,"why":"Epenthesis is the insertion of an extra sound into a word; here a vowel is added to break up the \"lm\" cluster. No sound is lost (aphesis), swapped (metathesis) or replaced (th-stopping).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25 (Module 2)","q":"<p>In the Creole sentence \"She did done cook when we reach\", the pre-verbal markers \"did\" and \"done\" indicate, respectively,</p>","options":["future time and progressive aspect","progressive aspect and negation","past (anterior) time and completed action","habitual aspect and plural subject"],"correct":2,"why":"\"Did\" places the event in the past, before another past event, and \"done\" shows the action was already completed. Together they mean \"She had already finished cooking when we arrived\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A lawyer uses expressions such as \"pursuant to\" and \"hereinafter\" when drafting a contract but greets her friends with \"Wha' happening?\" This change is BEST explained by the concept of</p>","options":["accent","dialect","pidgin","register"],"correct":3,"why":"Register is the variety of language chosen to suit the situation, purpose and audience, ranging from formal to informal. The lawyer is the same speaker with the same dialect; she adjusts her formality to the context.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27 (Module 2)","q":"<p>On the Creole continuum, the variety that is CLOSEST to Standard English is the</p>","options":["mesolect","idiolect","basilect","acrolect"],"correct":3,"why":"The acrolect is the most prestigious variety, nearest to the standard. The basilect is furthest from it and the mesolect lies in between. An idiolect is an individual's personal way of speaking, not a point on the continuum.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The animal called \"dog\" in English is \"hond\" in Dutch and \"perro\" in Spanish. This illustrates that language is</p>","options":["non-instinctive","systematic","arbitrary","dynamic"],"correct":2,"why":"Language is arbitrary because there is no natural link between a word and what it refers to; each language community agrees on its own label. Different words for the same animal show this clearly.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29 (Module 2)","q":"<p>During her oral presentation, Kezia folds her arms tightly and avoids looking at her audience. Which communicative behaviour is she displaying?</p>","options":["Proxemics","Haptics","Chronemics","Kinesics"],"correct":3,"why":"Kinesics is communication through body movement, posture, gestures, facial expression and eye behaviour. Folded arms and avoiding eye contact may signal nervousness or defensiveness. Proxemics is about space, chronemics about time and haptics about touch.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30 (Module 2)","q":"<p>At a funeral, Mr Hodge wears a black suit and speaks in a low, slow voice. Which TWO communicative behaviours are illustrated?</p>","options":["Chromatics and paralanguage","Proxemics and chromatics","Haptics and proxemics","Chronemics and kinesics"],"correct":0,"why":"Chromatics is the use of colour to communicate (black signals mourning in many Caribbean cultures). Paralanguage covers vocal features such as pitch, volume and rate, so a low, slow voice signals respect and sorrow.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following scenario carefully and then answer Items 31–35.</i></p>\n<p>Mrs Daniella Fleming is the general manager of Bayside Suites, a hotel in Grace Bay, Providenciales. Head office has decided that all staff must complete a new online customer-service training course by the end of the month. Mrs Fleming must pass this information to her four department heads, who will then inform their teams. Several members of the housekeeping staff speak mainly Spanish and do not have work email accounts.</p></blockquote><p>Mrs Fleming's communication of the decision to her department heads is an example of</p>","options":["grapevine communication","upward vertical communication","horizontal communication","downward vertical communication"],"correct":3,"why":"Information moving from a manager to subordinates in the organisational structure is downward vertical communication. Horizontal communication occurs between people at the same level, and the grapevine is the informal network.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32 (Module 3)","q":"<p>When the four department heads meet to agree on a shared training timetable, they are engaging in</p>","options":["upward communication","external communication","downward communication","horizontal communication"],"correct":3,"why":"The department heads are at the same level in the hotel, so communication among them is horizontal (lateral). It is used to coordinate work across departments.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which of the following barriers is the housekeeping staff MOST likely to face in receiving the message?</p>","options":["A language difference and no access to the email channel","Physical noise in the hotel lobby","Semantic barriers caused by medical jargon","Psychological barriers caused by grief"],"correct":0,"why":"The scenario states that several housekeeping staff speak mainly Spanish and have no work email. Both the language of the message and the channel chosen would prevent them from receiving and understanding it. Nothing suggests noise, medical jargon or grief.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which of the following would be the MOST effective way to repair the barrier faced by the housekeeping staff?</p>","options":["A notice placed on the hotel's public website","A short face-to-face briefing with a Spanish-speaking supervisor, plus a printed notice in Spanish and English","A longer email with more detailed instructions in English","A reminder sent to the department heads' email accounts only"],"correct":1,"why":"Repair must address both causes: a face-to-face channel removes the dependence on email and allows immediate feedback, while Spanish interpretation and a bilingual notice remove the language barrier. The other options still rely on email or English only.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Mrs Fleming sends a single email to her department heads and does not invite questions or replies. Which model of communication BEST describes this?</p>","options":["Interactive model","Circular feedback model","Transactional model","Linear model"],"correct":3,"why":"The linear model (for example, Shannon and Weaver) sees communication as a one-way flow from sender to receiver with no feedback. The interactive and transactional models include feedback and exchange between participants.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following scenario carefully and then answer Items 36–40.</i></p>\n<p>Two days before a by-election, a short video circulates on WhatsApp. It appears to show a candidate, Mr Rafael Lake, saying that he will close the island's only public library. The candidate's face and voice look and sound real, but he insists he never said those words. Within hours, a page called <i>Real Patriots 758</i> shares the video with the caption: \"Everybody already voting against Lake. Don't be the last one left behind!\"</p></blockquote><p>The video of Mr Lake is BEST described as</p>","options":["parody","a deepfake","an editorial","satire"],"correct":1,"why":"A deepfake is audio or video manipulated with artificial intelligence to make a real person appear to say or do something they did not. The realistic face and voice and the candidate's denial point to a deepfake, not humour (satire, parody) or opinion writing (editorial).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The caption \"Everybody already voting against Lake. Don't be the last one left behind!\" uses which propaganda technique?</p>","options":["Plain folks","Glittering generalities","Testimonial","Bandwagon"],"correct":3,"why":"The bandwagon technique persuades people to act because \"everyone else\" is doing so, playing on the fear of being left out. Testimonial uses a famous person's endorsement, plain folks presents the speaker as ordinary, and glittering generalities use vague virtue words.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Residents who forward the video to their friends because they genuinely believe it is real are spreading</p>","options":["propaganda","misinformation","disinformation","malinformation"],"correct":1,"why":"Misinformation is false information shared without the intention to deceive. The people who created the video to mislead voters spread disinformation, but residents who honestly believe it are passing on misinformation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Releasing the video two days before the by-election, when there is little time for it to be checked, takes advantage of</p>","options":["kairos","pathos","logos","ethos"],"correct":0,"why":"Kairos is the appeal to timing, using the most opportune moment to have an effect. The video is timed so that voters see it close to the vote and before it can be disproved.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which of the following is the MOST responsible action for a voter who receives the video?</p>","options":["Share it in family groups to ask what others think","Judge its accuracy by the number of times it has been shared","Accept it as true because the face and voice look real","Check whether reputable news outlets and the candidate's official channels have confirmed or denied it"],"correct":3,"why":"Verifying content against credible, accountable sources is the best defence against fake news. Sharing spreads it further, realism can be faked, and popularity is not evidence of truth.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following scenario carefully and then answer Items 41–43.</i></p>\n<p>Akeem, a CAPE student at a secondary school in St. John’s, has been asked to deliver a five-minute speech at a Parent-Teacher Association meeting. His aim is to persuade parents to support a ban on single-use plastic containers in the school canteen, even though reusable containers may cost a little more.</p></blockquote><p>Which of the following should Akeem consider when profiling his audience?<br><br>I. The parents' existing knowledge of plastic pollution<br>II. The parents' likely concerns about cost<br>III. Akeem's own favourite environmental slogans</p>","options":["I only","I and II only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":1,"why":"Audience profiling focuses on the listeners: what they already know, what they value and what objections they may have. Akeem's own preferences (III) are about the speaker, not the audience.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Akeem plans to say, \"Our canteen uses about 400 plastic containers each week, which is more than 15 000 in one school year.\" This statement is an appeal to</p>","options":["ethos","logos","pathos","kairos"],"correct":1,"why":"Logos is the appeal to logic and evidence, such as statistics and facts. The figures build a reasoned case rather than appealing to emotion (pathos), the speaker's credibility (ethos) or timing (kairos).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which organisational strategy would be MOST suitable for Akeem's persuasive speech?</p>","options":["Problem and solution","Classification","Spatial order","Chronological order"],"correct":0,"why":"A persuasive speech that seeks support for a change works well when it first establishes a problem (plastic waste) and then presents the proposed solution (a ban with reusable containers). The other patterns suit narrative, descriptive or explanatory speeches.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Select the most appropriate answer for Items 44 and 45.</i></p></blockquote><p>The idea that electronic media have connected people worldwide so that events in one place can be experienced almost instantly everywhere is referred to as the</p>","options":["global village","information overload","netiquette","digital divide"],"correct":0,"why":"Marshall McLuhan used \"global village\" to describe how electronic media shrink distance, making the world behave like one small community. The digital divide is unequal access to technology, information overload is having too much information, and netiquette is online etiquette.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which of the following is an example of intrapersonal communication?</p>","options":["A minister speaking to the nation on television","A student silently thinking through how to answer an interview question","A teacher addressing the whole school at assembly","Two friends chatting on the bus"],"correct":1,"why":"Intrapersonal communication takes place within oneself, such as thinking, reflecting or self-talk. The others are public, interpersonal and mass communication respectively.","fb":[]}]},{"id":"assess_2bi","title":"Paper 01 · Practice Paper B","desc":"<p><b>Paper 01 · Practice Paper B</b> · 1 hour 30 minutes</p>\n<ol>\n<li>This test consists of <b>45 items</b>: 15 on Module 1, 15 on Module 2 and 15 on Module 3. Answer <b>ALL</b> items.</li>\n<li><b>Items 1–7</b> are based on a listening extract. In the examination, the extract is NOT printed; it is read to you <b>twice</b> after you have had five minutes to read the items. For practice, ask a partner or your teacher to read it aloud before you look at the printed text.</li>\n<li>The remaining items are based on scenarios, extracts or dialogues, or stand alone. Answer each item based on the information given.</li>\n<li>Each item has four suggested answers lettered (A), (B), (C), (D). Read each item and decide which choice is <b>best</b>.</li>\n<li>If you cannot answer an item, go on to the next one and return to it later.</li>\n</ol>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"mc","title":"Item 1 (Module 1)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Items 1–7. In the examination this speech is read aloud twice. Listen carefully before answering.</i></p>\n<blockquote><b>Before the Wind Comes</b><br>\nGood evening, neighbours. Some of you were here for Irma. Some of you were not.<br>\nThose of us who were here remember the roar, like a freight train that would not stop passing.<br>\nWe remember roofs lifting like the lids of pots.<br>\nWe remember, too, the neighbour who shared her water, the stranger who cleared our road.<br>\nSo tonight I am not here to frighten you. I am here to prepare you.<br>\nCheck your shutters in June, not in September.<br>\nStore water for three days, not three hours.<br>\nKnow which neighbour lives alone, and knock on that door.<br>\nBecause the storm will test our roofs, but it will also test our hearts.<br>\nAnd I know which of those two this community will never lose.</blockquote>\n<p style=\"font-size:.85em\">Original speech written for this practice paper. It is delivered by a community council member at a meeting in Road Town, Tortola.</p></blockquote><p>The MAIN purpose of the speech is to</p>","options":["criticise the government for its response to past hurricanes","recount the damage caused by Hurricane Irma","encourage residents to prepare for storms and look out for one another","entertain residents with memories of the past"],"correct":2,"why":"The speaker states directly, \"I am not here to frighten you. I am here to prepare you,\" and gives practical instructions (check shutters, store water, check on neighbours). Memories of Irma support the purpose; they are not the purpose itself.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 2 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which device is used in the line \"roofs lifting like the lids of pots\"?</p>","options":["Hyperbole","Simile","Personification","Metaphor"],"correct":1,"why":"The comparison between roofs and pot lids is made with \"like\", which makes it a simile. It creates a vivid, familiar image of how easily roofs were torn away.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 3 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The repetition of \"We remember\" at the start of successive lines is an example of</p>","options":["alliteration","onomatopoeia","anaphora","irony"],"correct":2,"why":"Anaphora is the repetition of a word or phrase at the beginning of successive lines or clauses. It builds rhythm and stresses shared experience. Alliteration repeats initial consonant sounds within a line, which is not the main effect here.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 4 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The lines \"Check your shutters in June, not in September\" and \"Store water for three days, not three hours\" are effective MAINLY because they</p>","options":["exaggerate the dangers of storms","use short, contrasting instructions that are easy to remember","describe the history of hurricane seasons","use technical vocabulary to impress the audience"],"correct":1,"why":"Each line is a brief command with a built-in contrast (June/September, days/hours). This makes the advice clear and memorable for listeners, which is important in an oral message. The language is simple, not technical or exaggerated.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 5 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The line \"the storm will test our roofs, but it will also test our hearts\" suggests that</p>","options":["residents should be afraid of the coming season","community spirit is as important as physical preparation","houses in Road Town are poorly built","the speaker does not trust the residents"],"correct":1,"why":"The parallel phrases link physical damage (roofs) with community values (hearts). The speaker is saying that kindness and solidarity matter as much as shutters and water.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 6 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following BEST describes the tone of the speech?</p>","options":["Fearful and hopeless","Sarcastic and bitter","Neutral and detached","Reassuring and urging"],"correct":3,"why":"The speaker calms the audience (\"not here to frighten you\") and expresses confidence in the community, while firmly urging them to act. There is no sarcasm, hopelessness or detachment.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 7 (Module 1)","q":"<p>A listener in the meeting misses some of the instructions because she is planning what she will say during question time. This is an example of</p>","options":["selective listening due to bias","rehearsing a response instead of listening","a physical barrier to listening","information overload from the speaker"],"correct":1,"why":"Preparing one's own reply while someone is still speaking is a common internal barrier to effective listening. Attention is divided, so parts of the message are lost. There is no bias, physical noise or excessive information mentioned.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 8 (Module 1)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following scenario carefully and then answer Items 8–11.</i></p>\n<p>The Ministry of Education wants to find out why many secondary students in Guyana arrive late for the first class of the day. A research team will collect data from students, parents and school administrators in four schools over one term.</p></blockquote><p>Which method would BEST allow the researchers to see directly what happens at the school gate each morning?</p>","options":["Questionnaires","Document analysis","Telephone interviews","Observation"],"correct":3,"why":"Observation allows researchers to witness behaviour as it naturally occurs, such as when students arrive and what delays them. The other methods depend on what people report or on existing records.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 9 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The researchers use the same questionnaire in all four schools and get similar results when they repeat it a month later. This shows that the instrument is</p>","options":["reliable","valid","credible","biased"],"correct":0,"why":"Reliability refers to consistency: an instrument is reliable if it produces similar results when used again under similar conditions. Validity is whether it measures what it is meant to measure.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 10 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following is a DISADVANTAGE of using interviews with parents in this research?</p>","options":["They provide detailed, in-depth responses.","They are time-consuming and may be influenced by the interviewer.","They allow the researcher to clarify questions.","They allow follow-up questions."],"correct":1,"why":"Interviews take a long time to conduct and transcribe, and the interviewer's tone or wording may influence answers (interviewer bias). The other options are advantages of interviews.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 11 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following would provide the MOST credible data on how often students are late?</p>","options":["A newspaper letter written by a parent","Opinions shared on a radio talk show","Official school attendance registers","Students' posts on social media"],"correct":2,"why":"Attendance registers are official, systematic records kept daily by the school, so they are accurate and accountable. The other sources are personal opinions and may be exaggerated or incomplete.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 12 (Module 1)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following extract carefully and then answer Items 12–15.</i></p>\n<blockquote>Mangroves are often dismissed as muddy, mosquito-filled swamps. In fact, they are among the hardest-working ecosystems on the island. Their tangled roots trap sediment that would otherwise cloud coral reefs. They provide nurseries where young snapper and lobster grow safely before moving out to sea. During storms, a healthy mangrove belt absorbs the force of waves and protects homes behind it. When mangroves are cleared for marinas and parking lots, all of these services disappear at once: reefs choke, fish stocks fall and the coastline becomes exposed. Protecting what remains of our mangroves on Ambergris Caye is therefore not a luxury for nature lovers. It is a practical investment in the island's food, economy and safety.</blockquote>\n<p style=\"font-size:.85em\"><b>SOURCE:</b> Adapted from Marisol Hazel, \"The Island's Quiet Guardians\", <i>Caribbean Environment Review</i>, 2024. Marisol Hazel is a marine biologist who has studied Caribbean coastal wetlands for fifteen years.</p></blockquote><p>Which of the following is the BEST summary of the main point of the extract?</p>","options":["Marinas and parking lots are needed for the island's economy.","Mangroves provide vital services, so protecting them is a practical investment for the island.","Mangroves are muddy places full of mosquitoes.","Young snapper and lobster grow in mangroves."],"correct":1,"why":"A good summary captures the overall claim, not a single detail. The writer lists the services mangroves provide and concludes that protecting them supports food, economy and safety. Option C is the view the writer rejects, and C is only one supporting detail.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 13 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The sentence \"When mangroves are cleared ... reefs choke, fish stocks fall and the coastline becomes exposed\" is organised MAINLY by</p>","options":["definition","chronological order","cause and effect","spatial order"],"correct":2,"why":"The sentence shows what results (effects) when mangroves are removed (cause). There is no time sequence, description of space or definition of a term.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 14 (Module 1)","q":"<p>The writer opens with the view that mangroves are \"muddy, mosquito-filled swamps\" MAINLY in order to</p>","options":["describe the scenery of the island","persuade readers to avoid visiting Ambergris Caye","show that she agrees with developers","introduce a common misconception that she will then challenge"],"correct":3,"why":"The writer immediately follows with \"In fact\", showing that she is setting up a popular but mistaken belief so that she can correct it. This contrast technique makes her argument more striking.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 15 (Module 1)","q":"<p>Which of the following MOST supports the credibility of the extract?</p>","options":["The extract uses vivid words such as \"choke\".","The extract was adapted for a practice paper.","The writer mentions a specific place, Ambergris Caye.","The writer is a marine biologist with fifteen years' experience studying coastal wetlands."],"correct":3,"why":"The writer's qualifications and long experience in the exact field she is writing about make her a trustworthy source. Vivid language and place names add interest but do not establish expertise.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 16 (Module 2)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following dialogue carefully and then answer Items 16–20.</i></p>\n<p>Two students, Tevin from St. Kitts and Mireille from St. Lucia, are chatting during a regional youth conference in Bridgetown.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<b>Tevin:</b> Mireille, me aks the waiter fuh tea and he bring coffee! He nuh understand me at all.<br>\n<b>Mireille:</b> Ou pa ka konprann? Sorry, I mean, you think he didn't understand you? In St. Lucia, my grandmother speaks Kwéyòl to me but my teachers always told us to use English in class.<br>\n<b>Tevin:</b> Same thing by me. But now people does post in dialect on Instagram all the time.<br>\n<b>Mireille:</b> True. Last week our Prime Minister even used a Kwéyòl proverb in a speech. The crowd loved it.\n</blockquote></blockquote><p>Tevin's pronunciation of \"ask\" as \"aks\" is an example of</p>","options":["metathesis","aphesis","vowel lengthening","epenthesis"],"correct":0,"why":"Metathesis is the reversal of the order of sounds in a word; the \"s\" and \"k\" sounds in \"ask\" are swapped to give \"aks\". No sound is dropped (aphesis) or added (epenthesis).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 17 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Tevin's statement \"He nuh understand me\" shows which grammatical feature of Caribbean Creole?</p>","options":["Reduplication for emphasis","Plural marking with \"dem\"","Use of a pre-verbal negator","Use of the habitual marker \"does\""],"correct":2,"why":"In many Caribbean Creoles, negation is shown by a particle such as \"nuh\", \"no\" or \"doh\" placed directly before the verb, rather than by \"does not\" or \"did not\" as in Standard English.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 18 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Kwéyòl, which Mireille's grandmother speaks, is BEST described as</p>","options":["an indigenous Caribbean language","an English-lexicon Creole","a French-lexicon Creole","a dialect of Standard French"],"correct":2,"why":"St. Lucian Kwéyòl draws most of its vocabulary from French (its lexifier), while its grammar was shaped by West African languages. It is a Creole language in its own right, not a dialect of French, and not indigenous.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 19 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Mireille's teachers insisting that students \"use English in class\" reflects the attitude that</p>","options":["Standard English is the language of education and advancement","Kwéyòl is the language of national identity","Creole should be taught as a foreign language","English and Kwéyòl have equal status in schools"],"correct":0,"why":"Many Caribbean education systems have treated Standard English as the language of schooling, examinations and social mobility, while Creole was discouraged in the classroom. This is an attitude shaped by colonial history.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 20 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The Prime Minister's use of a Kwéyòl proverb, and the crowd's positive response, illustrates which function of language?</p>","options":["Recording historical events","Transmitting technical information","Excluding outsiders from the group","Expressing and reinforcing cultural identity"],"correct":3,"why":"Using a Creole proverb in a public speech signals pride in a shared heritage and builds solidarity with the audience. It shows language as a marker of national and cultural identity, which reflects more positive modern attitudes to Creole.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 21 (Module 2)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Select the most appropriate answer for Items 21–30.</i></p></blockquote><p>In which of the following territories is Papiamentu an official language?</p>","options":["Sint Maarten and Saba","Aruba and Curaçao","Suriname and Guyana","Martinique and Guadeloupe"],"correct":1,"why":"Papiamentu, a Creole with Portuguese, Spanish, Dutch and African influences, is an official language in Aruba, Curaçao and Bonaire. Sint Maarten's main languages are English and Dutch, Suriname's national lingua franca is Sranan Tongo, and Martinique and Guadeloupe use French and French Creole.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 22 (Module 2)","q":"<p>A child born in Aruba learns to speak naturally only after hearing Papiamento, Dutch and Spanish spoken around her. This shows that language is</p>","options":["arbitrary","static","non-instinctive (acquired)","symbolic"],"correct":2,"why":"Language is non-instinctive: humans have the capacity for language but must learn a specific language through exposure in a community. No child is born speaking a particular language.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 23 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The words \"selfie\", \"unfriend\" and \"livestream\" have recently entered everyday Caribbean speech. This illustrates that language is</p>","options":["maturational","dynamic","arbitrary","systematic"],"correct":1,"why":"A dynamic language changes over time, adding new words to meet new needs (here, social media). Maturational refers to how an individual's language develops as they grow.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 24 (Module 2)","q":"<p>In many Caribbean Creoles, \"thing\" is pronounced \"ting\" and \"that\" is pronounced \"dat\". This phonological feature is known as</p>","options":["aphesis","consonant cluster reduction","th-stopping","metathesis"],"correct":2,"why":"Th-stopping replaces the \"th\" sounds of Standard English with the stop consonants /t/ and /d/. It is one of the most widespread phonological features of English-lexicon Caribbean Creoles.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 25 (Module 2)","q":"<p>The Creole expressions \"pretty-pretty\" (very pretty) and \"fool-fool\" (very foolish) illustrate</p>","options":["aphesis","serial verbs","calquing","reduplication"],"correct":3,"why":"Reduplication is the repetition of a word to intensify or change its meaning. It is a lexical and grammatical feature found in many Caribbean Creoles and in West African languages.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 26 (Module 2)","q":"<p>In the sentence \"Dem a play cricket\", the word \"a\" marks</p>","options":["completed action","plural nouns","future time","progressive (continuous) action"],"correct":3,"why":"In many English-lexicon Creoles, \"a\" (or \"de\", \"ah\") before the verb shows an ongoing action, equivalent to \"They are playing cricket\". Completed action is marked by \"done\", and future by \"go\" or \"gwine\".","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 27 (Module 2)","q":"<p>When doctors at a hospital discuss a patient's \"hypertension\" and \"BP readings\" with one another, they are using</p>","options":["jargon","Creole","dialect","slang"],"correct":0,"why":"Jargon is the specialised vocabulary of a profession or field. It allows experts to communicate precisely among themselves but may confuse outsiders such as patients.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 28 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Which of the following factors MOST influences a person's attitude towards Creole in the Caribbean?</p>","options":["The history of colonialism and the link between language and social class","The number of vowels in Creole words","The weather in the territory","The person's height and age at school entry"],"correct":0,"why":"Attitudes to Creole have been shaped by colonial history, in which European languages were associated with power and education and Creole with enslaved and working-class people. These associations with social class continue to influence attitudes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 29 (Module 2)","q":"<p>At a job interview, Kyle sits very close to the interviewer and touches her arm while speaking. Which TWO communicative behaviours are being displayed?</p>","options":["Proxemics and haptics","Chronemics and chromatics","Paralanguage and proxemics","Kinesics and chronemics"],"correct":0,"why":"Proxemics is the use of personal space (sitting very close), and haptics is communication through touch (touching her arm). In a formal interview, both may be seen as inappropriate.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 30 (Module 2)","q":"<p>Arriving thirty minutes late to a business meeting may be interpreted as a lack of respect. This is an aspect of</p>","options":["haptics","chronemics","chromatics","kinesics"],"correct":1,"why":"Chronemics is the study of how time is used and perceived in communication, including punctuality and waiting time. Attitudes to time vary across cultures and contexts.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 31 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following scenario carefully and then answer Items 31–35.</i></p>\n<p>A youth health network in Dominica is planning a three-month campaign to reduce the consumption of sugary drinks among students aged 12–18. The coordinator, Ms Nadine Arrindell, must first write to the Minister of Public Health requesting funding, then design messages that will reach students in their schools and online.</p></blockquote><p>Ms Arrindell's request to the Minister for funding should be presented in the form of a</p>","options":["memo","text message","formal letter accompanied by a proposal","social media post"],"correct":2,"why":"A request to a government minister requires a formal written channel. A formal letter introduces the request, and a proposal sets out the problem, objectives, activities and budget. Memos are for internal use within an organisation.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 32 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which section of the proposal should explain WHY the campaign is needed?</p>","options":["The budget","The statement of the problem (rationale)","The timeline","The list of references"],"correct":1,"why":"The rationale or statement of the problem gives the background and evidence showing that sugary-drink consumption among students is a concern. The budget and timeline describe cost and schedule.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 33 (Module 3)","q":"<p>To reach students aged 12–18 MOST effectively, which combination of channels would be BEST for the campaign?</p>","options":["School assemblies and short videos on popular social media platforms","Billboards and letters to parents","Newspaper advertisements and radio news","Television documentaries and printed brochures"],"correct":0,"why":"Audience profiling shows that teenagers spend much of their time at school and on social media. Combining face-to-face assemblies with short videos on platforms they use places the message where the audience already is.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 34 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A campaign poster shows a popular local soca artist saying, \"I swapped soda for water, and so can you.\" This is an example of</p>","options":["name-calling","testimonial","card stacking","plain folks"],"correct":1,"why":"A testimonial uses a well-known or admired person to endorse a product or idea. Students who admire the artist may be persuaded to follow his example.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 35 (Module 3)","q":"<p>A message that shows a young person's damaged teeth with the caption \"This could be your smile\" relies MAINLY on</p>","options":["ethos","kairos","logos","pathos"],"correct":3,"why":"Pathos appeals to the audience's emotions, here fear and concern about appearance. It does not present statistics (logos), establish the speaker's credibility (ethos) or rely on timing (kairos).","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 36 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following scenario carefully and then answer Items 36–40.</i></p>\n<p>At a staff meeting, Mr Brooks, the vice principal, explains a new examination timetable. The air-conditioning unit is rattling loudly. Mr Brooks uses terms such as \"modular sittings\" and \"concurrent windows\" without explaining them. Several teachers who are already upset about extra supervision duties stop listening. Ms Lake, at the back, nods but later admits she misunderstood the dates.</p></blockquote><p>The rattling air-conditioning unit is an example of</p>","options":["physical noise","physiological noise","semantic noise","psychological noise"],"correct":0,"why":"Physical (external) noise is any sound or environmental distraction that interferes with the transmission of the message. Semantic noise relates to meaning and psychological noise to the receiver's emotions or attitudes.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 37 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Mr Brooks's use of \"modular sittings\" and \"concurrent windows\" without explanation creates a</p>","options":["physical barrier","cultural barrier","semantic barrier","physiological barrier"],"correct":2,"why":"A semantic barrier occurs when the receiver does not share the sender's understanding of words or terms, such as unexplained jargon. The teachers cannot decode the message accurately.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 38 (Module 3)","q":"<p>The teachers who \"stop listening\" because they are upset about extra duties are affected by</p>","options":["a technological barrier","a psychological barrier","a linguistic barrier","a physical barrier"],"correct":1,"why":"Psychological barriers arise from the receiver's emotions, attitudes or prejudices. The teachers' resentment prevents them from paying attention to the message.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 39 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Ms Lake's nod, even though she misunderstood, shows that</p>","options":["the channel was inappropriate","the message was encoded correctly","the sender should check understanding rather than rely on non-verbal feedback alone","feedback was clear and accurate"],"correct":2,"why":"A nod can be misleading feedback. Mr Brooks should confirm understanding, for example by asking teachers to restate key dates or by following up with a written timetable.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 40 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which of the following would BEST repair the communication problems at the meeting?<br><br>I. Moving to a quieter room or switching off the unit<br>II. Defining the technical terms and giving a written copy of the timetable<br>III. Speaking faster to finish the meeting quickly</p>","options":["I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"correct":0,"why":"Option I removes the physical noise and option II removes the semantic barrier while giving a permanent record. Speaking faster (III) would make understanding harder and does not address any barrier.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 41 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Read the following scenario carefully and then answer Items 41–43.</i></p>\n<p>Zara, a CAPE student, is preparing a heritage project about traditional Curaçao Carnival costume-making for an audience of young people living in the diaspora in the Netherlands and the United States.</p></blockquote><p>Which of the following would be the MOST effective digital medium for Zara to show the step-by-step making of a costume to her audience?</p>","options":["A short video posted on a video-sharing platform","A text message","A radio interview","A printed brochure"],"correct":0,"why":"Video combines sound, moving images and demonstration, which suits a step-by-step craft process. A video-sharing platform is also easily accessible to young people abroad. Radio and text cannot show the process visually.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 42 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which of the following BEST explains why digital media technology is suitable for Zara's audience?</p>","options":["It allows young people overseas to access and share the content instantly from anywhere.","It guarantees that every viewer will agree with the content.","It is the only form of communication young people understand.","It removes the need for any planning."],"correct":0,"why":"Digital media overcomes distance, allowing the diaspora audience to view, comment on and share the content at any time. This connects them to their cultural heritage, an example of the global village.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 43 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which of the following is a POSSIBLE disadvantage of using social media to share Zara's work?</p>","options":["The work can reach many people at little cost.","The content may be copied or used without permission.","The audience can respond with comments.","The content can include images and sound."],"correct":1,"why":"Once posted online, content can easily be downloaded, altered or reused without credit, raising issues of copyright and ownership. The other options are advantages of social media.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 44 (Module 3)","q":"<blockquote><p><i>Select the most appropriate answer for Items 44 and 45.</i></p></blockquote><p>In the communication process, the stage in which the receiver interprets the sender's message is called</p>","options":["feedback","decoding","transmitting","encoding"],"correct":1,"why":"Decoding is the receiver's interpretation of the symbols (words, gestures, images) into meaning. Encoding is done by the sender, transmitting is sending through a channel, and feedback is the receiver's response.","fb":[]},{"t":"mc","title":"Item 45 (Module 3)","q":"<p>Which model of communication sees both participants as sending and receiving messages at the same time, within a shared context?</p>","options":["Linear model","Shannon and Weaver model","Transactional model","Aristotle's model"],"correct":2,"why":"The transactional model views communication as simultaneous: participants are communicators who encode and decode at the same time, influenced by context and their fields of experience. Linear models (including Shannon and Weaver and Aristotle's) show one-way flow.","fb":[]}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 02 Specimen Papers","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_2bm","title":"Paper 02 · Practice Paper A","desc":"<p><b>READ THE FOLLOWING INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY.</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>This paper consists of THREE questions: Section A (Module 1), Section B (Module 2) and Section C (Module 3).</li>\n<li>Answer ALL questions.</li>\n<li>Each question is worth 25 marks: Content 10, Organization 7, Expression 8.</li>\n<li>Questions 1 and 2 must be answered in NO MORE than 500 words; Question 3 in NO MORE than 300 words.</li>\n<li>You are advised to take some time to read through the paper and plan your answers.</li>\n<li>Time allowed: 2 hours 30 minutes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><i>All extracts, scenarios, names, organisations and statistics in this practice paper were written for practice purposes.</i></p>","minutes":150,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Module 1","q":"<p><b>SECTION A · MODULE 1 – GATHERING AND PROCESSING INFORMATION</b></p>\n<p>1. Read the extract below carefully and then answer the question that follows.</p>\n<h4 style=\"text-align:center\">Whose Sand Is It Anyway?</h4>\n<p>Every Sunday morning for as long as I can remember, my grandmother walked from her house in Old Road to the bay below, dipped her feet in the water and declared the week officially begun. Last month, when I tried to take my own children down the same path, a security guard in a pressed grey uniform told us that the beach was \"for guests only\".</p>\n<p>Our experience is not unusual. A 2024 survey by a local coastal heritage foundation found that of 37 beaches traditionally used by residents, 11 now have no clearly marked public access route, and a further 8 can be reached only through hotel car parks or private gates. In other words, half of our shoreline has quietly become harder to reach for the very people who live here.</p>\n<p>Developers will argue that tourism pays the bills, and they are not wrong. Tourism brings in roughly four of every five dollars that circulate in our economy. Yet a beach is not a hotel amenity like a pool or a spa. It is a public commons, protected in principle by the island's own planning ordinance, which states that the foreshore \"shall remain accessible to all\".</p>\n<p>The problem is not the law; it is enforcement. Access paths are narrowed during construction and never reopened. Signs are blown away in storms and never replaced. Each small loss seems too minor to fight, but together they add up to a shoreline locked behind fences.</p>\n<p>Some will say I am being sentimental. Perhaps. But think of the fisherman who can no longer launch his boat, the school that can no longer hold its swimming programme, the teenager who has nowhere free to spend a hot afternoon. A people cut off from the sea is a people cut off from part of itself.</p>\n<p>The solution is neither radical nor expensive. Government must publish a register of every public access point, mark each one with permanent signage and fine any property that blocks it. Residents, meanwhile, must use these paths and report every closure. Rights that are not exercised are soon forgotten.</p>\n<p>The sea has always belonged to all of us. We must not allow it to be sold one gate at a time.</p>\n<p><small>SOURCE: Adapted from Carla Brooks-Richardson (2025). \"Whose Sand Is It Anyway?\" <i>The Island Voice</i>, St. John's, 14 June 2025.</small></p>\n<p><small><i>Carla Brooks-Richardson is an environmental planner who has worked for fifteen years on coastal zone management projects in the northeastern Caribbean. She is a founding member of a residents' beach-access lobby group and writes a weekly column on heritage and development.</i></small></p><p>Write an essay, in NO MORE than 500 words, in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>state the writer's MAIN message in no more than 30 words</li>\n<li>state the writer's purpose</li>\n<li>discuss TWO organizational strategies OR TWO language techniques OR any combination of both which the writer uses to achieve the purpose</li>\n<li>evaluate the credibility of the information presented.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>MARKING KEY · Question 1 (Content 10 marks)</h4>\n<p><b>• State the writer's main message [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks for a statement which effectively captures all of the points in the following. Award 1 mark for a statement which captures two of the points. Award no mark for a statement of the topic ONLY (e.g. \"beaches\").</p>\n<blockquote>Public access to Antigua's beaches is gradually being lost (1) through weak enforcement rather than weak laws (2), so government and residents must act to keep the shoreline public (3).<br><b>OR</b><br>Beaches are a public commons (1) that are being closed off one path at a time (2); a register, signage, fines and residents' use of access paths can protect them (3).</blockquote>\n<p><b>• State the writer's purpose [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks for a response which has a clearly stated purpose verb and captures two other elements from the following. Award 1 mark for a purpose verb and one other element. Award no mark for a purpose verb without an acceptable explanation.</p>\n<p>The writer's purpose is to</p>\n<ul>\n<li>highlight/raise awareness that residents are losing access to beaches</li>\n<li>explain that the cause is poor enforcement rather than the absence of law</li>\n<li>persuade/convince/urge government and residents to take specific action (register, signage, fines, use and report access paths).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Discuss TWO organizational strategies OR language techniques OR any combination [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks each for correct identification, example and explanation of any two organizational strategies or language techniques or combination thereof. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]<br>Award 1 mark for correct naming of ONE strategy or technique with example but no explanation.<br>Award 1 mark for correct naming of two or more strategies or techniques without example(s) or explanation(s).</p>\n<p><i>Organizational strategies</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Anecdote / personal narrative</b> – the grandmother's Sunday walk and the guard's \"for guests only\" (paragraph 1). Used to make the issue concrete and emotional and to win the reader's sympathy before the argument begins.</li>\n<li><b>Statistical data</b> – 37 beaches, 11 with no marked access, 8 reachable only through private property (paragraph 2). Used to show that the problem is widespread and measurable, not just one family's experience.</li>\n<li><b>Concession and refutation</b> – \"Developers will argue that tourism pays the bills, and they are not wrong. ... Yet a beach is not a hotel amenity\" (paragraph 3). Used to acknowledge the opposing view, appear fair, and then overturn it.</li>\n<li><b>Contrast</b> – a beach versus \"a pool or a spa\" (paragraph 3). Used to distinguish a public commons from private property.</li>\n<li><b>Cause and effect</b> – paths narrowed during construction and signs not replaced leading to a shoreline \"locked behind fences\" (paragraph 4). Used to explain how access is lost gradually.</li>\n<li><b>Examples</b> – the fisherman, the school swimming programme, the teenager (paragraph 5). Used to show the range of people affected.</li>\n<li><b>Problem and solution / call to action</b> – register, permanent signage, fines; residents must use and report paths (paragraph 6). Used to show the problem is solvable and to involve readers in the solution.</li>\n<li><b>Authoritative source</b> – quotation from the planning ordinance, \"shall remain accessible to all\". Used to show that the writer's position is supported by law.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Language techniques</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rhetorical question in title</b> – \"Whose Sand Is It Anyway?\" Used to provoke reflection and suggest that ownership is being contested.</li>\n<li><b>Metaphor</b> – \"a shoreline locked behind fences\"; \"sold one gate at a time\". Used to present the loss of access as a gradual theft or imprisonment of public property.</li>\n<li><b>Parallelism / repetition</b> – \"never reopened ... never replaced\"; \"a people cut off from the sea is a people cut off from part of itself\". Used to emphasise the pattern of neglect and the seriousness of the loss.</li>\n<li><b>Aphorism</b> – \"Rights that are not exercised are soon forgotten.\" Used to give the argument a memorable, wise-sounding conclusion that urges action.</li>\n<li><b>Inclusive pronouns / emotive language</b> – \"our shoreline\", \"all of us\", \"sentimental\". Used to build solidarity with residents.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Evaluate the credibility of the information presented [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark each for a clear explanation of any two of the following reasons given as to whether the information is credible or not. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]</p>\n<p>The information may be considered credible because</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the writer is an environmental planner with fifteen years' experience in coastal zone management and is qualified to discuss access and planning</li>\n<li>specific, dated statistics from a named survey (2024) are provided</li>\n<li>the planning ordinance is quoted directly</li>\n<li>the article is recent (2025), so the information is current</li>\n<li>it agrees with what readers may already observe about hotel development on the island.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The information may be considered less than credible because</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the writer is a founding member of a beach-access lobby group and may be biased</li>\n<li>the survey was carried out by a heritage foundation that may share her views; its methods are not explained</li>\n<li>the claim that tourism brings in \"four of every five dollars\" is not sourced</li>\n<li>the opening relies on a personal anecdote, which is emotional rather than factual.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Organization [7 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent paragraph development with thematic cohesion, appropriate use of transitional devices and connectives, and an effective conclusion.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of the above criteria with one or two weaknesses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adequate level of organization with several weaknesses evident.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unsatisfactory level of organization with weaknesses evident in all areas.</td><td>0–2</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Expression [8 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent, effective and error-free use of language.</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Some ability to use language effectively and accurately, but there are inconsistencies in accurate usage.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frequent inaccurate use of language OR insufficient information presented.</td><td>1–2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Incomprehensible response OR no response.</td><td>0</td></tr>\n</table><h4>Model answer</h4><p>Across the Caribbean, the push for tourism earnings often collides with residents' rights to land and sea. In \"Whose Sand Is It Anyway?\", Carla Brooks-Richardson's main message is that <b>Antigua's beaches are gradually being closed to residents through weak enforcement of access laws, so government and citizens must act to keep the shoreline public.</b> Her purpose is to persuade officials and ordinary residents that the loss of beach access is serious but solvable, and to urge them to act. She achieves this mainly through personal anecdote and metaphor, and her information is largely, though not entirely, credible.</p>\n<p>The first strategy the writer uses is personal anecdote. She opens with her grandmother's Sunday ritual of walking \"from her house in Old Road to the bay below\" and contrasts it with a guard telling her own children that the beach is \"for guests only\". This short story turns an abstract planning issue into a concrete, personal injustice: readers can picture a local family being turned away from a beach their family has used for generations. By beginning with emotion rather than figures, the writer wins the reader's sympathy before presenting her argument, which supports her purpose of persuading.</p>\n<p>Secondly, the writer uses metaphor to reinforce her message. She describes the shoreline as \"locked behind fences\" and warns that the sea must not be \"sold one gate at a time\". These images present the loss of access as a slow theft, in which each closed path is another piece of public property sold off. The final metaphor is especially effective because it is placed in the closing line, leaving the reader with a memorable picture of what will happen if nothing is done. In this way, the technique moves readers from understanding the problem to wanting to stop it.</p>\n<p>The information is credible to a large extent. The writer is an environmental planner with fifteen years of experience in coastal zone management, so she is qualified to discuss planning and access. She also supports her claims with specific figures from a 2024 survey, for example that 11 of 37 beaches lack marked access, and she quotes the planning ordinance directly, which suggests careful research. However, her credibility is reduced because she is a founding member of a beach-access lobby group and may therefore be biased, and the survey comes from a heritage foundation that may share her views. In addition, the claim that tourism produces \"four of every five dollars\" is unsourced. Readers should therefore accept her main argument while verifying the statistics independently.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, Brooks-Richardson combines a moving personal story with powerful metaphor to persuade readers that public beaches must be protected, and her expertise largely outweighs the risk of bias.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Module 2","q":"<p><b>SECTION B · MODULE 2 – LANGUAGE AND COMMUNITY</b></p>\n<p>2. Read the excerpt below carefully and then answer the question that follows.</p>\n<p><i>Ms Arrindell, a teacher at Hummingbird Ridge High School in The Valley, Anguilla, is coaching three students, Jaden, Keisha and Rohan, for an inter-school debate. The following exchange was recorded during a practice session.</i></p>\n<p><b>Ms Arrindell:</b> Good afternoon, team. Today we are going to practise rebuttals. Jaden, please summarise the opposition's first argument.</p>\n<p><b>Jaden:</b> Miss, dem say social media does make young people lazy.</p>\n<p><b>Ms Arrindell:</b> They say social media makes young people lazy. Remember, in the debate you must say, \"The opposition argues that...\" Try again.</p>\n<p><b>Jaden:</b> The opposition argue dat social media make young people lazy.</p>\n<p><b>Ms Arrindell:</b> Argues. Good, much better. Keisha, how would you answer him?</p>\n<p><b>Keisha:</b> Miss, I would say is not true, because plenty people does use it to learn tings.</p>\n<p><b>Ms Arrindell:</b> [laughing] Girl, you right, you right! But careful how you put it. Dem judges does take off marks for dat. Say, \"Many people use it to learn things.\"</p>\n<p><b>Rohan:</b> [whispering to Jaden] She tink she smart.</p>\n<p><b>Ms Arrindell:</b> Rohan, wha you sayin dey? Stop mout'in an pay attention! You is next.</p>\n<p><b>Rohan:</b> Sorry, Miss.</p>\n<p><b>Ms Arrindell:</b> Now, Rohan, give me your rebuttal in proper debate language.</p>\n<p><b>Rohan:</b> Ladies and gentlemen, the opposition claim dat social media is a waste of time, but... uh... I forget de rest, Miss.</p>\n<p><b>Ms Arrindell:</b> Don't fret. Is only practice. Take a breath and start again.</p><p>Write an essay, in NO MORE than 500 words, in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>state ONE possible reason each for the teacher's use of TWO language varieties as demonstrated in the excerpt</li>\n<li>identify, with the use of examples from the excerpt, ONE grammatical feature of the teacher's language</li>\n<li>identify, with the use of examples from the excerpt, ONE phonological feature of the STUDENTS' language</li>\n<li>identify and explain ONE challenge that a Creole-speaking student in this team might face in mastering the Standard English required for formal debating.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>MARKING KEY · Question 2 (Content 10 marks)</h4>\n<p><b>• State ONE possible reason each for the teacher's use of TWO language varieties [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks EACH for the identification and brief explanation of the TWO language varieties used by the teacher. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]<br>Award 1 mark for only naming the two language varieties without explanation.</p>\n<p><i>Use of Standard English / acrolect</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Standard English is the expected variety for formal debating and will be expected by the judges.</li>\n<li>The teacher is modelling the variety the students must produce (\"They say social media makes young people lazy\").</li>\n<li>Standard English is used to correct students' Creole forms (\"Argues\"; \"Say, 'Many people use it to learn things'\").</li>\n<li>It is the variety associated with formal instruction and the serious, instructional part of the session.</li>\n<li>It is part of the linguistic repertoire of the speech community of which the teacher is a member.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Use of Anguillan Creole</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li>To build rapport and praise warmly (\"Girl, you right, you right!\").</li>\n<li>To rebuke and manage behaviour (\"Stop mout'in an pay attention!\").</li>\n<li>To reassure and reduce anxiety (\"Don't fret. Is only practice.\").</li>\n<li>To relate to students in their home variety and encourage participation.</li>\n<li>It is part of the linguistic repertoire of the speech community and expresses shared identity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Identify, with examples, ONE grammatical feature of the teacher's language [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks for the identification with relevant example of any ONE of the following. Award no mark for identifying a phonological feature, OR for a grammatical feature without a relevant example, OR for an example taken from the students' lines only.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Habitual aspect marker \"does\"</b> – \"Dem judges does take off marks\" (Standard English: judges take off marks).</li>\n<li><b>Zero copula</b> with adjectival predicate – \"you right\" (you are right).</li>\n<li><b>Absence of subject–verb agreement / invariant \"is\"</b> – \"You is next\" (you are next).</li>\n<li><b>Subject deletion with existential \"is\"</b> – \"Is only practice\" (It is only practice).</li>\n<li><b>Plural / demonstrative \"dem\"</b> – \"Dem judges\" (those judges / the judges).</li>\n<li><b>Invariable word order in questions / no auxiliary</b> – \"wha you sayin dey?\" (What are you saying there?).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Identify, with examples, ONE phonological feature of the students' language [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks for the identification with relevant example of any ONE of the following. Award no mark for identifying a grammatical feature, OR for a feature without a relevant example.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Stopping of dental fricatives</b> – /θ/ and /ð/ realised as /t/ and /d/: \"dat\" (that), \"tings\" (things), \"tink\" (think), \"de\" (the), \"dem\" (them).</li>\n<li>Any other acceptable feature with a relevant example from the students' lines.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Identify and explain ONE challenge in mastering Standard English [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for identification and 1 mark for any reasonable explanation of ONE challenge. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Interference / transfer from Creole</b> – because Creole and English share most vocabulary, students may not notice grammatical differences (Jaden's \"The opposition argue dat\" still lacks the -s ending).</li>\n<li><b>Negative attitudes / anxiety</b> – fear of being judged for \"improper\" speech may cause students to freeze (Rohan: \"I forget de rest\").</li>\n<li><b>Belief that they already speak English</b> – students living in an officially English-speaking territory may not see the need to change their forms.</li>\n<li><b>Limited exposure / models</b> – Standard English may be used mainly in class, giving few opportunities to practise it naturally.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Organization [7 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent paragraph development with thematic cohesion, appropriate use of transitional devices and connectives, and an effective conclusion.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of the above criteria with one or two weaknesses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adequate level of organization with several weaknesses evident.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unsatisfactory level of organization with weaknesses evident in all areas.</td><td>0–2</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Expression [8 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent, effective and error-free use of language.</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Some ability to use language effectively and accurately, but there are inconsistencies in accurate usage.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frequent inaccurate use of language OR insufficient information presented.</td><td>1–2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Incomprehensible response OR no response.</td><td>0</td></tr>\n</table><h4>Model answer</h4><p>In Caribbean classrooms, teachers seldom speak only one language variety; instead, they move along the Creole continuum to suit their purposes. In the excerpt, Ms Arrindell, a debate coach in The Valley, switches between Standard English and Anguillan Creole while preparing her team. Her choices are deliberate, serving both instructional and relational goals. This essay will explain why she uses each variety, identify a grammatical feature of her speech and a phonological feature of her students' speech, and explain a challenge her students may face in mastering Standard English.</p>\n<p>Ms Arrindell uses Standard English because it is the variety expected in formal debating. She opens with \"Today we are going to practise rebuttals\" and immediately corrects Jaden's \"dem say\" to \"They say social media makes young people lazy.\" Since debate judges will reward Standard English, she models it so that students can imitate it. The acrolect therefore marks the serious, instructional part of the session.</p>\n<p>However, she uses Creole to build rapport and manage behaviour. She praises Keisha with \"Girl, you right, you right!\" and rebukes Rohan with \"Stop mout'in an pay attention!\" Because Creole is the students' home variety, her praise sounds sincere and her reprimand is immediate and clear. She also reassures Rohan with \"Don't fret. Is only practice,\" lowering his anxiety so that he will try again.</p>\n<p>One grammatical feature of the teacher's language is the habitual aspect marker \"does\". She says, \"Dem judges does take off marks for dat,\" where Standard English would use the simple present, \"Judges take off marks.\" In Creole, \"does\" signals an action that happens regularly, and it does not change for person or number.</p>\n<p>A phonological feature of the students' language is the stopping of dental fricatives. The \"th\" sounds /θ/ and /ð/ are pronounced as /t/ and /d/, as in Jaden's \"dat\" for \"that\", Keisha's \"tings\" for \"things\", and Rohan's \"tink\" for \"think\" and \"de\" for \"the\". This feature is common across English-lexicon Creoles in the Caribbean.</p>\n<p>One challenge these students may face is interference from Creole. Because Creole and English share most of their vocabulary, students may not notice where the grammar differs. For instance, when Jaden \"corrects\" himself, he changes the vocabulary but still says \"The opposition argue\", without the Standard English -s ending, and Rohan does the same with \"the opposition claim\". Since the forms look so similar, learners need explicit instruction in differences such as subject–verb agreement before they can use the formal variety confidently.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, Ms Arrindell's code-switching shows how Caribbean teachers use both varieties purposefully, and it highlights the linguistic awareness her students need to succeed in formal debate.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Module 3","q":"<p><b>SECTION C · MODULE 3 – SPEAKING AND WRITING</b></p>\n<p>3. Read the scenario below carefully and then answer the question that follows.</p>\n<p>The Board of Management of your secondary school has invited student representatives to present proposals at its next meeting. As president of the Student Council, you have been asked to deliver a 10-minute presentation proposing that one classroom be converted into a \"Calm Room\": a supervised space where students experiencing stress or anxiety may go for short periods during the school day.</p>\n<p>The board includes the principal, a parent representative, a representative of the school's church sponsor and an official from the Ministry of Education. Some board members have already expressed concern about the cost of the project and about students using the room to \"skip class\".</p><p>In an essay of NO MORE than 300 words, discuss the plan of action that you and your team will undertake as you prepare for this presentation by</p>\n<ul>\n<li>explaining TWO message points related to ethos or kairos appeals that you could use to persuade the board</li>\n<li>justifying ONE language register and ONE dialectal variety that would be considered appropriate for your audience</li>\n<li>justifying the use of ONE digital media technology that could enhance the delivery of your presentation to the board.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>MARKING KEY · Question 3 (Content 10 marks)</h4>\n<p><b>(a) Explain TWO message points related to ethos or kairos appeals [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark each for a clear identification of TWO message points related to either ethos or kairos appeals. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]<br>Award 1 mark each for a clear explanation of how each message point relates to ethos or kairos. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]</p>\n<p><i>Message points related to ethos</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"As your elected Student Council, we surveyed over 400 students before preparing this proposal.\" <br>Explanation: establishes the speaker's credibility and the legitimacy of the proposal.</li>\n<li>\"The school counsellor has agreed to supervise the room, and the room will operate under a sign-in system.\" <br>Explanation: borrows the authority of a trusted professional and answers the \"skipping class\" concern.</li>\n<li>\"Similar quiet rooms are recommended by the Ministry's own student-wellbeing guidelines.\" <br>Explanation: appeals to an authoritative source the board respects.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Message points related to kairos</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"With CSEC and CAPE examinations only a few months away, student stress is at its peak; now is the time to act.\" <br>Explanation: uses timing to show urgency.</li>\n<li>\"With World Mental Health Day just passed, our school has an opportunity to show leadership.\" <br>Explanation: links the proposal to a timely public occasion.</li>\n<li>\"The new budget cycle begins next month, so approving the proposal now allows costs to be planned.\" <br>Explanation: uses the right moment to address the cost concern.</li>\n<li>Any other acceptable message point and relevant explanation of its connection to ethos or kairos.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>(b) Justify ONE language register and ONE dialectal variety [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for a clear identification of an appropriate dialectal variety and 1 mark for a brief justification of its use. Award 1 mark for a clear identification of a relevant register and 1 mark for an acceptable justification. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Formal register</b> – the board is an official decision-making body of high-status adults; formality signals respect and seriousness; the setting is a formal meeting.</li>\n<li><b>Standard English</b> – the accepted variety for official meetings and education; understood by all members, including any whose first language is not English; projects competence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>(c) Justify ONE digital media technology [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for identification of any relevant digital media technology/device and 1 mark for an acceptable explanation of its use. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Presentation software: PowerPoint, Canva, Google Slides, Prezi; hardware: laptop, projector, smart TV, tablet; short digital video of student testimonials; online survey dashboard.</li>\n<li>Justification may include: displays survey data clearly in charts; shows a floor plan or mock-up of the room; keeps the presentation within ten minutes; looks professional to a high-status audience; easy to share with board members afterwards.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Unless specified as digital or online, do not accept: radio, television, video or text messaging.</p>\n<h4>Organization [7 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent paragraph development with thematic cohesion, appropriate use of transitional devices and connectives, and an effective conclusion.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of the above criteria with one or two weaknesses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adequate level of organization with several weaknesses evident.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unsatisfactory level of organization with weaknesses evident in all areas.</td><td>0–2</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Expression [8 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent, effective and error-free use of language.</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Some ability to use language effectively and accurately, but there are inconsistencies in accurate usage.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frequent inaccurate use of language OR insufficient information presented.</td><td>1–2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Incomprehensible response OR no response.</td><td>0</td></tr>\n</table><h4>Model answer</h4><p>Student wellbeing has become an urgent concern in Caribbean schools. As Student Council president presenting our \"Calm Room\" proposal to the Board of Management, my team must persuade a cautious, high-status audience. Our plan therefore rests on credible and timely message points, appropriate language and clear visual support.</p>\n<p>Our first message point will use ethos: \"As your elected Student Council, we surveyed 412 students, and our counsellor, Mrs Hodge, has agreed to supervise the room through a sign-in system.\" This builds credibility because it shows that the proposal is researched and endorsed by a trusted professional, and it directly answers the fear that students will skip class. Our second point will use kairos: \"With CSEC and CAPE examinations only months away, student stress is at its peak, so now is the moment to act.\" This appeals to timing, showing the board that delay would hurt students when support matters most.</p>\n<p>We will use a formal register because the board is an official decision-making body whose members, including a Ministry official, hold authority over us. Formality shows respect and that we take the matter seriously. Our dialectal variety will be Standard English, since it is the expected variety for official meetings and will be understood by every board member, including any whose first language is not English.</p>\n<p>Finally, we will use a PowerPoint presentation displayed on the boardroom's smart TV. Slides can present our survey results as simple charts and show a floor plan of the proposed room, making our evidence easy to grasp and helping us keep to ten minutes.</p>\n<p>With these choices, our team can deliver a proposal that is credible, timely and professional.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_2bq","title":"Paper 02 · Practice Paper B","desc":"<p><b>READ THE FOLLOWING INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY.</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>This paper consists of THREE questions: Section A (Module 1), Section B (Module 2) and Section C (Module 3).</li>\n<li>Answer ALL questions.</li>\n<li>Each question is worth 25 marks: Content 10, Organization 7, Expression 8.</li>\n<li>Questions 1 and 2 must be answered in NO MORE than 500 words; Question 3 in NO MORE than 300 words.</li>\n<li>You are advised to take some time to read through the paper and plan your answers.</li>\n<li>Time allowed: 2 hours 30 minutes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><i>All extracts, scenarios, names, organisations and statistics in this practice paper were written for practice purposes.</i></p>","minutes":150,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Module 1","q":"<p><b>SECTION A · MODULE 1 – GATHERING AND PROCESSING INFORMATION</b></p>\n<p>1. Read the extract below carefully and then answer the question that follows.</p>\n<h4 style=\"text-align:center\">The Plate We Import</h4>\n<p>Walk through any supermarket in the northeastern Caribbean and play a simple game: try to fill a basket with food grown within fifty miles. You will leave with a few bunches of callaloo, some limes, perhaps a bag of locally caught fish, and very little else.</p>\n<p>The numbers behind that basket are sobering. According to the Eastern Caribbean Food Policy Unit's 2023 report, small island states in our subregion import between 70 and 90 per cent of the food they eat. In 2022 alone, the Unit estimated that the subregion spent more than US$1.2 billion on food imports, money that left our economies and never returned.</p>\n<p>This dependence is not only an economic problem; it is a security problem. When a hurricane closes a port, when a global price spike doubles the cost of flour, or when a shipping line cancels a route, our plates empty faster than our pockets. The pandemic years taught us this lesson painfully, yet we seem determined to forget it.</p>\n<p>Some argue that small islands simply lack the land to feed themselves. That argument confuses \"everything\" with \"more\". No one is suggesting that Jamaica will grow its own wheat. But hydroponic units on rooftops, school gardens, community plots on unused government land and modern fish farming can supply far more fresh produce than we do today. In Barbados, a pilot programme supported 300 backyard growers who together produced an estimated 40 tonnes of vegetables in a single year.</p>\n<p>Others claim that young people are not interested in farming. Yet farming in the twenty-first century looks very different from the back-breaking image in our grandparents' memories. It involves sensors, apps, drip irrigation and marketing on social media. Show a teenager a tablet-controlled greenhouse and watch how quickly \"farming\" becomes \"agri-tech\".</p>\n<p>What is missing is not land or talent but will. Governments must offer duty-free status on growing equipment, guarantee that a share of school meals is bought locally and train extension officers for the modern era. Consumers, too, must choose local whenever they can.</p>\n<p>Every meal is a decision. For too long, we have let others make it for us.</p>\n<p><small>SOURCE: Adapted from Dr Anselm Lake (2024). \"The Plate We Import\". <i>Caribbean Policy Review</i>, Issue 18, March 2024.</small></p>\n<p><small><i>Dr Anselm Lake is an agricultural economist who lectures at a regional university and advises two Caribbean governments on food security policy. He is also a director of a company that manufactures hydroponic growing systems.</i></small></p><p>Write an essay, in NO MORE than 500 words, in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>state the writer's MAIN message in no more than 30 words</li>\n<li>state the writer's purpose</li>\n<li>discuss TWO organizational strategies OR TWO language techniques OR any combination of both which the writer uses to achieve the purpose</li>\n<li>evaluate the credibility of the information presented.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>MARKING KEY · Question 1 (Content 10 marks)</h4>\n<p><b>• State the writer's main message [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks for a statement which effectively captures all of the points in the following. Award 1 mark for a statement which captures two of the points. Award no mark for a statement of the topic ONLY.</p>\n<blockquote>Caribbean islands' heavy dependence on imported food (1) threatens both their economies and their food security (2); with political will, modern local production can significantly reduce this dependence (3).<br><b>OR</b><br>Small islands import most of their food (1), leaving them vulnerable to shocks (2), but government incentives, agri-tech and consumer choice can increase local production (3).</blockquote>\n<p><b>• State the writer's purpose [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks for a response which has a clearly stated purpose verb and captures two other elements from the following. Award 1 mark for a purpose verb and one other element. Award no mark for a purpose verb without an acceptable explanation.</p>\n<p>The writer's purpose is to</p>\n<ul>\n<li>highlight/inform readers of the extent of food import dependence</li>\n<li>explain/show that this dependence is a security risk, not only an economic one</li>\n<li>refute the arguments that islands lack land and that youth are uninterested</li>\n<li>persuade/urge governments and consumers to support local food production.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Discuss TWO organizational strategies OR language techniques OR any combination [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks each for correct identification, example and explanation of any two organizational strategies or language techniques or combination thereof. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]<br>Award 1 mark for correct naming of ONE strategy or technique with example but no explanation.<br>Award 1 mark for correct naming of two or more strategies or techniques without example(s) or explanation(s).</p>\n<p><i>Organizational strategies</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Statistical data</b> – 70 to 90 per cent; US$1.2 billion; 300 growers and 40 tonnes. Used to show the scale of the problem and that the solution is realistic.</li>\n<li><b>Authoritative source</b> – the Eastern Caribbean Food Policy Unit's 2023 report. Used to suggest the figures are researched and reliable.</li>\n<li><b>Concession and refutation / anticipating objections</b> – \"Some argue that small islands simply lack the land...\"; \"Others claim that young people are not interested...\" Used to address the reader's doubts and overturn them, strengthening the argument.</li>\n<li><b>Cause and effect</b> – hurricane closes port, price spike, cancelled shipping lead to empty plates. Used to show why dependence is a security risk.</li>\n<li><b>Example / case study</b> – Barbados backyard-grower pilot. Used to show that the solution works elsewhere in the region.</li>\n<li><b>Problem and solution / call to action</b> – duty-free equipment, local purchasing for school meals, extension officers, consumers choosing local. Used to give the reader concrete steps.</li>\n<li><b>Direct address / opening scenario</b> – \"play a simple game\". Used to involve the reader personally from the start.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Language techniques</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Metaphor / imagery</b> – \"our plates empty faster than our pockets\". Used to make the abstract risk vivid and personal.</li>\n<li><b>Contrast / wordplay</b> – \"confuses 'everything' with 'more'\"; \"'farming' becomes 'agri-tech'\". Used to reframe the opposing argument and modernise the image of farming.</li>\n<li><b>Aphorism / short final sentences</b> – \"Every meal is a decision. For too long, we have let others make it for us.\" Used to end memorably and make readers feel responsible.</li>\n<li><b>Inclusive pronouns</b> – \"our economies\", \"we seem determined to forget\". Used to build solidarity with a Caribbean readership.</li>\n<li><b>Emotive diction</b> – \"sobering\", \"painfully\", \"back-breaking\". Used to stir concern and contrast old and new images of farming.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Evaluate the credibility of the information presented [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark each for a clear explanation of any two of the following reasons. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]</p>\n<p>The information may be considered credible because</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the writer is an agricultural economist, lecturer and government adviser, and is qualified to discuss food policy</li>\n<li>statistics are attributed to a named, dated regional source</li>\n<li>a regional case study (Barbados) supports the claims</li>\n<li>the article is published in a policy journal and is recent (2024)</li>\n<li>it agrees with what readers know about import dependence and pandemic shortages.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The information may be considered less than credible because</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the writer is a director of a company that manufactures hydroponic systems, and he recommends hydroponics and duty-free equipment, a conflict of interest</li>\n<li>the Barbados figure is \"estimated\" and the pilot programme is not named</li>\n<li>the claim about young people's attitudes is not supported by evidence</li>\n<li>the import range (70–90 per cent) is broad and not specific to Jamaica.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Organization [7 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent paragraph development with thematic cohesion, appropriate use of transitional devices and connectives, and an effective conclusion.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of the above criteria with one or two weaknesses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adequate level of organization with several weaknesses evident.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unsatisfactory level of organization with weaknesses evident in all areas.</td><td>0–2</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Expression [8 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent, effective and error-free use of language.</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Some ability to use language effectively and accurately, but there are inconsistencies in accurate usage.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frequent inaccurate use of language OR insufficient information presented.</td><td>1–2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Incomprehensible response OR no response.</td><td>0</td></tr>\n</table><h4>Model answer</h4><p>Since the pandemic, food security has moved from the margins to the centre of Caribbean policy debate. In \"The Plate We Import\", Dr Anselm Lake's main message is that <b>Caribbean islands' heavy reliance on imported food endangers their economies and security, but political will and modern local production can reduce it.</b> His purpose is to alert readers to the scale of the problem and to persuade governments and consumers to support local agriculture. He achieves this through concession and refutation and through vivid metaphor, although his credibility is partly weakened by a conflict of interest.</p>\n<p>One organizational strategy Lake uses is concession and refutation. He anticipates two objections: \"Some argue that small islands simply lack the land\" and \"Others claim that young people are not interested in farming.\" He then overturns each one, arguing that the goal is \"more\", not \"everything\", and that modern farming is \"agri-tech\". By answering the reader's doubts before they can harden, Lake makes his proposal appear realistic and well considered. This supports his persuasive purpose because the reader is left with fewer reasons to reject local production.</p>\n<p>In addition, Lake uses metaphor to make the risk personal. He warns that when ports close or prices rise, \"our plates empty faster than our pockets\". This image turns statistics about imports into something every reader can picture: an empty plate at home. The inclusive pronoun \"our\" strengthens the effect, suggesting that every Caribbean household is affected. As a result, the reader feels the urgency of the problem rather than merely understanding it, and is more likely to accept the call to action.</p>\n<p>The information is credible to some extent. Lake is an agricultural economist who lectures at a regional university and advises two governments, so he is well qualified. His statistics are attributed to a named, dated source, the Eastern Caribbean Food Policy Unit's 2023 report, and the Barbados pilot provides regional evidence. However, the author bio reveals that he directs a company that manufactures hydroponic systems, and he recommends both hydroponics and duty-free growing equipment. This conflict of interest means readers should question whether his solutions are entirely objective. Moreover, the Barbados figure is only \"estimated\" and the programme is not named, so it cannot easily be verified.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, Lake persuades effectively through refutation and imagery, but his business interests mean his recommendations should be read with some caution.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Module 2","q":"<p><b>SECTION B · MODULE 2 – LANGUAGE AND COMMUNITY</b></p>\n<p>2. Read the excerpt below carefully and then answer the question that follows.</p>\n<p><i>The following is an extract from a call-in segment of \"Talk Yuh Talk\", a morning radio programme in Barbados. The topic is whether newsreaders on local radio should be allowed to read some news items in Bajan.</i></p>\n<p><b>Host (Vernon):</b> Good morning, good morning! Lines are full this morning, people. First caller, you're on the air.</p>\n<p><b>Mrs Gumbs:</b> Good morning, Vernon. I'm a retired teacher, and I must say I am disappointed with this whole idea. The news is serious business. When I hear somebody reading the news and saying \"de gov'ment say dem going fix de road\", I feel ashamed. What will visitors think of us? We spent years teaching children to speak properly, and now radio wants to undo it.</p>\n<p><b>Host:</b> [pauses] Mm-hmm. So you feel it lowers the standard.</p>\n<p><b>Mrs Gumbs:</b> Exactly. The language of the news should be the language of education.</p>\n<p><b>Host:</b> Thank you, Mrs Gumbs. Next caller, go ahead.</p>\n<p><b>Dwayne:</b> Yo, Vernon, wha' happenin', boss! Respec' to de elders, but I hadda call in. Dis is we language, man. When my granny hear de news in big English, she does turn off de radio 'cause she cyan follow it. If dey read it how we does talk, everybody does understan'. Why we must sound like CNN to be serious?</p>\n<p><b>Host:</b> [laughing] Okay, okay, Dwayne, I hear you, bredda!</p>\n<p><b>Dwayne:</b> Is true, Vernon! In Jamaica and Trinidad dey have shows in dey own talk, and nobody dying from it.</p>\n<p><b>Host:</b> [lowers voice, more serious] Let's be fair to both sides. Mrs Gumbs is worried about standards, and Dwayne is worried about access. Maybe there is room for both. Let's take a short break and come right back with more of your calls.</p><p>Write an essay, in NO MORE than 500 words, in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identify, using ONE adjective in EACH case, the attitude to Bajan expressed by Mrs Gumbs and by Dwayne, supporting your answer with evidence from the excerpt</li>\n<li>explain ONE factor that may have shaped EACH caller's attitude</li>\n<li>identify and explain ONE communicative behaviour (verbal or non-verbal) used by the host to manage the discussion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>MARKING KEY · Question 2 (Content 10 marks)</h4>\n<p><b>• Identify, using ONE adjective in each case, the attitude of EACH caller, with evidence [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks EACH for an appropriate adjective with relevant supporting evidence from the excerpt. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]<br>Award 1 mark for an appropriate adjective without evidence, OR evidence without a clear adjective.</p>\n<p><i>Mrs Gumbs</i> – negative / disapproving / prescriptive / unfavourable / ashamed / purist</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"I am disappointed with this whole idea\"; \"I feel ashamed\"; \"What will visitors think of us?\"; \"speak properly\"; \"the language of the news should be the language of education\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Dwayne</i> – positive / favourable / proud / loyal / accepting / nationalistic</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Dis is we language, man\"; \"If dey read it how we does talk, everybody does understan'\"; \"Why we must sound like CNN to be serious?\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Explain ONE factor that may have shaped EACH caller's attitude [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks EACH for the identification and explanation of a relevant factor. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]<br>Award 1 mark for naming a factor without explanation.</p>\n<p><i>Mrs Gumbs</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Education / occupation</b> – as a retired teacher she was trained to correct Creole and promote Standard English as the language of schooling.</li>\n<li><b>Age / generation</b> – older speakers grew up when Creole was openly stigmatised in schools and public life.</li>\n<li><b>Concern for external image</b> – \"What will visitors think of us?\" reflects a tourism-dependent society's sensitivity to outsiders' judgements.</li>\n<li><b>Colonial legacy / prestige of the standard</b> – association of English with status, power and \"seriousness\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Dwayne</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Identity / nationalism</b> – sees Creole as \"we language\", a marker of belonging.</li>\n<li><b>Accessibility / family experience</b> – his grandmother cannot follow formal English news.</li>\n<li><b>Age / generation</b> – younger speakers are more likely to have been exposed to positive views of Creole.</li>\n<li><b>Exposure to regional media</b> – awareness that Jamaica and Trinidad use their Creoles on air normalises Creole in the media.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Identify and explain ONE communicative behaviour used by the host [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for identification with evidence and 1 mark for a reasonable explanation of its function. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Paraphrasing / summarising</b> – \"So you feel it lowers the standard\"; \"Mrs Gumbs is worried about standards, and Dwayne is worried about access.\" Shows active listening and neutrality.</li>\n<li><b>Code-switching / accommodation</b> – \"I hear you, bredda!\" to Dwayne versus formal \"Thank you, Mrs Gumbs.\" Builds rapport by matching each caller.</li>\n<li><b>Change of tone / paralinguistic cues</b> – \"[lowers voice, more serious]\"; \"[laughing]\"; \"[pauses]\". Signals a shift from banter to balanced moderation.</li>\n<li><b>Turn-taking management</b> – \"First caller, you're on the air\"; \"Next caller, go ahead\"; \"Let's take a short break\". Controls the flow of the programme.</li>\n<li><b>Back-channelling</b> – \"Mm-hmm\". Encourages the caller to continue.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Organization [7 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent paragraph development with thematic cohesion, appropriate use of transitional devices and connectives, and an effective conclusion.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of the above criteria with one or two weaknesses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adequate level of organization with several weaknesses evident.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unsatisfactory level of organization with weaknesses evident in all areas.</td><td>0–2</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Expression [8 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent, effective and error-free use of language.</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Some ability to use language effectively and accurately, but there are inconsistencies in accurate usage.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frequent inaccurate use of language OR insufficient information presented.</td><td>1–2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Incomprehensible response OR no response.</td><td>0</td></tr>\n</table><h4>Model answer</h4><p>Few topics provoke Caribbean audiences like the question of which language belongs in public life. In the \"Talk Yuh Talk\" excerpt, two callers debate whether Bajan should be used for radio news, revealing sharply opposed attitudes, while the host carefully manages the exchange. This essay will identify each caller's attitude, explain a factor that may have shaped it, and examine one communicative behaviour used by the host.</p>\n<p>Mrs Gumbs's attitude to Bajan is disapproving. She says she is \"disappointed with this whole idea\" and that hearing the news read in Creole makes her \"feel ashamed\". She also insists that \"the language of the news should be the language of education\", implying that only Standard English is suitable for serious matters. Her words clearly show that she considers the vernacular inferior.</p>\n<p>By contrast, Dwayne's attitude is proud. He declares, \"Dis is we language, man,\" and asks, \"Why we must sound like CNN to be serious?\" He views Bajan not as a fault but as a legitimate, even superior, means of reaching the whole community.</p>\n<p>Mrs Gumbs's attitude has likely been shaped by her occupation. As a retired teacher, she spent years \"teaching children to speak properly\", which suggests that she was trained to correct Creole and promote Standard English as the only acceptable classroom variety. Because her professional identity was built on this mission, she sees Creole on the news as undoing her life's work.</p>\n<p>Dwayne's attitude, however, appears to have been shaped by concern for accessibility rooted in family experience. He explains that his grandmother \"does turn off de radio 'cause she cyan follow it\" when the news is read in formal English. For him, language is therefore a question of who is included or excluded. His awareness that Jamaica and Trinidad broadcast in \"dey own talk\" further strengthens his view that Creole can be used publicly without harm.</p>\n<p>Finally, the host, Vernon, uses paraphrasing to manage the discussion. After Mrs Gumbs speaks, he restates her view: \"So you feel it lowers the standard.\" Later, with a lowered, more serious voice, he summarises both positions: \"Mrs Gumbs is worried about standards, and Dwayne is worried about access.\" This behaviour shows active listening, confirms that he has understood each caller, and keeps him neutral. As a result, a potentially heated exchange is calmed and listeners are invited to consider both sides.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, the excerpt shows how education and personal experience produce contrasting language attitudes, and how skilful paraphrasing allows a host to keep such a debate balanced.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Module 3","q":"<p><b>SECTION C · MODULE 3 – SPEAKING AND WRITING</b></p>\n<p>3. Read the scenario below carefully and then answer the question that follows.</p>\n<p>Following heavy rains, health officials in The Bahamas have reported a sharp increase in dengue fever cases, especially in densely populated New Providence neighbourhoods such as Bain Town, Grant's Town and Fox Hill. Many cases have been linked to mosquitoes breeding in water collected in discarded tyres, flower pots and uncovered drums.</p>\n<p>Your youth group, in partnership with a community council, has been asked to design a four-week campaign, \"Tip It, Toss It, Cover It\", to encourage households to remove standing water. Your target audience is heads of households aged 18 to 60 in these neighbourhoods. Many work long hours, some speak Spanish or Haitian Creole as a first language, and most use smartphones daily.</p><p>In an essay of NO MORE than 300 words, discuss the plan of action that your group will undertake as you prepare this campaign by</p>\n<ul>\n<li>analysing TWO characteristics of your target audience that would influence the design of your campaign</li>\n<li>explaining TWO message points related to logos or pathos appeals that you could use to persuade households to act</li>\n<li>justifying the use of ONE digital media channel to deliver your campaign to this audience.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>MARKING KEY · Question 3 (Content 10 marks)</h4>\n<p><b>(a) Analyse TWO characteristics of the target audience [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark each for identification of TWO relevant characteristics and 1 mark each for an explanation of how each would influence the campaign. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Long working hours / limited time</b> – messages must be short, simple and actionable; activities scheduled for evenings or weekends.</li>\n<li><b>Linguistic diversity</b> – materials in English, Spanish and Haitian Creole; heavy use of images and icons.</li>\n<li><b>Daily smartphone use</b> – campaign can be delivered digitally with reminders.</li>\n<li><b>Heads of households / parents</b> – responsible for children and elderly relatives, so protecting family is motivating.</li>\n<li><b>Residents of densely populated neighbourhoods</b> – neighbour-to-neighbour action and community clean-ups are effective.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>(b) Explain TWO message points related to logos or pathos [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark each for a clear identification of TWO message points related to either logos or pathos appeals. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]<br>Award 1 mark each for a clear explanation of how each message point relates to logos or pathos. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]</p>\n<p><i>Logos</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A single bottle cap of water can breed hundreds of mosquitoes in about a week.\" Explanation: a factual, logical reason to remove even small amounts of water.</li>\n<li>\"Health officials report that most dengue cases this year came from neighbourhoods with uncovered containers.\" Explanation: evidence from an authoritative source.</li>\n<li>\"Tipping containers takes five minutes a week; a dengue illness can mean a week or more off work.\" Explanation: logical comparison of cost and benefit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Pathos</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Your children play in that yard; don't let a flower pot put them in hospital.\" Explanation: arouses protective love and fear.</li>\n<li>\"Protect your neighbours as you would your family.\" Explanation: appeals to community pride and solidarity.</li>\n<li>Any other acceptable message point and relevant explanation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>(c) Justify ONE digital media channel [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for identification of any relevant digital channel and 1 mark for an acceptable justification. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]</p>\n<ul>\n<li>WhatsApp broadcast lists / community groups; Facebook community pages; Instagram or TikTok short videos; YouTube; SMS text blasts specified as digital.</li>\n<li>Justification may include: already used daily by the audience; can be read quickly between work shifts; supports images, voice notes and multilingual versions; allows weekly reminders; low cost; easy sharing between neighbours.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Organization [7 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent paragraph development with thematic cohesion, appropriate use of transitional devices and connectives, and an effective conclusion.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of the above criteria with one or two weaknesses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adequate level of organization with several weaknesses evident.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unsatisfactory level of organization with weaknesses evident in all areas.</td><td>0–2</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Expression [8 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent, effective and error-free use of language.</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Some ability to use language effectively and accurately, but there are inconsistencies in accurate usage.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frequent inaccurate use of language OR insufficient information presented.</td><td>1–2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Incomprehensible response OR no response.</td><td>0</td></tr>\n</table><h4>Model answer</h4><p>With dengue cases rising after heavy rains, our youth group must design a \"Tip It, Toss It, Cover It\" campaign that busy households will actually act on. Our plan begins with the audience.</p>\n<p>First, many heads of household work long hours, so our messages must be short and immediately practical: a weekly five-minute checklist rather than long explanations. Secondly, the audience is linguistically diverse, with many residents speaking Spanish or Haitian Creole. We will therefore produce every message in English, Spanish and Haitian Creole and rely heavily on simple images of tyres, pots and drums so that no one is excluded.</p>\n<p>Our first message point will appeal to logos: \"A single bottle cap of water can breed hundreds of mosquitoes in about a week.\" This fact gives a clear, logical reason why even small containers matter. Our second point will appeal to pathos: \"Your children play in that yard; don't let a flower pot put them in hospital.\" Because our audience is responsible for children and elderly relatives, this message stirs protective love and a healthy fear, motivating immediate action.</p>\n<p>To deliver the campaign, we will use WhatsApp community broadcast lists. Most of our audience already uses smartphones daily, and WhatsApp is the app they check between shifts. It allows us to send short videos, pictures and voice notes in all three languages, and to post a reminder every Saturday morning. Residents can also forward messages to neighbours at no cost, spreading the campaign quickly through each neighbourhood.</p>\n<p>By shaping our messages around the audience's time, language and habits, we can turn awareness into real action against dengue.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_2bu","title":"Paper 02 · Practice Paper C","desc":"<p><b>READ THE FOLLOWING INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY.</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>This paper consists of THREE questions: Section A (Module 1), Section B (Module 2) and Section C (Module 3).</li>\n<li>Answer ALL questions.</li>\n<li>Each question is worth 25 marks: Content 10, Organization 7, Expression 8.</li>\n<li>Questions 1 and 2 must be answered in NO MORE than 500 words; Question 3 in NO MORE than 300 words.</li>\n<li>You are advised to take some time to read through the paper and plan your answers.</li>\n<li>Time allowed: 2 hours 30 minutes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><i>All extracts, scenarios, names, organisations and statistics in this practice paper were written for practice purposes.</i></p>","minutes":150,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Module 1","q":"<p><b>SECTION A · MODULE 1 – GATHERING AND PROCESSING INFORMATION</b></p>\n<p>1. Read the extract below carefully and then answer the question that follows.</p>\n<h4 style=\"text-align:center\">Switched Off, Tuned In?</h4>\n<p>At 7:45 every morning, the students of a secondary school in Anguilla hand their phones to their form teachers, who lock them in a metal cabinet until the final bell. When the policy was introduced in September 2023, parents protested and students called it \"prison rules\". Eighteen months later, few want to go back.</p>\n<p>I spent two weeks at the school researching this article, and the change in atmosphere is striking. At lunch, students talk, play dominoes and argue about football instead of scrolling in silence. \"We actually know each other now,\" one fifth-former told me. Teachers report that lessons start on time because no one is finishing a message under the desk.</p>\n<p>The school's own data supports these impressions. Reported incidents of cyberbullying during school hours fell from 41 in the year before the ban to 9 in the year after. The average mark in internal mathematics examinations rose by six percentage points. The principal is careful not to claim that the ban alone caused this, but she believes it \"removed a constant distraction\".</p>\n<p>Critics raise fair objections. Phones are powerful learning tools; students research, photograph notes and use dictionary apps. Parents worry about reaching their children in an emergency, a real concern in a region that lives with hurricane season. And some argue that schools should teach responsible use rather than simply confiscate.</p>\n<p>Yet these objections can be answered without abandoning the policy. The Anguillan school provides shared tablets for research, and the front office will contact any student within minutes. A weekly digital-citizenship lesson teaches students to manage screen time. Banning phones during the school day, in other words, is not a rejection of technology. It is a decision about when and how technology serves learning.</p>\n<p>Across the Caribbean, ministries are watching. Before others follow, they should study the evidence carefully, consult students and parents, and invest in the alternatives that made this policy work. A phone ban without tablets, training and trust is merely a lock on a cabinet. With them, it may be the key to a more connected school.</p>\n<p><small>SOURCE: Adapted from Marisol Hodge-Carty (2025). \"Switched Off, Tuned In?\" <i>Regional Education Weekly</i> (online edition), 3 February 2025.</small></p>\n<p><small><i>Marisol Hodge-Carty is an education journalist based in the Leeward Islands. She holds a master's degree in educational research and has reported on schools across the OECS for twelve years. Her visit to the school was arranged by the school's principal.</i></small></p><p>Write an essay, in NO MORE than 500 words, in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>state the writer's MAIN message in no more than 30 words</li>\n<li>state the writer's purpose</li>\n<li>discuss TWO organizational strategies OR TWO language techniques OR any combination of both which the writer uses to achieve the purpose</li>\n<li>evaluate the credibility of the information presented.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>MARKING KEY · Question 1 (Content 10 marks)</h4>\n<p><b>• State the writer's main message [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks for a statement which effectively captures all of the points in the following. Award 1 mark for a statement which captures two of the points. Award no mark for a statement of the topic ONLY.</p>\n<blockquote>A school-day phone ban has improved interaction, behaviour and results at one Anguillan school (1), and objections can be answered (2), but other schools should copy it only with evidence, consultation and alternatives such as tablets and training (3).<br><b>OR</b><br>Banning phones during school hours can benefit learning (1) if it is supported by alternatives (2) and introduced after careful study and consultation (3).</blockquote>\n<p><b>• State the writer's purpose [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks for a response which has a clearly stated purpose verb and captures two other elements from the following. Award 1 mark for a purpose verb and one other element. Award no mark for a purpose verb without an acceptable explanation.</p>\n<p>The writer's purpose is to</p>\n<ul>\n<li>inform/report on the effects of one school's phone ban</li>\n<li>show that common objections to phone bans can be addressed</li>\n<li>advise/persuade Caribbean ministries to adopt bans only with evidence, consultation and supporting resources.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Discuss TWO organizational strategies OR language techniques OR any combination [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks each for correct identification, example and explanation of any two organizational strategies or language techniques or combination thereof. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]<br>Award 1 mark for correct naming of ONE strategy or technique with example but no explanation.<br>Award 1 mark for correct naming of two or more strategies or techniques without example(s) or explanation(s).</p>\n<p><i>Organizational strategies</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Narrative / descriptive opening</b> – phones locked in a metal cabinet at 7:45. Used to engage the reader with a concrete scene.</li>\n<li><b>First-hand observation</b> – \"I spent two weeks at the school\". Used to suggest the account is based on direct research.</li>\n<li><b>Testimonial / quotation</b> – \"We actually know each other now\"; the principal's \"removed a constant distraction\". Used to give voices from those affected and add authenticity.</li>\n<li><b>Statistical data</b> – cyberbullying incidents fell from 41 to 9; mathematics average up six points. Used to support the claimed benefits with measurable evidence.</li>\n<li><b>Before-and-after contrast</b> – \"prison rules\" versus \"few want to go back\"; scrolling in silence versus playing dominoes. Used to show the extent of change.</li>\n<li><b>Concession and refutation</b> – \"Critics raise fair objections...\" followed by \"Yet these objections can be answered\". Used to appear balanced and then strengthen the case.</li>\n<li><b>Problem and solution / recommendation</b> – ministries should study evidence, consult and invest in alternatives. Used to guide policy-makers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>Language techniques</i></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rhetorical question / wordplay in title</b> – \"Switched Off, Tuned In?\" Used to provoke curiosity and capture the paradox that removing phones increases connection.</li>\n<li><b>Metaphor / extended image</b> – \"merely a lock on a cabinet ... the key to a more connected school\". Used to show that the ban only succeeds with support.</li>\n<li><b>Antithesis / paradox</b> – \"not a rejection of technology. It is a decision about when and how technology serves learning.\" Used to reframe the debate.</li>\n<li><b>Hedging / cautious diction</b> – \"careful not to claim that the ban alone caused this\"; \"may be the key\". Used to appear objective and measured.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Evaluate the credibility of the information presented [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark each for a clear explanation of any two of the following reasons. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]</p>\n<p>The information may be considered credible because</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the writer is an experienced education journalist with a master's degree in educational research</li>\n<li>it is based on two weeks of first-hand observation</li>\n<li>specific figures and direct quotations are provided</li>\n<li>the writer acknowledges objections and avoids claiming direct causation, suggesting objectivity</li>\n<li>the article is recent (2025).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The information may be considered less than credible because</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the visit was arranged by the principal, who may have presented the school favourably</li>\n<li>the data is the school's own and has not been independently verified</li>\n<li>evidence comes from only one school, which may not be typical of the region</li>\n<li>the student quoted is unnamed, and only one student view is reported.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Organization [7 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent paragraph development with thematic cohesion, appropriate use of transitional devices and connectives, and an effective conclusion.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of the above criteria with one or two weaknesses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adequate level of organization with several weaknesses evident.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unsatisfactory level of organization with weaknesses evident in all areas.</td><td>0–2</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Expression [8 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent, effective and error-free use of language.</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Some ability to use language effectively and accurately, but there are inconsistencies in accurate usage.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frequent inaccurate use of language OR insufficient information presented.</td><td>1–2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Incomprehensible response OR no response.</td><td>0</td></tr>\n</table><h4>Model answer</h4><p>Smartphones in classrooms remain one of the most debated issues in Caribbean education. In \"Switched Off, Tuned In?\", Marisol Hodge-Carty's main message is that <b>a school-day phone ban improved behaviour and results at one Anguillan school, but others should adopt it only with evidence, consultation and supporting resources.</b> Her purpose is to report on the ban's effects and to advise ministries on how to introduce similar policies responsibly. She achieves this mainly through statistical data and concession and refutation, and her information is credible, though with limitations.</p>\n<p>The first strategy the writer uses is statistical data. She reports that cyberbullying incidents during school hours \"fell from 41 in the year before the ban to 9 in the year after\" and that mathematics averages rose \"by six percentage points\". These figures turn impressions into measurable outcomes, persuading policy-makers that the benefits are real. Importantly, she adds that the principal does not claim the ban \"alone caused this\", which makes the data appear honestly presented and supports her advisory purpose.</p>\n<p>Secondly, the writer uses concession and refutation. She openly admits that \"Critics raise fair objections\", listing phones as learning tools, emergency contact during hurricane season and the need to teach responsible use. She then answers each one: shared tablets for research, a front office that contacts students \"within minutes\", and weekly digital-citizenship lessons. This structure makes her appear balanced, while showing ministries exactly what must accompany a ban. Consequently, her final recommendation feels earned rather than one-sided.</p>\n<p>The information is credible to a large extent. Hodge-Carty holds a master's degree in educational research and has reported on OECS schools for twelve years, so she is qualified to judge school policy. She also spent two weeks observing the school, providing first-hand evidence and direct quotations. However, the bio reveals that her visit \"was arranged by the school's principal\", who may have shown the school in its best light. Furthermore, the statistics are the school's own and come from only one institution, so they may not apply across the region. Readers should therefore treat the findings as promising rather than conclusive.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, through clear data and a fair handling of objections, Hodge-Carty presents a persuasive yet measured case, although her evidence needs wider confirmation.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Module 2","q":"<p><b>SECTION B · MODULE 2 – LANGUAGE AND COMMUNITY</b></p>\n<p>2. Read the excerpt below carefully and then answer the question that follows.</p>\n<p><i>Shanice works at the front desk of a resort in Simpson Bay, Sint Maarten. The following exchanges were recorded, with permission, during one hour of her shift. Marlon is a co-worker from Sint Maarten; Mr Dalrymple is a guest from Scotland; Ms Clarke is a guest from Jamaica.</i></p>\n<p><b>Shanice</b> [to Mr Dalrymple]: Good afternoon, sir, and welcome to the resort. May I have your reservation number, please?</p>\n<p><b>Mr Dalrymple:</b> Aye, it's here somewhere. Sorry, lass, I'm a wee bit tired after the flight.</p>\n<p><b>Shanice:</b> No problem at all, sir. Take your time. Your room will be ready shortly. In the meantime, would you like a complimentary drink in the lobby?</p>\n<p><b>Mr Dalrymple:</b> That'd be grand, thank you.</p>\n<p><b>Shanice</b> [turning to Marlon, lowering her voice]: Marlon, 412 ready yet? Housekeeping still dey? De man vex tired, boy.</p>\n<p><b>Marlon:</b> Dem nearly done. Gimme two minutes. Wha' happen to you? You look like you ain' eat lunch.</p>\n<p><b>Shanice:</b> Cha, I ain' had a chance. Is one set a check-ins since twelve o'clock.</p>\n<p><b>Ms Clarke</b> [approaching the desk]: Hi, darlin'. Mi a check out today, but mi cyaan find mi key card. Mi did leave it inna di room mi tink.</p>\n<p><b>Shanice</b> [smiling]: No worries, ma'am, it happens all the time. Mi wi— I mean, I'll just print you a receipt. You can leave the card in the room. Did you enjoy your stay?</p>\n<p><b>Ms Clarke:</b> Yes, man! Di beach nice bad!</p><p>Write an essay, in NO MORE than 500 words, in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identify, with examples from the excerpt, TWO language registers used by Shanice and explain why EACH is appropriate to its context</li>\n<li>explain TWO possible reasons for Shanice's code-switching in the excerpt</li>\n<li>identify, with examples, ONE feature that shows dialectal variation among the speakers in the excerpt.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>MARKING KEY · Question 2 (Content 10 marks)</h4>\n<p><b>• Identify TWO language registers used by Shanice, with examples, and explain why each is appropriate [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks EACH for the identification of a register with a relevant example and an explanation of its appropriateness. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]<br>Award 1 mark for naming a register with an example but no explanation, OR for naming two registers without examples.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Formal / consultative register</b> – \"Good afternoon, sir, and welcome to the resort. May I have your reservation number, please?\"; \"Did you enjoy your stay?\". Appropriate because she is speaking to guests in a professional service role; polite, complete Standard English reflects the resort's image and the social distance between staff and strangers.</li>\n<li><b>Casual / intimate register</b> – \"Marlon, 412 ready yet? ... De man vex tired, boy\"; \"Cha, I ain' had a chance.\" Appropriate because she is speaking privately to a peer and co-worker; informality signals familiarity and saves time during a busy shift.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Explain TWO possible reasons for Shanice's code-switching [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks EACH for a reason with relevant explanation/evidence. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]<br>Award 1 mark for a reason stated without explanation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Change of addressee / audience</b> – she uses Standard English with guests and Creole with Marlon.</li>\n<li><b>Role and relationship</b> – professional role with guests versus solidarity with a fellow Sint Maartener and colleague.</li>\n<li><b>Privacy / exclusion</b> – lowering her voice and switching to Creole so that the tired guest does not fully understand her comment about him (\"De man vex tired\").</li>\n<li><b>Accommodation / convergence</b> – her near-slip into Jamaican-type Creole (\"Mi wi—\") reflects an instinct to converge with Ms Clarke's variety before she corrects to the expected professional variety.</li>\n<li><b>Emotional expression</b> – Creole conveys frustration and fatigue more naturally (\"Cha, I ain' had a chance\").</li>\n<li><b>Workplace norms</b> – the resort expects Standard English for guest interaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>• Identify, with examples, ONE feature showing dialectal variation among the speakers [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks for a correctly identified feature with relevant examples from at least two speakers. Award 1 mark for a feature with an example from only one speaker.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Lexical variation</b> – Scottish English \"aye\", \"lass\", \"wee\", \"grand\"; Jamaican Creole \"nice bad\" (very nice); Sint Maarten English \"vex tired\" (extremely tired).</li>\n<li><b>First-person pronoun</b> – Jamaican Creole \"mi\" (\"mi cyaan find mi key card\") versus Sint Maarten \"I\" (\"I ain' had a chance\").</li>\n<li><b>Negation</b> – Sint Maarten \"ain'\" (\"I ain' had a chance\") versus Jamaican \"cyaan\" (\"mi cyaan find\").</li>\n<li><b>Intensifiers</b> – \"vex tired\" (Sint Maarten), \"nice bad\" (Jamaica), \"wee bit\" (Scotland): each variety intensifies or softens differently.</li>\n<li><b>Past marking</b> – Jamaican \"did leave\" versus Sint Maarten \"ain' had\".</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Organization [7 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent paragraph development with thematic cohesion, appropriate use of transitional devices and connectives, and an effective conclusion.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of the above criteria with one or two weaknesses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adequate level of organization with several weaknesses evident.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unsatisfactory level of organization with weaknesses evident in all areas.</td><td>0–2</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Expression [8 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent, effective and error-free use of language.</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Some ability to use language effectively and accurately, but there are inconsistencies in accurate usage.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frequent inaccurate use of language OR insufficient information presented.</td><td>1–2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Incomprehensible response OR no response.</td><td>0</td></tr>\n</table><h4>Model answer</h4><p>The hospitality industry, the backbone of Sint Maarten's economy, brings together speakers from across the world. In the excerpt, Shanice, a front-desk worker in Simpson Bay, moves smoothly between styles and varieties as she deals with a Scottish guest, a Jamaican guest and a local co-worker. Her choices demonstrate how speakers adapt language to context. This essay will identify two registers she uses, explain two reasons for her code-switching, and examine one feature of dialectal variation among the speakers.</p>\n<p>Firstly, Shanice uses a formal register with guests. She greets Mr Dalrymple with \"Good afternoon, sir, and welcome to the resort. May I have your reservation number, please?\" The polite titles, complete sentences and Standard English are appropriate because she is acting in a professional role with a stranger, and the resort's reputation depends on courteous service.</p>\n<p>In contrast, she uses a casual register with her co-worker. She asks Marlon, \"412 ready yet? Housekeeping still dey?\" and complains, \"Cha, I ain' had a chance.\" This informal style, with clipped questions and an exclamation, suits a private exchange between colleagues and peers who share a home variety. It also saves time during a busy shift.</p>\n<p>One reason for Shanice's code-switching is a change of addressee and relationship. With guests she maintains professional distance in Standard English, but with Marlon she switches to Sint Maarten English to express solidarity and familiarity. Her language therefore marks who is \"staff\" and who is \"customer\".</p>\n<p>A second reason is privacy. When she turns to Marlon, she lowers her voice and says, \"De man vex tired, boy.\" By switching to Creole, she can comment on the guest's mood without him easily understanding, since a Scottish visitor is unlikely to be familiar with Sint Maarten English. Code-switching here functions as a way of excluding an outsider from the conversation.</p>\n<p>Finally, the excerpt shows lexical variation among the speakers' dialects. Each speaker uses a distinctive intensifier: Mr Dalrymple says he is \"a wee bit tired\", a Scottish English expression; Shanice describes him as \"vex tired\", meaning extremely tired in Sint Maarten English; and Ms Clarke praises the beach as \"nice bad\", meaning very nice in Jamaican Creole. Although all three speak varieties of English, their vocabulary reflects their different regions of origin.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, Shanice's shifts in register and code reveal a skilled communicator, while the guests' speech reminds us that English exists as many regional dialects.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Module 3","q":"<p><b>SECTION C · MODULE 3 – SPEAKING AND WRITING</b></p>\n<p>3. Read the scenario below carefully and then answer the question that follows.</p>\n<p>You have recently been appointed as a shift supervisor at Harbourline Logistics, a cargo-handling company at the port in Providenciales. The company employs 60 workers across three shifts, ranging in age from 19 to 63. Some workers are recent migrants whose first language is Spanish or Haitian Creole.</p>\n<p>Last month, a change in safety procedures for operating forklifts was posted only in a long English-language email sent at 11:00 p.m. Several workers did not read it, others misunderstood the technical terms, and two minor accidents followed. Workers now complain that \"management never tells us anything\", while managers complain that \"staff don't read their messages\". Your manager has asked you to recommend how communication can be improved.</p><p>In an essay of NO MORE than 300 words, discuss the plan of action that you will recommend to your manager by</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identifying and explaining TWO barriers to effective communication evident in the scenario</li>\n<li>proposing ONE strategy to overcome EACH barrier you have identified</li>\n<li>justifying ONE channel of communication that should be used for future safety announcements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","points":25,"fb":"<h4>MARKING KEY · Question 3 (Content 10 marks)</h4>\n<p><b>(a) Identify and explain TWO barriers to effective communication [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark each for identification of TWO relevant barriers and 1 mark each for an explanation linked to the scenario. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Language / semantic barrier</b> – the email was only in English; workers whose first language is Spanish or Haitian Creole could not fully understand it.</li>\n<li><b>Technical jargon</b> – workers \"misunderstood the technical terms\".</li>\n<li><b>Inappropriate channel / medium</b> – a long email is unsuitable for urgent safety instructions to shift workers who may not check email.</li>\n<li><b>Poor timing</b> – sent at 11:00 p.m., when many workers were off duty or asleep.</li>\n<li><b>Information overload / message length</b> – a \"long\" email discouraged reading.</li>\n<li><b>Attitudinal / relationship barrier</b> – mutual blame (\"management never tells us anything\" / \"staff don't read their messages\") shows mistrust.</li>\n<li><b>Lack of feedback</b> – one-way communication with no check for understanding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>(b) Propose ONE strategy to overcome EACH barrier [4 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 2 marks EACH for a relevant strategy clearly matched to a barrier and briefly explained. [2 + 2 = 4 marks]<br>Award 1 mark for a strategy that is relevant but not clearly matched or explained.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Translate key messages into Spanish and Haitian Creole; use pictograms and simple language.</li>\n<li>Replace jargon with plain language and demonstrate procedures physically.</li>\n<li>Deliver safety changes in person at the start of every shift.</li>\n<li>Send messages during working hours; give advance notice.</li>\n<li>Summarise in short bullet points or one-page visuals.</li>\n<li>Hold open forums / two-way meetings; appoint shift communication representatives to rebuild trust.</li>\n<li>Require sign-off or a quick verbal check of understanding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>(c) Justify ONE channel of communication [2 marks]</b></p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for identification of an appropriate channel and 1 mark for an acceptable justification. [1 + 1 = 2 marks]</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Face-to-face shift briefing / toolbox talk</b> – reaches every worker at the start of each shift; allows demonstration and immediate questions; supports interpreters; confirms understanding.</li>\n<li><b>WhatsApp group with voice notes and images</b> – familiar, immediate, multilingual, can be replayed.</li>\n<li><b>Illustrated multilingual posters at forklift stations</b> – permanent reminder at the point of use.</li>\n<li>Any other acceptable channel with a relevant justification.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Organization [7 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent paragraph development with thematic cohesion, appropriate use of transitional devices and connectives, and an effective conclusion.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of the above criteria with one or two weaknesses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adequate level of organization with several weaknesses evident.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unsatisfactory level of organization with weaknesses evident in all areas.</td><td>0–2</td></tr>\n</table>\n<h4>Expression [8 marks]</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Descriptor</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent, effective and error-free use of language.</td><td>8</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Very good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>7</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Good use of language though there may be a few lapses evident.</td><td>5–6</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Some ability to use language effectively and accurately, but there are inconsistencies in accurate usage.</td><td>3–4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Frequent inaccurate use of language OR insufficient information presented.</td><td>1–2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Incomprehensible response OR no response.</td><td>0</td></tr>\n</table><h4>Model answer</h4><p>At a busy port, a misunderstood safety message can cost more than time. As the new shift supervisor at Harbourline Logistics, I recommend the following plan to repair our communication.</p>\n<p>The first barrier is a language barrier. The forklift procedure was sent only in English and used technical terms, yet several workers speak Spanish or Haitian Creole as a first language. As a result, some read the message but misunderstood it, which contributed to two accidents. To overcome this, every safety notice should be written in plain language, translated into Spanish and Haitian Creole, and supported by simple pictures showing each step.</p>\n<p>The second barrier is an inappropriate channel and timing. A long email sent at 11:00 p.m. is unsuitable for shift workers, many of whom were off duty or asleep and may not check email at all. To solve this, safety changes should be announced during working hours and summarised in no more than five bullet points, with a requirement that each worker sign to confirm that they have understood.</p>\n<p>For future safety announcements, I recommend face-to-face briefings at the start of every shift. A ten-minute \"toolbox talk\" reaches all workers on all three shifts, allows the supervisor to demonstrate procedures on the actual forklift, and lets workers ask questions immediately, with a bilingual colleague interpreting where needed. This two-way channel also helps rebuild trust, since workers will no longer feel that \"management never tells us anything\".</p>\n<p>By removing these barriers and choosing a personal, interactive channel, Harbourline can keep its workers both informed and safe.</p>"}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 032 Specimen Papers","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_2es","title":"Paper 032 · Practice Paper A · 2026: Natural disasters (Homeowners)","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Communication Studies · Paper 032</b><br>2 hours 15 minutes · 60 marks</p>\n<p><b>Topic (2026):</b> Natural disasters &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Intended audience:</b> Homeowners</p>\n<p><b>READ THE FOLLOWING INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY.</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>This paper consists of TWO sections.</li>\n<li>Answer ALL questions in each section.</li>\n<li>Section A (20 marks) is based on the central stimulus. Read it carefully before you answer Questions 1 and 2.</li>\n<li>Section B (40 marks) requires an original reflective OR expository piece and a justification paragraph.</li>\n<li>You are advised to take some time to read through the paper and plan your answers.</li>\n<li>You may bring a single single sheet of double-sided A4 paper with no more than 10 points related to the topic of your response.</li>\n</ol>","minutes":135,"items":[{"t":"text","title":"Central stimulus","q":"<p><b>SECTION A</b></p>\n<p><i>Instructions: Read the extract below carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</i></p>\n<p><i>The following public notice was published in a Grenadian daily newspaper and on the Ministry's website at the start of the 2026 Atlantic hurricane season to inform homeowners of a new home-strengthening programme.</i></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><b>PUBLIC NOTICE: THE SAFE ROOF INITIATIVE</b></p>\n<p>From Monday 1 June 2026, the Ministry responsible for housing, in collaboration with the national disaster management agency, will launch the Safe Roof Initiative for owner-occupied homes in Grenada. Homeowners are invited to apply for a free structural inspection, during which a certified inspector will assess roof fastenings, window openings and drainage around the property. Eligible households will receive a voucher covering up to 75 per cent of the cost of hurricane straps and storm shutters, redeemable at approved hardware suppliers.</p>\n<p>Applications must be submitted through the Ministry's online portal, accompanied by photographs of the roof and a scanned copy of the property title or land-lease document. The deadline for applications is Friday 31 July 2026. Homeowners who are unable to apply online may obtain a paper application form at the Ministry's front office in St. George's or at designated community centres between 9:00 a.m. and 3:00 p.m. on weekdays.</p>\n<p>Homeowners are further reminded to register for the free emergency text-alert service by sending the word READY to 7233.</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1(a)","q":"<p>1. (a) Explain TWO challenges an elderly homeowner who lives alone in a hillside neighbourhood is likely to face in receiving the information in this public notice.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 1(a)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li>The homeowner may not buy a daily newspaper (cost, habit or no delivery to hillside areas) and therefore would not see the notice in print.</li>\n<li>The homeowner may have no internet access, smartphone or computer, or limited digital literacy, and so cannot view the Ministry's website or use the online portal.</li>\n<li>Poor eyesight may make small newspaper print or on-screen text difficult to read.</li>\n<li>The formal, technical register (\"owner-occupied\", \"structural inspection\", \"redeemable\", \"land-lease document\") may pose comprehension challenges even for those who can read.</li>\n<li>Many Caribbean people prefer orature and are more receptive to information delivered orally; a written notice may be overlooked.</li>\n<li>Living alone, the homeowner has no family member to pass on or explain the information.</li>\n<li>The homeowner's first language may not be English (e.g. Spanish- or Haitian Creole-speaking residents).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Award 2 marks each for TWO complete and clearly explained challenges which the homeowner may face in receiving the information.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for a clear identification of a challenge which is not explained.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 + 2 = 4 marks]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>One challenge is that the homeowner may not have internet access or the skills to use a computer. The notice is published on the Ministry's website and the application is done through an online portal, so an elderly person without a smartphone or data would simply never see it there.</p><p>A second challenge is the formal, technical language. Words such as \"structural inspection\", \"owner-occupied\" and \"redeemable\" may confuse an elderly reader who did not complete much formal schooling, so even if the homeowner sees the notice in the newspaper, he or she may not understand what is being offered.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1(b)","q":"<p>1. (b) Justify TWO appropriate traditional media, other than the printed medium, that may be used for communicating information about the Safe Roof Initiative to homeowners in hillside and outlying neighbourhoods.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 1(b)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li><b>Radio</b> – cheap to own and maintain; battery-operated sets work during power outages in hurricane season; reaches wide areas, including hillside communities; can be heard while the listener is busy with other tasks; the message can be repeated several times per day; call-in programmes allow homeowners to ask questions about the voucher.</li>\n<li><b>Television</b> – reaches most households; combines visual and aural stimuli so that an inspector can demonstrate what hurricane straps and shutters look like; message can be repeated during the news.</li>\n<li><b>Mobile loudspeaker / public address vehicle</b> – reaches homeowners where they live; suits those who prefer orature; effective in small, close-knit neighbourhoods; however, the message is brief and may be distorted.</li>\n<li><b>Word of mouth</b> (e.g. through community councils, churches, neighbours) – trusted and effective for those who prefer orature; quick among small groups; best corroborated by another medium because details such as deadlines may be distorted.</li>\n<li><b>Community or town-hall meetings</b> – face-to-face explanation; homeowners can ask questions and collect paper forms on the spot.</li>\n<li><b>Telephone</b> – short, personal calls to registered elderly homeowners; costly for large audiences.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Award 2 marks each for a complete and clear justification of TWO appropriate traditional media, other than the printed medium.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for only naming TWO appropriate traditional media other than the printed medium but without adequate justification.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 + 2 = 4 marks]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>Radio is an appropriate medium because almost every household, even in the hills, owns a radio, and battery radios still work when the electricity fails during hurricane season. The Ministry could repeat the announcement several times a day and invite homeowners to call in with questions about the voucher.</p><p>A mobile loudspeaker van is also appropriate. It can drive through neighbourhoods such as hillside communities and deliver the message directly to people at home, which suits elderly residents who prefer to receive information orally rather than by reading.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1(c)","q":"<p>1. (c) Comment on ONE local source of information which a homeowner could consult if he or she has questions about whether the roof of the house is strong enough to withstand a hurricane.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 marks]</p>","points":2,"fb":"<p><b>Question 1(c)</b> &nbsp;[2 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li>A certified building inspector or licensed contractor/engineer – trained to assess roof fastenings and familiar with local building codes.</li>\n<li>Staff at the Ministry's building permits/front office in St. George's – an official source that can explain the inspection criteria.</li>\n<li>Experienced staff at approved hardware suppliers – can advise on the correct type of strap or shutter for the roof.</li>\n<li>Community council members or Red Cross volunteers trained in disaster preparedness – accessible within the neighbourhood and socially aware.</li>\n<li>Testimonials from neighbours whose homes have already been inspected under the initiative.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Award 2 marks for a satisfactory explanation of ONE local source of information which a homeowner could consult.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for an acceptable identification of a relevant local source of information but without adequate explanation.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>The homeowner could consult a licensed local contractor. Contractors are trained in building codes and have repaired many roofs after past hurricanes, so they can inspect the fastenings and give reliable, practical advice.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2(a)","q":"<p>2. (a) Explain TWO reasons why the public notice by the Ministry uses the dialectal variety and the language register which it does.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 2(a)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>DIALECTAL VARIETY</b></p><p>Standard English/acrolect is used in the notice because</p><ul>\n<li>it is the official language variety used by the government for official communication</li>\n<li>there is an association of seriousness and authority with Standard English in messages to the public</li>\n<li>there is the assumption that most people understand written Standard English even if they do not speak it</li>\n<li>the notice involves legal documents (property title, land lease) where precision is needed.</li></ul>\n<p><b>LANGUAGE REGISTER</b></p><p>A formal register is used in the notice because</p><ul>\n<li>this register conveys the seriousness of hurricane preparedness</li>\n<li>the communication is official, from a Ministry and a national emergency unit, as opposed to casual</li>\n<li>precise technical terms (e.g. \"structural inspection\", \"certified inspector\") avoid ambiguity about eligibility and procedures.</li></ul>\n<p>Award 1 mark for correct identification of the use of Standard English dialectal variety and 1 mark for an acceptable explanation of the choice. [2 marks]</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for correct identification of the use of a formal language register and 1 mark for an acceptable explanation. [2 marks]</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for correct identification of dialectal variety and language register without explanation. [1 mark]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>The notice uses Standard English because it is the official variety used by the government for public communication, and it gives the notice authority so that homeowners take the programme seriously.</p><p>It uses a formal register because it is an official message from a Ministry and involves legal and technical matters such as property titles and structural inspections. Formal, precise wording reduces confusion about who is eligible and what documents are required.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2(b)","q":"<p>2. (b) Based on the public notice, list the TWO MOST important message points which an audience of homeowners would need to know.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 marks]</p>","points":2,"fb":"<p><b>Question 2(b)</b> &nbsp;[2 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li>From Monday 1 June 2026, homeowners can apply for a free structural inspection of their homes.</li>\n<li>Eligible households receive a voucher covering up to 75 per cent of the cost of hurricane straps and storm shutters.</li>\n<li>Apply through the Ministry's online portal with photographs of the roof and a copy of the property title or land lease by Friday 31 July 2026.</li>\n<li>Paper application forms are available at the Ministry's front office in St. George's or at community centres, 9:00 a.m. to 3:00 p.m. on weekdays.</li>\n<li>Register for free emergency text alerts by sending READY to 7233.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Award 1 mark each for TWO clearly stated message points which the audience would need to know.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><ul><li>Homeowners can get a free roof inspection and a voucher covering up to 75 per cent of the cost of hurricane straps and shutters.</li><li>Applications, with roof photos and the property title, must be submitted online or on a paper form by Friday 31 July 2026.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2(c)","q":"<p>2. (c) Create a slogan which you could use to encourage homeowners to strengthen their homes before the hurricane season, using ONE literary technique.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 2(c)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>POSSIBLE SLOGANS</b></p><ul>\n<li>\"Don't let your roof take flight – strap it tight tonight!\" – Use of <b>rhyme</b> (flight/tight/tonight) makes the message memorable and creates urgency to act now.</li>\n<li>\"Before the wind comes knocking, lock your home down.\" – Use of <b>personification</b> presents the hurricane as an unwelcome visitor, encouraging homeowners to prepare before it \"arrives\".</li>\n<li>\"Secure it, Shutter it, Sleep Safe.\" – Use of <b>alliteration</b> creates a catchy rhythm and links preparation with peace of mind.</li>\n<li>\"Is your roof ready to ride out the storm?\" – Use of <b>rhetorical question</b> prompts homeowners to reflect honestly on their home's condition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Any other relevant slogan accompanied by an explanation of the related use of a literary technique.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for a relevant slogan which could be used to encourage homeowners to strengthen their homes, 1 mark for inclusion of a literary technique used to appeal to the audience and 2 marks for the explanation of the technique which is linked to the issue of hurricane preparedness.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for an explanation which is unclear or incomplete.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>Slogan:</b> \"Don't let your roof take flight – strap it tight tonight!\"</p><p><b>Technique:</b> rhyme (\"flight\", \"tight\", \"tonight\").</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> The rhyme makes the slogan catchy and easy for homeowners to remember when they are in the hardware store. \"Tonight\" adds urgency, suggesting that preparation should not be delayed until a storm is already forecast, and \"take flight\" creates a vivid image of what can happen to an unsecured roof.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3(a)","q":"<p><b>SECTION B</b></p><p>3. (a) Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic <b>NATURAL DISASTERS</b>. Your piece should be suitable for an audience of <b>Homeowners</b>.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[25 marks]</p>","points":25,"fb":"<p><b>Question 3(a)</b> &nbsp;[25 marks]</p>\n<p>The candidate writes EITHER a reflective piece (for example, a personal essay, memoir extract or journal entry) OR an expository piece (for example, a journalistic article, feature or explanatory essay) of between 200 and 250 words on the topic <b>NATURAL DISASTERS</b>, suitable for an audience of homeowners.</p>\n<p><b>Possible content (not exhaustive):</b></p><ul><li>Reflective: a personal memory of a hurricane, flood or earthquake and its effect on a family home; lessons learned about preparation; the emotional cost of losing possessions; rebuilding and community spirit.</li>\n<li>Expository: how to prepare a home for hurricane season (straps, shutters, drainage, insurance, documents); why Caribbean homes are vulnerable; the role of building codes; how homeowners can protect important papers and plan evacuation.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Rubric for Creative Piece</b></p>\n<table><tr><th>Criteria</th><th>Marks to be awarded</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces an excellent sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>24–25 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces a very good sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>20–23 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces a good sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>15–19 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces an adequate sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>11–14 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces an unsatisfactory sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>6–10 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces a weak sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>0–5 marks</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Creativity factors (10):</b> unique/idiosyncratic use of genre; audience engagement; originality of ideas and events.</p>\n<p><b>Organisation factors (7):</b> introduction, development and conclusion; thematic/episodic coherence; labelled sections and subsections where appropriate.</p>\n<p><b>Expression factors (8):</b> appropriateness of register to genre; grammatical language; facilitative punctuation; careful proofreading.</p>\n<p><i>Teacher note:</i> place the script in a band first, then confirm the mark using the Creativity / Organisation / Expression split. A response that mixes genres without control, or that ignores the stated audience, should not normally be placed above the \"good\" band.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>What the Wind Taught Me</b></p>\n<p>For years, my grandmother's house was the strongest thing I knew. Its concrete walls had survived more storms than I had birthdays, so I assumed it would survive anything. I learned otherwise one September night, when the wind found the one weakness we had ignored: a corner of the roof that was nailed, not strapped.</p>\n<p>I remember the sound first. It was not a howl but a slow, grinding complaint, like a door being forced open by someone patient. Then the galvanise lifted, and rain poured into the room where my grandmother kept her photographs and her Bible. We spent the rest of the night in the bathroom, listening to the house argue with the sky.</p>\n<p>In the morning, the walls were still standing, but the inside of the house was ruined. A neighbour who had installed hurricane straps the year before had lost only a few shingles. The difference between our homes was not luck; it was a few hundred dollars and one Saturday afternoon of work.</p>\n<p>Now, every June, I think about that night. Preparing a home is not a sign of fear; it is an act of love for the people and the memories inside it. Shutters, straps and cleared drains cost far less than rebuilding a life. The wind does not negotiate, so every homeowner must decide, before the season begins, whether the roof will hold.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3(b)","q":"<p>3. (b) Based on your response in 3(a), write a paragraph of no more than 50–100 words justifying the following:</p><ul><li>The purpose</li><li>The intended audience</li><li>The context of communication</li></ul><p style=\"text-align:right\">[15 marks]</p>","points":15,"fb":"<p><b>Question 3(b)</b> &nbsp;[15 marks]</p>\n<p>The justification must relate directly to the candidate's own response in 3(a) and address all three elements.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Purpose:</b> e.g. to reflect on a personal experience of a natural disaster and, through it, persuade homeowners to prepare; OR to inform/explain how homeowners can protect property before a disaster. The candidate should link purpose to features such as vivid narration or step-by-step explanation.</li>\n<li><b>Intended audience:</b> homeowners (possibly a sub-group such as first-time homeowners, elderly homeowners or owners of older wooden houses), with reasons why the register, tone and content suit them (e.g. practical concerns about cost, family safety, property value).</li>\n<li><b>Context of communication:</b> where, when and how the piece would be shared, e.g. a Sunday newspaper feature or radio reading at the start of hurricane season (June), a Ministry social media page alongside the Safe Roof Initiative, or a community council meeting before the season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Rubric for Justification of Reflective/Expository Piece</b></p>\n<table><tr><th>Criteria</th><th>Marks to be awarded</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers an excellent explanation of how the piece is related to the candidate's stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated to the audience.</td><td>13–15 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a good explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>10–12 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a satisfactory explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>8–9 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a fair explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>5–7 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a weak explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>3–4 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers an unacceptable explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>0–2 marks</td></tr></table>\n<p><i>Teacher note:</i> a paragraph that addresses only two of the three elements cannot normally score above the \"fair\" band.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>My purpose was to reflect on a personal experience of hurricane damage in order to persuade homeowners to strengthen their houses before the season begins. The intended audience is Grenadian homeowners, especially those who believe that concrete walls alone are enough protection. The piece would be published in the Sunday newspaper and shared on the Ministry's social media pages in early June, at the start of hurricane season, when homeowners are deciding whether to apply for inspections and buy supplies. Its emotional, reflective tone suits readers who respond to lived experience.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_2f2","title":"Paper 032 · Practice Paper B · 2027: Non-communicable diseases in children (Parents)","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Communication Studies · Paper 032</b><br>2 hours 15 minutes · 60 marks</p>\n<p><b>Topic (2027):</b> Non-communicable diseases in children &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Intended audience:</b> Parents</p>\n<p><b>READ THE FOLLOWING INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY.</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>This paper consists of TWO sections.</li>\n<li>Answer ALL questions in each section.</li>\n<li>Section A (20 marks) is based on the central stimulus. Read it carefully before you answer Questions 1 and 2.</li>\n<li>Section B (40 marks) requires an original reflective OR expository piece and a justification paragraph.</li>\n<li>You are advised to take some time to read through the paper and plan your answers.</li>\n<li>You may bring a single single sheet of double-sided A4 paper with no more than 10 points related to the topic of your response.</li>\n</ol>","minutes":135,"items":[{"t":"text","title":"Central stimulus","q":"<p><b>SECTION A</b></p>\n<p><i>Instructions: Read the extract below carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</i></p>\n<p><i>The following public service announcement was sent home in printed form with primary school pupils and posted on the Ministry's social media pages to inform parents of a new school health screening programme.</i></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><b>HEALTHY KIDS, STRONG FUTURE: CHILDHOOD SCREENING PROGRAMME</b></p>\n<p>The Ministry of Public Health wishes to inform parents and guardians that, beginning Monday 12 April 2027, free health screenings will be conducted at all public and subsidised primary schools on Sint Maarten. Trained nurses will record each child's height, weight, blood pressure and blood-glucose level. Recent national data indicate that approximately one in four primary school pupils is overweight, placing these children at increased risk of Type 2 diabetes and hypertension in later life.</p>\n<p>Participation is voluntary. Parents must complete and return the attached consent form to the class teacher no later than Friday 26 March 2027. Results will be shared confidentially with parents only. Where readings fall outside the healthy range, families will be referred, at no cost, to a paediatric nutritionist at the nearest public health clinic.</p>\n<p>Parents are also invited to a free information evening, \"Lunchboxes That Love Back\", to be held in each school hall on the evening of Thursday 22 April 2027 at 6:00 p.m.</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1(a)","q":"<p>1. (a) Explain TWO challenges a working parent who commutes daily between the Dutch and French sides of the island is likely to face in receiving or acting on the information in this announcement.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 1(a)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li>The printed notice depends on the child delivering it; it may be lost, forgotten or damaged in the school bag, so the parent never receives it.</li>\n<li>Long working hours and a daily commute leave little time to read school notices or social media posts carefully, so the message may be overlooked or the 26 March deadline missed.</li>\n<li>The parent may be more comfortable in French, Spanish or another language than in English, making the notice difficult to understand.</li>\n<li>Technical medical terms (\"blood-glucose level\", \"hypertension\", \"paediatric nutritionist\") may pose comprehension challenges.</li>\n<li>The parent may not follow the Ministry's social media pages, so the online version will not appear in his or her feed.</li>\n<li>Work schedules may prevent attendance at the 6:00 p.m. information evening.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Award 2 marks each for TWO complete and clearly explained challenges which the parent may face.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for a clear identification of a challenge which is not explained.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 + 2 = 4 marks]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>First, the printed notice relies on the child bringing it home. Young children often forget papers in their bags, so a busy parent who leaves early and returns late may never see the notice or the consent form before the deadline.</p><p>Second, a parent who commutes from the French side may be more fluent in French than English. Medical terms such as \"blood-glucose level\" and \"paediatric nutritionist\" could make the English-only notice difficult to understand, so the parent may not realise the screening is free and important.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1(b)","q":"<p>1. (b) Justify TWO appropriate digital media or technologies that the Ministry could use, in addition to its social media pages, to remind parents of the consent-form deadline and the information evening.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 1(b)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li><b>WhatsApp class/parent groups or broadcast lists</b> – most Caribbean parents use WhatsApp daily; messages arrive directly on the phone and can be translated; voice notes suit parents who prefer oral communication; teachers can resend reminders close to the deadline.</li>\n<li><b>SMS/bulk text messaging</b> – reaches any mobile phone without data; short, direct reminders with dates; high open rates.</li>\n<li><b>Email from the school</b> – allows the consent form to be attached and printed or signed electronically; creates a record.</li>\n<li><b>School or parent-portal app with push notifications/calendar invites</b> – adds the dates directly to the parent's calendar with automatic reminders.</li>\n<li><b>Short video on YouTube/TikTok or the school website</b> – a nurse can demonstrate the screening, reassuring anxious parents; visual and aural appeal.</li>\n<li><b>QR code on the printed notice</b> linking to an online consent form – parents can respond instantly from their phones.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Award 2 marks each for a complete and clear justification of TWO appropriate digital media.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for only naming TWO appropriate digital media without adequate justification.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 + 2 = 4 marks]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>WhatsApp parent groups would be effective because nearly every parent uses WhatsApp daily on the phone. Teachers could send short reminders a few days before 26 March, and voice notes could be recorded in English and French for parents who prefer to listen.</p><p>SMS text messages would also work because they reach any mobile phone, even without data. A brief text such as \"Screening consent forms due Friday 26 March\" arrives directly and is likely to be read within minutes.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1(c)","q":"<p>1. (c) Comment on ONE local source of information which parents could consult if they have concerns about their child's weight or diet.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 marks]</p>","points":2,"fb":"<p><b>Question 1(c)</b> &nbsp;[2 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li>The family doctor/general practitioner or paediatrician – knows the child's medical history and can give personalised, confidential advice.</li>\n<li>The paediatric nutritionist at Collective Prevention Services – a free, official, qualified source named in the announcement.</li>\n<li>The school nurse or health educator – easily accessible at school and trained in child health.</li>\n<li>Community health fairs or church health ministries run by trained volunteers – accessible and trusted within the community.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Award 2 marks for a satisfactory explanation of ONE local source of information which parents could consult.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for an acceptable identification of a relevant local source but without adequate explanation.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>Parents could consult the paediatric nutritionist at the Collective Prevention Services clinic. This is a qualified, official source that offers free advice tailored to children, so parents receive accurate guidance without worrying about cost.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2(a)","q":"<p>2. (a) Suppose the Ministry decides to record a short radio version of this announcement for parents. Explain ONE reason why it should retain Standard English and ONE reason why it might use a more consultative (less formal) register in the radio version.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 2(a)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>DIALECTAL VARIETY</b></p><p>Standard English/acrolect should be retained because</p><ul>\n<li>it is the official variety associated with government and health authorities, lending credibility</li>\n<li>it is widely understood across the island's multilingual population, even by those who do not speak it</li>\n<li>it signals the seriousness of health risks such as diabetes and hypertension.</li></ul>\n<p><b>LANGUAGE REGISTER</b></p><p>A consultative (less formal) register might be used because</p><ul>\n<li>radio is an oral, intimate medium; a warm, conversational tone (\"As a parent, you want...\") engages listeners better than a read-out official notice</li>\n<li>simpler wording replaces technical terms (\"blood sugar\" for \"blood-glucose level\"), aiding comprehension</li>\n<li>a friendly tone reassures parents who may feel anxious or judged about their child's weight, encouraging participation.</li></ul>\n<p>Award 1 mark for correct identification of the Standard English dialectal variety and 1 mark for an acceptable explanation. [2 marks]</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for correct identification of a consultative/less formal register and 1 mark for an acceptable explanation. [2 marks]</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for correct identification of dialectal variety and register without explanation. [1 mark]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>The Ministry should keep Standard English because it is the official variety of government communication and is widely understood across Sint Maarten's multilingual population, so the message retains its credibility and seriousness.</p><p>However, it could use a consultative register on radio because listeners respond better to a warm, conversational voice. Saying \"blood sugar\" instead of \"blood-glucose level\" and speaking directly to parents would reassure those who feel anxious about their child's weight and make them more likely to sign the consent form.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2(b)","q":"<p>2. (b) Based on the public service announcement, list the TWO MOST important message points which an audience of parents would need to know.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 marks]</p>","points":2,"fb":"<p><b>Question 2(b)</b> &nbsp;[2 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li>Free health screenings (height, weight, blood pressure, blood glucose) begin at primary schools on Monday 12 April 2027.</li>\n<li>Return the signed consent form to the class teacher by Friday 26 March 2027; participation is voluntary.</li>\n<li>Results are confidential and shared only with parents; children outside the healthy range receive a free referral to a paediatric nutritionist.</li>\n<li>About one in four primary pupils is overweight and at risk of Type 2 diabetes and hypertension.</li>\n<li>Free information evening, \"Lunchboxes That Love Back\", Thursday 22 April 2027 at 6:00 p.m. in school halls.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Award 1 mark each for TWO clearly stated message points which the audience would need to know.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><ul><li>Free health screenings for primary school children begin on Monday 12 April 2027.</li><li>Parents must return the signed consent form to the class teacher by Friday 26 March 2027.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2(c)","q":"<p>2. (c) Create a slogan which you could use to encourage parents to pack healthier lunches for their children, using ONE literary technique.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 2(c)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>POSSIBLE SLOGANS</b></p><ul>\n<li>\"Pack it with love, not with sugar.\" – <b>Antithesis</b> sets love against sugar, suggesting that real care means healthy choices, not treats.</li>\n<li>\"Today's lunchbox is tomorrow's health.\" – <b>Metaphor</b> equates daily food choices with a child's future well-being.</li>\n<li>\"Fresh fruit, fit future.\" – <b>Alliteration</b> creates a catchy, memorable phrase linking fruit to long-term health.</li>\n<li>\"Swap the soda, save the smile.\" – <b>Alliteration and parallelism</b> give a simple, positive action and a reward.</li>\n<li>\"Would you fill their future with sugar?\" – <b>Rhetorical question</b> prompts parents to reflect on their choices.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Any other relevant slogan accompanied by an explanation of the related use of a literary technique.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for a relevant slogan which could encourage parents to pack healthier lunches, 1 mark for inclusion of a literary technique used to appeal to the audience and 2 marks for the explanation of the technique which is linked to the issue of non-communicable diseases in children.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for an explanation which is unclear or incomplete.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>Slogan:</b> \"Today's lunchbox is tomorrow's health.\"</p><p><b>Technique:</b> metaphor.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> The metaphor presents the lunchbox as the child's future health, suggesting that small daily choices add up to diabetes or wellness later. This appeals to parents' love and sense of responsibility, persuading them that packing water and fruit instead of soda and chips is an investment in their child's future.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3(a)","q":"<p><b>SECTION B</b></p><p>3. (a) Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic <b>NON-COMMUNICABLE DISEASES IN CHILDREN</b>. Your piece should be suitable for an audience of <b>Parents</b>.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[25 marks]</p>","points":25,"fb":"<p><b>Question 3(a)</b> &nbsp;[25 marks]</p>\n<p>The candidate writes EITHER a reflective piece (for example, a personal essay, memoir extract or journal entry) OR an expository piece (for example, a journalistic article, feature or explanatory essay) of between 200 and 250 words on the topic <b>NON-COMMUNICABLE DISEASES IN CHILDREN</b>, suitable for an audience of parents.</p>\n<p><b>Possible content (not exhaustive):</b></p><ul><li>Reflective: a parent's or older sibling's experience of a child's diagnosis (e.g. Type 2 diabetes, asthma); memories of food culture and changing family habits; guilt, fear and hope; the moment a family decided to change.</li>\n<li>Expository: causes of rising childhood obesity in the Caribbean (sugary drinks, fast food, screen time, reduced outdoor play); warning signs; practical strategies for parents (meal planning, water instead of juice, family activity); the role of schools and screenings.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Rubric for Creative Piece</b></p>\n<table><tr><th>Criteria</th><th>Marks to be awarded</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces an excellent sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>24–25 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces a very good sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>20–23 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces a good sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>15–19 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces an adequate sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>11–14 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces an unsatisfactory sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>6–10 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces a weak sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>0–5 marks</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Creativity factors (10):</b> unique/idiosyncratic use of genre; audience engagement; originality of ideas and events.</p>\n<p><b>Organisation factors (7):</b> introduction, development and conclusion; thematic/episodic coherence; labelled sections and subsections where appropriate.</p>\n<p><b>Expression factors (8):</b> appropriateness of register to genre; grammatical language; facilitative punctuation; careful proofreading.</p>\n<p><i>Teacher note:</i> place the script in a band first, then confirm the mark using the Creativity / Organisation / Expression split. A response that mixes genres without control, or that ignores the stated audience, should not normally be placed above the \"good\" band.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>The Sweetness We Called Love</b></p>\n<p>When I was nine, my mother rewarded every good report with a cold soda and a box of fried chicken. Sweetness was how our family said, \"I am proud of you.\" It never occurred to any of us that love could be measured in teaspoons of sugar.</p>\n<p>Then my younger brother, Jaden, began drinking water constantly and falling asleep in class. I remember my mother's face in the clinic when the nurse read out his blood-sugar level. She held the paper as if it were written in a foreign language. Type 2 diabetes, at eleven. That was a disease for grandparents, we thought, not for a boy who still played cricket in the yard.</p>\n<p>The months that followed were difficult. Jaden cried over the snacks he could no longer have, and my mother blamed herself. However, slowly, our kitchen changed. Juice became water with lime. Fried chicken became baked fish on Sundays. We began walking along the boardwalk together in the evenings, and somewhere along the way the walks became the reward.</p>\n<p>Today Jaden's readings are healthy, and I have learned that the habits we hand our children are as lasting as our surnames. To every parent reading this: the sweetness you offer at the table is not the only way to show love. Sometimes the most loving gift is a glass of water, a plate of vegetables and an hour of your time.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3(b)","q":"<p>3. (b) Based on your response in 3(a), write a paragraph of no more than 50–100 words justifying the following:</p><ul><li>The purpose</li><li>The intended audience</li><li>The context of communication</li></ul><p style=\"text-align:right\">[15 marks]</p>","points":15,"fb":"<p><b>Question 3(b)</b> &nbsp;[15 marks]</p>\n<p>The justification must relate directly to the candidate's own response in 3(a) and address all three elements.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Purpose:</b> e.g. to reflect on a family's experience of a child's NCD in order to encourage parents to change habits; OR to inform/explain causes, warning signs and prevention strategies. Purpose should be linked to features of the piece (narrative detail, emotive appeal, clear explanation).</li>\n<li><b>Intended audience:</b> parents and guardians (possibly of primary-school children), with reasons why tone and content suit them (e.g. non-judgemental tone avoids defensiveness; practical tips suit busy parents; emotional appeal draws on parental love).</li>\n<li><b>Context of communication:</b> e.g. read aloud at the \"Lunchboxes That Love Back\" information evening; printed in a PTA newsletter; posted on the Ministry's social media alongside the screening campaign; broadcast on a radio parenting programme.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Rubric for Justification of Reflective/Expository Piece</b></p>\n<table><tr><th>Criteria</th><th>Marks to be awarded</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers an excellent explanation of how the piece is related to the candidate's stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated to the audience.</td><td>13–15 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a good explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>10–12 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a satisfactory explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>8–9 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a fair explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>5–7 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a weak explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>3–4 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers an unacceptable explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>0–2 marks</td></tr></table>\n<p><i>Teacher note:</i> a paragraph that addresses only two of the three elements cannot normally score above the \"fair\" band.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>My purpose was to reflect on my family's experience of my brother's diabetes in order to persuade parents to rethink using sugary food as a reward. The intended audience is parents of primary school children, who may not realise that NCDs can begin in childhood; the gentle, personal tone avoids blaming them. The piece would be read aloud at the \"Lunchboxes That Love Back\" information evening and printed in the PTA newsletter during the screening campaign, when parents are already thinking about their children's health.</p>"}]},{"id":"assess_2fc","title":"Paper 032 · Practice Paper C · 2028: Single parenthood (Teenagers)","desc":"<p><b>CAPE Communication Studies · Paper 032</b><br>2 hours 15 minutes · 60 marks</p>\n<p><b>Topic (2028):</b> Single parenthood &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Intended audience:</b> Teenagers</p>\n<p><b>READ THE FOLLOWING INSTRUCTIONS CAREFULLY.</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>This paper consists of TWO sections.</li>\n<li>Answer ALL questions in each section.</li>\n<li>Section A (20 marks) is based on the central stimulus. Read it carefully before you answer Questions 1 and 2.</li>\n<li>Section B (40 marks) requires an original reflective OR expository piece and a justification paragraph.</li>\n<li>You are advised to take some time to read through the paper and plan your answers.</li>\n<li>You may bring a single single sheet of double-sided A4 paper with no more than 10 points related to the topic of your response.</li>\n</ol>","minutes":135,"items":[{"t":"text","title":"Central stimulus","q":"<p><b>SECTION A</b></p>\n<p><i>Instructions: Read the extract below carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</i></p>\n<p><i>The following extract is taken from a feature article published in a Vincentian weekend newspaper and shared on the newspaper's website. It introduces a new youth support initiative.</i></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><b>\"Not Alone\": New Programme Reaches Out to Teens in Single-Parent Homes</b></p>\n<p>Nearly half of the island's secondary school students live in households headed by a single parent, according to a recent survey conducted by the Department of Youth Affairs. While many of these young people thrive, counsellors report that a significant number carry responsibilities well beyond their years: minding younger siblings, preparing meals and, in some cases, taking part-time jobs to help with household bills.</p>\n<p>In response, the Department of Youth Affairs, in partnership with three secondary schools and a local youth charity, will launch \"Not Alone\" in September 2028. The programme offers weekly peer-support circles led by trained youth facilitators, free after-school homework assistance and confidential sessions with school counsellors. A dedicated helpline, 555-TEEN, will operate from 4:00 p.m. to 10:00 p.m., Monday to Saturday.</p>\n<p>\"Our aim is not to label any family,\" explained the programme coordinator. \"Single-parent households are often full of love and resilience. We simply want to make sure that no teenager feels they have to carry everything by themselves.\" Students may register through their school's guidance office or by scanning the QR code displayed on posters around the school.</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1(a)","q":"<p>1. (a) Explain TWO reasons why a teenager from a single-parent household might NOT receive or respond to the information in this article.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 1(a)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li>Few teenagers read weekend newspapers or visit newspaper websites; they get information mainly from social media, so the article may never reach them.</li>\n<li>Fear of stigma or embarrassment: a teenager may not want peers to know he or she lives in a single-parent home, and so may ignore or avoid registering.</li>\n<li>Heavy home responsibilities (minding siblings, part-time work) leave little time to read, attend support circles or visit the guidance office.</li>\n<li>Lack of a smartphone or mobile data may prevent scanning the QR code or reading the article online.</li>\n<li>The formal journalistic register and statistics may seem distant and unrelatable to teenagers.</li>\n<li>Distrust of adults/authority figures or concern about confidentiality may discourage response.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Award 2 marks each for TWO complete and clearly explained reasons.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for a clear identification of a reason which is not explained.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 + 2 = 4 marks]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>One reason is that most teenagers do not read weekend newspapers. They get their news from TikTok and Instagram, so an article in the newspaper or on its website is unlikely to reach them at all.</p><p>A second reason is the fear of stigma. A teenager may feel embarrassed about being seen as someone who \"needs help\" because of a single-parent home, so even after reading the article, he or she may avoid registering at the guidance office where classmates could notice.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1(b)","q":"<p>1. (b) Justify the use of ONE traditional medium AND ONE digital medium that the Department of Youth Affairs could use to promote \"Not Alone\" to teenagers.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 1(b)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Traditional media – acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li><b>School assembly presentation or drama skit</b> – reaches all students at once; a skit by peers makes the issue relatable without singling anyone out.</li>\n<li><b>Radio (youth programme/call-in show)</b> – popular with teens during commutes; a youth presenter can normalise the topic; listeners remain anonymous.</li>\n<li><b>Word of mouth through peer facilitators</b> – teenagers trust peers more than adults; personal recommendation reduces stigma.</li>\n<li><b>Television</b> – visual and aural appeal; testimonies from teens can be broadcast during youth programmes.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Digital media – acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li><b>TikTok/Instagram reels</b> – where teenagers spend much of their time; short, engaging videos with relatable creators; easily shared.</li>\n<li><b>WhatsApp/text broadcast</b> – private, direct; helpline number can be saved instantly.</li>\n<li><b>Podcast or YouTube series</b> – teens can listen privately to stories from peers in single-parent homes.</li></ul>\n<p>Award 2 marks each for a complete and clear justification of ONE appropriate traditional medium and ONE appropriate digital medium.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for only naming an appropriate medium without adequate justification.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 + 2 = 4 marks]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>Traditional:</b> A short drama skit at school assemblies would reach every student at once. Because it is performed by peers and shows the whole school, no individual teenager is singled out, which reduces the stigma of seeking help.</p><p><b>Digital:</b> TikTok would be effective because teenagers spend hours scrolling it daily. A short video featuring relatable youth facilitators and the 555-TEEN number can be watched privately and shared easily among friends.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1(c)","q":"<p>1. (c) Comment on ONE local source of information, other than the \"Not Alone\" programme, which a teenager could consult if he or she is struggling with responsibilities at home.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 marks]</p>","points":2,"fb":"<p><b>Question 1(c)</b> &nbsp;[2 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li>A trusted form teacher or sports coach – sees the student daily, notices changes and can refer to support.</li>\n<li>A church youth leader or pastor – a trusted, community-based adult, often with counselling experience.</li>\n<li>An extended family member (grandparent, aunt, uncle) – knows the family situation and may offer practical help.</li>\n<li>A social worker at the Department of Social Development – trained professional who can link the family to services.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Award 2 marks for a satisfactory explanation of ONE local source of information.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for an acceptable identification of a relevant local source but without adequate explanation.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>The teenager could talk to a church youth leader. Youth leaders are trusted adults in the community who often have counselling experience and already know the family, so the teen may feel comfortable sharing private concerns.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2(a)","q":"<p>2. (a) The article is written in Standard English and a formal register. Explain TWO reasons why a youth facilitator speaking to teenagers in a \"Not Alone\" peer-support circle might choose to use Vincentian Creole and a casual or intimate register instead.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 2(a)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>DIALECTAL VARIETY</b></p><p>Vincentian Creole/the vernacular might be used because</p><ul>\n<li>it is the home language of many teenagers and the variety in which they express emotion most naturally</li>\n<li>it signals solidarity and shared identity, reducing the social distance between facilitator and participants</li>\n<li>it avoids the \"classroom\" or \"official\" feel of Standard English, which may make teens feel judged or lectured.</li></ul>\n<p><b>LANGUAGE REGISTER</b></p><p>A casual or intimate register might be used because</p><ul>\n<li>the support circle is a small, private, trusting setting rather than an official one</li>\n<li>it encourages openness and honesty about sensitive family matters</li>\n<li>it positions the facilitator as a peer or friend rather than an authority figure.</li></ul>\n<p>Award 1 mark for correct identification of the Creole dialectal variety and 1 mark for an acceptable explanation of the choice. [2 marks]</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for correct identification of a casual/intimate register and 1 mark for an acceptable explanation. [2 marks]</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for correct identification of dialectal variety and register without explanation. [1 mark]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>The facilitator might use Vincentian Creole because it is the language many teenagers speak at home and use to express their feelings. Speaking it creates solidarity and shows the facilitator is \"one of them\", not a teacher giving a lecture.</p><p>A casual, intimate register would suit the small, private setting of a support circle. Relaxed, friendly language helps teenagers feel safe enough to open up honestly about difficult situations at home without fear of being judged.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2(b)","q":"<p>2. (b) Based on the article, list the TWO MOST important message points which an audience of teenagers would need to know.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[2 marks]</p>","points":2,"fb":"<p><b>Question 2(b)</b> &nbsp;[2 marks]</p>\n<p><b>Acceptable answers include:</b></p><ul>\n<li>\"Not Alone\" begins in September 2028, offering free weekly peer-support circles, homework help and confidential counselling.</li>\n<li>Call the helpline 555-TEEN, 4:00 p.m. to 10:00 p.m., Monday to Saturday.</li>\n<li>Register through the school's guidance office or by scanning the QR code on school posters.</li>\n<li>The programme does not label families; its aim is that no teenager should carry responsibilities alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Award 1 mark each for TWO clearly stated message points which the audience would need to know.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><ul><li>From September 2028, \"Not Alone\" offers free peer-support circles, homework help and confidential counselling.</li><li>Teens can call 555-TEEN (4–10 p.m., Monday to Saturday) or register at the guidance office or by scanning the QR code.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2(c)","q":"<p>2. (c) Create a slogan which you could use to encourage teenagers in single-parent homes to seek support, using ONE literary technique.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p>","points":4,"fb":"<p><b>Question 2(c)</b> &nbsp;[4 marks]</p>\n<p><b>POSSIBLE SLOGANS</b></p><ul>\n<li>\"Share the load, lighten the road.\" – <b>Rhyme</b> (load/road) makes the message memorable and suggests that life becomes easier when burdens are shared.</li>\n<li>\"Your backpack wasn't built for the whole house.\" – <b>Metaphor</b> presents home responsibilities as an overloaded backpack, a vivid, relatable image for students.</li>\n<li>\"Strong enough to stand, brave enough to ask.\" – <b>Parallelism</b> reframes asking for help as a sign of courage, countering stigma.</li>\n<li>\"Who said you had to carry it alone?\" – <b>Rhetorical question</b> challenges the belief that teens must cope by themselves.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Any other relevant slogan accompanied by an explanation of the related use of a literary technique.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for a relevant slogan which could encourage teenagers to seek support, 1 mark for inclusion of a literary technique used to appeal to the audience and 2 marks for the explanation of the technique which is linked to the issue of single parenthood.</p>\n<p>Award 1 mark for an explanation which is unclear or incomplete.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\">[4 marks]</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>Slogan:</b> \"Strong enough to stand, brave enough to ask.\"</p><p><b>Technique:</b> parallelism.</p><p><b>Explanation:</b> The two balanced phrases place standing strong and asking for help side by side as equal signs of courage. This appeals to teenagers' pride by suggesting that seeking support is brave rather than weak, which helps to reduce the stigma that might stop them from calling the helpline or joining a peer circle.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3(a)","q":"<p><b>SECTION B</b></p><p>3. (a) Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic <b>SINGLE PARENTHOOD</b>. Your piece should be suitable for an audience of <b>Teenagers</b>.</p><p style=\"text-align:right\">[25 marks]</p>","points":25,"fb":"<p><b>Question 3(a)</b> &nbsp;[25 marks]</p>\n<p>The candidate writes EITHER a reflective piece (for example, a personal essay, memoir extract or journal entry) OR an expository piece (for example, a journalistic article, feature or explanatory essay) of between 200 and 250 words on the topic <b>SINGLE PARENTHOOD</b>, suitable for an audience of teenagers.</p>\n<p><b>Possible content (not exhaustive):</b></p><ul><li>Reflective: growing up with one parent; a moment of realising a parent's sacrifice; taking on adult responsibilities; feelings of absence, resilience, gratitude or resentment; the role of extended family and community.</li>\n<li>Expository: common challenges for teenagers in single-parent homes (finances, time, emotional strain); myths and stigma; strengths of single-parent families; practical coping strategies; where teens can find support.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Rubric for Creative Piece</b></p>\n<table><tr><th>Criteria</th><th>Marks to be awarded</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces an excellent sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>24–25 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces a very good sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>20–23 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces a good sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>15–19 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces an adequate sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>11–14 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces an unsatisfactory sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>6–10 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate produces a weak sample in terms of creativity, organisation and expression.</td><td>0–5 marks</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Creativity factors (10):</b> unique/idiosyncratic use of genre; audience engagement; originality of ideas and events.</p>\n<p><b>Organisation factors (7):</b> introduction, development and conclusion; thematic/episodic coherence; labelled sections and subsections where appropriate.</p>\n<p><b>Expression factors (8):</b> appropriateness of register to genre; grammatical language; facilitative punctuation; careful proofreading.</p>\n<p><i>Teacher note:</i> place the script in a band first, then confirm the mark using the Creativity / Organisation / Expression split. A response that mixes genres without control, or that ignores the stated audience, should not normally be placed above the \"good\" band.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p><b>The Kitchen Light</b></p>\n<p>For most of my childhood, I measured my mother's day by the kitchen light. It was on when I woke up at six, because she was packing lunches before her shift at the hotel. It was on again at eleven at night, when she sat with a calculator and a pile of bills. I used to think the light simply never went off.</p>\n<p>My father left when I was four, so I do not remember a different kind of home. At school, however, I noticed the questions. \"Who's coming to parents' night?\" \"Why doesn't your dad pick you up?\" I learned to answer quickly and change the subject, as if our family were a secret instead of a story.</p>\n<p>At fifteen, I began minding my two younger brothers after school. Some afternoons I resented it. I wanted to be at football practice, not stirring rice and checking spelling homework. Then one night I came into the kitchen and found my mother asleep at the table, her head beside the calculator. In that moment, I understood that she was carrying far more than I was.</p>\n<p>Now I see our home differently. It was not \"broken\"; it was built by one person working twice as hard. To any teenager who lives under a light like mine: your family is not less because it is smaller. Still, you do not have to carry everything alone. Talk to someone. Even the strongest kitchen light needs to be switched off sometimes.</p>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3(b)","q":"<p>3. (b) Based on your response in 3(a), write a paragraph of no more than 50–100 words justifying the following:</p><ul><li>The purpose</li><li>The intended audience</li><li>The context of communication</li></ul><p style=\"text-align:right\">[15 marks]</p>","points":15,"fb":"<p><b>Question 3(b)</b> &nbsp;[15 marks]</p>\n<p>The justification must relate directly to the candidate's own response in 3(a) and address all three elements.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Purpose:</b> e.g. to reflect on growing up in a single-parent home in order to reassure teenagers and reduce stigma; OR to inform/explain the challenges and strengths of single-parent families and where to find help. Purpose should be linked to features such as personal narration, imagery or clear explanation.</li>\n<li><b>Intended audience:</b> teenagers (e.g. secondary students aged 14–18, particularly those in single-parent homes), with reasons why tone, voice and content suit them (e.g. a peer voice is more credible than an adult lecture; relatable school details; a hopeful, non-judgemental tone).</li>\n<li><b>Context of communication:</b> e.g. read aloud at the launch of a peer-support circle; posted as a spoken-word video on the \"Not Alone\" TikTok/Instagram page; published in a school magazine or youth page of the newspaper; shared during a guidance assembly at the start of the school year.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Rubric for Justification of Reflective/Expository Piece</b></p>\n<table><tr><th>Criteria</th><th>Marks to be awarded</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers an excellent explanation of how the piece is related to the candidate's stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated to the audience.</td><td>13–15 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a good explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>10–12 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a satisfactory explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>8–9 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a fair explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>5–7 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers a weak explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>3–4 marks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Candidate offers an unacceptable explanation of how the piece is related to the stated purpose, the intended audience and the context in which it is intended to be disseminated.</td><td>0–2 marks</td></tr></table>\n<p><i>Teacher note:</i> a paragraph that addresses only two of the three elements cannot normally score above the \"fair\" band.</p><h4>Model answer</h4><p>My purpose was to reflect on growing up in a single-parent home so that teenagers in similar situations feel understood and are encouraged to seek support. The intended audience is secondary school students, especially those carrying responsibilities at home; the personal, peer voice is more relatable to them than an adult lecture. The piece would be performed as a spoken-word video on the \"Not Alone\" Instagram and TikTok pages in September, during the programme's launch, when teenagers are scrolling and the helpline is newly available.</p>"}]}]},{"pkg":"Paper 032 Past Tasks 2016-2025","quizzes":[{"id":"assess_2fg","title":"Paper 032 · 2016 tasks","desc":"<p>In 2016 Paper 032 had three compulsory essay questions, one per module, each marked out of 20. The examiners described performance as a cause for serious concern: the share of candidates earning Grades I–III fell sharply from 14 per cent in 2015 to 4 per cent, and no candidate earned a Grade I. Most candidates could not write coherent summaries, evaluate information by referring to the writer's strategies, or analyse purpose, dialectal variation, register and communicative behaviours, and their content was poorly organised and expressed. The examiners again called urgently for proper training of private candidates and their teachers in every part of the syllabus.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Module 1: Summary and validity of a newspaper feature on youth crime","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2016 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract written in the style of a 2007 newspaper feature. The original extract is not reproduced in the subject report.</em></p>\n<h4>Saving Our Young Men from the Gun</h4>\n<p><b>Dr Marcia Ferreira-Joseph</b><br><em>Dr Ferreira-Joseph is a criminologist who has spent eleven years researching youth gangs in East Port of Spain. She lectures in criminology at a regional university.</em></p>\n<p>Every Monday morning, the headlines tell the same grim story. Figures released last month by the National Crime Monitoring Unit show that 41 per cent of persons charged with murder between January and September 2007 were under the age of 25, compared with 23 per cent in 2001. Behind each statistic is a young man who has stopped seeing a future for himself.</p>\n<p>My own interviews with 160 young offenders, conducted between 2004 and 2007, point to three recurring factors. Most had left school before the age of fifteen; more than two-thirds had no steady employment; and nearly half said that no adult in their household regularly asked where they were going at night. Into that vacuum steps the gang, offering money, protection and, most dangerously, a sense of belonging.</p>\n<p>The national response has been to call for longer sentences and more police patrols. These measures have their place, but they deal with the problem after the trigger has been pulled. A 2006 review of prison records found that six out of every ten young men released from custody were charged again within three years. Locking them up, on its own, is clearly not working.</p>\n<p>There is a better way. In Bellevue Heights, a community mentoring project started in 2005 by a church group and local businesses pairs at-risk boys with trained adult mentors and offers evening classes in trades such as welding and electrical installation. Of the 85 boys who have completed the programme, only seven have since come into contact with the police. The project's coordinator, Mr Desmond Alleyne, admits that the numbers are small and the programme is only two years old, but the early signs are encouraging.</p>\n<p>If we are serious about reducing crime, we must invest as heavily in keeping boys in school and in work as we do in police vehicles and prison cells. The gun offers our young men a future of sorts. We must offer them a better one.</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then answer the following.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write a summary of the extract in no more than <b>65 words</b>.</li>\n<li>In no more than <b>150 words</b>, evaluate the factors that help to establish the validity of the information in the extract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your response should be written in continuous prose, using Standard English.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p><b>Summary</b></p>\n<p>The writer reports that young men now commit a far larger share of serious violent crime in Trinidad. She traces this trend to early school-leaving, joblessness and weak parental supervision, which leave boys open to gangs offering money and belonging. Since harsher punishment has not stopped reoffending, she urges investment in mentoring, education and job training, <span>which have already produced promising results</span>.</p>\n<p><b>Evaluation of validity</b></p>\n<p>Several factors help to establish the validity of this information. Firstly, <span>the writer's credentials lend authority</span>: as a criminologist who has studied youth gangs for eleven years, she is qualified to explain why young men turn to crime. Secondly, her claims rest on specific, attributed evidence, such as figures from the National Crime Monitoring Unit and her own interviews with 160 offenders, so readers can verify her conclusions rather than simply trust them. Furthermore, the data were current when the article appeared, since they cover the period up to September 2007. Her balance also strengthens credibility, as she concedes that police patrols \"have their place\" and includes the coordinator's admission that the programme's numbers are small. However, <span>validity is slightly weakened</span> because she promotes a solution she clearly favours and offers only one community project as proof. Overall, the information is largely valid.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Summary: content</b> (6/6): All four key ideas are present, accurately paraphrased and free of personal comment, within 65 words.</li><li><b>Evaluation: content</b> (7/8): Four validity factors are identified, illustrated and explained, with one limitation weighed; one mark is lost because the point on currency is asserted rather than explained (why recent data matter for a crime trend).</li><li><b>Organisation</b> (3/3): Continuous prose with a clear topic sentence, ordered points, transitions (<i>Firstly, Secondly, Furthermore, However, Overall</i>) and a conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (3/3): Accurate Standard English with varied sentence structures, correct agreement and tense, and precise punctuation.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Module 2: Dialectal variation and communicative behaviours in a Nevisian humorous story","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2016 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract: an original humorous village story on the same kind of subject. The original extract is not reproduced in the subject report.</em></p>\n<h4>The Big Mosquito of Brown Hill</h4>\n<p>Everybody in Brown Hill village agreed that the rains of that October were the heaviest anyone could remember. What they could not agree on was the size of the mosquito.</p>\n<p>Miss Ivy was the first to see it. She came flying down the hill road one Saturday morning, her head-tie crooked and her slippers in her hand, and she did not stop until she reached the standpipe where the women were filling their buckets.</p>\n<p>\"Lawd, unu! Unu hear wha happen to me?\" she gasped, slapping her chest. \"One mosquito bite me last night. Me nah lie. De ting big so!\" She stretched her arms as wide as they would go.</p>\n<p>Miss Pearline sucked her teeth, long and loud, and kept on filling her bucket. \"Ivy, you always a mek up story. Mosquito cyaan big so.\"</p>\n<p>\"Me tell you wha me see! He did siddung pon me windowsill like he a wait fuh me. When he fly off, de whole house shake.\"</p>\n<p>By now a small crowd had gathered. Old Mr Cuthbert leaned on his cutlass and shook his head slowly. \"Is true, you know,\" he said in a low voice, and everybody turned to look at him. \"Me goat dem nah come home since Thursday. Me tink de mosquito carry dem gone.\"</p>\n<p>The children squealed and clutched one another. Young Jermaine, who was home from the high school in Charlestown for the weekend, rolled his eyes. \"Mr Cuthbert, mosquitoes cannot carry goats. They are insects. They weigh less than a gram.\"</p>\n<p>Miss Ivy put both hands on her hips and looked him up and down. \"Hear de big-school boy! He gone Charlestown two month and now he cyaan talk like we again.\"</p>\n<p>The women burst out laughing, slapping their thighs, and even Miss Pearline had to cover her mouth. Jermaine lowered his head and grinned.</p>\n<p>\"All right, all right,\" he said. \"But Miss Ivy, if dat mosquito did really big so, how come he nah bite you two time?\"</p>\n<p>\"Because me run, boy!\" she shouted, and the whole standpipe erupted again.</p>\n<p>Nobody ever found the mosquito. Mr Cuthbert's goats turned up a week later in his cousin's pasture across the ghaut, fat and unbothered.</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then answer the following.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write an analysis of the extract, discussing the <b>dialectal variation</b> and the <b>communicative behaviours</b> used by the characters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your response should be written in continuous prose, using Standard English.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p>The extract presents a comic village dispute in which the narrator uses Standard English while the villagers speak Nevisian Creole, and their language and non-verbal behaviour together create both humour and a strong sense of community.</p>\n<p>The dialectal variation is clearest in the grammar of the villagers' speech. Miss Ivy uses <span>\"me\" as a subject pronoun</span> (\"Me nah lie\"), the negator \"nah\" and the progressive marker \"a\" in \"he a wait fuh me\", all features of the basilect. Mr Cuthbert's \"Me goat dem\" shows the Creole plural marker \"dem\" placed after the noun, while \"Mosquito cyaan big so\" omits the verb \"be\", a zero copula. Pronunciation is also represented: \"de\", \"ting\" and \"tink\" reflect the stopping of the \"th\" sound. Jermaine's speech shows the widest variation. He first uses formal Standard English (\"They weigh less than a gram\"), but after being teased he <span>shifts towards the mesolect</span> with \"if dat mosquito did really big so\", a deliberate code-switch that restores his place in the group.</p>\n<p>The communicative behaviours reinforce these verbal messages. Miss Ivy slaps her chest and stretches her arms wide, gestures that exaggerate the mosquito's size and display her alarm. Miss Pearline's long, loud steups communicates disbelief without a word, and her refusal to stop filling her bucket signals dismissal. When Miss Ivy places her hands on her hips and looks Jermaine up and down, her posture and eye movement challenge his air of superiority, while his lowered head and grin show good-humoured submission. Paralinguistic features such as Mr Cuthbert's low voice, which draws everyone's attention, and the crowd's shared laughter and thigh-slapping also reveal the participatory, communal nature of the exchange.</p>\n<p>Overall, the dialectal features mark the speakers as members of one community, and the communicative behaviours turn a simple story into a lively shared performance.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Dialectal variation</b> (7/7): Accurate identification of grammatical, phonological and code-switching features, each illustrated and explained in context.</li><li><b>Communicative behaviours</b> (6/7): Kinesic and paralinguistic behaviours are identified and interpreted; one mark is lost because the final point on paralanguage is listed rather than fully linked to the characters' purpose.</li><li><b>Organisation</b> (3/3): Clear introduction, one paragraph per area of the task, logical sequencing and a linked conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (3/3): Fluent, accurate Standard English with apt subject terminology and varied sentence structures.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Module 3: Purpose, argument, audience and context of an academic article on peer culture","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2016 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract written in the style of an article from an adolescent psychology reader. The original extract is not reproduced in the subject report.</em></p>\n<h4>Rethinking Peer Pressure</h4>\n<p>Few phrases cause more anxiety among adults than \"peer pressure\". Parents fear it, teachers blame it and the media present it as the main reason teenagers smoke, drink or drop out of school. Yet a growing body of research suggests that this picture is incomplete and, in some ways, misleading.</p>\n<p>Adolescence is the period in which young people begin to separate their identity from that of their families. Turning towards friends is not a sign that something has gone wrong; it is a normal and necessary stage of development. Peers provide a testing ground where teenagers practise negotiating, resolving conflict and deciding what kind of person they want to be.</p>\n<p>Peer influence is also far from uniformly negative. A 2003 survey of 1,200 secondary students in four Caribbean territories found that students were more likely to report that friends had encouraged them to study, join a sports team or attend church than to report pressure to drink or skip class. Teenagers tend to choose friends who already share their values, so a group often strengthens existing habits rather than creating new ones.</p>\n<p>None of this means that harmful influence does not exist. Young people with weak family support, low self-esteem or few interests outside their group are especially vulnerable. However, the most effective response is not to forbid friendships or to lecture teenagers about the dangers of their peers. Studies of school peer-mentoring programmes show that when older students are trained to support younger ones, attendance and grades improve and reports of bullying fall.</p>\n<p>Adults, therefore, should stop treating the peer group as an enemy. Schools can create structured opportunities, such as clubs, peer counselling and student leadership roles, in which positive peer influence can flourish. Parents can come to know their children's friends instead of dismissing them. Policy makers can fund community youth spaces where adolescents meet under the guidance of trained adults. Working with the peer group, rather than against it, is the surest way to protect young people during these turbulent years.</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then answer the following.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In no more than <b>250 words</b>, write an essay in which you discuss the <b>writer's purpose and argument</b>, the <b>intended audience</b>, and <b>THREE suitable contexts</b> for the extract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your response should be written in continuous prose, using Standard English.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (20/20)</h4><p>In this extract, the writer's purpose is <span>to challenge the popular belief that peer pressure is mainly harmful</span> and to persuade adults to work with, rather than against, adolescent peer groups. The writer argues that turning towards friends is a normal stage of development, that research shows peers often encourage positive behaviour, and that structured peer programmes protect teenagers better than bans or lectures.</p>\n<p>Although parents are addressed, the intended audience is wider. The formal, academic register, the reference to a 2003 survey and the focus on \"school peer-mentoring programmes\" suggest that <span>teachers, guidance counsellors and psychology students</span> are the main readers. The final paragraph, which gives recommendations to schools, parents and policy makers in turn, confirms that the writer targets all adults responsible for young people's welfare.</p>\n<p>Consequently, the extract would suit several contexts. Firstly, it could be assigned as reading for a lecture on adolescent development at a teachers' college, where trainee teachers need research-based insight into student behaviour. Secondly, it would be appropriate at <span>a professional development workshop for school guidance counsellors</span> planning a peer-mentoring programme, since it provides evidence for such initiatives. Thirdly, a principal could present its main points at a Parent-Teacher Association meeting called to address concerns about student conduct, because it would reassure parents and give them practical advice.</p>\n<p>Overall, the writer's measured, evidence-based approach makes the extract valuable for any adult audience seeking to guide, rather than control, adolescents.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Purpose and argument</b> (6/6): Precise purpose verb and a clear, accurate statement of the writer's argument.</li><li><b>Intended audience</b> (4/4): Several audiences beyond parents are identified and each is supported with evidence from register, content and structure.</li><li><b>Contexts</b> (4/4): Three distinct, specific situations are given, each justified by the content of the extract.</li><li><b>Organisation</b> (3/3): Logical paragraphing that follows the task, with clear transitions and a conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (3/3): Accurate, concise Standard English with varied sentence structures, all within the word limit.</li></ul>"}]},{"id":"assess_2fk","title":"Paper 032 · 2017 tasks","desc":"<p>In 2017 Paper 032 again consisted of three compulsory essay questions, one per module, each marked out of 20. Results improved on 2016, with 37 per cent of candidates earning Grades I–III and 10 per cent earning Grade I, but the examiners still described performance as below par and said their 2016 comments applied equally. Most candidates could not separate main points from supporting details in a summary, evaluate the validity and reliability of information, or analyse purpose, dialectal variation, register, communicative behaviours and strategies with well-organised content. The examiners repeated their urgent call for proper training of private candidates and their instructors.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Module 1: Summary and validity of a newspaper feature on childhood obesity","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2017 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract written in the style of a 2007 newspaper feature. The original extract is not reproduced in the subject report.</em></p>\n<h4>Our Children Are Eating Themselves Sick</h4>\n<p><b>Sharlene Ramdeen</b><br><em>Ms Ramdeen is a registered dietitian with fifteen years of experience in paediatric nutrition. She chairs the Caribbean Association of Nutrition Professionals, which campaigns for greater use of local produce in school meals.</em></p>\n<p>Walk past any primary school at lunchtime and the problem is plain to see. Children who once carried roti or a pelau to school now queue for fried chicken, chips and a large soft drink. The results are showing up on the scale. The National School Health Survey, carried out by the Ministry of Health in 2006, found that one in every four children aged eight to eleven was overweight, compared with one in ten in 1996.</p>\n<p>Excess weight in childhood is not simply a matter of appearance. Doctors at the paediatric clinic of a major public hospital report that they are now treating children as young as nine for Type 2 diabetes, a disease once seen almost only in adults. Overweight children are also more likely to suffer from high blood pressure, joint pain and low self-esteem, and most of them will remain overweight as adults.</p>\n<p>The causes are not hard to find. Fast-food outlets have multiplied near schools, many parents work long hours and rely on convenience meals, and physical education has been squeezed out of a crowded timetable. Some people even claim that sales of soft drinks to children have doubled in five years.</p>\n<p>The solution must involve everyone. Schools should ban the sale of sugary drinks on their compounds and restore at least two periods of physical education a week. Parents can pack home-cooked lunches and limit screen time. Government can follow the example of a pilot programme in three Tobago schools, where lunches made with local fruit, vegetables and provisions were introduced in 2005; within a year, the share of overweight pupils there fell from 24 to 19 per cent.</p>\n<p>We cannot keep blaming the children. They eat what we make available to them. If we want a healthier generation, the adults must change first.</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then answer the following.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write a summary of the extract in no more than <b>65 words</b>.</li>\n<li>In no more than <b>150 words</b>, evaluate the factors that help to establish the validity of the information in the extract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your response should be written in continuous prose, using Standard English.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (20/20)</h4><p><b>Summary</b></p>\n<p>The writer warns that childhood obesity has risen sharply and now exposes children to adult illnesses such as diabetes and high blood pressure. She attributes the problem to cheap fast food near schools, busy parents and reduced physical activity. She therefore calls on schools, parents and government to share responsibility by offering healthier meals and more exercise, <span>as a successful school pilot suggests</span>.</p>\n<p><b>Evaluation of validity</b></p>\n<p>The validity of this information is supported by several factors. Most importantly, <span>the writer is a credible authority</span>; a registered dietitian with fifteen years' experience in children's nutrition is well placed to judge their diet and its effects. In addition, her central claim is backed by a named, official source, the Ministry of Health's 2006 survey, whose figures were recent when the article was published and therefore reflect the current situation. The Tobago pilot further strengthens her case, since its measurable results show that her proposed solution works in practice. Nevertheless, <span>reliability is reduced in places</span>. The claim about soft-drink sales rests on what \"some people\" say, and the hospital doctors are not named, so neither can be checked. Moreover, her association campaigns for local produce, which may have influenced her recommendations. On balance, the information is largely valid.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Summary: content</b> (6/6): All four key ideas are present, main points are separated from supporting details and everything is paraphrased within 65 words.</li><li><b>Evaluation: content</b> (8/8): Credibility, source, currency and evidence of results are each explained, and threats to reliability (vague attribution, possible bias) are weighed before a verdict.</li><li><b>Organisation</b> (3/3): Continuous prose with a topic sentence, logical sequence, transitions (<i>Most importantly, In addition, Nevertheless, Moreover, On balance</i>) and a conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (3/3): Accurate, varied Standard English with correct agreement, consistent tense and precise punctuation.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Module 2: Registers, dialectal variation, attitudes to language and communicative behaviours in a Trinidadian folk story","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2017 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract: an original village folk story on the same kind of subject. The original extract is not reproduced in the subject report.</em></p>\n<h4>Kavi and the Footprints</h4>\n<p>Kavi was the first to reach school on Monday, and he was bursting with news. Before Miss Pereira could finish writing the date on the board, his hand was waving in the air.</p>\n<p>\"Miss! Miss! It have douens by the river, Miss! Me and mih cousin see dey footprints yesterday. Dey feet turn backwards, Miss, I swear!\"</p>\n<p>The class erupted. Some children shrieked; others laughed and slapped their desks. Miss Pereira turned slowly from the board, put down her chalk and folded her arms.</p>\n<p>\"Good morning, Kavi. Firstly, we do not shout in this classroom. Secondly, the correct sentence is, 'There are douens by the river. My cousin and I saw their footprints.' You will never pass your examinations, or find a decent job, if you insist on speaking in that broken way.\"</p>\n<p>Kavi's eyes dropped to his desk. \"Yes, Miss,\" he mumbled. \"Sorry, Miss.\" For the rest of the morning he did not raise his hand again.</p>\n<p>That afternoon he trudged home and flung his bag on the gallery. His grandmother, Tanty Elsie, was shelling pigeon peas in the shade. She looked at his long face and stopped working.</p>\n<p>\"Wha wrong wid you, boy? You face long like a Monday.\"</p>\n<p>\"Teacher say I does talk broken, Tanty. She say I go never get a work.\"</p>\n<p>Tanty Elsie sucked her teeth, long and hard, and wagged a finger at the road as if the teacher were standing there. \"Broken? Is not broken nothing! Is we own language dat. Mih mother talk so, mih grandmother talk so, and dey raise good, good children. Doh mind she, eh. You must learn de English for school, yes, but doh ever shame for how we does talk home.\"</p>\n<p>Kavi nodded and sat close beside her on the step. After a while he asked quietly, \"Tanty, douen real?\"</p>\n<p>She leaned in until her face was almost touching his and lowered her voice. \"Real? Chile, when I was small, it had a little one by de river who used to call mih name in de night. Allyuh children doh believe nothing again.\" Then she threw back her head and laughed, and Kavi laughed too.</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then answer the following.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In no more than <b>300 words</b>, write an analysis of the extract, discussing the <b>language registers</b>, <b>dialectal variation</b>, <b>attitudes to language</b> and <b>communicative behaviours</b> evident in it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your response should be written in continuous prose, using Standard English.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p>The extract contrasts the formal language of the classroom with the Trinidadian Creole of home, revealing opposing attitudes to language and the non-verbal behaviours that accompany them.</p>\n<p>Miss Pereira uses a <span>formal register</span>, with Standard English, ordered markers such as \"Firstly\" and \"Secondly\", and she corrects Kavi as an authority figure would. In contrast, Tanty Elsie and Kavi use an <span>intimate register</span>, marked by affectionate address (\"boy\", \"Chile\") and personal storytelling.</p>\n<p>Dialectal variation is evident in the Creole grammar of their speech. The existential \"It have douens\" replaces \"There are\", the habitual marker \"does\" appears in \"how we does talk\", and \"go\" marks the future in \"I go never get a work\". Phonological features such as \"dey\", \"dat\" and \"wid\" show \"th\" stopping, while \"Doh\" is the Creole form of \"Don't\".</p>\n<p>The extract presents two attitudes to Creole. Miss Pereira regards it as \"broken\" and believes it will prevent Kavi from passing examinations or finding \"a decent job\", a negative attitude linking Standard English to success. Tanty Elsie rejects this, insisting \"Is we own language dat\", which shows pride in Creole as part of family identity, though she also accepts that Kavi must learn English for school.</p>\n<p>Communicative behaviours reinforce these messages. Miss Pereira's folded arms and deliberate pause convey disapproval, and Kavi's lowered eyes and mumbling show shame and submission. At home, Tanty Elsie's long steups and wagging finger express indignation, while her leaning in and lowered voice create intimacy and suspense as she tells her story.</p>\n<p>Overall, the two settings show that a speaker's register, dialect and gestures shift with the situation, and that Kavi's confidence depends on whether his language is valued.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Language registers</b> (4/4): Formal and intimate registers are correctly identified and supported with textual features.</li><li><b>Dialectal variation</b> (3/4): Grammatical and phonological Creole features are named precisely and quoted; one mark is lost because the phonological features are listed without comment on what they show about the speakers.</li><li><b>Attitudes to language</b> (3/3): Negative and positive attitudes are identified, supported and explained, with nuance.</li><li><b>Communicative behaviours</b> (3/3): Kinesics, eye contact, proxemics and paralanguage are interpreted in context.</li><li><b>Organisation</b> (3/3): One paragraph per element of the task, clear transitions and a linking conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (3/3): Accurate, fluent Standard English with apt terminology, within the 300-word limit.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Module 3: Central concerns, audience and purpose of a magazine feature on heritage languages","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2017 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract written in the style of a magazine feature. The original extract is not reproduced in the subject report.</em></p>\n<h4>The Language We Did Not Pass On</h4>\n<p>My mother spoke to her mother in Kwéyòl every day of her life. She spoke to me, however, only in English. When I asked her why, years later, she told me about the morning her teacher made her stand in the corner of the classroom with a sign around her neck because she had answered a question in \"patois\". \"I promised myself,\" she said, \"that no child of mine would ever be shamed like that.\"</p>\n<p>Her promise was made out of love, but it came at a price. When my grandmother grew old and her English faded, I could barely hold a conversation with her. Her stories, her jokes and her songs went with her when she died.</p>\n<p>My family's story is not unusual. Across the Caribbean, from Kwéyòl in St Lucia and Dominica to Bhojpuri in Trinidad, parents of my mother's generation chose not to pass on the languages they grew up with. Many believed that their children would only succeed if they spoke \"proper\" English.</p>\n<p>The research tells a different story. Linguists now agree that children who grow up with two languages do not fall behind; many of them develop stronger problem-solving and listening skills. A child can learn Standard English at school and still speak the language of her grandparents at home.</p>\n<p>Today I am raising two daughters, and I am trying to undo some of the damage. I have enrolled us in Saturday Kwéyòl classes at a community centre. We play recordings of old folk songs in the car, and their great-aunt speaks to them only in Kwéyòl when she visits. They laugh at my accent, but they are learning.</p>\n<p>So, to every mother and father reading this: your language is not something to hide. Speak it to your children at the dinner table, in the kitchen and at bedtime. Teach them the songs and the proverbs. The schools will teach them English. Only you can give them this.</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then answer the following.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In no more than <b>250 words</b>, write an essay in which you discuss the <b>central concerns</b>, the <b>intended audience</b> and the <b>writer's purpose</b> in the extract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your response should be written in continuous prose, using Standard English.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p>The extract explores the loss of Caribbean heritage languages across generations. Its <span>central concerns</span> are the shame that colonial-style schooling attached to Creole and other home languages, the cost of that shame to family relationships and cultural memory, and the possibility of reclaiming these languages today. The writer also challenges the belief that speaking a home language hinders success, citing research that bilingual children do not fall behind.</p>\n<p>The intended audience is primarily Caribbean parents, as the direct address \"to every mother and father reading this\" and the practical advice about dinner tables and bedtime show. The writer targets parents because they decide which language is spoken at home. However, the audience is wider. Readers of a magazine feature, especially young Caribbean adults who, like the writer, never learnt their family's language, would identify with her personal story, while <span>educators and grandparents</span> are implicitly addressed through the references to schooling and to older relatives.</p>\n<p>The writer's purpose is <span>to persuade Caribbean parents to pass on their heritage languages</span> to their children, by exposing the shame that silenced earlier generations and showing that bilingualism is an advantage. She achieves this through a moving personal anecdote that appeals to emotion, factual reference to linguistic research that appeals to reason, and her own example as a mother taking Kwéyòl classes, which makes the change seem achievable.</p>\n<p>Ultimately, the extract is a heartfelt call to action, urging readers to value their languages as a gift that only families can give.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Central concerns</b> (6/6): Four central concerns are identified accurately and supported from the extract.</li><li><b>Intended audience</b> (3/4): Parents and several wider audiences are identified and justified; one mark is lost because the claim that educators are addressed is asserted with only general support.</li><li><b>Writer's purpose</b> (4/4): A precise purpose verb with a clear object, linked to the strategies the writer uses.</li><li><b>Organisation</b> (3/3): Paragraphs follow the order of the task, with clear transitions and a conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (3/3): Fluent, accurate Standard English with varied sentence structures, within 250 words.</li></ul>"}]},{"id":"assess_2fo","title":"Paper 032 · 2018 tasks","desc":"<p>In 2018 Paper 032 had three compulsory essay-type questions, one per module, each worth 20 marks. The mean was 30.21 out of 60 (about 50 per cent). This was better than 42 per cent in 2017, but the examiners still described performance as a cause for serious concern. Their main message was that most candidates could not separate main points from supporting details in a summary, could not evaluate information, and could not analyse a sample of language for purpose, dialectal variation, register and communicative behaviours. They also called for better training of private candidates and of the people who teach them, across the whole syllabus.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Module 1: Summary, audience, purpose and contexts in an essay on biometrics","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2018 task)</b></p><h4>Fingerprints at the Gate: The Uses and Abuses of Biometrics</h4>\n<p>Across the region, biometrics are moving from science fiction to the supermarket. One commercial bank now lets customers withdraw cash with a fingerprint. Several governments are planning national identity cards that store a scan of the holder’s face. The appeal is obvious. Passwords are forgotten and cards are lost, but your thumb is always with you. You are, quite literally, your own key.</p>\n<p>Biometrics are excellent at one job: confirming that the person standing in front of a guard or a machine is who he claims to be. When a customs officer compares your face with the photograph in your passport, or a supervised scanner checks your fingerprint at an office door, the system works. Someone can see that a real finger, and not a rubber copy, is being pressed against the glass.</p>\n<p>The trouble begins when we treat biometrics as secrets. They are not. You leave your fingerprints on every glass you drink from, and your face is posted on social media many times a week. In one widely reported experiment, a fingerprint copied from a high-resolution photograph fooled four out of five low-cost scanners.</p>\n<p>Worse, a biometric cannot be changed. If a criminal steals your password, the bank issues you a new one in minutes. If a criminal steals the digital file of your fingerprint, what then? You cannot be issued a new thumb. The same fingerprint may also unlock your phone, your bank account and your workplace. A single breach of one poorly guarded database can therefore expose you everywhere at once.</p>\n<p>None of this means biometrics should be abandoned. It means they should be used wisely: as one part of a system that also uses cards, PINs and human supervision, and never as a single key that, once copied, opens every door. Before we hand our fingerprints to every bank, ministry and gym that asks for them, we should ask how they will be stored, who can see them, and what happens when, not if, something goes wrong.</p>\n<p><i>Adapted from an essay by Dr Marcus Ellery, information-security consultant, Caribbean Digital Rights Forum (online), 2019</i></p><p>Read the extract carefully and then, in essay form:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) summarize the key points of the extract;</li>\n<li>(b) identify the intended target audience and the features of the extract which suit this audience;</li>\n<li>(c) briefly discuss what the writer hopes to achieve;</li>\n<li>(d) briefly explain TWO suitable communication contexts in which the information could be disseminated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>The subject report does not give a word limit for this question. About 400–450 words is a sensible target.</i></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (20/20)</h4><p>In his essay on the uses and abuses of biometrics, Dr Marcus Ellery argues that fingerprint and face scans are valuable for confirming identity, but they become dangerous when they are treated as secret, all-purpose keys.</p>\n<p>The writer first concedes that biometrics are attractive because, unlike passwords or cards, they cannot be forgotten or misplaced. He explains that they work best when a person or a supervised machine checks them face to face. However, he maintains that they are not truly private, because people leave fingerprints on everyday objects and post their faces online, so biometrics can be copied. Moreover, a stolen password can be replaced but a stolen biometric cannot. Because one biometric is often used for many systems, a single breach exposes its owner everywhere. He therefore concludes that biometrics should be only one layer of security, and that citizens should question how their data will be stored and protected.</p>\n<p>The intended audience is ordinary Caribbean adults, particularly bank customers and citizens who are being asked to enrol in biometric systems, as well as the officials who are introducing them. Several features suit this audience. The writer uses familiar examples, such as withdrawing cash at a bank and having a passport checked by customs, so that non-specialists can relate to a technical issue. He replaces jargon with plain language and vivid images, for instance, “You cannot be issued a new thumb,” which makes the risk memorable. In addition, the inclusive pronoun “we” in the final paragraph presents readers and decision-makers as partners who share responsibility.</p>\n<p>Through this essay, the writer hopes to <span>caution the public against accepting biometric systems uncritically and to persuade institutions to adopt them only with proper safeguards</span>. He admits the strengths of the technology before exposing its weaknesses. This balance makes his warning seem reasonable rather than alarmist, and his closing questions are meant to prompt readers to demand accountability.</p>\n<p>Two other communication contexts would suit this information. Firstly, it could be presented on a radio call-in programme. Radio reaches a wide audience across age groups and islands, and listeners could phone in to ask how their own data is protected. Secondly, it could be delivered at a public consultation held by a government ministry before a national identity card is launched. In that face-to-face setting, the speaker could use slides to show how a fingerprint is copied, and citizens could question officials directly about safeguards.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, Ellery’s essay is a carefully reasoned warning, written for a lay Caribbean audience, that biometrics make a useful lock but a poor master key.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Summary — content</b> (4/4): All key points are present, paraphrased and kept separate from supporting details.</li><li><b>(b) Audience and suitable features</b> (3/3): A precise audience is given, and three features are each linked to that audience.</li><li><b>(c) Writer’s purpose</b> (2/2): Two precise purposes are given, with an explanation of how the essay achieves them.</li><li><b>(d) Two communication contexts</b> (3/3): Both contexts are named, and each one’s suitability for the audience is explained.</li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): It has an introduction, a body paragraph for each part and a conclusion, with clear topic sentences and transitions.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (4/4): It uses varied, complete sentences, accurate agreement and tense, and precise word choice.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Module 2: Registers, dialectal varieties and video presentation of a novel extract","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2018 task)</b></p><p>The new engineer had been on Bellfield Estate for less than a day when he lost his temper.</p>\n<p>‘Which of you is responsible for the mill?’ Mr Edward Hartley demanded, standing in the doorway of the boiling-house with his hat in his hand. ‘I was informed that the rollers would be cleaned before nightfall. They have not been touched.’</p>\n<p>The men at the coppers glanced at one another. Nobody answered. At length Joseph Barrett, the head cartman, wiped his hands on his trousers and stepped forward, keeping his eyes on the ground.</p>\n<p>‘Mi neva know seh is today yuh did want it, sah,’ he said. ‘Mass Charles always seh di rolla fi clean a Satideh.’</p>\n<p>Hartley frowned. ‘I beg your pardon? Who is Mass Charles? Speak plainly, man.’</p>\n<p>‘Him a di ole busha, sah. Him dead las’ crop.’</p>\n<p>‘I have no idea what a busha is, and I do not care what was done last crop. I am in charge now, and I expect my instructions to be carried out.’</p>\n<p>Behind the coppers, Miss Della, who sold cane-juice to the workers, sucked her teeth long and loud. ‘Hear him, nuh,’ she muttered to the girl beside her. ‘Him jus’ come, an’ a gwaan like him own di whole a Jamaica. Him cyaan even undastan’ wha di man a seh.’</p>\n<p>The girl giggled behind her hand. Hartley wheeled round.</p>\n<p>‘Is something amusing, madam?’</p>\n<p>Miss Della folded her arms and looked at him steadily, saying nothing at all. The silence in the boiling-house grew as thick as the steam.</p>\n<p>Joseph tried again, more carefully this time, choosing each word as though he were lifting it from a high shelf. ‘What I mean, sir, is that the old overseer always had the rollers cleaned on a Saturday. Nobody tell — nobody told us the order was changed. We will clean them tonight, sir.’</p>\n<p>Hartley’s shoulders relaxed a little, though his jaw remained set. ‘Very well. Tonight, then. And in future, Barrett, I should be obliged if you would tell me these things at once.’</p>\n<p>‘Yes, sah,’ said Joseph. But when the Englishman had gone, he turned to the others and shook his head slowly. ‘Unu see it? Dis one a go gi wi trouble.’</p>\n<p><i>Practice extract written in the style of an early twentieth-century Caribbean novel set on a Jamaican sugar estate in the 1890s</i></p><p>In no more than 300 words, write an analysis of the extract in which you:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>discuss TWO language registers used in the extract;</li>\n<li>discuss TWO dialectal varieties evident in the extract;</li>\n<li>comment on how a video presentation of the extract could highlight the miscommunication and tension of the scene.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p>The extract shows communication breaking down between Edward Hartley, a newly arrived English engineer, and the workers on a Jamaican sugar estate, largely because the two sides use different registers and dialectal varieties.</p>\n<p>Two registers are evident. Hartley uses a <span>formal register</span> throughout, in complete, carefully structured sentences such as “I should be obliged if you would tell me these things at once.” This impersonal style asserts his authority and keeps a social distance from the workers. In contrast, Miss Della uses a <span>casual register</span> when she mutters “Hear him, nuh” to the girl beside her. She is speaking to a peer in an unguarded, mocking tone that she does not intend Hartley to hear.</p>\n<p>Two dialectal varieties are also present. Hartley speaks Standard British English, while Joseph and Miss Della speak Jamaican Creole. Joseph’s first reply, “Mi neva know seh is today yuh did want it,” shows the Creole pronoun “mi”, the negator “neva” and the pre-verbal past marker “did”. Miss Della’s “a gwaan” uses the progressive marker “a”, and Joseph’s “Unu see it?” contains the plural pronoun “unu”. Significantly, Joseph shifts towards Standard English with “What I mean, sir”, showing that he can code-switch to repair the misunderstanding.</p>\n<p>A video presentation could highlight the miscommunication and tension effectively. Close-up shots could capture Joseph’s lowered eyes and Hartley’s set jaw, while a reaction shot of the girl giggling would reveal the workers’ hidden mockery. The director could also hold a long, silent pause after Hartley asks, “Is something amusing, madam?”, letting the steam and Miss Della’s folded arms convey hostility. Finally, the audible contrast between Hartley’s clipped accent and the rhythm of the workers’ Creole would make the language barrier immediately clear to viewers.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Two registers</b> (4/4): Both are correctly named, illustrated and explained by situation.</li><li><b>Two dialectal varieties</b> (4/4): Standard English and Jamaican Creole are both identified, with several accurate Creole features and a point about code-switching.</li><li><b>Video presentation</b> (4/4): Camera, pause and sound techniques are each linked to miscommunication or tension.</li><li><b>Organization</b> (3/4): The paragraphing and topic sentences are clear, but the answer ends on the video point with no closing sentence. <b>One mark was lost here.</b></li><li><b>Expression</b> (4/4): It uses accurate, fluent Standard English with precise linguistic terms.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Module 3: Audience, purpose and alternative contexts for an article on social media and mental health","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2018 task)</b></p><h4>Can Social Media Help Us Feel Better?</h4>\n<p>For years we have been told that our phones are making us miserable. Scroll through any feed and you will find the evidence: filtered holidays, perfect bodies and friends who always seem to be having more fun than you. It is no wonder that a 2018 survey of 1 200 secondary-school students across four Eastern Caribbean islands found that six in ten felt ‘worse about themselves’ after an hour online.</p>\n<p>Yet the same survey revealed something surprising. Nearly half of those students said they had first found the courage to talk about their anxiety after reading someone else’s story on Instagram or in a WhatsApp group.</p>\n<p>‘Social media is a tool, not a villain,’ says Andrea Mills-Baptiste, a clinical psychologist who runs a youth counselling service in Castries. ‘For a teenager in a small community, where everybody knows your business, an online support group can feel safer than walking into a counsellor’s office.’</p>\n<p>Across the region, young people are taking that idea seriously. In Barbados, a group of university students started the hashtag #TalkItOutBim. It now has thousands of posts in which young people share how they cope with exam stress, grief and loneliness. Some mental-health organisations have begun offering free chat sessions through their social media pages. Trained volunteers answer messages late into the night, when many young people feel most alone.</p>\n<p>None of this means we should spend more time online. Ms Mills-Baptiste recommends a simple rule: notice how you feel after you close an app. If you feel connected and encouraged, keep going. If you feel envious, anxious or empty, unfollow, mute or log off.</p>\n<p>Perhaps the question is not whether social media is good or bad for our mental health, but whether we are using it, or letting it use us.</p>\n<p><i>Adapted from a feature article by Renée Charles, ‘Health &amp; You’ section, The Weekend Chronicle, 4 August 2019</i></p><p>In an essay of no more than 250 words, discuss:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the intended audience of the extract;</li>\n<li>what the writer hopes to achieve;</li>\n<li>TWO alternative contexts through which the information could be communicated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (18/20)</h4><p>Renée Charles’s feature article on social media and mental health is aimed mainly at Caribbean teenagers and young adults, and also at the parents and educators who worry about them. The content suits this audience because it reflects their daily experience of “filtered holidays” and “perfect bodies”, cites a survey of secondary-school students in the region and mentions familiar platforms such as Instagram and WhatsApp. Moreover, the conversational tone, direct address and short paragraphs make it easy for young readers to follow.</p>\n<p>The writer hopes to <span>challenge the popular belief that social media only harms mental health</span> and to <span>persuade young readers to monitor how online activity affects them</span>. She achieves the first aim by setting the negative statistic against the “surprising” finding about online support. She achieves the second by ending with the psychologist’s practical rule and a thought-provoking final question.</p>\n<p>The information could also be communicated in two other contexts. Firstly, a guidance counsellor could present it at a school assembly or mental-health workshop. There, students could ask questions face to face and be referred to support services immediately. Secondly, it could become a short video series on the platforms the article discusses, in which young people describe how they cope. This context would reach the audience where they already spend time, and the peer-led format would make the message more believable.</p>\n<p>Ultimately, the article speaks to young people in their own space, urging them to use social media rather than let it use them.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Intended audience and suitability</b> (4/4): It gives a precise main and secondary audience, with several reasons from the extract.</li><li><b>What the writer hopes to achieve</b> (4/4): Two precise purposes are given, each linked to the techniques that achieve it.</li><li><b>Two alternative contexts</b> (3/4): Both are explained, but the workshop context is not linked to the article’s point about privacy in small communities, which was the strongest reason for choosing it. <b>One mark was lost here.</b></li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): It has an introduction with the audience, a paragraph for each sub-task and a conclusion, with clear transitions.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (3/4): It is accurate and fluent overall, but the long second sentence of the first paragraph packs in three pieces of evidence and loses some clarity. <b>One mark was lost here.</b></li></ul>"}]},{"id":"assess_2fs","title":"Paper 032 · 2019 tasks","desc":"<p>In 2019 Paper 032 again had three compulsory essay-type questions, one per module, each worth 20 marks, for a total of 60. The mean fell from 30.21 (50 per cent) in 2018 to 26.52 (44 per cent). The examiners concluded that neither the candidates nor their instructors seemed to know what the syllabus requires, and they said their 2018 comments applied just as much in 2019. The same weaknesses came up again: summaries that did not separate main points from supporting details, and weak analysis of purpose, dialectal variation, register, communicative behaviours and strategies. The examiners repeated their call for better training of private candidates and of the people who teach them.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Module 1: Summary, persuasive techniques and evaluation of an editorial on crime","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2019 task)</b></p><h4>Criminals Are Still Citizens</h4>\n<p>Every time a fresh murder makes the front page, the same cry rises from talk shows and street corners: lock them up and throw away the key. The anger is understandable. Families who have buried a son or daughter deserve justice, and nobody should pretend otherwise. But anger is not a policy, and a nation that forgets that criminals are people too will pay for that forgetfulness many times over.</p>\n<p>Consider the conditions in which we keep our prisoners. The island’s main correctional facility was built in 1912 to hold 400 men. Last month it held more than 1 100. Cells meant for two now hold six. Only one in five inmates has access to any education or skills programme. Is it any wonder, then, that the Department of Correctional Services reports that almost half of all released prisoners are back behind bars within three years?</p>\n<p>Some will say that prisoners have forfeited their rights and that money spent on them is money stolen from schools and hospitals. This newspaper disagrees. A man who leaves prison unable to read, without a trade and with nothing but the bitterness he learned in an overcrowded cell is not a problem solved; he is a problem postponed. Every one of these men will one day be our neighbour again. The only question is what kind of neighbour he will be.</p>\n<p>We have seen what the alternative looks like. Kevin, a former inmate who asked that his surname not be printed, learned welding during a short-lived pilot programme in 2015. Today he runs a small business and employs three young men from his community. ‘Somebody saw me as a person,’ he told us. ‘That changed everything.’</p>\n<p>Treating offenders humanely is not being soft on crime. It is being serious about it. We call on the Ministry of National Security to expand education and training in every prison, to reduce overcrowding, and to work with churches and employers to help released prisoners find honest work. Punishment may satisfy our anger; rehabilitation is what will make our streets safer.</p>\n<p><i>Adapted from an editorial, The Sunday Sentinel, 8 September 2019</i></p><p>Read the extract carefully and then, in essay form:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) summarize the issues which concern the writer in the extract;</li>\n<li>(b) explain TWO organizational strategies or language techniques which the writer uses to present his point of view;</li>\n<li>(c) giving THREE reasons, justify whether you consider the argument presented to be convincing or not.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><i>The subject report does not give a word limit for this question. About 350–400 words is a sensible target.</i></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p>In the editorial “Criminals Are Still Citizens”, the writer argues that public demands for harsher punishment ignore the inhumane conditions in prisons, and that only rehabilitation will reduce crime.</p>\n<p>The writer is concerned, first, that public anger after violent crimes is producing calls for revenge rather than sensible policy. The writer is also troubled by severe overcrowding and the lack of education programmes in prison, and links these to the high rate of reoffending. In addition, the editorial rejects the view that prisoners have lost all rights. It argues that offenders released without rehabilitation will simply return to crime in the community. Finally, the writer urges the government to expand training, ease overcrowding and help former inmates find honest work.</p>\n<p>One technique the writer uses to present this point of view is <span>statistical evidence</span>. The prison built for 400 men now holds “more than 1 100”, and almost half of released prisoners reoffend within three years. These figures support the writer’s claims with concrete evidence, so the concern appears factual rather than merely sentimental. A second strategy is <span>concession followed by rebuttal</span>. The writer first admits that grieving families “deserve justice” and acknowledges the objection that money spent on prisoners is “stolen from schools and hospitals”. Only then does the writer counter it with the claim that an unreformed prisoner is “a problem postponed”. This organization makes the writer seem fair-minded and answers opposing views before readers can settle on them.</p>\n<p>I consider the argument convincing for three reasons. Firstly, the evidence is specific and is attributed to an official source, the Department of Correctional Services, which makes it more credible. Secondly, the anecdote about Kevin, the former inmate who now employs three young men, gives a human face to the abstract idea of rehabilitation and shows that it can work in practice. Thirdly, the argument appeals to the self-interest of the newspaper’s readers, the general public, by reminding them that every prisoner “will one day be our neighbour again”. Even readers who feel no sympathy for criminals are therefore persuaded that rehabilitation protects their own safety.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, the editorial combines evidence, balance and emotional appeal to argue persuasively that treating criminals as people is not weakness but a practical route to safer streets.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>(a) Summary of the writer’s concerns</b> (4/4): The main issues are all captured concisely and paraphrased, with supporting details left out.</li><li><b>(b) Two strategies or techniques</b> (4/4): Two valid strategies are named, illustrated and clearly linked to how they present the writer’s view.</li><li><b>(c) Three reasons: convincing or not</b> (3/4): Three distinct reasons are given, but the second relies on a single anecdote without admitting that one success story is limited evidence, so the evaluation is a little less critical than it could be. <b>One mark was lost here.</b></li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): It has an introduction, a paragraph for each part and a conclusion, with clear topic sentences and transitions.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (4/4): It uses varied, accurate sentences, correct agreement and tense, precise vocabulary and controlled punctuation.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Module 2: Registers, Creole features and communicative behaviours in a short story","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2019 task)</b></p><h4>Not Quiet Enough</h4>\n<p>(1) When I first come to London I rent a room in a tall grey house near the station. The landlady, Mrs Pruett, is thin like a broomstick and she watch me every morning from behind her curtain. She don’t like my singing, she don’t like my cooking, and I think she don’t like me neither, but she like my money well enough.</p>\n<p>(2) One evening I feeling homesick, so I put on my yellow dress and I sing the old songs my granny teach me — loud, because what is the use of a song you keep inside? Next thing I know, somebody banging the door.</p>\n<p>(3) ‘Miss Joseph, I must ask you to stop that noise at once,’ Mrs Pruett say, very stiff. ‘This is a respectable house. Other tenants are entitled to their rest.’</p>\n<p>(4) I look at her straight, I fold my arms, and I tell her, ‘Is only a song, madam. Where I come from, people does sing when they sad. Nobody does call police for that.’ Then I laugh, because her face gone red like a pepper, and I turn my back and go on singing, softer this time, but still singing. I hear her suck in her breath and the door slam behind her. After that I don’t say one word to her for a whole week. When she pass me on the stairs, I look at the wall.</p>\n<p>(5) Two days later a policeman come. He take off his helmet and say, ‘Good evening, madam. We have received a complaint regarding a disturbance of the peace at this address. Would you mind telling me what occurred?’</p>\n<p>(6) I tell him everything, and he write in his little book, and all the time he looking at me like I is a puzzle he can’t solve. When he gone, I sit down on the bed and I cry, not because I frighten, but because nobody here understand that singing is how we does keep from falling down.</p>\n<p>(7) Later, in court, a man in a wig read out, ‘You are hereby charged that on the night of the fourteenth instant you did wilfully cause a disturbance.’ I don’t understand half the words, but I understand enough. In this country, it ain’t enough to be good. You have to be quiet too.</p>\n<p><i>Practice extract written in the style of a mid-twentieth-century short story narrated by a Caribbean woman living in London</i></p><p>In no more than 300 words, write an analysis of the extract in which you:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>discuss the language registers used in the passage;</li>\n<li>discuss the linguistic features of Creole found in the passage;</li>\n<li>comment on the communicative behaviours displayed by the narrator in a specified section of the passage (in this practice extract, paragraph 4).</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (20/20)</h4><p>This narrative by a Caribbean woman in London contrasts the formal English of the people who judge her with the narrator’s own Creole-influenced voice.</p>\n<p>Three registers can be identified. The <span>frozen register</span> appears in the court charge, “You are hereby charged that on the night of the fourteenth instant…”, a fixed legal formula with archaic words such as “hereby” and “instant”. A <span>formal register</span> is used by Mrs Pruett and the policeman. They address a tenant in complete sentences with polite, impersonal phrasing, such as “Would you mind telling me what occurred?” By contrast, the narrator tells her story in a <span>casual register</span>, with conversational asides such as “what is the use of a song you keep inside?” that draw readers in.</p>\n<p>The narrator’s speech displays several features of Caribbean Creole. Verbs often lack past-tense marking, as in “When I first come to London” and “my granny teach me”. Third-person “-s” is absent, as in “she don’t like my singing”. “Does” acts as a habitual marker in “people does sing when they sad”, where the copula is also left out before the adjective “sad”. Also, the double negative in “she don’t like me neither” follows Creole rather than Standard English grammar.</p>\n<p>In paragraph 4, the narrator’s communicative behaviours convey defiance. Her direct eye contact and folded arms show that she will not be intimidated, while her laughter at the landlady’s red face expresses open mockery. Turning her back and singing on is a deliberate snub, although singing “softer” suggests a small concession. Finally, her week-long silence and refusal to look at Mrs Pruett on the stairs use the absence of communication itself to show lasting resentment.</p>\n<p>Her language and behaviour thus set her apart in a society demanding that she be “quiet too”.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Language registers</b> (4/4): Three registers are correctly named, illustrated and explained by situation.</li><li><b>Linguistic features of Creole</b> (4/4): Five accurate grammatical features are each supported with a quotation.</li><li><b>Communicative behaviours (paragraph 4)</b> (4/4): Kinesic, paralinguistic and silent behaviours are identified and interpreted in context.</li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): It has an introduction, three focused body paragraphs and a closing sentence, all within 300 words.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (4/4): It uses precise linguistic terms in fluent, accurate Standard English.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Module 3: Audience, objectives and contexts for an article on surviving a natural disaster","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2019 task)</b></p><h4>How to Survive a Hurricane: A Step-by-Step Guide</h4>\n<p>Hurricane season runs from 1 June to 30 November, and every year someone in the region says, ‘It won’t hit us.’ Don’t be that person. Whether you live in a concrete house in Antigua or a wooden one in Dominica, the steps below could save your life.</p>\n<p><b>Before the storm</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Know your zone.</b> Find out whether your home is in a flood-prone or low-lying area and where your nearest official shelter is.</li>\n<li><b>Build a survival kit.</b> Pack enough for at least three days for every member of your household:\n<ul>\n<li>four litres of water per person per day</li>\n<li>canned food and a manual can-opener</li>\n<li>a battery-powered radio, flashlights and spare batteries</li>\n<li>a first-aid kit and a two-week supply of any prescription medicine</li>\n<li>copies of birth certificates, passports and insurance papers in a waterproof bag</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><b>Secure your home.</b> Trim trees, clear drains and board up or shutter windows before the warning is issued, not after.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>During the storm</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stay indoors, away from windows, in the strongest room of the house.</li>\n<li>Listen to official updates on your radio. Do not rely on rumours circulating on WhatsApp.</li>\n<li>Beware the ‘eye’. If the wind suddenly drops, do not go outside. The second half of the storm may be on its way.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>After the storm</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stay away from fallen power lines and flooded roads; just 15 centimetres of moving water can knock an adult off his feet.</li>\n<li>Boil or treat water before drinking it until the authorities say the supply is safe.</li>\n<li>Check on elderly neighbours and anyone living alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Remember: the hurricane does not care how many storms you have survived before. Preparation is not panic. It is common sense.</p>\n<p><i>Adapted from an online article by Natasha Greaves, Caribbean Preparedness Network website, June 2019</i></p><p>In an essay of no more than 250 words, discuss:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the intended audience of the extract and why the information presented is suitable for that audience;</li>\n<li>TWO objectives which the writer hopes to achieve;</li>\n<li>THREE contexts, other than written media, in which the information could be communicated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p>Natasha Greaves’s online guide is intended for Caribbean householders, especially adults responsible for preparing their families and homes for the hurricane season. The information suits this audience because it is <span>clearly formatted</span>: headings divide the advice into what to do before, during and after the storm, while numbered steps and bullet points make the checklist easy to follow under pressure. The language is also <span>simple and direct</span>, using imperatives such as “Know your zone”, and the references to Antigua, Dominica and WhatsApp rumours make the content <span>relevant</span> to local readers.</p>\n<p>The writer hopes to achieve two objectives. Firstly, she aims to break down the complacency of readers who believe “It won’t hit us”. Secondly, she seeks to equip households to prepare a survival kit and take specific safety precautions before, during and after a storm.</p>\n<p>The information could be communicated in three contexts other than written media. It could be broadcast as a series of radio announcements, since battery-powered radios remain a reliable source of information when electricity fails. It could be presented at a community preparedness meeting, where a disaster officer could demonstrate how to pack a kit and residents could ask about local shelters. Finally, a short television or online video could show viewers how to shutter windows and avoid flood waters, because a visual demonstration makes practical instructions easier to remember.</p>\n<p>Ultimately, the guide’s clear structure and practical tone make it an effective tool for a regional audience that cannot afford to be unprepared.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Intended audience and suitability</b> (4/4): It gives a precise audience and explains suitability through formatting, language and relevance.</li><li><b>Two objectives</b> (3/4): Both objectives are precise, but the second is not linked to the features of the extract that achieve it, unlike the first. <b>One mark was lost here.</b></li><li><b>Three non-written contexts</b> (4/4): There are three valid contexts, each with a reason tied to audience or medium.</li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): It has an introduction with the audience, a paragraph for each sub-task and a conclusion, with clear transitions.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (4/4): It uses concise, accurate and varied Standard English.</li></ul>"}]},{"id":"assess_2fw","title":"Paper 032 · 2020 tasks","desc":"<p>Because of the Covid-19 pandemic, the 2020 examination was made up of Papers 01, 031 and 032 only; Paper 02 was not set. Paper 032, written by private candidates, had three essay questions, one per module, each marked out of 20 for content, organization and expression. The mean was 26.38 out of 60 (about 44 per cent), and only 9 per cent of candidates earned the top two grades. The examiners found that candidates could not write a coherent summary, separate main points from supporting details, comment on a writer's purpose as shown by the writer's choices, or evaluate dialect, register and communicative behaviours, and they urged instructors to become far more knowledgeable about the syllabus.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Module 1: Audience, purpose and engagement strategies in a public appeal","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2020 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract written for this task. The original extract is not reproduced in the subject report.</em></p>\n<h4>One Pint, Three Lives: Why Your Blood Matters This Carnival Season</h4>\n<p><b>Marcia Hodge-Lake</b>, Public Health Columnist<br><em>The Bayside Courier, Wednesday 12 June 2019</em></p>\n<p>Last July, nineteen-year-old Kemar was riding home from football practice when a car swerved across the Coastal Road. By the time the ambulance reached Bayside General Hospital, he had lost almost half the blood in his body. He survived because seven strangers had rolled up their sleeves weeks earlier. Kemar never met them. He thinks about them every day.</p>\n<p>Would you have been one of those seven?</p>\n<p>Here is the hard truth. The National Blood Bank needs about 40 units of blood every week to keep our hospital running. Last year it averaged only 26. When stocks run low, planned surgeries are postponed, mothers who haemorrhage during childbirth are put at risk, and children with sickle cell disease wait longer for the transfusions that keep them alive. Every pint you give can be separated into red cells, plasma and platelets, so one donation can help up to three patients.</p>\n<p>Many of you, I know, have your excuses ready. &ldquo;I fraid needle.&rdquo; &ldquo;I too busy.&rdquo; &ldquo;Somebody else will do it.&rdquo; I hear you. But the pinch lasts two seconds, the whole visit takes under an hour, and &ldquo;somebody else&rdquo; is exactly who the blood bank has been waiting for since last year. Young people between 18 and 30 are healthy, energetic and, let's be honest, first in line for every fete this season. Why not be first in line for something that matters?</p>\n<p>The Ministry of Health's &ldquo;Bleed for Life&rdquo; drive comes to the Sandbox Tree College auditorium on Saturday 22 June, from 9 a.m. to 3 p.m. Eat a good breakfast, drink plenty of water and bring a valid ID. Every donor will receive a free &ldquo;I Gave Life&rdquo; T-shirt and a ticket to the Youth Splash concert.</p>\n<p>Bring a friend. Bring your whole crew. Post your selfie with the hashtag #OnePintThreeLives and dare your friends to match you.</p>\n<p>Kemar will be there, volunteering at the registration desk. Go and shake his hand. Then roll up your sleeve, and let somebody else's story end the way his did.</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then write an essay in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identify the intended audience of the extract</li>\n<li>state the writer's main purpose</li>\n<li>explain <b>TWO</b> ways in which the writer engages the audience</li>\n<li>justify <b>TWO</b> contexts, other than the printed word, that would be suitable for communicating the message in the extract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>The subject report describes these tasks but does not give the exact wording or a word limit. Write in continuous prose and aim for about 300&ndash;350 words.</em></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p>The extract &ldquo;One Pint, Three Lives&rdquo; is a newspaper column aimed at <span>young adults between the ages of 18 and 30</span>, particularly those who plan to attend carnival events. The writer's main purpose is <span>to persuade these readers to donate blood</span> at the Ministry of Health's drive on 22 June.</p>\n<p>One way in which the writer engages her audience is by opening with a personal anecdote. The account of Kemar, a teenage footballer who survived a road accident only because strangers had given blood, puts a human face on an otherwise abstract shortage. Since Kemar is the same age as the readers, they can easily picture themselves either as the victim or as one of his donors, and this <span>emotional identification</span> makes them more willing to act.</p>\n<p>A second technique is the writer's conversational tone, created through direct address and rhetorical questions. She speaks to the reader as &ldquo;you&rdquo;, asks whether the reader would have been one of the seven donors and even quotes, in Creole, the excuses that young people typically give. By <span>anticipating these objections and then answering them</span> in a friendly way, she lowers the audience's resistance and makes donating seem simple and normal.</p>\n<p>Apart from print, the message would suit a short video posted on social media platforms such as Instagram and TikTok. Young adults spend a great deal of time on these platforms, and a clip of Kemar telling his own story, followed by the hashtag challenge, could be shared widely and cheaply within hours. The message would also be effective as a live presentation at a college assembly. Speaking face to face, a health officer could answer students' questions about safety and eligibility immediately, <span>removing the fear that often stops first-time donors</span>.</p>\n<p>Overall, by combining a moving true story with a warm, persuasive voice, the writer makes a serious appeal attractive to a young audience, and her message would be just as effective on screen or in person.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Intended audience</b> (2/2): Audience identified precisely and supported by evidence from the extract.</li><li><b>Writer's main purpose</b> (2/2): Purpose stated clearly with a purposive verb and linked to the blood drive.</li><li><b>Two ways the writer engages the audience</b> (4/4): Two distinct strategies named, illustrated and fully explained in terms of their effect.</li><li><b>Two contexts other than the printed word</b> (4/4): Two suitable contexts, each justified by its benefit to this audience and message.</li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): Introduction, one paragraph per task with clear topic sentences, and a conclusion; logical transitions throughout.</li><li><b>Expression and grammar</b> (3/4): Accurate, varied Standard English; <b>one mark lost</b> because a few sentences in paragraphs 2 and 3 are long and could have been tightened.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Module 2: Creole features, dialectal varieties and non-verbal communication in a short story","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2020 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract written for this task. The original short story is not reproduced in the subject report.</em></p>\n<h4>Island Song</h4>\n<p>Jewel stood in the wings of the Parish Hall stage, twisting the hem of her blue dress round and round her finger until the cloth creased. Out front, three hundred chairs creaked. Somewhere among them sat her mother, who had sold sugar cake every Saturday for a year to pay for singing lessons.</p>\n<p>&ldquo;Gyal, stop fidget so,&rdquo; Tanty Merle hissed, straightening the ribbon in Jewel's hair. &ldquo;You go mash up the dress before you even reach the microphone.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>&ldquo;Tanty, me belly a turn over. What if me voice crack? Everybody from Rocky Gully come to see me.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>&ldquo;Look, chile, you does practise that song morning, noon and night. Me hear it in me sleep! Nobody cyan sing &lsquo;Island Song&rsquo; better than you. You mother tell the whole market you singing tonight. She en tell nobody she frighten for you too.&rdquo; Tanty Merle laughed, but her own hands were trembling.</p>\n<p>Then the MC, Mr Collymore, stepped to the microphone, adjusted his bow tie and cleared his throat. &ldquo;Ladies and gentlemen, it is my great pleasure to introduce our final performer this evening. She is a fifth-form student of Breadfruit Valley Secondary School and the winner of this year's parish junior competition. Please welcome Miss Jewel Thomas.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>Jewel's knees locked. She swallowed twice, pressed her palms flat against her dress and walked out with her eyes fixed on the floorboards. The spotlight was hot on her face. For two long bars of the music she made no sound at all. Then she lifted her chin, found her mother's face in the third row, and sang.</p>\n<p>When the last note faded, the hall was silent for one terrible second. Then the applause came like rain on a galvanise roof. Jewel let out a breath she seemed to have been holding for an hour. Her shoulders dropped, her hands flew to her mouth, and tears ran freely down her cheeks as she bowed once, twice, three times.</p>\n<p>Backstage, she threw herself into Tanty Merle's arms. &ldquo;Tanty, dem clap fuh me! Dem did really like it!&rdquo;</p>\n<p>&ldquo;Like it? Chile, dem love it. Look you mother dey &ndash; she bawling more than you!&rdquo;</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then write an analysis in which you comment on</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>TWO</b> linguistic features of Caribbean Creole English found in the extract</li>\n<li><b>TWO</b> dialectal varieties found in the extract, explaining why the characters use them</li>\n<li>how Jewel's non-verbal communication reflects her emotions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>The subject report describes these tasks but does not give the exact wording or a word limit. Write in continuous prose and aim for about 350&ndash;400 words.</em></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (20/20)</h4><p>The extract recounts the night on which Jewel, a fifth-form student, sings at a parish concert. The writer uses Caribbean Creole English for the private conversation backstage and Standard English for the public announcement, while Jewel's body language traces her journey from apprehension to relief.</p>\n<p>One linguistic feature of Creole in the extract is the use of <span>preverbal markers</span>, rather than verb endings, to show tense and aspect. In &ldquo;you does practise that song&rdquo;, the marker &ldquo;does&rdquo; signals a habitual action, and in &ldquo;me belly a turn over&rdquo; the marker &ldquo;a&rdquo; shows an action in progress, where Standard English would use &ldquo;you practise&rdquo; and &ldquo;my stomach is turning&rdquo;. A second feature is the Creole <span>pronoun system</span>. &ldquo;Me&rdquo; functions as both subject and possessive, as in &ldquo;me hear it&rdquo; and &ldquo;me voice&rdquo;, while &ldquo;dem&rdquo; serves as the third-person plural subject in &ldquo;dem clap fuh me&rdquo;. These forms make the dialogue authentic and mark the speakers as members of the Rocky Gully community.</p>\n<p>Two dialectal varieties appear in the extract. Jewel and Tanty Merle speak Creole because they are relatives sharing an intimate, emotional moment; Creole is their home language and allows them to express fear and encouragement naturally. In contrast, Mr Collymore uses Standard English, as in &ldquo;it is my great pleasure to introduce our final performer&rdquo;, because he is addressing three hundred people at a formal public event where his role as master of ceremonies demands correctness and respect.</p>\n<p>Jewel's non-verbal communication reveals her changing emotions. Before she performs, she twists the hem of her dress, swallows twice and keeps her eyes on the floorboards. These gestures and her avoidance of eye contact show that she is <span>apprehensive</span> and doubts whether she can meet her community's expectations. When she lifts her chin and searches for her mother's face, her posture signals <span>newfound resolve</span>. After the applause, her deep exhalation and dropped shoulders show <span>relief</span>, while her hands flying to her mouth and her tears express overwhelmed disbelief. Finally, throwing herself into Tanty Merle's arms conveys <span>elation and gratitude</span>.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, the writer's choice of language varieties and detailed description of Jewel's body language allow readers to share both the setting and the emotions of her momentous night.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Two linguistic features of Creole</b> (4/4): Two features correctly named, illustrated and explained against Standard English.</li><li><b>Two dialectal varieties</b> (4/4): Both varieties identified with evidence and convincingly justified by speaker, audience and situation.</li><li><b>Jewel's non-verbal communication and emotions</b> (4/4): Several behaviours accurately linked to precise, developing emotions (apprehension, resolve, relief, elation).</li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): Clear introduction, one paragraph per task with topic sentences, and a conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression and grammar</b> (4/4): Fluent, accurate and varied Standard English with precise terminology.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Module 3: Purposes, audience and contexts for a magazine article on hurricane preparedness","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2020 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract written for this task in the style of a general-interest magazine. The original extract is not reproduced in the subject report.</em></p>\n<h4>Before the Wind Blows: A Family Guide to Hurricane Season</h4>\n<p><b>Desmond Arrindell</b>, emergency planner with the Leeward Islands Disaster Preparedness Office<br><em>Island Living magazine, June issue</em></p>\n<p>Every year on 1 June, the same promise is made in thousands of Caribbean homes: &ldquo;We'll get ready when a storm is coming.&rdquo; And every year, when a system finally forms in the Atlantic, supermarket shelves empty within hours and hardware stores run out of plywood. If the hurricanes of 2017 taught us anything, it is that the families who recovered fastest were the ones who prepared long before the first warning.</p>\n<p>Here is a simple plan any household can follow this month.</p>\n<p><b>1. Make a family plan.</b> Sit down with everyone in your home, including the children, and agree on where you will shelter, how you will contact one another if phone lines fail and who will check on elderly neighbours. Write the plan down and stick it on the fridge.</p>\n<p><b>2. Build your kit gradually.</b> You do not need to spend a fortune in one weekend. Add a few items to each grocery trip: one gallon of water per person per day for at least seven days, canned food, a manual can opener, batteries, a torch, a battery-powered radio, a first-aid kit and a two-week supply of any prescription medicines.</p>\n<p><b>3. Protect your papers.</b> Place birth certificates, passports, insurance policies and land titles in a sealed plastic bag or waterproof box. Photograph each document and email the pictures to yourself.</p>\n<p><b>4. Secure your home.</b> Trim overhanging branches, clear gutters and check that your roof straps are firmly fastened. If you own shutters, test them now, not when the wind is already rising.</p>\n<p><b>5. Know your shelter.</b> Find out which shelter serves your district and how you will get there if you do not own a car.</p>\n<p>Preparing early does more than protect property. It brings peace of mind, saves money and, most importantly, saves lives. As one grandmother in Sandy Bay told me after the last storm, &ldquo;Me house roof gone, but me family still here, because we was ready.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>So start tonight. Your future self will thank you.</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then write an essay in which you discuss</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>TWO</b> purposes which the writer hopes to achieve in the extract</li>\n<li>who the intended audience is and <b>ONE</b> reason why the information is suitable for this audience</li>\n<li><b>TWO</b> suitable contexts, other than a general-interest magazine, in which the information could be communicated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>The subject report gives these tasks but not a word limit. Write in continuous prose and aim for about 300&ndash;350 words.</em></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (18/20)</h4><p>&ldquo;Before the Wind Blows&rdquo; is a magazine article in which an emergency planner advises Caribbean families on how to prepare for the hurricane season.</p>\n<p>The writer's first purpose is <span>to inform</span> households of the practical steps needed to prepare for a hurricane. He sets out five clear actions, from making a family plan to locating the nearest shelter, so that readers know exactly what to do. His second purpose is <span>to persuade</span> readers to act immediately rather than wait for a storm warning. He reminds them that shelves empty within hours once a system forms and ends with the urgent command &ldquo;So start tonight&rdquo; in order to overcome the common habit of delay.</p>\n<p>The intended audience is <span>heads of households across the Caribbean</span>, particularly parents and caregivers who are responsible for their families' safety. The information is suitable for them because it is presented as a numbered checklist with bold headings and written in plain, everyday language. A busy parent can scan the steps quickly, understand them without technical knowledge and follow the advice on an ordinary budget, since the writer recommends building the kit gradually during regular grocery trips.</p>\n<p>One suitable context, other than a general-interest magazine, is a <span>community meeting</span> organised by the district disaster office in a church hall or community centre. Officers could demonstrate how to pack a kit and waterproof important documents, and residents could ask questions about their own shelters, making the advice more relevant to each district. Another suitable context is a <span>radio broadcast</span> in the weeks before the season begins. Radio reaches rural households and continues to work when electricity and the internet fail, so the message would reach people who may never buy a magazine.</p>\n<p>By combining a practical guide with an urgent appeal, the writer ensures that his message is not only understood but also acted upon.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Two purposes of the writer</b> (4/4): Two distinct purposes stated with purposive verbs and supported from the extract.</li><li><b>Intended audience and one reason for suitability</b> (4/4): Audience precise; reason grounded in format, language and relevance.</li><li><b>Two suitable contexts</b> (3/4): Both contexts are appropriate and justified; <b>one mark lost</b> because the radio context does not explain how the checklist would be adapted for listeners (e.g. repetition or a call-in segment).</li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): Clear introduction, one paragraph per task with topic sentences, and a conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression and grammar</b> (3/4): Accurate Standard English; <b>one mark lost</b> because several sentences begin in the same way (\"He…\", \"The…\"), which limits sentence variety.</li></ul>"}]},{"id":"assess_2g0","title":"Paper 032 · 2022 tasks","desc":"<p>Paper 032 again had three essay questions, one per module, each marked out of 20. Performance continued to improve: the share of candidates earning Grades I&ndash;III rose from 11.25 per cent in 2020 to 16.98 per cent in 2021 and 21.15 per cent in 2022, suggesting that instructors and candidates are becoming more familiar with the syllabus. Even so, most candidates could not write a coherent summary that separated main points from supporting details, struggled to evaluate whether content suited a given audience, and lacked the analytical skills to comment on register, communicative behaviours and dialectal varieties. The examiners again called urgently for better training of private candidates and their instructors in every part of the syllabus.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Module 1: Summary and usefulness of an article on sport and academic performance","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2022 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract written for this task on the same topic. The original online article is not reproduced in the subject report.</em></p>\n<h4>The Link Between Sports and Academic Performance</h4>\n<p><em>Education Insight (online), posted in the &ldquo;Teaching and Learning&rdquo; section</em></p>\n<p>When budgets tighten or examinations draw near, physical education and after-school sport are often the first activities to be cut. Yet a growing body of research suggests that schools may be removing the very thing that helps students learn.</p>\n<p>Studies in several countries have found that students who take part in regular physical activity tend to earn higher grades and score better on standardised tests than their inactive peers. One explanation is biological. Exercise increases the flow of blood and oxygen to the brain and stimulates the release of chemicals that improve memory and concentration. Students who move regularly often find it easier to stay focused in class.</p>\n<p>Sport also teaches habits that carry over into the classroom. An athlete who must attend training four afternoons a week quickly learns to plan homework around practice sessions. Coaches demand punctuality, persistence and the ability to accept criticism, qualities that teachers value just as highly. Many student-athletes report that a busy schedule actually makes them more organised, not less.</p>\n<p>There are emotional benefits as well. Belonging to a team gives young people a sense of identity and connection to their school. Success on the field builds confidence, and that self-belief can encourage students to attempt challenging work in mathematics or science. Some schools have also found that students at risk of dropping out attend more regularly when they know that missing classes will cost them a place on the team.</p>\n<p>None of this means that more sport is always better. When training schedules become excessive, fatigue and missed lessons can harm achievement. The key, researchers agree, is balance: moderate, well-organised participation supported by academic monitoring.</p>\n<p>For educators, the message is clear. Physical activity should not be treated as a luxury to be sacrificed when examinations approach. Instead, it should be seen as part of a strategy for improving academic outcomes.</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then write an essay in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>summarize the main message of the extract in no more than <b>30 words</b></li>\n<li>explain <b>TWO</b> ways in which the information could be useful to Caribbean educators</li>\n<li>justify the use of <b>TWO</b> other media through which the information could be communicated to educators.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>The subject report gives the 30-word limit for the summary but no overall word limit. Write in continuous prose.</em></p>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (20/20)</h4><p>Regular participation in sport improves students' academic performance by sharpening concentration, building discipline and time management, and raising confidence and attendance, provided training is balanced with study.</p>\n<p>This information could be useful to Caribbean educators in two important ways. Firstly, it gives principals evidence to resist the common practice of <span>cancelling physical education periods</span> in the weeks before CSEC and CAPE examinations. Since exercise improves memory and concentration, school leaders could instead timetable short activity sessions to help students revise more effectively. Secondly, the findings could help guidance counsellors and form teachers to reach <span>students who are at risk of dropping out</span>. By linking a place on a school team to regular attendance and satisfactory grades, educators could turn students' love of football, cricket or athletics into a reason to remain in school.</p>\n<p>The information could be communicated effectively through a <span>professional development workshop</span> for teachers. In a face-to-face session, a facilitator could present the research with charts and allow teachers to ask questions and plan together how to apply the ideas in their own schools. It could also be shared through an <span>email newsletter</span> from the Ministry of Education. Such a newsletter would reach every principal and teacher on the island simultaneously and at almost no cost, and it could include links to the full studies for those who wish to read further.</p>\n<p>Consequently, if it is shared through the right media, this research could encourage Caribbean schools to treat sport as a partner in academic success rather than a distraction from it.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Summary (30 words)</b> (4/4): Main message and all key ideas captured concisely, paraphrased and within the limit.</li><li><b>Two ways the information is useful to Caribbean educators</b> (4/4): Two distinct, practical uses, each applied to the Caribbean and explained.</li><li><b>Two other media, justified</b> (4/4): Two appropriate media, each justified by features that suit an audience of educators.</li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): Separate paragraphs for each task with topic sentences, transitions and a conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression and grammar</b> (4/4): Accurate, varied Standard English with precise word choice and correct punctuation.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Module 2: Dialectal varieties, non-verbal behaviour and registers in a short story","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2022 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract written for this task. The extract from Merle Collins' story is not reproduced in the subject report; this original passage uses a character named Angel and her neighbours so that the same skills can be practised.</em></p>\n<p>The news reached Morne Clair before Angel did. By the time the bus set her down at the junction, every verandah on the hill had an occupant.</p>\n<p>&ldquo;Look she dey, look!&rdquo; Miss Iris called across the road to Tanty Vera. &ldquo;Six year in Trinidad an' she come back wid suitcase big so. Dem say she turn nurse now.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>&ldquo;Nurse? Hm. Me hear she married some big-shot man over dey an' he lef' she,&rdquo; Tanty Vera answered, not lowering her voice at all. &ldquo;Is so dem young people does go. Dey forget whey dey come from.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>Old Ma Zabette, rocking on the step of the rum shop, sucked her teeth. &ldquo;Bondyé! Zot ka palé twòp.&rdquo; Then, to make sure the neighbours understood: &ldquo;All-you talk too much. Is my godchild that.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>Angel heard every word. She shifted the strap of her bag higher on her shoulder and kept walking up the steep road, past the standpipe where she had once carried water before school, past the church where she had sung in the choir.</p>\n<p>At the gate of her mother's house, a young man in a pressed white shirt was waiting with an envelope. &ldquo;Good afternoon. Are you Miss Angela Baptiste?&rdquo;</p>\n<p>&ldquo;Yes, I am.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>&ldquo;I am from the Ministry of Health. The Permanent Secretary asked me to deliver this personally.&rdquo; He cleared his throat and read aloud: &ldquo;Dear Miss Baptiste, we are pleased to confirm your appointment as Staff Nurse at the Morne Clair Health Centre, with effect from the first of next month. We trust that you will serve your community with distinction.&rdquo;</p>\n<p>Across the road, Miss Iris's mouth fell open. Tanty Vera suddenly found something very interesting to examine in her yard.</p>\n<p>Angel thanked the officer, folded the letter carefully and turned around. For a long moment she looked at the row of verandahs. Then her mother came running out of the house, apron still on, crying, &ldquo;Angel! Mi chile come home! Mi chile come home!&rdquo;</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then write an analysis of no more than <b>300 words</b> in which you comment on</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>THREE</b> dialectal varieties used in the passage</li>\n<li><b>TWO</b> non-verbal behaviours which Angel could have displayed to the neighbours</li>\n<li><b>TWO</b> language registers used in the extract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p>The extract presents Angel's return to Morne Clair, where her neighbours' gossip is silenced by news of her appointment as a nurse.</p>\n<p>Three dialectal varieties are used. The first is <span>English-lexicon Creole</span>, spoken by Miss Iris and Tanty Vera in lines such as &ldquo;Look she dey&rdquo; and &ldquo;Is so dem young people does go&rdquo;. They use it because they are relaxed neighbours gossiping in their home community, where Creole is the language of everyday intimacy. The second is <span>French Creole</span>, which Ma Zabette uses when she exclaims &ldquo;Bondyé! Zot ka palé twòp&rdquo;. As an elderly villager, she turns to the ancestral language to express strong disapproval, then switches to English Creole so that everyone understands her rebuke. The third is <span>Standard English</span>, used by the Ministry officer and by Angel in &ldquo;Yes, I am&rdquo;, because the exchange is an official transaction between strangers.</p>\n<p>Angel could have displayed two non-verbal behaviours to the neighbours. Firstly, she could have <span>smiled and waved</span> as she passed the verandahs. This gesture would signal that she bore no grudge and had not, as Tanty Vera claimed, forgotten where she came from. Secondly, after receiving the letter, she could have <span>stood upright and held the neighbours' gaze</span>. This steady eye contact would communicate quiet confidence and silently answer the false rumours about her.</p>\n<p>Two registers are also evident. The <span>casual register</span> appears in the neighbours' loud exchange across the road, marked by Creole, exclamations such as &ldquo;Look she dey, look!&rdquo; and no concern for privacy. In contrast, the officer's letter is in the <span>formal register</span>, seen in fixed expressions such as &ldquo;we are pleased to confirm&rdquo; and &ldquo;with effect from&rdquo;, which suit an official appointment.</p>\n<p>These choices show how language reflects relationships and social occasions in a Caribbean village.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Three dialectal varieties</b> (5/6): All three identified with evidence and reasons; <b>one mark lost</b> because the justification for Standard English is briefer than the other two and does not mention the narrator's use of it.</li><li><b>Two non-verbal behaviours Angel could have displayed</b> (4/4): Two plausible behaviours, each with a clear explanation of what it would signify to the neighbours.</li><li><b>Two language registers</b> (2/2): Casual and formal registers correctly identified and illustrated.</li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): Introduction, one paragraph per task with topic sentences, a conclusion and clear transitions.</li><li><b>Expression and grammar</b> (4/4): Accurate, concise Standard English within the word limit.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Module 3: Audience, objectives and medium of an online journal article on emotional intelligence","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2022 task)</b></p><p><em>Practice extract written for this task on the same topic and in the same kind of source. The original article is not reproduced in the subject report, so the version below was written for practice.</em></p>\n<h4>What Emotional Intelligence Really Means</h4>\n<p><em>Posted by Dr Leon Hazelwood, Professor of Psychology, in the online journal</em> Mind Matters</p>\n<p>Over the past decade, &ldquo;emotional intelligence&rdquo; has become one of the most popular phrases in schools, offices and self-help books. Job advertisements ask for it. Parenting websites promise to build it. Some writers have even claimed that it matters twice as much as IQ in predicting success. As a researcher who has studied the concept for many years, I believe it is time to clear up some confusion.</p>\n<p>Emotional intelligence, properly understood, is the ability to reason accurately about emotions and to use emotional information to guide thinking. It includes four related skills: perceiving emotions correctly in faces, voices and situations; using emotions to help thought, such as recognising that a calm mood suits careful planning; understanding how emotions combine and change over time; and managing emotions in oneself and others.</p>\n<p>What emotional intelligence is <em>not</em> is equally important. It is not the same as being cheerful, optimistic or agreeable. It is not high self-esteem, nor is it simply good manners or persistence. These are valuable qualities, but they are personality traits, not mental abilities. When popular writers lump them all together under a single label, the term loses its meaning and the claims made for it become impossible to test.</p>\n<p>Research so far suggests that emotional intelligence does predict some important outcomes. People who score higher on ability tests tend to have better relationships and may handle social situations at work more effectively. However, the effects are modest. No responsible scientist would claim that emotional intelligence alone guarantees success in school or in a career.</p>\n<p>Why does this matter? Because schools and employers are spending large sums on programmes that promise to raise emotional intelligence. Readers deserve to know what such programmes can realistically achieve. If we define the concept clearly, we can measure it, teach the skills that genuinely belong to it and avoid being misled by exaggerated promises.</p>\n<p>Readers who wish to explore the studies mentioned here can follow the links below. I welcome your comments and questions.</p><p>Read the extract carefully and then write an essay of no more than <b>250 words</b> in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identify the intended audience and explain <b>ONE</b> reason why the information is suitable for this audience</li>\n<li>comment on <b>TWO</b> objectives which the writer hoped to achieve by communicating the information to his audience</li>\n<li>discuss <b>TWO</b> advantages of the writer's use of an online journal as the medium to present the information to his audience.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p>The intended audience is <span>educated non-specialists</span>, such as teachers, parents and employers, who have encountered the popular term &ldquo;emotional intelligence&rdquo;. The information suits them because the writer avoids jargon and breaks a complex idea into four clearly explained skills, using everyday examples such as reading emotions in &ldquo;faces, voices and situations&rdquo;. Readers without psychological training can therefore follow his argument easily.</p>\n<p>The writer hopes to achieve two objectives. His first objective is <span>to clarify</span> the meaning of emotional intelligence by defining it precisely as the ability to reason about emotions, so that readers share an accurate understanding of the term. His second objective is <span>to correct misconceptions</span>. By stressing that optimism and self-esteem are personality traits rather than abilities, and that the benefits of emotional intelligence are &ldquo;modest&rdquo;, he wants schools and employers to make informed decisions before investing in costly programmes.</p>\n<p>Using an online journal offers two clear advantages. Firstly, the article can reach a <span>large, scattered audience simultaneously and economically</span>. A teacher in Jamaica and a manager in Trinidad can read it at the same moment, without the printing and distribution costs of a magazine. Secondly, the online medium allows the writer to include <span>hyperlinks</span> to the studies he mentions and to invite comments. Readers can verify his evidence immediately and raise questions, making communication two-way.</p>\n<p>Overall, the writer's accessible style and well-chosen medium help him to replace confusion with an accurate understanding of emotional intelligence.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Intended audience and one reason for suitability</b> (4/4): Audience precise; reason developed with evidence from the extract.</li><li><b>Two objectives of the writer</b> (4/4): Two distinct objectives stated with purposive verbs and linked to the audience.</li><li><b>Two advantages of an online journal</b> (4/4): Both advantages are specific to online dissemination and clearly explained.</li><li><b>Organization</b> (3/4): One paragraph per task with topic sentences, transitions and a conclusion; <b>one mark lost</b> because there is no introductory sentence orienting the reader to the extract before the first task is answered.</li><li><b>Expression and grammar</b> (4/4): Accurate, concise and varied Standard English within the word limit.</li></ul>"}]},{"id":"assess_2g4","title":"Paper 032 · 2024 tasks","desc":"<p>In 2024 Paper 032 had three essay-type questions, one per module, each worth 20 marks (60 in all): a summary-and-contexts task on an extract about adolescent romantic relationships, a language analysis of an extract from Samuel Selvon's <i>The Lonely Londoners</i>, and an audience-and-media analysis of an article on why teens stop listening to their parents. The examiners said that performance \"continues to give cause for serious concern\": the mean fell to 25.59 out of 60 (about 43 per cent) from 28.81 in 2023, and the highest score was only 39. Instead of long commentary, the report reproduces three marked scripts (10, 17 and 17 out of 20). Their margin annotations show that marks were awarded sub-task by sub-task, so every bullet must be fully developed, explained and supported from the extract, in accurate Standard English.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Module 1: Summary, contexts and digital media for an extract on romantic relationships","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2024 task)</b></p><h4>First Love, Fast Love? What Every Caribbean Family Should Know About Teen Romance</h4>\n<p><i>By Dr Anneliese Mohammed-Greaves, adolescent health specialist. Published in</i> Island Family Life <i>magazine, February 2024</i></p>\n<p>Ask any secondary school teacher in the region and you will hear the same thing: by Form Two, the corridors are buzzing with who \"likes\" whom. Romantic relationships usually begin in early adolescence, and they are far more common than many parents care to admit. The 2023 Caribbean Youth Wellness Survey, which questioned 2 100 students aged 12 to 19 in four territories, found that 68 per cent of 17-year-olds had already been in at least one romantic relationship.</p>\n<p>These early relationships are often short-lived. Among 12- to 14-year-olds, the typical relationship lasts only a few weeks and is driven largely by curiosity and status among friends. Between 15 and 17, relationships last several months, and by 18 to 21 many last a year or longer, with deeper trust and commitment. In other words, young people are practising.</p>\n<p>That practice matters. Teen relationships are where young people first learn to express feelings, negotiate disagreements, recover from rejection and consider another person's needs. Handled well, they build the communication skills that adult partnerships depend on.</p>\n<p>Handled badly, they can do real harm. One in five students in the survey reported that a partner had checked their phone without permission, demanded their passwords or told them whom they could not see. Many did not recognise this as controlling behaviour; several described it as \"proof that he really cares\".</p>\n<p>This is why adults must stop treating teen romance as either a joke or a crime. Banning relationships simply drives them underground, where young people have no one to ask for advice. Instead, parents, teachers and counsellors should talk openly and early about what healthy relationships look like: mutual respect, honesty, privacy and the freedom to say no.</p>\n<p>\"Young people will fall in love whether we approve or not,\" says Dr Mohammed-Greaves. \"Our job is to make sure they know the difference between love and control.\"</p><p>Write an essay, in NO MORE than 300 words, in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>summarize the main message of the extract, in about 30 words</li>\n<li>explain TWO contexts of communication within which it would be useful to share this information</li>\n<li>justify the use of ONE digital media through which the message could be communicated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p>Dr Mohammed-Greaves argues that teenage romantic relationships, though often brief, are a normal stage of development that builds emotional skills, but young people need open guidance from adults to recognise unhealthy behaviour.</p>\n<p>The first context in which this information would be useful is <span>small-group communication</span>, such as a Health and Family Life Education class discussion led by a guidance counsellor. Participants would be fifteen- to seventeen-year-olds, the group the survey describes. Because a small group allows two-way exchange and immediate feedback, students could test the warning signs against realistic scenarios, for example a partner demanding passwords, while the counsellor corrects myths such as the belief that jealousy is \"proof\" of love.</p>\n<p>A second context is <span>interpersonal communication</span> between a parent and a teenager at home. The extract specifically urges adults to talk rather than forbid, and a private, one-to-one conversation offers the trust and privacy that a teenager needs before admitting to a relationship. The parent can also read the teenager's non-verbal cues and adjust the message, which a ban never could.</p>\n<p>The most effective digital medium for this message would be a series of short videos on Instagram Reels, posted by a youth health organisation. Firstly, the target audience already spends hours on this platform, so the message would meet them where they are. Secondly, the medium is multimodal: brief dramatised skits could <i>show</i> controlling behaviour, making it easier to recognise than a printed list. Finally, comments, polls and shares make it interactive, and a teenager who is too shy to ask an adult could watch privately and follow a link to a helpline. Although some parents may never see these videos, the medium reaches the people most at risk, which is the extract's chief concern.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Summary of main message</b> (4/4): Accurate, complete main idea in about 30 words, paraphrased rather than lifted, with no statistics or minor details.</li><li><b>Two contexts of communication</b> (4/4): Two correctly named contexts (small group, interpersonal), each explained with participants, purpose and a link to the extract.</li><li><b>One digital medium justified</b> (3/4): One specific digital medium with three developed reasons linked to audience and purpose. The mark was lost here: the platform's privacy and safety risks for minors are not weighed against its advantages.</li><li><b>Organisation</b> (4/4): An introduction that is also the summary, one paragraph per sub-task, clear transitions (Firstly, Secondly, Finally) and a reasoned final sentence.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (4/4): Accurate Standard English with precise subject terminology, within the 300-word limit.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Module 2: Dialectal varieties, non-verbal communication and linguistic features in The Lonely Londoners","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2024 task)</b></p><p><i>Practice passage written in the style of Selvon's novel, with the same characters and situation the 2024 extract dealt with: Moses, \"the boys\" and newcomers who have been given his address.</i></p>\n<p>It was a grey Sunday evening in Bayswater, and Moses was lying down on the bed in his basement room, watching the damp spread on the ceiling. Somebody start to knock on the door like they bringing news of war.</p>\n<p>\"Who it is?\" Moses call out.</p>\n<p>\"Is me, Jackson. I have two fellars here just come off the boat-train.\"</p>\n<p>Moses get up slow-slow and open the door. Jackson standing up there grinning, and behind him two young fellars in thin tropical suits, shivering, each holding a cardboard grip.</p>\n<p>\"Who tell you my address?\" Moses ask, looking straight at Jackson.</p>\n<p>\"Well, everybody know is you does see about the new boys,\" Jackson say. \"Back home they hear 'bout you.\"</p>\n<p>\"And what they does hear? That Moses running a hotel?\"</p>\n<p>One of the fellars, a fellar name Cecil, clear his throat. \"Mister Moses, the fellars at the station tell we to come by you, and they tell we how you would show we where to get a room.\"</p>\n<p>Moses steups long and loud. He pass his hand over his face and shake his head, mumbling something nobody could hear. He thinking about the rent he ain't pay yet, and the factory work that finish Friday. But he look down at Cecil shoes, soaking wet, and he remember the day he himself land at Waterloo with nobody to meet him.</p>\n<p>\"Allyou eat anything since morning?\" he ask. The two boys shake their head.</p>\n<p>Moses take down his coat from the nail. \"Come. It have a Jamaican fellar in Harrow Road does rent rooms. We go see.\"</p>\n<p>On the steps they meet the landlady, Mrs Pemberton, coming down with the rent book.</p>\n<p>\"Good evening, Mrs Pemberton,\" Moses say, very proper. \"These gentlemen are friends of mine from Trinidad. I am only showing them the way. I shall settle the rent on Friday without fail.\"</p>\n<p>Mrs Pemberton look at them over her glasses and say nothing.</p>\n<p>When they reach the road Jackson laugh. \"Boy, you does talk English good when you want!\"</p>\n<p>\"You don't know how much talking I does have to do to keep a roof over my head in this city,\" Moses say, and he turn up his collar against the cold.</p><p>Write an essay, in NO MORE than 300 words, in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>discuss TWO possible reasons for the dialectal varieties which the author allows Moses to use in the extract</li>\n<li>discuss how the author conveys Moses' feelings and emotions through TWO examples of non-verbal communication which he displays in the extract</li>\n<li>explain TWO linguistic features of the characters' speech.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (20/20)</h4><p>In this extract, Moses, a Trinidadian in London, is reluctantly drawn into helping two newcomers. The author's language choices for him are deliberate, and his feelings emerge as much through gesture as through words.</p>\n<p>One reason the author lets Moses speak Trinidadian Creole with the boys, as in \"Who tell you my address?\", is to <span>create an authentic character and show solidarity</span>. Using his community's language among other West Indians marks him as one of them and makes their shared code a kind of home in a cold city. A second reason is to show <span>situational code-switching</span>. With Mrs Pemberton, Moses shifts to formal Standard English: \"I shall settle the rent on Friday without fail.\" She controls his housing, so he uses the prestige variety to earn her respect. His reply to Jackson's teasing exposes the pressure on immigrants to perform \"proper\" English to survive.</p>\n<p>Moses' feelings are also conveyed non-verbally. His long, loud steups, a vocalic cue, signals irritation at Jackson for giving out his address again. Then he passes his hand over his face and shakes his head. These kinesic cues suggest weariness and anxiety rather than anger, as he is thinking about his unpaid rent. When he takes his coat from the nail, his action shows that sympathy has won over frustration.</p>\n<p>Two linguistic features stand out in the characters' speech. The first is the <span>unmarked past tense</span>. In \"Who tell you my address?\" the base verb replaces \"told\"; context alone shows the action is past. The second is <span>pronoun case</span>. In Cecil's \"tell we\", the subject form \"we\" is used in object position where Standard English requires \"us\", because Creole does not distinguish these forms. Both features follow consistent Creole rules and are not careless errors.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Two reasons for dialectal varieties</b> (4/4): Two distinct, valid authorial purposes (identity/solidarity; situational code-switching), each supported by a quotation.</li><li><b>Two examples of non-verbal communication</b> (4/4): Two correctly classified cues, each linked to a specific feeling and to the context in the extract.</li><li><b>Two linguistic features</b> (4/4): Two precisely named features, each with an example from the extract and its Standard English equivalent.</li><li><b>Organisation</b> (4/4): A purposeful introduction, one paragraph per bullet, clear topic sentences and no repetitive conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (4/4): Accurate Standard English with correct agreement and spelling (none of the \"shows\"/\"dialectual\" slips from the real script), within 300 words.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Module 3: Audience, reader interest and newspaper publication of an article on why teens stop listening to their parents","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2024 task)</b></p><h4>Why Has My Teenager Stopped Listening?</h4>\n<p><i>By Dr Renée Baptiste-Charles, child and adolescent psychologist. Weekend Family section,</i> The Leeward Sentinel<i>, 19 May 2022</i></p>\n<p>\"Can you please put your dish in the sink?\" \"Have you finished your homework?\" \"Turn off that phone and come to dinner!\"</p>\n<p>If these words seem to disappear into thin air the moment they leave your mouth, you are not alone, and your teenager is not necessarily being rude. According to new brain research, your child may be doing exactly what nature designed them to do.</p>\n<p>In a recent study, scientists scanned the brains of 46 children and teenagers aged 7 to 16 while they listened to short recordings of their own mothers' voices and the voices of women they had never met. Younger children's brains lit up far more for Mum. At around 13 years old, however, something changed: the reward and attention centres of the teenage brain began to respond more strongly to unfamiliar voices than to familiar ones.</p>\n<p>\"It is not that teenagers stop loving their parents,\" the lead researcher explained. \"Their brains are preparing them to connect with the wider world, to learn from new people and to become independent adults.\" In other words, the eye-roll at the breakfast table is a sign of healthy development.</p>\n<p>That does not mean parents should give up. Here is what the research suggests:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Talk side by side.</b> Conversations in the car or while cooking feel less like interrogations than face-to-face lectures.</li>\n<li><b>Ask, don't tell.</b> Open questions such as \"What do you think?\" invite teenagers to share opinions.</li>\n<li><b>Choose your battles.</b> Save firm instructions for issues that really matter.</li>\n<li><b>Let other trusted voices help.</b> A coach, aunt or youth leader may get through when you cannot.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So the next time your words bounce off a closed bedroom door, take heart. Your teenager is not ignoring you forever. They are simply growing up.</p><p>Write an essay, in NO MORE than 250 words, in which you</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identify an intended <u>audience</u> and give ONE reason why this information would be fitting for this group</li>\n<li>explain TWO ways in which the writer seeks to capture and hold the reader's interest</li>\n<li>discuss TWO potential <b>disadvantages</b> of publishing this article in a newspaper.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p>This article uses brain research to explain why teenagers tune out their parents and offers advice for staying connected.</p>\n<p>The intended audience is <span>parents of children aged twelve to sixteen</span>. The information is fitting for them because it reframes behaviour they find hurtful as normal development and gives them strategies, such as talking \"side by side\", that they can use immediately.</p>\n<p>The writer first <span>captures</span> interest with a string of familiar commands: \"Can you please put your dish in the sink?\" Parents instantly recognise their own voices, and this humorous identification draws them in before any science appears. She then <span>holds</span> interest by presenting a surprising research finding in plain language. At about thirteen, the teenage brain responds more to strangers' voices than to a mother's. The writer explains that this is healthy, and she addresses the reader directly as \"you\". This reassurance encourages parents to read on for the tips.</p>\n<p>Publishing in a newspaper, however, has drawbacks. Firstly, it may <span>miss much of the target audience</span>. Many younger, working parents now read on their phones rather than buying a paper, so those who most need the advice may never see it. Secondly, print is <span>static and short-lived</span>. The article cannot include a video of the brain scans or a link to the full study, and readers cannot easily share it or ask questions. Once the weekend edition is discarded, the tips are lost, though parents need to revisit them.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Audience and ONE reason</b> (2/2): A specific intended audience with one well-developed, text-based reason.</li><li><b>Two ways to capture and hold interest</b> (5/5): Two distinct techniques, each quoted or referenced and explained in terms of its effect on the reader.</li><li><b>Two disadvantages of newspaper publication</b> (4/5): Two relevant, developed disadvantages linked to audience and purpose. The mark was lost here: the claim about parents' reading habits is asserted rather than supported.</li><li><b>Organisation</b> (4/4): A brief introduction, one paragraph per bullet and clear sequencing (first/then; Firstly/Secondly).</li><li><b>Expression</b> (4/4): Accurate, varied Standard English within 250 words, with none of the agreement and possessive errors highlighted in the real script.</li></ul>"}]},{"id":"assess_2g8","title":"Paper 032 · 2025 tasks","desc":"<p>The May–June 2025 subject report does not include a Paper 032 section: it reports only on Paper 01 (mean 59.71 out of 90) and Paper 02 (weighted mean 74.94 out of 150, about 50 per cent). Overall, about 93 per cent of candidates earned Grades I–V; Module 1 and Module 2 performance fell noticeably, while Module 3 improved by about 14 per cent. The three tasks below are therefore Paper 032-style practice tasks modelled on the 2025 Paper 02 questions (same skills, same topics), rescaled to 20 marks, and the examiner observations are those the report gives for the matching Paper 02 question. The examiners' main message was that candidates must <i>explain</i>, not just name: say how a technique serves the purpose, give correct Creole examples for every feature labelled, and explain each promotional strategy and how it will be used.</p>","minutes":90,"items":[{"t":"essay","title":"Question 1 · Module 1: Message, techniques and credibility of an extract on dying languages","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2025 task)</b></p><h4>When the Bush Goes Silent</h4>\n<p><i>By Dr Marisol Fuentes-Baptiste, Senior Lecturer in Ethnobotany, Caribbean Institute for Indigenous Knowledge. Adapted from</i> Caribbean Heritage Review<i>, March 2024.</i></p>\n<p>Ask an elder in the Rupununi savannahs what to do for a child’s fever and she will walk you to a shrub behind her house, call it by a name you cannot find in any dictionary, and tell you exactly which leaves to pick and at what hour. Ask her granddaughter, who speaks only English, and she will shrug and point you to the health centre forty miles away.</p>\n<p>That shrug is the sound of knowledge disappearing. Across the Caribbean and the Guianas, indigenous languages are dying, and with them a vast, unwritten pharmacy. When a language falls silent, the plants do not vanish at once, but what people know about them does.</p>\n<p>Between 2021 and 2022, our institute interviewed 41 traditional healers in two indigenous communities. Of the 214 plant remedies they described, 158 – almost three in every four – were known by name and use in only one language. No English or Spanish name existed for them, and no written record. In one of the two communities, the number of fluent speakers has fallen from roughly 3,000 in 1980 to fewer than 400 today, and most of those speakers are over sixty.</p>\n<p>The reason is simple. Plant knowledge is not stored in books; it is stored in words. The name of a plant often describes where it grows, what it treats or how it must be prepared. When children stop learning the language, they lose the key that unlocks the information. Every time an elder dies without passing on her words, a whole pharmacy is buried with her.</p>\n<p>Some will say that modern medicine has made these remedies unnecessary. Yet nearly a quarter of the medicines in use today were first developed from plants, and researchers routinely begin their search by asking traditional healers. One healer told our team that a bark tea she prepares “settles the sugar” in diabetic patients – a claim no laboratory has yet tested.</p>\n<p>The solution is within reach. Schools in indigenous communities can teach in the mother tongue; elders can be paid to record plant names and uses; and regional universities can work with communities so that knowledge is shared on their terms. What we cannot do is wait. Language loss is quiet, but it is final.</p>\n<p><i>The survey was funded by the Green Canopy Trust, a regional conservation charity that supports language revival projects.</i></p><p>Read the extract “When the Bush Goes Silent” carefully. In an essay of no more than 500 words:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>state the writer’s main message and purpose;</li>\n<li>identify and comment on the writer’s use of any TWO organizational strategies or literary techniques, showing how each helps the writer achieve her purpose;</li>\n<li>evaluate the credibility of the information presented in the extract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (20/20)</h4><p>In “When the Bush Goes Silent”, ethnobotanist Dr Marisol Fuentes-Baptiste argues that <span>when an indigenous language dies, the community’s knowledge of medicinal plants dies with it, because the names and healing uses of many plants exist only in that language and are passed on by word of mouth</span>. Her purpose is to inform readers of this hidden cost of language loss and to sensitise families, schools and governments so that they record and teach indigenous languages before the remaining elderly speakers are gone.</p>\n<p>The writer’s first strategy is the use of statistical data. She reports that of 214 plant remedies described by 41 healers, 158, almost three in four, were known in only one language, and that fluent speakers in one community have fallen from about 3,000 in 1980 to fewer than 400. <span>These figures are empirical evidence drawn from fieldwork, so they show that the link between language and plant knowledge is measurable rather than a matter of opinion.</span> They also convey the magnitude of the loss: if three-quarters of the remedies are tied to a language with only a few hundred ageing speakers, the reader can see that most of this knowledge could disappear within a generation. This sense of scale and urgency directly supports her aim of sensitising readers to act now.</p>\n<p>The second technique is metaphor. The writer calls indigenous knowledge “a vast, unwritten pharmacy” and states that when an elder dies, “a whole pharmacy is buried with her”. <span>By comparing an elder’s memory to a store of medicines, she turns an abstract idea, “traditional knowledge”, into something concrete and valuable that every reader understands.</span> The image of a pharmacy being buried also suggests waste and finality, so readers feel the loss emotionally as well as intellectually. In this way the metaphor moves the audience from simply knowing about language death to caring about it, which her purpose requires.</p>\n<p>The information is largely credible, although not simply because it contains statistics or was published recently. <span>The most reliable guide is the source:</span> the writer is a senior lecturer in ethnobotany at a regional institute, the article appears in a heritage journal, and the figures come from a named survey with a stated method and time frame, so they can be checked. Its 2024 date means the data is likely to be up to date. However, the survey is small and was conducted by the writer’s own institute and funded by a charity that supports language revival, so both have an interest in the conclusion. The claim that a bark tea “settles the sugar” is anecdotal, and the writer herself admits it has not been tested. Therefore, the extract is a credible starting point, but its strongest claims should be confirmed by independent research before they are accepted as fact.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, through carefully chosen statistics and a memorable central metaphor, the writer convincingly shows that saving a language may also mean saving medicine, while her evidence, though credible, would benefit from independent support.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Main message</b> (2/2): Full statement of the claim and the reason behind it, not a compressed one-liner.</li><li><b>Purpose</b> (2/2): Precise purpose verbs (inform, sensitise) linked to an audience and intended action.</li><li><b>Strategy 1: statistics</b> (2/2): Correctly identified; comment explains empirical basis, magnitude and urgency in relation to purpose.</li><li><b>Technique 2: metaphor</b> (2/2): Correctly identified with quotation; comment explains how the comparison makes the idea concrete and emotive.</li><li><b>Credibility</b> (4/4): Source-based judgement that weighs expertise and method against bias and anecdotal evidence.</li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): Introduction, one paragraph per sub-task in question order, clear transitions and a linking conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (4/4): Accurate, fluent Standard English with precise vocabulary and varied sentences.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 2 · Module 2: Dialectal variation, Creole grammar and registers in a narrative","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2025 task)</b></p><h4>Captain’s Wall</h4>\n<p>Everybody on Coconut Lane called him Captain, although nobody could remember him ever going to sea. Every afternoon he sat on the low wall outside Mrs Joseph’s parlour, his cap pushed back, and settled whatever argument the street brought him.</p>\n<p>Mr Wilfred the shoemaker was his closest friend. Wilfred’s only son, Marcus, had gone to New York four years before, and for the last year no letter had come. The men who gathered in Wilfred’s shop in the evenings, to sit among the smell of leather and glue, had their own opinion.</p>\n<p>“Wilfred, you boy forget you, man,” Boyie said one evening, slapping a sole on the bench. “Big city does do people so.”</p>\n<p>“Leave the man alone,” somebody else said, and they all laughed and changed the subject to cricket.</p>\n<p>Later, when the shop was closed, I saw Wilfred go and sit beside Captain on the wall. They spoke softly, the way my father and mother spoke at night.</p>\n<p>“Captain, I does dream about that boy every night,” Wilfred said. “I ain’t sleep good since Christmas.”</p>\n<p>Captain put a hand on his shoulder. “Marcus is a man, like any of we. He don’t have to write every week. He gone to make he life. The boy busy, that is all. Let we wait and see.”</p>\n<p>I was ten, and I did not understand. “Captain, Mr Wilfred son dead?” I asked him the next day.</p>\n<p>“Dead? Who tell you so? Go home and eat you food, boy.”</p>\n<p>Three weeks later my mother read the newspaper aloud at the kitchen table, as she did every Sunday:</p>\n<p style=\"margin-left:2em\"><b>BIRTHS</b><br>WILFRED – On 2nd March, at Brooklyn, New York, to Marcus and Janelle Wilfred, a son, Joseph Anthony. Grandson of Mr Clement Wilfred of Coconut Lane, San Juan. Thanks be to God.</p>\n<p>That evening the shoemaker’s shop was full. The men shook Wilfred’s hand and Boyie, who had said the boy forgot him, bought the first round. Captain did not come in. He sat on his wall and smiled.</p>\n<p>“I tell all you,” he said to nobody in particular. “He is a man like any of we.”</p><p>Read the extract from the short story “Captain’s Wall” carefully. In an essay of no more than 500 words:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identify and explain the writer’s use of dialectal variation in the extract;</li>\n<li>identify, with examples, TWO grammatical features of the dialectal variety used by the character Captain;</li>\n<li>explain the writer’s use of any TWO language registers in the extract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (20/20)</h4><p>The extract “Captain’s Wall”, set on Coconut Lane in San Juan, Trinidad, relates how the residents respond when Mr Wilfred’s son in New York stops writing. The writer uses dialectal variation, Creole grammar and a range of registers to make this community and its relationships authentic.</p>\n<p>The writer uses dialectal variation by narrating in Standard English while the characters speak Trinidadian Creole English. The adult narrator, looking back, uses Standard English, as in “Everybody on Coconut Lane called him Captain”, which signals distance and reflection and makes the story accessible to a wide readership. <span>In contrast, the dialogue is in Creole, for example Boyie’s “Big city does do people so”, because this is the natural language of the lane.</span> Even the narrator, as a ten-year-old, uses Creole when he asks, “Captain, Mr Wilfred son dead?”, which shows that the child belongs to the community he later describes in Standard English. The variation therefore creates realism, establishes the Trinidadian setting and reveals the characters’ social identity and close-knit relationships.</p>\n<p>Captain’s speech displays several grammatical features of Trinidadian Creole. <span>The first is the use of the subject pronoun in the object position</span>, as in “He is a man, like any of we” and “Let we wait and see”, where Standard English would require the object pronoun “us”. The second is the use of the subject pronoun to mark possession: in “He gone to make he life”, “he” replaces the possessive “his”. The same sentence also shows the past participle used without an auxiliary, “he gone” instead of “he has gone”. Captain further uses the base verb with a third person singular subject, “He don’t have to write”, rather than “doesn’t”, and the zero copula in “The boy busy”, where the verb “is” is omitted.</p>\n<p>The writer also uses different registers to reflect the relationships between characters. <span>The intimate register is used between Captain and Wilfred</span>, who are close friends. Sitting alone on the wall at night, Wilfred confides, “I does dream about that boy every night”, and Captain responds with a reassuring hand on his shoulder. This private sharing of feelings, which Wilfred does not reveal to the men in the shop, shows deep trust. By contrast, the casual register is used among the men in the shoemaker’s shop. Their talk is relaxed and teasing, as in “you boy forget you, man”, and they quickly “changed the subject to cricket”. These men interact regularly but lack the close personal bond that Wilfred shares with Captain. The writer’s use of these two registers highlights that Wilfred’s grief can only be shared with his closest friend.</p>\n<p>In conclusion, through the contrast between Standard English narration and Creole dialogue, Captain’s distinctive grammar, and the shift from casual to intimate talk, the writer presents a convincing Caribbean community in which language reveals identity, closeness and care.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Dialectal variation</b> (4/4): Both varieties identified with examples, and their purposes (realism, setting, identity, relationships) explained.</li><li><b>Two grammatical features</b> (4/4): Subject pronoun in object position and subject pronoun for possession, each correctly named and exemplified from Captain’s speech.</li><li><b>Two registers</b> (4/4): Intimate and casual registers each identified, linked to the relationship and supported from the extract.</li><li><b>Organization</b> (4/4): One paragraph per sub-task in question order, with an introduction, transitions and a linking conclusion.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (4/4): Accurate Standard English with correct linguistic terminology throughout.</li></ul>"},{"t":"essay","title":"Question 3 · Module 3: Promoting online banking to senior citizens","q":"<p><b>Practice extract (written to match the 2025 task)</b></p><h4>Briefing Note: Seniors Go Digital Project</h4>\n<p><b>From:</b> Chairperson, Bayleaf Hill Community Action Group<br><b>To:</b> Project Team<br><b>Date:</b> 6 January 2025</p>\n<p>Our local credit union, Island Unity Credit Union, has asked us to help increase online banking among members aged 60 and over. Its recent survey of 320 senior members found the following.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Only 18 per cent use online or mobile banking; 82 per cent still visit a branch at least twice a month.</li>\n<li>The average senior spends about $25 on taxis or bus fares for each branch visit and waits roughly 50 minutes in line.</li>\n<li>64 per cent own a smartphone, but most use it only for calls, WhatsApp and Facebook.</li>\n<li>Main reasons for not banking online: fear of fraud or “pressing the wrong button” (58 per cent); not knowing how to start (47 per cent); poor eyesight or small screens (21 per cent); preferring to speak to a person (39 per cent).</li>\n<li>71 per cent said they would try online banking if “someone patient showed them how”.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Resources available:</b> the community centre (free on weekday mornings), twelve sixth-form volunteers from Bayleaf Hill Secondary, two credit union staff members for one week, the Senior Fellowship that meets at St Peter’s Church every Wednesday, a slot on the local radio station’s Sunday morning programme, and a small budget for prizes.</p>\n<p><b>Target:</b> 60 seniors registered and completing one transaction online by the end of the two-week campaign.</p><p>You are a member of a community action group that has been given the responsibility of informing senior citizens in your community about the benefits of online banking and persuading them to make greater use of it. Read the briefing note carefully. In no more than 500 words:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>clearly state ONE message point you would communicate to the senior citizens about the benefits of online banking, and explain why it suits this audience;</li>\n<li>explain THREE separate promotional strategies that could be used to motivate the senior citizens to engage with online banking;</li>\n<li>specify a timetable of activities that could be implemented over a two-week period to motivate the senior citizens to use online banking.</li>\n</ul>\n<p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","points":20,"fb":"<h4>Exemplar response (19/20)</h4><p><b>Proposal: Seniors Go Digital – Two-Week Campaign</b></p>\n<p>The message point our group will communicate is: <span>“Bank from your own armchair: online banking saves you the bus fare, the long line and the trip, while you stay in control of your own money.”</span> This message suits our senior citizens because it addresses their real experience. According to the credit union survey, the average senior spends about $25 and waits roughly 50 minutes for each branch visit, so the savings in money, time and physical effort are immediate and personal. The phrase “stay in control” also responds to their fear of fraud by presenting online banking as a way to remain independent rather than as a risk.</p>\n<p>The first promotional strategy is in-person training at the community centre. Credit union staff and sixth-form volunteers would guide seniors, one-to-one, through downloading the app, logging in and checking a balance on their own phones. <span>Since 71 per cent said they would try online banking if someone patient showed them how, hands-on practice with trusted experts would build the confidence they currently lack.</span></p>\n<p>The second strategy is family and peer mentoring through the Senior Fellowship at St Peter’s Church. Each senior would be paired with a grandchild, relative or a fellow member who already banks online. Because many seniors prefer dealing with a person, learning from a familiar face in a trusted setting would reduce anxiety and provide ongoing support at home.</p>\n<p>The third strategy is recorded video tutorials with large text, shared on WhatsApp and promoted on the Sunday radio programme. Short clips could be replayed at any time to reinforce learning, and they would especially help seniors who have limited mobility. <span>To add motivation, every senior who completes a first online transaction would enter a prize draw for a grocery voucher.</span></p>\n<p><b>Two-week timetable of activities</b></p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Days</th><th>Activity</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Week 1, Mon</td><td>Launch on radio; message point announced; WhatsApp tutorial 1 (downloading the app) released.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Week 1, Tue–Thu</td><td>Morning training sessions at the community centre with credit union staff: registration and logging in.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Week 1, Wed</td><td>Senior Fellowship meeting: seniors paired with mentors; fraud-safety talk.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Week 1, Fri</td><td>Tutorial 2 (checking balances and paying a bill) released; mentors practise with seniors at home over the weekend.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Week 2, Mon–Tue</td><td>Follow-up training at the centre for those who need extra help; first online bill payments made.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Week 2, Wed</td><td>Fellowship meeting: seniors share success stories; mentors check progress.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Week 2, Thu</td><td>Tutorial 3 (transfers and security tips) released.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Week 2, Fri</td><td>Prize draw broadcast on radio; registrations counted against the target of 60.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><span>This timetable uses all three strategies in a logical sequence, moving from introduction to practice and finally to independent use, so that motivation is sustained throughout the two weeks.</span></p>\n<p>We are confident this plan will help our senior citizens bank safely and independently.</p><h4>How the marks were earned</h4><ul><li><b>Message point</b> (2/2): Relevant, customised message point with a clear explanation of why it suits senior citizens.</li><li><b>Three promotional strategies</b> (6/6): Three separate strategies, each identified (1 mark) and explained in terms of how it motivates seniors (1 mark).</li><li><b>Two-week timetable</b> (4/4): A dated sequence of activities that deploys all three strategies across the full two weeks.</li><li><b>Organization</b> (3/4): Logical order with headings and clear links, but the one-sentence conclusion is abrupt and does not return to the target or message point, so 1 mark was lost here.</li><li><b>Expression</b> (4/4): Clear, accurate and persuasive Standard English suited to a community proposal.</li></ul>"}]}]}];
